// =============================================================================
//  Armageddon as Aura  -  celestialrayone.armageddon-aura
//
//  Makes skills that use skills.txt srvdofunc 124 (Armageddon, and in D2R 3.3
//  also Hurricane) bootstrap correctly when they are driven as an aura rather
//  than cast by hand.
//
//  THE DEFECT
//
//  SKILLS_SrvDo124 opens with a cast-context gate:
//
//      used = UNITS_GetUsedSkill(unit);          // [[unit+0x100]+0x18]
//      if (!used || SkillId(used) != skillId) return 0;
//
//  Nothing on the aura side ever installs that pointer. The item_aura
//  (itemstatcost 151) driver calls the server skill do-handler with its
//  itemCast argument set, and the do-handler skips its entire used-skill
//  resolution block on that path. Same for the periodic re-fire of a learned
//  aura and for the re-fire after a respawn. So 124 is called on schedule,
//  self-rejects, and never creates the aura state nor schedules the
//  ACTIVESTATE periodic event that actually performs the skill.
//
//  THE FIX
//
//  Wrap 124. If the used-skill pointer already names this skill we are on a
//  real cast: step aside, the stock handler runs untouched. Otherwise resolve
//  the skill from the unit's own skill list with the SAME resolver and the
//  SAME selection flag that srvdofunc 146 uses (0x33DD40, selectionFlag =
//  false), install it as the used skill for the duration of one call, run the
//  stock handler through the trampoline, then restore the previous pointer.
//
//  Resolving it exactly the way 146 does matters: 124 stores the periodic
//  seed at skillNode+0x24 and 146 reads it back from the node its own
//  resolver returns. A different node means the seed lands somewhere 146
//  never looks.
//
//  SEQUENCE FREEZE
//
//  Stock 124 sets the unit's "skill action taken this frame" flag
//  (unit+0x124 bit 0x40) immediately after its gate. The per-frame animation
//  code refuses to advance the current sequence while that bit is set. Fine
//  for a one-shot cast; fatal for an aura, which re-sets it every few frames
//  and freezes a player mid-leap or mid-whirlwind forever. On the borrowed
//  path only, this plugin restores the bit to whatever it was before the
//  call. Real casts never reach that code.
//
//  THE AURA REFRESH THREW AWAY THE PENDING METEOR ROUND
//
//  Stock 124 ends by deleting every pending ACTIVESTATE event (type 5) of the
//  skill and scheduling a new one at frame + Param4 (delete call 0x5760FA,
//  set call 0x576124, Param4 read from record +0x1C4 at 0x5760DB). Right for
//  a cast. As an aura, 124 runs again on every aura refresh (every perdelay
//  frames, floored at 5, on an aligned frame grid: 0x434CC0, 0x437230), so a
//  meteor round only survives if it falls due before the next refresh.
//
//  Events due on the same frame run in the order they were scheduled:
//  0x48B5C0 hands 0x48C4D0 the bucket's oldest node (0x48C130 slot) and it
//  walks toward newer ones. With one item and perdelay == Param4 the meteor
//  round is scheduled inside the item tick, before that tick re-arms itself,
//  so it runs first and survives. With two items on the same skill, the
//  first item's re-arm is scheduled before the round (the second item's
//  refresh deleted and rescheduled it), runs first and deletes it again.
//  Every round is lost. perdelay < Param4 loses every round with any number
//  of items, and perdelay a multiple of Param4 loses the round that lands on
//  a refresh frame.
//
//  On the borrowed path only, the plugin hides the pending type 5 event from
//  124's delete (type byte set to 0xFD for the length of the call), then
//  removes the round 124 scheduled with the engine's own delete and gives
//  the kept round that round's skill level and cast id. The kept round keeps
//  its frame and its place in that frame's queue, so meteors fall every
//  Param4 frames whatever perdelay is and however many items grant the aura.
//
//  Ported from the ESR 2.4 byte patch (hook 363780 -> 191-byte cave at
//  386C60). No cave is needed here; the loader owns the trampoline, which
//  also frees 386C60 and removes the srvdofunc-73 dispatch landmine that
//  cave sat on.
// =============================================================================

// Event node layout. Proven by EVENT_SetEvent's store block (0x48BF55):
//   mov [rax+4],ebp (frame) / mov [rax+18h],ecx (customId) /
//   mov [rax+1Ch],ecx (customParam) / mov [rax+20h],ecx (third argument)
// and by EVENT_Delete's node walk (0x48B8F0):
//   mov rbp,[rbx+38h] (next event of this unit) / cmp [rbx+8],r15 (unit) /
//   movzx eax,byte [rbx] (type) / cmp [rbx+18h],r14d (customId, 0 = any)

// Type 5 = ACTIVESTATE. Its handler (0x435520) runs the aurastate's states.txt
// srvactivefunc (146 for Armageddon) with customId as the skill id,
// customParam as the skill level and the third argument as the cast id.
// 124 and 146 both use the skill id as customId.

// EVENT_Delete(game, unit, eventType, customId). Called through its entry.
// The entry itself is not fingerprinted because the aura-reactivation plugin
// inline-hooks it (its hook passes every type 5 delete to the stock code);
// the node walk below it is fingerprinted instead.

// mov r12d,[rax+1C4h] / mov ebx,[rbp+170h] / mov r9d,r14d / mov r8d,5 /
// mov rdx,rsi / mov rcx,rbp / add ebx,r12d / call EVENT_Delete /
// mov rcx,rsi / call UNITS_GetCastId / mov [rsp+30h],eax / mov r9d,ebx /
// mov [rsp+28h],r13d / mov r8d,5 / mov rdx,rsi / mov [rsp+20h],r14d /
// mov rcx,rbp / call EVENT_SetEvent

struct NativeSite {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* expected;
    std::uint32_t       expectedSize;
    const char*         name;
};

// Every fingerprint above already proved this is the expected image, so
// reading the dispatch table is safe by this point. Slot 124 must resolve
// to the function we are about to hook, otherwise skills.txt srvdofunc 124
// dispatches somewhere else and hooking it would achieve nothing.

// NOTE: there is deliberately no process-wide or session-wide lock here.
//
// An earlier revision took a named mutex in the Local\ namespace to refuse a
// second copy of this plugin. That namespace is per session, not per process,
// so any D2R process that had not finished exiting still owned the name and
// the next launch refused itself for no reason. The observed symptom was the
// Extensions tab showing the plugin as errored on one launch and loading
// cleanly on the next, with nothing wrong with the game image at all.
//
// Nothing is lost by removing it. D2RLoader already refuses to load two
// plugins with the same id, including across the global and mod scopes, and
// InstallInlineHook below refuses a second hook on the same function, which
// this plugin already reports with a clear message. Those are per-process
// guards on the thing that actually matters, which is the hook.

// Ascendancies
//
// The ascendancy system in one plugin:
//   - the Ascendancy window: a plugin panel, one page per ascendancy (portrait,
//     the five tiers, the next-tier line), with Ascend / Renounce Ascendancy.
//   - an "Ascendancy" entry on the character sheet: a + while the Orb of
//     Creation is consumed and no ascendancy is chosen, a - afterwards.
//   - a "New ascension tier unlocked" notice on the HUD.
//   - the host: ascend and renounce requests, tier upgrades on boss kills and
//     Primordial Soulstones, the old Just Killed kill credit (Kill Ledger,
//     Rathma's Eternal Skull), and the one-time migration of Ascendancy Stones.
// The tier bonuses are applied with the game's own property code, the same way
// monprop.txt applies monster properties, into a stat list of their own state
// on the character. Nothing of it is saved: the plugin builds it from the
// saved stats (which ascendancy, which tiers) every time.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Saved stats (itemstatcost.txt, Saved 1, CSvBits above 0)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   ascendancy_stat   which ascendancy, 1-based [[ascendancy]] block, 0 none
//   progress_stat     bit 0-4 tiers 1-5, bit 5 the Lich King bonus, bit 6 the
//                     character has ascended at least once (the sheet keeps a -)
//   soulstone_stat    Primordial Soulstones collected, up to soulstone_cap
//   orb_stat          the Orb of Creation is consumed (the mod's softcode sets
//                     it; renouncing sets it back to 0)
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Bonus list (host)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   3D4620 (target, property, state, flags) is the property call monprop uses
//   (0, null, unit, null, 0, 0, property, 0, 40h, null). With a state id its
//   property functions find or create that state's own stat list on the unit
//   (3D5230) and write into it, and the stat change runtime arms auras and
//   on-hit events as for monprop. A property is 16 bytes: int16 id, int16 type
//   (0 property, 1 property group), int32 param, int32 min, int32 max.
//   Property codes resolve through the game's own name linkers, as the cube
//   and monprop rows resolve theirs: Properties at data tables +270h, then
//   PropertyGroups at +278h, A121F0 (linker, name, 0) -> id or below 0.
//   Removing the list: 2F5940 (unit, state) finds it, 3354C0 turns the state
//   off, 2F7920 unlinks it, 2F4180 (data context, list) frees it.
//   Death deletes every aura event (type 8/9) but not this list (its owner type
//   is 4), so the list is rebuilt after a resurrect. A rebuild takes two frames:
//   the state goes off in one, and in the next the old list is swapped for the
//   new one with the state back on, so the clients always receive the new stats
//   and the character's totals never drop for a frame.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Kill credit (host)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The old Just Killed curses applied their state through 433D20, the timed
//   state effect (D2RCore), with a 30h-byte block: +0 source unit, +8 target
//   unit, +10h skill, +14h level, +18h frames, +1Ch stat (-1 none), +20h stat
//   value, +24h state, +28h remove callback (null: the default one). Open
//   Wounds (5842F3) passes its stat in the block the same way. The plugin
//   calls it with the curse's own skill, level, state, frames and kill_check
//   value for every living player in the level of the dead boss.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Native pieces, D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image, all checked byte for byte at
// load. Nothing is installed when a check fails. No code is hooked.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   3D4620  property apply          A121F0  name linker lookup
//   300A90  data tables of context  34A0E0  data context of unit (+1BDh)
//   433D20  timed state effect (thunk into D2RCore)
//   2F5940  stat list by state      2F7920  unlink   2F4180  free
//   3354C0  state toggle            34B440  unit room
//   2EFC10  level id of room        34C2C0  unit is dead
//   846170  top-level panel by name (the character sheet)
//   856220  child widget by name    86DD50  text box set text
//   86EFB0  button enable           870200  button frame fields
//   1A0C00  play sound by name      846020  UI state (hovered, focused)
//   2F5020 / 2F48C0 / 2F7D10  stat total, own value read, own value write
//   34B9D0 unit type   34A330 unit id   8B2D0 / 9A480 local player
//   48FE80 server unit lookup   485290 / 485320 / 484CDB the client registry

// 3D4620, the property apply: mov rax, rsp / push rbx / push rsi / push r12 /
// push r13 / push r15 / sub rsp, 60h / mov rsi, [rsp+0C0h] (the property, 7th
// argument) / mov rbx, r9 / mov r15, r8 (the target) / mov r12, rdx /
// mov r13d, ecx / test rsi, rsi.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
    bool                callable;
};

using AssignPropertyFn = std::int64_t(__fastcall*)(std::uint32_t type, void* unit, void* target, void* mods,
    std::int32_t index, std::int32_t value, const NativeProperty* property, std::int32_t state, std::int32_t flags,
    void* list);

// Attack Rating
//
// Replaces the attack rating versus defense hit-chance formula with a
// configurable curve and configurable minimum and maximum hit chance. One
// formula drives the server-side hit roll and both hit-chance lines on the
// Character Screen, so the screen shows the chance the roll uses.
//
// Port of the D2R 2.4 attack rating overhaul (combat hook 329CB9, screen hook
// BFE7F8, server-accurate screen inputs BFE796, live defense export 1A2B37) to
// D2R 3.3. Every address, register and stack slot below was read out of the
// live D2RLoader.exe image and disassembled before being relied on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Combat: the hit roll, sub_14044BA60 (attacker, defender, skillAr, bMissile)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Shared by every attacker and defender. Player attackers: AR = 3483C0
//   (tohit + 5*dex - 35 + charstats ToHitFactor), 4503C0 applies stats
//   115/116/123/124, then the AR% bucket (skill ToHit, mastery, stat 119 and
//   the stat 179 per-monster-type bonus at 44BBC4). Monster attackers:
//   skillAr + tohit + 5*dex, times stat 119.
//
//     44BD0E  lea eax,[rdx+rbp]         AR
//     44BD11  .. 44BD2E                 negative Def moves onto AR, negative
//                                       AR onto Def, both floored at 0
//     44BD31  lea ecx,[rax+rbx]         <- hook: eax = AR, ebx = Def, both >= 0
//     44BD38  imul eax,eax,64h / idiv   100 * AR / (AR + Def)
//     44BD40  .. 44BD62                 2 * ratio * aLvl / (aLvl + dLvl), 5..95
//     44BD65  mov rcx,rsi               <- rejoin with the chance in edi
//     44BD68  call 34A1E0               seed, then roll = seed % 100
//     44BDBC  cmp ebx,edi / jge         hit when roll < chance
//
//   rsi = attacker, r14 = defender, r13d = attacker level (stat 12),
//   [rsp+38h] = defender level. ebp is 0 from 44BD17/44BD1D and the
//   prevent-heal block at 44BE41/44BE50 relies on that; the C++ call keeps
//   it. ebx is overwritten at 44BD6F. No branch from outside the function
//   body lands in 44BD32..44BD64, and [rsp+20h]/[rsp+28h] are only ever
//   outgoing argument slots.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Character Screen, average chance to hit: sub_141514700 (slotAr)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The tooltip builder 14E7FE0 passes slotAr = 28A590(player, slot). Through
//   27FB60 and 28D160 that is 3483C0(player) * (1 + (mastery + stat 119 +
//   skill ToHit) / 100).
//
//     15147C8  mov [rbp-31h],eax        monster class (monstats row)
//     15147E9  movsx edi,word [...]     monster level, raised to the terror
//                                       zone level at 151481B
//     1514867  call 3A26C0              monster archetype, defense at [rbp-1]
//     1514873  mov ebx,[rbp-1]          Def
//     151486C  .. 1514898               screen-only Def = 10 * Def / 12
//     151489A  mov rcx,r12              <- hook: r14d = slotAr, ebx = Def,
//                                       r12 = player, edi = monster level
//     15148AA  .. 151493B               players: slotAr += ToHitFactor again
//     151493E  .. 15149B3               restores r15,rsi,rbx,r13,r12,rdi,
//                                       ratio and level scaling
//     15149C0  .. 15149D3               5..95
//     15149D6  mov rcx,[rbp+2Fh]        <- rejoin: cookie, add rsp, pops, ret
//
//   The hook restores the six registers itself before jumping to 15149D6.
//   The only branches into 151489A..15149D5 target 151489A itself.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Character Screen, average chance to hit you: sub_1415149F0 ()
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Called from 14E8242; the result goes to the formatter 1514CA0.
//
//     1514A59  mov [rbp-71h],ecx        player Def = 347B10 + stat 33
//     1514A9E  movsx r13d,ax            monster class
//     1514ABA  movsx ebx,word [...]     monster level, terror zone max after
//     1514B34  call 3A26C0              monster archetype, AR at [rbp-5]
//     1514BF2  .. 1514C1D               screen-only AR = 10 * AR / 15
//     1514C1F  mov r8d,[rbp-71h]        <- hook: edx = AR, r14 = player
//     1514C72  mov rcx,[rbp+27h]        <- rejoin: the epilogue restores all
//
//   Branches into the replaced bytes come from 1514BF9 and 1514BFF, both to
//   1514C1F itself; 1514AB4 targets the rejoin point.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Character Screen clamps
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   14E801A  jne                                   hides the line at 0%
//   14E8066  cmp esi,5 / mov esi,5 / mov eax,5Fh   average chance to hit
//   1514CBD  cmp ecx,5 / mov edi,5 / mov eax,5Fh   average chance to hit you
//   The immediates take min_chance and max_chance and the jne becomes a jmp,
//   so a 0% chance is shown instead of dropping the line. The witnesses mask
//   these fields and the combat clamp immediates at 44BD45/44BD57, because
//   Hit Chance Bounds (ruffneckk-hit-chance-0-100.json) may own them.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Server-accurate average chance to hit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   4503C0 (attacker, defender, &AR, &Def) reads only the defender's type
//   (+0), class (+4), MonsterData (+10h, null-checked) and data-table
//   context (+1BDh), through 38E870, 3AEFF0, 3AF240, 3AF190 and 3AF2C0. A
//   zeroed stand-in with type 1, the screen's class and the player's context
//   byte is therefore a plain monster of that class. Checked again against
//   D2RLoader 1.3.0: the five callees still read nothing else of the
//   defender, and the attacker stats go through the loader's stat reader.
//
//   The stat 179 bonus is taken from the same place the hit roll takes it.
//   D2RLoader 1.3.0 replaced the vanilla layer loop (2F84B0 + 449FB0) with
//   one call into D2RCore; the old loop after it is dead code:
//
//     44BBA8  movsx eax,word [rax+50h]    monstats MonType
//     44BBAF  jle   44BC0A                no bonus when MonType <= 0
//     44BBB1  mov   r9d,eax               MonType
//     44BBB4  movzx ecx,byte [rsi+1BDh]   attacker data-table context
//     44BBBB  mov   rdx,rsi               attacker
//     44BBBE  mov   r8d,0B3h              stat 179
//     44BBC4  call  [rip+disp32]          D2RCore!ReadWideMonsterTypeBonus
//     44BBCA  add   edi,eax               into the AR% bucket
//     44BBCC  jmp   loader trampoline     which jumps back to 44BC0A
//
//   disp32 and the trampoline rel32 belong to the loader and move between
//   loader builds, so the witness skips them. In the dumped 1.3.0 process
//   the slot that call reads holds D2RCore!ReadWideMonsterTypeBonus.
//
//   The plugin does not read that slot. At plugin load Windows does not
//   report it as part of the game image's mapping (1.1.1 refused with exactly
//   that, D2RLoader log 2026-09-15), so its memory state at load is not
//   something to rely on. The plugin calls the same export directly instead:
//   it resolves ReadWideMonsterTypeBonus from D2RCore.dll by name, follows
//   the export's jmp to the implementation, and checks the implementation's
//   entry byte for byte before the character screen part is armed. That
//   entry proves the four argument registers the hit roll loads:
//
//     push r15 / r14 / r13 / r12 / rsi / rdi / rbx, sub rsp,20h
//     mov  esi,r9d         MonType, 32 bits
//     mov  ebx,ecx         data-table context, 32 bits
//     test rdx,rdx         unit
//     cmp  r8d,8000h       stat id, 32 bits
//
//   AR = slotAr * (B + flat) / B + (B + flat) * p179 / 100 with B = 3483C0,
//   which drops the doubled ToHitFactor and adds what the roll adds.
//   Per-instance flags (superunique, unique) cannot exist on a monster type;
//   the live defense below supplies them after the first hit.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Live values
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Each roll by a player against a monster stores that monster's class and
//   final Def; each roll by a monster against a player stores its class and
//   final AR. The screen uses them while its monster class matches.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Hook shape
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Each hook is an in-place rewrite that loads the arguments, calls a near
//   relay (jmp qword ptr [rip+0]) into C++ and jumps on. The call is made
//   from the host function's own frame, so its unwind data covers it. On
//   unload the relays are first pointed at native stubs that do what the
//   original code did, then the original bytes go back.

// The game's own handling of negative inputs: a negative defense is added to
// the attack rating, a negative attack rating to the defense, then both are
// floored at 0.

// RVA 0x44BB13, 190 bytes. Player AR, 4503C0, mastery, stat 119, the monstats
// MonType and the D2RCore stat 179 call (proves the natives). Ends on the loader's
// jump back; the dead vanilla loop after it is not checked.

struct Skip {
    std::uint32_t offset;
    std::uint32_t size;
};

struct Window {
    const char*         name;
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const Skip*         skips;
    std::uint32_t       skipCount;
};

struct Witness {
    Part   part;
    Window window;
};

struct Hook {
    const char*         name;
    Part                part;
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* code;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    std::uint32_t       rel32Offset;
};

struct Field {
    const char*   name;
    std::uint64_t rva;
    std::uint32_t size;
    FieldKind     kind;
};

// The relay page is deliberately kept: a thread may be inside a jump
// through it right now, and any site that could not be restored still
// needs it.

// Aura Percent Life Damage (celestialrayone.aura-percent-life)
//
// Every skill whose skills.txt srvdofunc is 66 (Holy Fire, Holy Shock in
// vanilla) or 81 (Holy Freeze in vanilla) can deal damage based on the current
// life of each target it pulses over, the way Static Field (srvdofunc 20) does.
// A calc column of the row (calc4 by default) holds the percent. On every
// pulse, for every target, the aura's element damage becomes the higher of its
// own EMin..EMax roll and Static Field's percent amount for that target. A row
// whose column is blank or evaluates to 0 or less stays vanilla.
//
// Everything below was read out of the live D2RLoader 1.3.1 image (D2R 3.3,
// base 0x140000000). Every byte the plugin relies on is checked at load and
// nothing is installed when one differs.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Static Field, the rules copied here
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   srvdofunc table 0x238EA00: slot 20 -> 0x5546B0 Static Field, slot 66 ->
//   0x5630E0 Holy Fire / Holy Shock, slot 81 -> 0x563720 Holy Freeze. No
//   other slot points at the 66 or 81 body.
//
//   Body 0x5546B0 (checked bytes 0x554709..0x5547DF):
//     0x554704  call 48FF40(&ctx, game, caster)   difficulty context
//     0x55471F  call 300830(game+106h, ctx[0])    difficultylevels.txt row
//     0x554730  calc2 (+194h) -> flat minimum, 0x554762 calc1 (+190h) ->
//               percent, both through the evaluator 0x3B5160
//     0x554773  cmp byte [ctx+1],1 / je: StaticFieldMin (+68h) is used
//               unless ctx[1] is 1
//     0x5547BE  EType (+284h) -> element
//   Per-target callback 0x5556A0:
//     life = stat 6 >> 8; a target below 1 is skipped.
//     With a StaticFieldMin, a target at or below maxlife (0x2F4D20) >> 8
//     x StaticFieldMin / 100 takes nothing.
//     percent = life x calc1 / 100, capped at life - 1, so the percent part
//     alone never kills; damage = max(calc2, percent << 8).
//     A negative resist for the element (stat id from AddElementalDamage
//     0x437980) divides the damage by (100 - resist) / 100, so negative
//     resistance never amplifies it.
//     Every x / y goes through the game's overflow-safe percentage routine
//     (MONSTERUNIQUE_CalculatePercentage), copied as Percentage() below.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where 66 and 81 take it
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Both bodies roll EMin..EMax once per pulse (0x437D00, into the EType slot
//   of a damage packet). Their per-target callbacks, 0x561280 (66) and
//   0x561480 (81), copy that packet for each target (0x4494B0) and then run
//   the same block, checked at load:
//     mov r9d,[rbx+4]        skill id
//     lea rax,[rsp+50h|60h] / mov [rsp+28h],rax      &packet copy
//     mov r8,rdi             target
//     mov eax,[rbx+8] / mov rdx,r14 / mov rcx,rsi / mov [rsp+20h],eax
//     call 560F60            (game, caster, target, skill id, level, &copy)
//     lea rax,[same copy] / mov r9d,1 / ... / call 44CE80  ExecuteEvents
//   at 0x56139E (66) and 0x5615F4 (81). 0x560F60 is the vanilla close-range
//   aura bonus, limited to vanilla skill ids 102, 114, 118, 119, 365, 368 and
//   369 and a no-op for every other row; those two calls are its only code
//   references. Its return value is never read.
//
//   So one inline hook on 0x560F60 sees every target of both srvdofuncs with
//   the caster, the skill id and level, and the target's own packet copy, right
//   before the damage is resolved. The hook calls the original first, then
//   raises the element slot of that copy to the percent amount when it is
//   higher than the roll. Nothing else in the packet changes, so the aura keeps
//   its own ResultFlags, HitFlags, HitClass and length.
//
//   Not touched, and no byte overlap with: holy-freeze-boss-damage (0x56150E,
//   0x561675), soft-hits (0x56357C, 0x563C0B), aura-zero-branch (0x5616F0),
//   ExecuteEvents hooks at 0x44CE80 (they run after this plugin's change).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The element
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   EType (+284h) picks the slot and the resist stat, exactly as
//   AddElementalDamage 0x437980 maps them (jump table 0x437B1C, case bodies
//   0x437A37..0x437AF7, both checked):
//     1 fire  +20h  stat 39     2 ltng +2Ch stat 41    3 mag  +30h stat 37
//     4 cold  +34h  stat 43     5 pois +38h stat 45    11 burn +24h no resist
//     12 frze +34h  stat 43     0 and above 12: physical +18h, no resist
//     6, 7, 8, 9 (life, mana, stamina, stun): no life damage, skipped
//   EType 10 (random) was already resolved by the pulse's roll to one of the
//   four elements in 0x1D195F8 (fire, ltng, cold, pois, checked); the plugin
//   uses whichever of those four slots holds the roll.
//   With dynamic-etype loaded, the row holds the cast's decided element while
//   the do-handler runs, which is when this hook is reached.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Witnesses, read out of the image and checked at load
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   AuraBonusEntry            0x560F60 prologue, the hook's expected bytes:
//                             mov rax,rsp / mov [rax+8],rcx / push rbx, rsi,
//                             rdi, r13 / sub rsp,58h. No relative operand.
//   FireShockCallWindow       0x561380..0x5613C1, the call block in 66's
//                             callback quoted in the header.
//   FreezeCallWindow          0x5615D6..0x561617, the same block in 81's.
//   StaticFieldBody           0x554709..0x5547DF, Static Field's argument
//                             setup: calc1/calc2/EType offsets and the floor.
//   ElementSwitch             0x437A37..0x437AFA, AddElementalDamage's cases.
//   ElementJumpTable          0x437B1C, its twelve case RVAs (EType 1..12).
//   RandomElementTable        0x1D195F8, EType 10's four elements.
//   The rest are the entries of the functions this plugin calls, the same
//   bytes the other ESR plugins check for them.

// Static Field's floor, in its own order: the caster's difficulty context, the
// difficultylevels.txt row of ctx[0], no floor when ctx[1] is 1, then a target
// at or below maxlife x StaticFieldMin / 100 is left alone.

// =============================================================================
//  Aura Reactivation  -  celestialrayone.aura-reactivation
//
//  Item auras (itemstatcost 151, item_aura) for D2R 3.3 under D2RLoader 1.3.0:
//
//    1. MULTIPLE AURAS PER ITEM. An item or a charm can carry several item_aura
//       stats and every one of them stays active.
//    2. AURAS COME BACK AFTER A RESURRECT for items the player still carries
//       (charms, the charm inventory page, anything else still merged).
//
//  Every address below was read out of the dumped D2RLoader 1.3.0 image and
//  disassembled before being relied on. Re-verified against 1.3.1: the aura
//  start (0x581970) and aura tick (0x437230) bodies differ only in two calls
//  each into D2RLoader's own thunk table, which moved from 0x3E2B57E to
//  0x3E2B652. Full-body byte witnesses guard the
//  three functions the plugin changes, so a different build refuses cleanly.
//
//  Compatible with the perdelay floor static patch
//  (celestialrayone-perdelay-floor.json). That patch changes the two bytes at
//  0x4373D6 inside the aura tick (cmp eax,ebx -> test eax,eax), which this
//  plugin never touches, so the aura tick witness accepts either form there
//  and still checks every other byte of the body.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  HOW AN ITEM AURA RUNS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  One periodic event per aura, event type 9. EVENT_SetEvent (0x48B720 ->
//  0x48BE80) stores it on the unit's event list:
//
//      node + 0x00  type (byte)          node + 0x18  customId  = item unit id
//      node + 0x02  flags (word)         node + 0x1C  customParam = skill id
//      node + 0x38  next event of the same unit, list head at unit + 0x148
//
//  0x581970  ActivateItemAura(game, unit, itemId, skillId, level, castId)
//            delete (9, itemId) at 0x581A20, then SetEvent(9, .., itemId, skillId)
//  0x437230  the type 9 event handler (game, unit, itemId, skillId, castId)
//            re-arm: delete (9, itemId) at 0x4373EE, then SetEvent again
//            removal: delete (9, itemId) at 0x437444 (stat gone, unit dead...)
//  0x581AE0  DeactivateItemAura(game, unit, itemId, skillId)
//            clears the aura state, frees its stat list, delete (9, itemId)
//
//  D2RCore reimplements the stat-change callback (stat_change_runtime.cpp) and
//  calls the two functions above. During an item refresh transaction it also
//  restores an aura's timing itself: ActivateItemAura, then its own
//  delete (9, itemId) at D2RCore.dll+0x3CCDD0 (1.3.1), then SetEvent. The gear refresh
//  (0x470CA0) runs that transaction on every weapon swap, equip change and
//  zone change that refreshes items.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  THE MULTI AURA DEFECT
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  EVENT_DeleteByTypeId (0x48B890) matches type and customId only, and a
//  customId of 0 matches every event of that type. Every delete above passes
//  the item id, so removing or refreshing one aura removes the events of all
//  other auras on the same item. Monprop auras have no item, D2RCore passes id
//  0, and one monprop aura wipes every item aura on the unit.
//
//  THE FIX: every one of those deletes removes only the event whose item id AND
//  skill id match. The engine's own delete still does the work: for the length
//  of the call, every other type 9 event it would have matched is given a type
//  it cannot match (0xFE, event types are 0..14), then restored.
//
//      0x581A20, 0x4373EE, 0x437444  call retargeted to a stub in a page the
//          plugin allocates; the stub hands the skill id to the plugin
//          (ebp at 0x581A20, r14d at both handler sites; each register is
//          written exactly once, at 0x58197A / 0x437250, and only restored
//          in the epilogue afterwards)
//      0x581AE0  inline hook, a line-for-line copy of the 240-byte body with
//          the item-and-skill delete. Its skill id does not survive to the
//          stock delete call, so the body cannot simply be patched.
//      0x48B890  inline hook. Only the call returning to D2RCore+0x3CCDD6 is
//          converted; the skill id is the flush record's skill D2RCore itself
//          loaded into [rsp+0x58] at +0x3CCD92 (1.3.1 offsets; the block is
//          byte-identical to 1.3.0 apart from its three slot displacements). The 147-byte flush block and
//          its function-pointer slots are verified before this is enabled.
//          Every other caller runs the stock delete untouched.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  THE RESURRECT DEFECT
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Player death (0x42E7C0) deletes every type 8 and type 9 event (0x42E820,
//  0x42E832). Equipped gear goes to the corpse and its auras start again when
//  the corpse is picked up, because re-equipping changes the stats. Charms stay
//  merged, nothing changes their stats, and nothing starts their auras again.
//
//  THE FIX: after the resurrect handler (0x4B6160) succeeds, every item whose
//  stat list is still attached to the player gets ActivateItemAura called once
//  per item_aura layer that has no event, with the same arguments D2RCore uses
//  (item unit id, skill, the player's total item_aura level, a new cast id).
//
//  The layers are read from the item's own stat list, in D2RCore's stat store
//  format (D2RCore replaced the game's stat storage; the game's 8-byte
//  entries are no longer used):
//
//      statList = [item+88h]; array = statList + ([statList+1Ch] < 0 ? A8h : 30h)
//      array = {entries, count}; entry = 16 bytes:
//          +00  uint64 key = statId << 32 | layer     (sorted by key)
//          +08  int32  value
//
//  Proven by D2RCore!ReadWideUnitStat (1.3.1: export +0x831DE0 -> core
//  +0x3D8BD0, key built at +0x3D8C28, 16-byte binary search at +0x3D8C7F,
//  value read at +0x3D8CB0), checked byte for byte at load. D2RCore's attach
//  sets the child list's parent at [list+78h] (+0x3DA5A8), so
//  STATLIST_GetOwner (0x2F8120) still names the player, and
//  RemoveWideDeathStats skips item lists (cmp [list+8],4 at +0x3DADAC), so
//  charms stay attached through death.
//
//  Zone changes delete no item aura events of their own. The only loss there
//  was the refresh transaction above, which the multi aura fix covers.
//
//  Left stock on purpose: 0x46F858, inside the item stat removal (0x46F710),
//  deletes every aura event of an item whose stats are leaving the unit. All
//  of that item's auras are going away there, so deleting by item is right.
//  In a refresh it first saves the item's aura timing (itemData+0x3C, the cast
//  id in item+0x12C), which D2RCore restores per aura through the flush.
//
//  Console command "aurareactivation" shows what is installed and counters.
//  Settings: d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.aura-reactivation.toml
// =============================================================================

// RELAY-EMITTER-BEGIN
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Relay page layout. One page the plugin allocates within rel32 reach of the
//  image; nothing inside D2R's own code is used as a cave.
//
//      +0x000  UNWIND_INFO for the common frame (sub rsp,38h)
//      +0x008  UNWIND_INFO for the stubs (no frame)
//      +0x010  RUNTIME_FUNCTION table, 4 entries
//      +0x100  common:  sub rsp,38h
//                       mov [rsp+20h],r10d      ; skill id  -> 5th argument
//                       mov [rsp+28h],r11d      ; site      -> 6th argument
//                       mov rax,<bridge>
//                       call rax
//                       add rsp,38h
//                       ret                      ; back to the stock call site
//      +0x140  start stub:   mov r10d,ebp  / mov r11d,0 / jmp common
//      +0x160  tick stub:    mov r10d,r14d / mov r11d,1 / jmp common
//      +0x180  remove stub:  mov r10d,r14d / mov r11d,2 / jmp common
//
//  The stock call sites reach a stub with the stock arguments already in
//  rcx, rdx, r8d and r9d. r10 and r11 are volatile and dead at a call. The
//  site has rsp aligned before its call, so the common frame's 38h keeps the
//  bridge call aligned too.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const std::uint8_t commonHead[]{
    0x48, 0x83, 0xEC, 0x38,        // sub  rsp,38h
    0x44, 0x89, 0x54, 0x24, 0x20,  // mov  [rsp+20h],r10d
    0x44, 0x89, 0x5C, 0x24, 0x28,  // mov  [rsp+28h],r11d
    0x48, 0xB8,                    // mov  rax,imm64
};

const std::uint8_t commonTail[]{
    0xFF, 0xD0,                    // call rax
    0x48, 0x83, 0xC4, 0x38,        // add  rsp,38h
    0xC3,                          // ret
};

// STAT-SCAN-BEGIN
// D2RCore stat store entry: 16 bytes, uint64 key = statId << 32 | layer at +0,
// int32 value at +8. Calls visit(skillId, value) for every item_aura (151)
// layer with a positive value.

struct CallSitePatch {
    std::uint64_t                rva;
    const std::uint8_t*          stock;
    std::size_t                  stubOffset;
    std::array<std::uint8_t, 5>  written;
    bool                         applied;
};

// DEACTIVATE-BEGIN
// 0x581AE0, instruction for instruction:
//   581B17  record = GetSkillsTxtRecord(game[106h], skillId); null -> return
//   581B37  record[24h] & aura mask == 0 -> return
//   581B43  record[A0h] < 0 -> return
//   581B60  state >= GetStateCount(game[106h]) -> return (no delete either)
//   581B71  ToggleState(unit, state, 0)
//   581B80  statList = GetStateStatList(unit, record[A0h])
//   581B93  statList: UnlinkStatList(unit, statList), FreeStatList(game[106h], statList)
//   581BB6  delete (9, itemId)                  <- item and skill here

// =============================================================================
//  Aura Zero Results  -  celestialrayone.aura-zero-results
//
//  D2R 3.3 under D2RLoader 1.3.1. An aura formula that returns 0 removes its
//  stat instead of leaving the last nonzero value in place:
//
//    1. AURA STATS. Results written through the shared aura callback:
//       aurastat/aurastatcalc of srvdofunc 65 (caster and targets), and the
//       caster passivestats and target aurastats of srvdofunc 66, 81 and 82.
//    2. PASSIVE STATS. passivestat/passivecalc results srvdofunc 65 writes
//       onto the caster's aura state.
//
//  Every address below was read out of the dumped D2RLoader 1.3.1 image and
//  disassembled before being relied on. Byte witnesses guard every function
//  the plugin changes or calls, so a different build refuses cleanly.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHY A 0 NEVER LANDS (server)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  0x238EA00  srvdofunc table; slot 65 (0x238EC08) -> 0x563D90.
//  0x563D90   srvdofunc 65. Evaluates aurastatcalc 1-6 once per tick in the
//             caster's context (stat ids skill+0x74, calcs skill+0x88) into
//             its args block, then applies them to the caster's aurastate
//             (0x432AE0) and to every target's auratargetstate (0x4327D0),
//             both through the callback 0x5616F0.
//  0x5616F0   aura callback(context, target)
//             rsi = context ([rsi] = game), r12 = args ([rcx+18h]),
//             r14 = target. 0x433D20 returns the state's stat list in r13
//             (copied to [r12+88h]). It REUSES the existing list when state,
//             skill and level match, so a stat changes only when written.
//             args: [r12] state id, [r12+14h+4j] stat id j, [r12+4Ch+4j]
//             result j, [r12+84h] "the aura did something" count, which the
//             handler reads afterwards to drain mana.
//    slot loop 0x561AD2 .. 0x561CDC, 6 slots:
//    0x561AD6  test r15d,r15d / je 0x561CC8      gate 1: result 0 -> skipped
//    0x561AE3  stat id <= 0 or >= the itemstatcost count -> skipped
//    0x561B2F  stat 110 (item_poisonlengthresist) -> 0x561DE0, which scales
//              every curable state's remaining time by result/100.
//    0x561B5B  call 0x55BFB0, the resistance rule: a negative resist result on
//              a non-player, non-hireling unit with 100+ base resist is /5.
//              Everything else, 0 included, comes back unchanged.
//    0x561B62  test eax,eax / je 0x561CC4        gate 2: 0 after it -> skipped
//    0x561B6A  the write: record 0x2D9730; "direct" stats (record+4 & 8) are
//              added to the unit; every other stat: read the list (0x2F5DF0),
//              toggle the state's gfx bit (0x3355A0) when it changes, floor
//              67/68 at the target's cold effect (0x449F40), write the list
//              (0x2F7C00), copy 68 into 69, count it at [r12+84h].
//    0x5617F9 .. 0x561AB7  the game's own zeroing pass. It runs only when a
//              different caster refreshed a list it did not create, and writes
//              0 exactly like the write above, minus the count. This plugin
//              gives a caster's own 0 results that same write.
//  0x561AE5  test edi,edi / jle 0x561CC8   stat id check: ids <= 0 skipped,
//              so strength (stat 0) never applies. strength_aura_stats turns
//              it into jl. aura_stats works with either form.
//  0x563E40 .. 0x563E5C  srvdofunc 65 zeroes its args block, then collects
//              aurastat ids and values for slots before the first empty
//              column (0x563EC6, js on id < 0). The slots after it keep id 0
//              and value 0, and so do all six when the mana check at 0x563EAE
//              skips the collection.
//  0x563FD9 .. 0x5640A8  srvdofunc 65's passivestat loop, 14 slots (stat ids
//              skill+0xBA, calcs skill+0xD8). 0x56403A je 0x564096 skips a 0;
//              0x564091 writes the rest onto the caster's list.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHAT THE PLUGIN CHANGES
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  aura_stats
//    0x561AD9  je 0x561CC8 -> 6 byte nop. A 0 result goes on to the stock
//              stat id checks.
//    0x561B37  call redirect, cleanse. A 0 returns 0 without cleansing. That
//              is what the game did before, since a 0 never got there.
//    0x561B5B  call redirect, resistance rule. The stock rule runs. When its
//              result is 0 the stat is written the way the zeroing pass writes
//              it, then the 0 goes back, so the stock je at 0x561B64 skips the
//              rest of the slot and the count stays untouched.
//    0x563E60  call redirect in srvdofunc 65, right after its args block is
//              zeroed: the six aurastat ids are set to -1 before the
//              collection, so a slot it does not fill carries no stat. A 0
//              result in a filled slot is now written, and without this an
//              unfilled slot (id 0, value 0) would be read as a strength 0
//              wherever stat id 0 is allowed, clearing a real strength value.
//              The stock call (0x34B6C0) still runs and its result is kept.
//  strength_aura_stats
//    0x561AE7  jle 0x561CC8 -> jl 0x561CC8 (0x561AE8, 8E -> 8C). Only ids < 0
//              (an empty column) are skipped, so stat id 0 (strength) applies
//              like any other aura stat. The same change as the 2.4 patch at
//              383FB2 and the basic-aura-stat-id-0.json memory patch, which
//              this replaces. When that memory patch already made the change
//              the plugin leaves the byte to it.
//  passive_stats
//    0x56403A  je 0x564096 -> 2 byte nop.
//    0x564091  call redirect, list write. Nonzero values go to the stock
//              write. A 0 is written only when the stat is on the list.
//
//  The redirected calls land on a page the plugin allocates within rel32
//  reach of the image; nothing inside D2R's own code is used as a cave.
//
//  Console command "aurazeroresults" shows what is installed and counters.
//  Settings: d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.aura-zero-results.toml
// =============================================================================

// 0x561AC0 .. 0x561CE2: the whole slot loop. Proves every register, offset and
// call the redirects and the zero write rely on: both gates, the stat 110
// branch, the resistance rule call, and the stock write the zero write
// mirrors (record lookup, direct test, list read, gfx toggle, cold floor,
// list write, 68 -> 69, the count at [r12+84h]). [rsp+20h] is only ever
// written there (fifth argument of the list write) and [rsp+28h] is never
// used, which is what lets the resistance stub pass r13 and r12 on. It is
// checked in two parts around the stat id check's opcode at 0x561AE8, which
// may be 8E (stock jle) or 8C (jl, ESR's basic-aura-stat-id-0 patch).

// 0x563E21 .. 0x563E6B: srvdofunc 65 zeroes the args block at rbp-80h (ids at
// rbp-6Ch .. rbp-58h), calls 0x34B6C0 with the unit and keeps eax at rbp-74h.
// rdx carries nothing into that call, which is what lets the stub hand the
// args block over in it.

// 0x563FFC .. 0x5640B2: the passivestat loop: the 0 gate at 0x56403A and the list
// write at 0x564091 (context, [rbp+8] caster list, stat, value, 0). The block
// gates in between (0x563FD9 .. 0x563FFC: caster list present, mana against
// cost, passivestate) are not checked: they only decide whether the loop runs,
// which this plugin does not touch, and the mana compare at 0x563FE7 is the
// spot ESR's 2.4 mana gate fix (384502, jle -> jl) changes.

// D2RLoader 1.3.x turns both stat list routines into jmp [rip+disp32] thunks
// into D2RCore; only the opcode is fixed, the displacement depends on the
// loader build.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Stub page. One page the plugin allocates within rel32 reach of the image.
//
//      +0x000  UNWIND_INFO, no frame (no stub touches rsp)
//      +0x010  RUNTIME_FUNCTION table, 4 entries
//      +0x100  mov rax,<bridge> / jmp rax                     cleanse
//      +0x120  mov [rsp+28h],r13 / mov [rsp+30h],r12 /
//              mov rax,<bridge> / jmp rax                     resistance rule
//      +0x140  mov rax,<bridge> / jmp rax                     passive list write
//      +0x160  lea rdx,[rbp-80h] /
//              mov rax,<bridge> / jmp rax                     srvdofunc 65 args
//
//  A stub is reached by the retargeted call, so the return address is on the
//  stack and the stock arguments are in place: the bridge is entered as if
//  the game had called it. The resistance stub also stores the state list
//  (r13) and the args (r12) into the fifth and sixth argument slots. Those are
//  the caller's [rsp+20h] (only ever written by the caller, right before each
//  five-argument call) and [rsp+28h] (never used by the caller). The args stub
//  passes srvdofunc 65's args block (rbp-80h, its frame) as the second
//  argument of a call that takes one.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct CallSitePatch {
    std::uint64_t               rva;
    const std::uint8_t*         stock;
    std::size_t                 stub;
    std::array<std::uint8_t, 5> written;
    bool                        applied;
};

struct GatePatch {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* stock;
    const std::uint8_t* nop;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    bool                applied;
};

// A 0 that did not stick means D2RCore's list write treats it differently from
// the stock routine. Said once, whatever diagnostics is set to; the console
// command keeps the count.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The zero write
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One slot whose result is 0, given the same way the stock write at 0x561B6A
// gives a nonzero one, without the count at [r12+84h]:
//   - direct stats are left alone: the stock write adds them to the unit, and
//     adding 0 changes nothing.
//   - the gfx bit is toggled when the list held a value (stock: when the list
//     value differs from the result, which is 0 here).
//   - 67/68 take the target's cold effect when it is above 0 (stock floor:
//     the result never goes below the cold effect), and 68 is copied into 69.
//   - nothing is written when the list already holds that value.

// 0x563E60, srvdofunc 65. rcx = the unit (the stock call's only argument), rdx
// = the args block the stub adds. The six aurastat ids get -1 before the
// collection fills the slots it reaches; the stock call's result goes back for
// the store at 0x563E68. This runs whatever the gate state is: -1 is skipped
// by the stat id check in both its stock and its stat-id-0 form.

// Absorb All Damage
//
// skills.txt auraeventfunc 40 absorbs every direct damage type of a hit
// (physical, fire, lightning, cold, magic and poison) instead of physical
// damage only.
//
// Everything below was read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process dump (md5
// 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d) with IDA, and out of D2RCore.dll 1.3.1
// (SHA-256 2a868d013d2e0830bd2d9e04b918b19e46a73cf726c833e70d089b948fdeb5a2).
// Every game address the plugin uses is guarded by a byte witness at load.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// auraeventfunc 40 as the game ships it (0x524470, table 0x238E5C0 + 40*8)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   (game, event, unit, attacker, hit, skillId, ...) -> 0 or 1
//   skill = 0x97790(context, skillId); state = skill+A0h (aurastate), bounded
//   by 0x2141A0; list = 0x2F5940(unit, state); skill+76h (aurastat2) must be
//   above 0 on that list; pool = skill+74h (aurastat1), both read through
//   0x2F5DF0 (context, list, stat, 0). context = byte at game+106h.
//   Then it subtracts ONLY hit+18h (physical) from the pool, writes the pool
//   back (0x2F7C00), and at 0 removes the state (0x3354C0, 0x2F7920,
//   0x2F4180). If something was absorbed and there is an attacker, it plays
//   the overlay skill+162h facing the attacker (0x349020). It reads nothing
//   else from the hit.
//   D2RCore dispatches it through its own wrapper (D2RCore 0x3D1CC0), which
//   reads table entry 40 and calls it with the same nine arguments.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What the plugin does
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   An inline hook on 0x524470 reads the pool the same way the original
//   does, takes damage out of the real hit in the order physical (+18h),
//   fire (+20h), lightning (+2Ch), cold (+34h), magic (+30h), poison (+38h),
//   then calls the original with a stand-in hit whose physical field holds
//   the total taken. The original then lowers the pool, removes the state at
//   0 and plays the overlay exactly as it always does, for any damage type.
//   Nothing of the original is rewritten.
//
//   The field offsets are the game's own: SUNITDMG_CalculateTotalDamage
//   (0x44DF10) labels them Phys/Fire/Ligt/Cold/Magc/Pois in its resistance
//   table and sums exactly these six into the hit's life damage (+134h) at
//   0x44EC71. The absorb event (11) fires at 0x44EB25, before resistances.
//
//   Poison: the hit carries damage per frame (+38h) and length (+3Ch). The
//   hit deals one frame of it as life damage, the poison state the rest, so
//   absorbing per-frame damage d costs d * (length + 1). When the states.txt
//   poison row (state 2) has record byte +3Ch set, the damage commit applies
//   poison per source instead (0x44D145 .. 0x44D262): entries at hit+40h,
//   count at hit+48h, 12 bytes each {source, damage, length}, then the rest
//   (+38h minus their damage, +3Ch minus their length) as one more poison
//   when hit byte +0Ch or +14h is set. The plugin absorbs those entries one
//   by one and keeps +38h equal to their sum plus the rest.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ABIs (x64)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   0x97790   (context, skillId) -> skills.txt record
//   0x2141A0  (context) -> states.txt rows      0x2141E0 (context) -> itemstatcost rows
//   0x2F5940  (unit, state) -> the state's stat list
//   0x2F5DF0  (context, list, stat, layer) -> value (loader stub to ReadWideListStat)
//   0x300A90  (context) -> data tables: states rows at +290h, count at +298h,
//             row size 44h

// The whole of auraeventfunc 40, 0x524470 .. 0x52473F. Its calls prove every
// helper address above except 0x300A90; its body proves it reads only +18h
// of the hit.

// 0x44D145, the damage commit's poison block: call 0x300A90, states count at
// +298h above 2, rows at +290h, row 2 * 44h, byte +3Ch, then the per-source
// loop over hit+40h / hit+48h in 12-byte steps.

using EventFuncFn = std::int64_t(__fastcall*)(void* game, std::uint64_t event, void* unit,
    void* attacker, void* hit, std::uint32_t skillId, std::uint32_t arg7, std::uint32_t arg8,
    std::uint64_t arg9) noexcept;

struct DirectKind {
    std::size_t offset;
    DamageKind  kind;
};

// Bound Baal AI
//
// Baal's AI was written for a Baal who always fights the players. While he
// fights on their side (bound with Bind Demon, or converted), three of his
// behaviours still treat the player as the enemy. This plugin fixes all three
// with rules the game already applies elsewhere. A normal, hostile Baal runs
// every original instruction untouched: each change below only runs while
// Baal's alignment is not evil.
//
// Written for D2R 3.3 on D2RLoader 1.3.1. Every address and byte below was read
// out of the live D2RLoader.exe process image (md5 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d)
// and disassembled before being relied on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Targeting
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Most monster AIs get their target from the shared target picker 0x595750
//   (the AI tick's category 2 target, called at 0x4A2C99). It checks alignment
//   first: an evil unit searches the players' side, any other unit scans the
//   nearby rooms for hostile units (enumerator 0x596340 mode 4, test callback
//   0x597E00 -> valid-target test 0x597840, range 35 at 0x595D61).
//
//   Baal skips it. The AI rows BaalCrab (135, think 0x5A8B80) and BaalCrabClone
//   (140, think 0x5A8CF0) use target mode 0 and call the Baal-only routine
//   0x5A9A10 (game, baal, int* bestScore, int* count, cull) instead. Its only
//   callers are the Baal thinks (0x5A8BB8, 0x5A8D81, 0x5A9031). It walks the
//   ten target lists at game+0x21E0: the eight player slots (each player
//   followed by his pets, hirelings and bound or converted monsters), then the
//   allied and the neutral monsters, and keeps the best score of the threat
//   routine called at 0x5A9B6A, 0x5A9BA6, 0x5A9C06 and 0x5A9C5A (D2RLoader
//   routes those to D2RCore's ScoreBaalTargetWithPrimeEvilThreat). Nothing in
//   it reads Baal's alignment, so a bound Baal attacks the player or one of his
//   allies and never considers a hostile monster.
//
//   The hook on 0x5A9A10: an evil Baal runs the original. Any other Baal gets
//   his target from 0x595750, with the arguments the AI tick passes it: the AI
//   control at monster data +30h (0x4A2AD0) and the difficulty byte from
//   0x48FF40 (0x4A2AEB, read back as the byte at 0x4A2C7A). bestScore becomes
//   Baal's own threat score for that target, from the routine the original
//   calls, and count the number of units within the picker's range that pass
//   the picker's own valid-target test 0x597840 and distance 0x596720 (as at
//   0x597E23/0x597E36), at least 1 when there is a target. Baal's skill choice
//   0x5A95E0 and skill handler 0x5AA860 then run unchanged on that target.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Clone
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   0x5AAE60, reached only from the skill handler's case 15 (call at 0x5AAD9B),
//   refuses while Baal has living minions or a living owner. A bound Baal whose
//   owner is dead or gone clones: the clone spawns as a hostile Baal, and the
//   routine rewrites the bound Baal's own owner to himself (0x4A5800), which
//   breaks the bind. The hook returns 0, the routine's own "no clone" answer,
//   which the caller ignores before idling, for every Baal that is not evil:
//   the rule spawning AIs such as the Vile Mother's follow, spawning only
//   while evil.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Festering Appendages
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   srvdofunc 140 (0x5859E0) spawns each tentacle with 0x492CF0, then only
//   stores the caster as its owner (0x34EF50, called at 0x585CA7). A tentacle
//   never gets an alignment, so it is hostile to the players. That call is
//   relayed: after the owner is stored, a tentacle whose owner is a monster
//   that is not evil takes the owner's alignment through 0x48E600, called the
//   way the Bind Demon conversion calls it at 0x51F1E1. That is the rule the
//   Hydra skill applies to every hydra a monster casts. An evil caster's
//   tentacles are not touched.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Unit fields read here
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   +0x00 unit type, 1 = monster (0x34B9D0 returns [unit]).
//   +0x10 monster data; its +0x30 is the AI control (0x4A2AB0..0x4A2AD8).
//   Alignment through the game's own getter 0x2F4190 (0 evil, 1 neutral,
//   2 good), as the AI think event calls it at 0x4A2A38.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Verified byte windows. Hooked windows hold whole instructions with no
// branch and no RIP-relative operand. The others are only read, to prove an
// address and its arguments at a native call site before the plugin calls it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// 0x5A9A10 (hooked) mov rax,rsp / mov [rax+20h],r9 / mov [rax+18h],r8 /
// push rbx / push rbp / push rsi / push rdi / push r12 / push r13 / push r14 /
// push r15 / sub rsp,48h

// 0x585C96 (tentacle loop of srvdofunc 140) mov rdx,rdi / mov rcx,r15 /
// call 0x4A6E50 (idle 15 frames) / mov rdx,rbx / mov rcx,rdi /
// call 0x34EF50 (store the caster as owner, relayed) / sub rbp,1 / jnz loop

// 0x4A2ADD (AI think event) mov r8,r14 (unit) / mov [rbp-51h],rax (AI control) /
// mov rdx,r15 (game) / lea rcx,[rbp+0Fh] / call 0x48FF40 / ... /
// movsd xmm0,[rax] / movsd [rbp-19h],xmm0 (the context the tick keeps)

// 0x4A2C7A (AI think event, category 2) movzx eax,byte [rbp-19h] (context
// byte 0) / lea r9,[rbp-31h] (distance) / mov r8,[rbp-51h] (AI control) /
// mov rdx,r14 / mov [rsp+28h],al / mov rcx,r15 / lea rax,[rbp-2Dh] (combat) /
// mov [rsp+20h],rax / call 0x595750

// 0x5A9058 (Uber Baal think) lea r9,[callback] / ... / lea r8,[context] / ... /
// mov rcx,rsi (game) / mov dword [rsp+20h],1 (mode 1: caller's callback) /
// mov r14,rax / call 0x596340

// 0x597E1A (allied hostile-target callback 0x597E00) mov rsi,r9 / mov rdi,r8 /
// mov r14,rdx / call 0x597840 (game, searcher, candidate) / mov rcx,rdi /
// test eax,eax / je / mov rdx,r14 / call 0x596720 (candidate, searcher) /
// mov ebp,eax / cmp eax,[rsi+20h] (range)

// 0x595D67 ... movdqa [rbp-50h],xmm0 / mov rcx,rsi / mov [rbp-34h],r12d /
// xor r9d,r9d / mov [rbp-30h],r14d (context +20h = range) / mov rdx,r15 /
// mov [rbp-28h],eax / mov [rbp-20h],r13b / mov dword [rsp+20h],4 / call 0x596340

// Around each threat call: the two argument moves (rdx = target, rcx = Baal),
// the call opcode, and the instruction after it. The rel32 is D2RLoader's and
// is decoded live instead.

// Bad Item Log Fix
//
// Stops a crash while a character is being added to a game: when the game's
// saved-item checker throws out one of the HIRELING's items, it writes a log
// header that reads the hireling as if it were a player, and dies.
//
// Everything below was read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image for
// D2R 3.3 (md5 8af7a47c...) and is checked byte for byte at load. Nothing is
// installed when a check fails.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where the checker runs
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   ClientAddPlayerToGame 483B20 empties the game's item id list (game
//   +1AA70 = 0) and loads the character: 41DF40 -> character reader 420280.
//   The reader takes the player's items (421D20), the corpses (41FB20) and
//   then the hireling section 421240, which spawns the hireling and reads the
//   hireling's item list with the HIRELING as the owner:
//     4215B7  call 421D20 (game, hireling, data, version, ...)
//   Every item goes through the saved-item checker 4229C0 (game, owner,
//   item, version), which returns true for an item the game deletes:
//     - an item without an id ("Item has no unique id!")
//     - an id already seen in this load (sorted list at game +1AA68, 541FA0)
//     - four vanilla row checks (uniqueitems/setitems row 122, 242, 205)
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The crash
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   For a deleted item the checker writes "Deleting bad item" and a header
//   into Documents\Diablo II\GameLogs\D2TEMP<date>.txt:
//     "\nGame %6d %s\nPlayer: %s\n\n%s"   (1D18408, formatted at 423CF8)
//   It takes the player name like this:
//     423BE0  48 8B 45 E8           mov  rax, [rbp-18h]   ; owner
//     423BE4  48 85 C0 / 74 10      test / je 423BF9
//     423BE9  48 8B 40 10           mov  rax, [rax+10h]   ; unit data
//     423BED  48 85 C0 / 74 07      test / je 423BF9
//     423BF2  4C 8B A8 F0 00 00 00  mov  r13, [rax+0F0h]  ; client
//     ...
//     423CB3  4D 8D 45 2C           lea  r8, [r13+2Ch]    ; character name
//   There is no check that the owner is a player. For a hireling [unit+10h]
//   is its MonsterData, and +0F0h there holds eight spaces
//   (2020202020202020h). The name pointer becomes 202020202020204Ch and
//   strnlen (12DF060, from the %s handler 12DDCC4) faults on it.
//   The checker's own duplicate-item line does it right: a non-player owner
//   hands over to its owner (loop at 422B0E through 4A53C0) before the
//   client is read. 421D20 does the same for its account id line (4226F3).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The change
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The 25 bytes 423BE0..423BF9 become
//     48 8B 4D E8                 mov  rcx, [rbp-18h]   ; owner
//     E8 xx xx xx xx              call ResolveLogClient (relay page)
//     4C 8B E8                    mov  r13, rax         ; client or 0
//     0F 1F 84 00 00 00 00 00     nop
//     0F 1F 44 00 00              nop                   ; ends at 423BF9
//   ResolveLogClient hands a non-player owner to its owner player the way
//   the duplicate-item line does, then reads the client exactly as the
//   checker always did for a player ([unit+10h] -> [+0F0h]). A player owner
//   gives the same client as before, so nothing changes for the player's own
//   items. A null client leaves the name empty, as the game already does for
//   an owner without one (423CBB). While a character is loading, the game's
//   own duplicate-item line cannot find the hireling's player either (it logs
//   "unknown(Merc?)" for the same item), so in that case the name stays empty.
//   Registers: everything the checker keeps across 423BE0..423BF9 is
//   non-volatile (rbx, rsi, r12, r13, r15); rax, rcx, rdx and r8 are written
//   again before their next read (423BFD, 423C69, 423C1B, 423C82, 423CAC).
//   The call is made at the checker's own aligned rsp, like its other calls.
//   The decision to delete the item is untouched: the game deletes exactly
//   what it deleted before, only the log header is now safe.

// 4A53C0..4A547D: D2GAME_GetMinionOwner, whole body. Null unless the unit is a
// monster; MonsterData [unit+10h] -> AI control [+30h] -> owner. Asserts when
// either pointer is missing, so ResolveLogClient checks both first.

// ===========================================================================
// Bound Demon Scaling (celestialrayone.bound-demon-scaling)
// ===========================================================================
//
// Bound demons scale like normal summons. A bound demon keeps the monster it
// was bound from for its looks, AI, skills, level and affixes, but its stats
// come from one monstats.txt row named in the TOML, the pseudo row:
//   - Life: the pseudo row's MinHP..MaxHP for the difficulty (monlvl.txt at
//     the demon's level unless the row has noRatio). It replaces the original
//     life and every bonus on it (champion, unique, minion, player count).
//     Bind Demon's calc1 and passivestats are added on top, as for a summon.
//   - Resistances (ResDm..ResPo), AC and ToBlock: the original row's part is
//     swapped for the pseudo row's. What affixes and monprop added stays.
//   - Damage and attack rating of every attack, elemental attacks included:
//     A1/A2/S1 and El1-El3 of the pseudo row.
//   - Life regeneration: the pseudo row's DamageRegen (calc7 still decides
//     who regenerates).
//   - MonProp: the pseudo row's monprop is added to the original monster's.
//   - Hit recovery: a hit only stuns a bound demon when the pseudo row's
//     monstats2 row (MonStatsEx) has the get-hit mode (mGH), and its own
//     model has it too. Its life is summon sized now, so without this its
//     former pack's hits would keep it in hit recovery right after binding.
//   - Resist cap: published as the "stats-row" service, version 1. Summon
//     Resist Cap 3.4.0 and later decide a bound demon's cap from the pseudo
//     row (Align, extra_monster_ids, uncap pairs).
// Server side.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. How a bound demon gets its stats (D2R 3.3.93847, D2RLoader image)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Bind conversion 51EF90 (game, owner, demon, skill, skill level, cast id,
//   newBind byte at [rsp+30h]). Every bind goes through it:
//     new bind  520CF0, call at 520F2D, newBind 1
//     restore   51E0F0 (called by the save restore 531790), call at 51E159,
//               newBind 0
//   It keeps the monster's own stat list. On a new bind it lowers the level
//   (stat 12) to the owner's (51F345..51F37A) and adds Bind Demon's
//   calc2-calc5 monster mods. Then the shared summon setup 55EF80 adds the
//   passivestats to the base stats, puts the aurastats on their own list and
//   sets max life = max life + calc1 percent. Then
//   D2RCore!ApplyBindDemonBoundStats (monpet.txt BoundStat1-5, empty in ESR)
//   and, at 51F453, life regeneration from the monster's own DamageRegen
//   (record +1F4h) through 542E70.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. What the monster brought from its spawn (543E90)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Base stats set from its monstats record (r13) for difficulty d:
//     damageresist 36 = int16 [+196h + 2d]    magicresist 37 = [+19Ch + 2d]
//     fireresist   39 = [+1A2h + 2d]          lightresist 41 = [+1A8h + 2d]
//     coldresist   43 = [+1AEh + 2d]          poisonresist 45 = [+1B4h + 2d]
//     toblock      20 = byte [+0F5h + d]
//   3A26C0 (context, row, game type, d, level, 7, out[14], extra) gives
//   out[0] min life, out[1] max life, out[2] AC; life = min + seed roll,
//   plus the player count percent, held at 7FFFFFh, then << 8 into 7 and 6.
//   Monprop: record +4Eh (word) indexes DataTables +13E8h (count +13F0h,
//   0x138 a row); props at +4 + 16 * (6d + i), chances at +124h + 6d + i,
//   each through 3D40B0 (valid) and 3D4620 (unit, prop, flag 40h).
//   The whole 56 byte spawn info (d at +0, game type flags at +4 / +8,
//   level at +10h, extra at +14h) is copied to MonsterData +78h at 544732.
//   Champion, unique and minion life bonuses multiply life later (49BD50).
//   Affix bonuses to these stats are flat additions. Stock 38E430 (the
//   resistance affixes) adds flat resistances and has Stone Skin double the
//   defense; ESR's Monster Affixes plugin replaces that routine (only Magic
//   Resistant adds anything, and Stone Skin no longer doubles defense), so
//   the rebase keeps every affix bonus exactly by moving only the row's part.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Damage per attack (588D50, MONSTERMODE_UpdateModeAttackStats)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Not stored: every attack mode recomputes min/max damage and attack
//   rating through 3A26C0 with the class id from 349860 (called at 588F89,
//   589013, 58909D, 5892CA) and reads El1-El3 mode/type/chance from the
//   monstats record loaded at 588E0F (mov r13,[rax], MonsterData +0).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Save and restore
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The save writes every base stat with itemstatcost Save Bits (D2RCore
//   WritePetFormatStats), maxhp, resistances, AC, toblock and hpregen
//   included. The restore 531790 spawns the class, rebinds it and only then
//   reads the saved stats back with a set (ReadPetStats -> 3D9B50). So a
//   restored demon has the stats it was saved with: this plugin's rebase on
//   restore is overwritten by the save, which itself came from a rebased
//   demon. A demon saved before this plugin keeps its saved numbers until a
//   new bind. Damage, attack rating and the cap are read live.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. What this plugin patches
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   - The two calls to 51EF90 (520F2D, 51E159) call the bind relay. Before
//     the native bind runs, the relay rebases the demon: base life set to
//     the pseudo row's, AC / ToBlock / resistances moved by (pseudo - own),
//     the pseudo row's monprop applied. The native bind then lowers the
//     level, adds its mods and runs the summon setup on top, unchanged.
//     The level used for the pseudo row is the one the bind will leave
//     (51F345..51F37A, mirrored).
//   - 51F453 (mov edi,[rcx+1F4h], 6 bytes) calls a stub: while the relay is
//     binding that demon, edi is the pseudo row's DamageRegen.
//   - The four 349860 calls in 588D50 call a relay that returns the pseudo
//     row for a bound demon (state 207, bind_demon, the test the game's own
//     demon save uses) and the real class id for every other unit.
//   - 588E0F (mov r13,[rax] / test r13,r13, 6 bytes, no branch lands inside;
//     the whole function and its jump table were audited) calls a stub that
//     swaps r13 for the pseudo row's record for a bound demon.
//   - The class id call 44FCD4 in the hit recovery test 44FBA0 calls a relay.
//     44FBA0 skips hit recovery for a hit under maxlife / 8..64 (by hit
//     class, two rolled bands) and for a monster whose monstats2 row has no
//     get-hit mode (1A2D30 -> 0975B0, mode flags at +0F0h). For a bound demon
//     whose pseudo row has no get-hit mode the relay hands it the pseudo row,
//     so the test skips; otherwise it gets its own class id, unchanged.
//   - After every rebased bind the plugin publishes the plugin communication
//     event "demon-bound", version 1 (DemonBoundEvent below) on the game
//     thread, so a display can re-read the demon at once. Monster Display
//     1.0.14 and later refreshes its panel on it.
//   Every site is checked against the bytes below before anything is
//   written; a part whose bytes differ stays off and says so.
//
// ===========================================================================

// The spawn's life roll (544179..5441CB): span = max - min, n = span + 1;
// the seed steps lo * 6AC690C5h + hi; the roll is new % n, or new & span
// when n is a power of two.

struct ResistColumn {
    std::uint32_t stat;
    std::size_t   offset;
    const char*   label;
};

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

using LevelStatsFn     = std::uint64_t (*)(std::uint64_t context, std::uint64_t row, std::uint64_t gameType,
                                           std::uint64_t difficulty, std::int32_t level, std::int32_t flags,
                                           std::int32_t* out, std::int32_t extra) noexcept;

// The monstats.txt row named by pseudo_row, -1 when there is none. Looked up
// through the table's Id linker the first time and again whenever the data
// tables move; the reverse lookup must give the same name back.

// The pseudo row's monprop, as the spawn applies a monster's own (5445A8..
// 5446BB): stop at the first invalid property; a non-zero chance is rolled
// on the unit's seed (% 100).

// 51EF90 gives up before touching anything without an owner, a demon or
// the skill's record; the rebase does the same. The rebase runs first, so
// the game's own bind code (owner, AI, alignment, bind_demon state, calc1
// life) never sees the monster's original stats.

// The class id 44FBA0 gives its get-hit mode test (44FCD4). A bound demon
// whose pseudo row has no get-hit mode gets the pseudo row, so the test finds
// no get-hit mode and skips hit recovery; every other unit, and a bound demon
// whose pseudo row has one, gets its own class id and the native test.

struct StatsRowService {
    using StatsRowFn = std::int32_t(__cdecl*)(void* unit) noexcept;

    std::uint32_t serviceSize;
    std::uint32_t serviceVersion;
    // Game thread, server unit: the monstats.txt row the unit takes its stats
    // from when it is a bound demon this plugin scales, -1 for every other
    // unit (and while the plugin is off or no pseudo row exists).
    StatsRowFn statsRow;
};

// Game thread, server unit: the monstats.txt row the unit takes its stats
// from when it is a bound demon this plugin scales, -1 for every other
// unit (and while the plugin is off or no pseudo row exists).

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relay page and patches
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One page within rel32 reach of every site, never freed (a thread may be
// inside a stub at any time):
//   +000h  jmp [RelayBind]        (520F2D, 51E159)
//   +040h  jmp [RelayClassId]     (588F89, 589013, 58909D, 5892CA)
//   +080h  record stub            (588E0F)
//   +0C0h  regen stub             (51F453)
//   +100h  jmp [RelayHitRecoveryClassId] (44FCD4)

// =============================================================================
//  Blacksmith  -  celestialrayone.blacksmith
//
//  The Blacksmith perks of the specialization system that need code:
//    Tempered Steel    +10% to the damage modifiers on the character's weapons
//    Mithril Weaving   +10% base defense on the character's armor
//    Spectral Arsenal  dropped weapons roll ethereal at 10% instead of 5%
//    Blessed Gear      a dropped item's base can go up one tier after rolling
//    Relic Hunter      +5% total chance for weapons and armor to drop unique
//    The Secret Pocket (a Jeweler perk) +5% total chance for rings, amulets and
//                      jewels to drop unique: it shares Relic Hunter's hook,
//                      because only one plugin can patch the unique roll
//    Weightless        -25% total strength and dexterity requirements
//    Additional Modifier  magic, rare and crafted weapons and armor made for
//                      the character get one more affix 25% of the time
//    Hidden Potential  (a Jeweler perk) the same for magic, rare and crafted
//                      rings and amulets
//    Brilliant Quality (a Jeweler perk) a jewel made for the character has one
//                      of its modifiers doubled 25% of the time
//                      (both ride on Additional Modifier's hook on 0x43EF10,
//                      which only one plugin can hold)
//    Durability Defense  +1% base defense per point of durability on armor
//    Modifier Magnitude  +10% to every listed modifier on the weapons
//    Ethereal Weapons  equipped ethereal weapons get +75% base damage instead
//                      of +50%, and +10% total spell damage while one is equipped
//    Unbreakable Gear  equipped gear loses no durability; +1% total damage per
//                      25 durability across the equipped gear
//    Extended Reach    +3 melee range with any weapon but a bow or crossbow,
//                      +25% projectile speed
//  A perk counts while the character's own value of its stat is above 0.
//
//  Built for D2R 3.3 under D2RLoader 1.3.1. Every address below was read out
//  of the dumped D2RLoader 1.3.1 image (md5 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d)
//  and disassembled before being relied on. Byte witnesses guard every site
//  and pin every native function the plugin calls (each witness contains the
//  game's own call to it), so a different build installs nothing.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHO A DROP BELONGS TO
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The treasure class roll 0x4404F0 (game, monster, killer, ...) homes the
//  killer once, at 0x44055B mov [rbp-80h],r8; rbp is its frame base for the
//  whole body. Each item it produces is built through one call:
//
//      4410B0  48 8B 54 24 58        mov  rdx, [rsp+58h]      ; dropping unit
//      4410B5  45 8B CE              mov  r9d, r14d           ; quality
//      4410B8  45 8B C7              mov  r8d, r15d           ; item class
//      4410BC  49 8B CC              mov  rcx, r12            ; game
//      4410BF  89 74 24 28           mov  [rsp+28h], esi      ; flags
//      4410C3  89 7C 24 20           mov  [rsp+20h], edi      ; set/unique row
//      4410C7  E8 B4 35 00 00        call 444680              ; spawn the item
//
//  0x444680 fills the drop request and calls the item builder 0x43CD50, which
//  allocates the unit and runs 0x43EF10 (base stats, then 0x442D60: quality,
//  set or unique row, affixes, ethereal, sockets). The killer is not passed
//  down, so the call at 4410C7 goes through a relay that reads [rbp-80h] and
//  calls SpawnWithKiller, which records the credited player (a player killer,
//  or the player owner of a minion killer through 0x4A53C0, the lookup the
//  quality roll itself uses for magic find) in a thread-local drop context
//  for exactly the duration of that one item. Every other way an item is made
//  (vendors, gambling, the cube, quests) runs with no context and is never
//  touched.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  RELIC HUNTER: the unique roll in D2GAME_ITEMS_RollItemQuality 0x4421B0
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Called only from the treasure class roll (0x440FD9) with the killer as its
//  third argument. After magic find and the treasure class modifier, ebx is
//  the unique range: the item is unique when rand(range) < 128, or always
//  when the range is 0 or below.
//
//      442427  2B D8                 sub  ebx, eax            ; final range
//      442429  85 DB                 test ebx, ebx            ; <- hook
//      44242B  0F 8E 1A 02 00 00     jle  44264B              ; return 7
//      442431  48 8B 8C 24 88 00..   mov  rcx, [rsp+88h]      ; <- continue
//      442439  E8 A2 7D F0 FF        call 34A1E0              ; seed
//      442443  E8 B8 16 D1 FF        call 153B00              ; rand(range)
//      442448  3D 80 00 00 00        cmp  eax, 80h
//
//  The 8 bytes at 442429 become a jump to a relay that calls
//  AdjustUniqueRange(range, killer [rsp+90h], class [rsp+0A0h]) and replays
//  the test and branch on the result. For a perk player and a matching item
//  the range becomes range * 100 / (100 + bonus), which multiplies the unique
//  chance by (100 + bonus) / 100. The Jeweler's The Secret Pocket (rings,
//  amulets and jewels) is a second entry in the same function: the roll can
//  carry only one patch, so a jeweler plugin of its own could not hook it.
//  Each perk whose types the item's class matches divides the range once. The frame (7 pushes, sub rsp,40h, homed
//  arguments) is pinned by the prologue witness. Only the fallthrough from
//  442429 enters 44242B (IDA cross references), and every volatile register
//  is dead at both 442431 and 44264B.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  SPECTRAL ARSENAL: the ethereal roll in 0x442D60
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  rdi is the item and rbp the drop request. The gates before it (weapon or
//  armor, durability, not low quality, not a set item, not a quest item) are
//  untouched.
//
//      4434C9  E8 B2 97 F2 FF        call 36CC80              ; item seed
//      4434D1  BA 64 00 00 00        mov  edx, 64h
//      4434D6  E8 25 06 D1 FF        call 153B00              ; rand(100)
//      4434DB  33 C9                 xor  ecx, ecx            ; <- hook
//      4434DD  83 F8 05              cmp  eax, 5              ;    (5 bytes)
//      4434E0  41 0F 42 CD           cmovb ecx, r13d          ; ethereal
//
//  The 5 bytes at 4434DB become a call to a relay that asks
//  EtherealThreshold(item, request) for the threshold, then replays
//  xor ecx,ecx / cmp eax,threshold, so the cmovb right after reads the same
//  flags it always did. Only the fallthrough from 4434DB enters 4434DD.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  BLESSED GEAR: the cube's own upgrade, applied after the drop has rolled
//  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The Horadric Cube's "useitem,mod,exc" and "useitem,mod,eli" outputs keep
//  the item and change only its base. The cube generator 0x5269C0 does it in
//  place with three calls:
//
//      526FEB  call 34DE10            ; class id = upgraded base (unit+4)
//      527009  call 43EF10            ; (game, &item, null, 0): base stats
//                                     ; only: block, speed, durability,
//                                     ; defense, damage, stack size
//      52702D  call 36E2D0            ; ethereal flag 400000h?
//      527043  call 3D3510            ; then the ethereal base conversion
//
//  The binder 0x529D50 takes the upgraded base from items.txt: ubercode
//  (record +88h) for exc, ultracode (+8Ch) for eli, an item being normal when
//  its code (+80h) equals normcode (+84h). 0x313EF0 turns a code into a class.
//
//  The plugin hooks the entry of 0x43EF10. After the original returns for a
//  drop with a perk player (the builder calls it with the request and 1),
//  the item has its final quality, set or unique row, affixes and ethereal
//  state. With chance_percent (rolled on the item's own seed through
//  0x36CC80 / 0x153B00, like every other item roll) it runs the cube's three
//  calls, and for an ethereal item also the durability step 0x442D60 runs
//  for a fresh ethereal drop (4434FF..443558): half the base max durability
//  plus one, and full current durability. The builder then finishes the item
//  as usual (Chronicle record, placement, packets), so the client only ever
//  sees the upgraded item.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WEIGHTLESS: the requirement percent
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  An item's strength and dexterity requirements are its items.txt reqstr
//  (record +11Ah) and reqdex (+11Ch) plus a percent of them, minus 10 for an
//  ethereal item. The percent is the character's weapon mastery requirement
//  bonus (D2RCore ReadWideWeaponMastery through the thunk 0x33D4F0, effect 6)
//  plus the item's item_req_percent (stat 91 through 0x2F5C60). It is built
//  in exactly two places:
//
//    ITEMS_CheckItemRequirementsForUnit 0x36BC50, the only requirement check
//    (equipping, mercenaries, the red inventory backgrounds, charms):
//      36BDE6  E8 05 17 FD FF        call 33D4F0     ; (unit, item, 0, 6)
//      36BDF4  41 8D 50 5B           lea  edx,[r8+5Bh]
//      36BDF8  E8 63 9E F8 FF        call 2F5C60     ; (item, 91, 0)
//      36BDFD  44 03 F0              add  r14d, eax  ; the percent
//    0x371FA0 (unit, item) = the same sum, which the tooltip builders
//    0x2BD480 and 0x2BF1B0 and the UI 0x1454B80 use for the numbers shown:
//      371FB6  E8 35 B5 FC FF        call 33D4F0     ; (unit, item, 0, 6)
//      371FC7  E8 94 3C F8 FF        call 2F5C60     ; (item, 91, 0)
//
//  Both mastery calls go to WeightlessMastery, which returns the mastery
//  value plus whatever turns the final percent p into
//      (100 + p) * (100 - reduction) / 100 - 100,
//  so the requirement before the ethereal 10 becomes
//      base * (100 + p) / 100 * (100 - reduction) / 100:
//  multiplied with every requirement modifier, as the perk says. The check,
//  the tooltip numbers and their colours therefore always agree. Only the
//  calls change; unusable-items keeps its entry hook on 0x36BC50.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  TEMPERED STEEL AND MITHRIL WEAVING: a bonus stat list on the item itself
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Each equipped weapon or armor piece of a character with the perk carries one
//  extra plain stat list of the plugin's, attached to the item the way 0x43EF10
//  gives an item its quest-difficulty list (43F407..43F487):
//      2F7300  alloc (flags, 0, owner type 4, item id)
//      2F2CB0  attach (item, list, 1)
//      2F7C00  set (data context, list, stat, value, 0)
//  and removed with 2F7920 unlink (item, list) + 2F4180 free (context, list).
//  The list is a child of the item, so the engine treats its stats exactly
//  like the item's own: the item's op 13 multiplies its base damage or defense
//  with the item's full enhanced damage, the item total goes to the character
//  only while the item's own list is attached (active weapon set, requirements
//  met), and the character's damagepercent, armor_override_percent and the rest
//  apply on top. The item's tooltip shows the raised values.
//
//  Never saved, never sent: the item serializer only writes lists found
//  through 2F5A00 with flag 40h or 2040h (37F031..37F069; the runeword list is
//  state 171 + 40h), and the plugin's list uses flag 10000h. The D2RCore
//  attach (+3DA4F0) links a list without 2000h into the parent's chain at +90h
//  and adds its stats; its death cleanup (+3DAD30) skips lists owned by items.
//  Server and client each keep their own lists, built from the same items, so
//  combat (server) and the character sheet and tooltips (client) agree.
//
//  The values, recomputed from the item's own stats (with the plugin's list's
//  values taken back out, so they never feed on themselves):
//    weapon, p = bonus_percent:
//      every raised stat, flat damage, ED 17/18 and per-level damage included:
//        own * p / 100, own = what the item's lists hold (ListTotals) - ours.
//        A list holds modifiers only: never the item's base values, never
//        what op 13 adds to the base damage or op 4/5 per level
//    armor, p = bonus_percent:
//      armorclass: (b + b * ED16 / 100) - (base + base * ED16 / 100),
//        b = base + base * p / 100: the base defense raised by p, then the
//        item's own enhanced defense on it, as if the item had dropped that way
//  ED16 (and the ED Ethereal Weapons puts on its raise) is the value op 13
//  multiplies with: the item's base value plus everything its lists hold, from
//  2F48C0 and ListTotals. Never the item's full stats array: that is the
//  engine's cache of each stat's total, and for a stat with an itemstatcost op
//  the engine stops committing it there while an op target on the same item
//  is not 0 (D2MOO sub_6FDB64A0, D2RCore +3DBEB0, see the enhanced-damage-fix
//  plugin). A weapon's enhanced damage, an armor piece's enhanced defense and
//  an equipped item's per-level damage are such stats: the cache holds no
//  value for them, or a stale one.
//  Every equipped item of a character in body locations 1 to 12 is checked
//  (INVENTORY_GetItemFromBodyLoc 0x3886D0 on UNITS_GetInventory 0x34A360), so
//  the swap weapons are ready when swapped in. An item that stops qualifying
//  (unequipped, given to a hireling, perk unlearned) loses its list on the next
//  pass; the plugin looks each one up again by id (SUNIT_GetServerUnit
//  0x48FE80 on the host, CLIENT_GetUnitByIdAndType 0x9A5D0 on a client).
//
//  A kept list address is the plugin's own only while the list is still in
//  that item's child chain, still carries flag 10000h and none of the game's
//  item list flags (40h modifiers and runeword, 2000h set, 80000000h a
//  socketed item's list), and still holds exactly the values last written to
//  it. Being in the chain is not enough: when the server sends an item again,
//  the client frees the item and every list on it and builds new ones, and the
//  allocator hands a freed address straight to one of the new lists (the
//  jeweler met this on 2026-10-24). Up to 1.3.6 the plugin then took the new
//  runeword or magic list for its own: it overwrote the item's real modifiers
//  with its shares, set to 0 the ones it no longer wanted (a runeword's poison
//  pierce and bleed vanished, its enhanced damage dropped), and when the item
//  left the slot it unlinked and freed that list while the item still pointed
//  at it. Only the client's copy changed, so re-equipping (the server sends
//  the item again) put it right, and a save never held it. Now a list that
//  fails the test is never written, unlinked or freed: the address is
//  forgotten and the item gets a new list.
//
//  Durability Defense and Modifier Magnitude use the same lists. Durability
//  Defense raises base defense by its percent per point of the piece's
//  durability (stat 72, or 73 with use_max_durability), multiplied with
//  Mithril Weaving's raise: base * (100 + mithril) / 100 * (100 + durability)
//  / 100. Modifier Magnitude raises every stat in its list on the weapons the
//  way Tempered Steel raises the damage stats; a stat both perks raise gets
//  both percents. Stats are handled per layer: for a stat with a parameter (a
//  skill, a class, an aura, a proc) only the value of each parameter rises,
//  the parameter stays, so +12 to a skill becomes +13 to the same skill. The
//  layers an item carries come from its lists (ListTotals). item_charged_skill
//  (204) is never raised: its value packs the charges and the maximum charges.
//
//  Passes run on D2RLoader's thread service: on the host's game thread every
//  4 game updates for every player in the game (the server client registry,
//  as specializations reads it), and on the UI thread every 100 ms for the
//  local player.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  ADDITIONAL MODIFIER: one more affix through the game's own affix roll
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Every magic affix is rolled by 0x58AE00 (item, 1, must pick, apply, prefix,
//  forced id), which hands the item to D2RCore SelectExpansionMagicAffix (or
//  the classic one for a classic item). GenerateMagicAffixes calls it once
//  for the prefix and once for the suffix (442CB3, 442CFB) with apply = 1 and
//  stores the ids in slot 0 through 36DB40 / 36DBE0 (item, id, slot). The
//  selector takes only spawnable affixes of the item's types and affix level,
//  the rare column for rare, crafted and tempered items, and no group already
//  on the item: it collects the groups of the item's prefix and suffix slots
//  (36CD50 / 36CDE0) before it picks.
//
//  After 0x43EF10 has rolled a new item, for a magic (4), rare (6) or crafted
//  (8) weapon or armor piece made for a character with the perk, and with
//  chance_percent on the item's own seed, the plugin makes the same call with
//  must pick = 1 and apply = 1, for a prefix or a suffix (whichever kind has a
//  free slot; when both do, the seed decides; when the first kind has nothing
//  left to give, the other is tried). The id goes into the first free slot of
//  that kind: slots 0 to 2 for rare and crafted items, slot 0 for magic items
//  (a magic item saves only slot 0 of each kind). With no free slot the affix
//  still applies; its values are in the item's magic stat list, which is saved
//  with the item, so they stay.
//
//  Made for the character: a treasure-class drop credited to it (the drop
//  context above), or any item whose build request names it as the unit
//  (request +00h, which the builder 0x43CD50 itself reads as a unit): Horadric
//  Cube outputs (the cube builds its request with the player at +00h,
//  527526..52754F) and the other items the game creates for a player. Vendor
//  stock is not: D2GAME_NPC_GenerateStoreItem 0x540EA0 builds it through
//  0x43D530 with the vendor as the unit.
//
//  Which Horadric Cube outputs: only those that build a new item. The cube
//  generator 0x5269C0 runs each output by its type byte (output +67h):
//    item code FCh, item type FDh, usetype FFh: a new item through the builder
//      at 527837 (game, request with the player at +00h, 0), which runs
//      0x43EF10 with the request and 1, so the perks apply once to the fresh
//      roll (a usetype output replaces the input with a brand-new item);
//    useitem FEh: keeps the input item. Without "mod" (+5Eh bit 0) it never
//      reaches the builder or 0x43EF10 (52710B..527448: sockets, repair,
//      recharge and the recipe's own mods are applied to the item as it is);
//      with "mod" the base changes in place through 0x43EF10 (game, &item,
//      null, 0) at 527009, and the hook only acts when a request is passed;
//    cloneitem FBh (the clone-item plugin): copied through 0x43D660, serialize
//      and decode, which never calls 0x43EF10 or the builder.
//  The other callers of 0x43EF10 are the builder and preset map items
//  (0x48DC50, a request with no unit). So an item that already exists never
//  gains an affix or a doubled modifier, however often it goes through a
//  recipe.
//
//  Hidden Potential (jeweler_7) is a second entry of the same routine for
//  rings and amulets. Brilliant Quality (jeweler_8): for a jewel made for a
//  character with the perk, with chance_percent on the item's seed, one of its
//  modifiers is doubled. The modifiers are the stats of the jewel's own
//  modifier list, the one 0x43EF10 itself finds with 2F5B80 (item, state 0,
//  flag 40h) for stat 356 (43F407): affixes, or a unique's properties, which
//  the item saves, and on a cube output the recipe's own properties (see
//  below). A modifier is what the tooltip shows as one line: the
//  damage pairs (17/18, 48/49, 50/51, 52/53, 54/55, 57/58, and 21-24/159/160
//  together), an itemstatcost dgrp group when every member is present with the
//  same value (all resistances, all attributes, ...), and every other stat and
//  parameter on its own. Encoded stats (encode 2, skill on event; 3, charges)
//  and stat 356 are left out. dgrp (word +3Ah) and encode (word +2Ch) come
//  from the itemstatcost record (tables +1258h, stride 144h, count +1260h, as
//  the record getter 0x2D9730 reads them), checked against the vanilla
//  groups once per data context. Each member is set to twice its value
//  through 2F7C00 on that list, so the change is saved with the jewel.
//  Gamble purchases count for these two while their gambling setting is on
//  (the default).
//
//  Brilliant Quality on a cube output waits for the recipe's own properties
//  (cubemain.txt mod 1 to mod 5). The generator adds them after the builder
//  has returned, in the loop at 527970..527A02: each goes through 0x3D3420,
//  which hands it to 0x3D5060 with state 0 and flag 40h, so 0x3D5230 finds
//  the jewel's modifier list through the same 2F5B80 (item, 0, 40h). A roll
//  inside the 0x43EF10 hook therefore never saw them. Two jumps to relays
//  move the roll for cube outputs behind them:
//      5275DF  48 C7 45 44 04 00 00 00   mov  qword ptr [rbp+44h], 4   ; relay 8
//      527A08  B8 00 02 00 00            mov  eax, 200h               ; relay 9
//  5275DF is in the straight-line setup of the cube's item request
//  (52752B..5275F1), which only usetype, item code and item type outputs run:
//  useitem outputs, "mod" outputs (+5Eh bit 0, tested at 526D6F) and
//  cloneitem outputs never reach it. Relay 8 replays the store and records the
//  request, rbp+20h, which is the very pointer the builder passes on to
//  0x43EF10 (43CD6E mov rsi,rdx ... 43CF12 mov r8,rsi), with the game from
//  [rbp-80h]. The hook holds the roll for the item built with that request
//  instead of rolling at once. Every full build ends the mark, so a request
//  that was set up but never built (a unique the player may carry only one
//  of) cannot leave a stale one behind.
//  527A08 is where the recipe mod loop and its skip branch (je at 527960)
//  join, before sockets, repair, recharge, placement and the client packets.
//  Relay 9 hands over the game [rbp-80h], the output record r15 (flags
//  +5Eh, type +67h, as 526D6F and 527104 read them) and the output's item
//  [rbp + [rsp+54h]*8 + 120h], as 527A18 reloads it, then replays the mov.
//  CubeOutputReady rolls the held jewel there when that output built it: the
//  same item, type FFh, FDh or FCh, no "mod". Both relays keep every volatile
//  register (xmm0 to xmm5 included); flags are dead at both sites. D2RLoader's
//  own jumps in the generator (527461, 527966) and the clone-item plugin's
//  checked ranges are untouched. If either site fails its check, Brilliant
//  Quality keeps rolling in the hook and the console says so.
//
//  Gambling builds the item for the vendor too, so it needs its own context.
//  The purchase 0x53D850 (game, player, vendor, ...) homes the player at
//  [rsp+58h] and the vendor at [rsp+68h] (53D8AA..53D8B8) and makes the item
//  through 0x541AB0 (game, vendor, class, item level, seed), which rolls the
//  gamble quality and calls 0x43D530 with the vendor:
//      53EB3D  49 8B CD              mov  rcx, r13
//      53EB40  E8 6B 2F 00 00        call 541AB0            ; <- relay
//      53EBDC  49 8B CD              mov  rcx, r13
//      53EBDF  E8 CC 2E 00 00        call 541AB0            ; <- relay
//  Both calls go through a relay that adds [rsp+58h] as a sixth argument;
//  GambleWithPlayer records the player for exactly that item. The gamble
//  screen's own preview items (0x541880 -> 541A4C) are not touched.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  ETHEREAL WEAPONS: the ethereal routine's +50%, raised to +75% while equipped
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The ethereal routine 0x3D3510 sets the ethereal flag and each weapon base
//  stat (21/22 one-hand, 23/24 two-hand, 159/160 throw) to 3 * base / 2, once,
//  when the item is made (drop roll 0x4434FF, cube 0x527043, property 0x3CCCCB).
//  It knows nothing of who equips the item, so the perk lives on the item's
//  bonus list instead: the items.txt base comes back exactly as ceil(2b/3), the
//  list adds base * 175 / 100 - b with the weapon's enhanced damage (op 13, 18
//  for min, 17 for max) applied on top, as the engine applies it to its base.
//  The total spell damage stats ride on one ethereal weapon per weapon set.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  UNBREAKABLE GEAR: no durability loss, total damage from the gear's durability
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Durability is lost in two places only:
//    0x441B10 (game, unit, item), 4% weapon / 10% armor per hit, called from the
//      melee hit's durability step 0x4526C0 at 45270C (the attacker's weapon)
//      and 4528CD (a random armor piece of the defender);
//    0x5590C0 (game, unit, item, chance, amount), a skill's own loss, called at
//      55881B. A stackable item spends quantity there instead, which stays.
//  The three calls go to relays 6 and 7, which jump to the plugin. The calls
//  are redirected rather than the routines' entries so another plugin's entry
//  hook on them keeps working. The damage bonus: the current durability of the
//  active gear (body locations 1 to 10) is pooled, divided by points per 1%, and
//  carried on one active item's bonus list (body armor first).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  EXTENDED REACH: melee range and projectile speed
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  weapons.txt rangeadder is items.txt +115h. Its only reader is 0x371F10,
//  called only by UNITS_GetMeleeRange 0x34A620 (unit): class bonus + the
//  rangeadder of INVENTORY_GetEquippedWeapon 0x385D20's weapon (right hand if it
//  is a weapon, else left). Server range checks and the client's walk to attack
//  both read it, so an inline hook on it adds the perk's range for a character
//  holding a weapon that is not a bow or crossbow. Projectile speed rides on
//  the same carrier as Unbreakable Gear's bonus.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  PASSES THAT STAY ALIVE, AND THE SYNC CHECK
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The host's lists feed combat, the client's feed the character sheet and the
//  tooltips, and both feed NPC prices, which count every stat in an item's full
//  stats. The server no longer needs the client's repair quote to match its own
//  price: the memory patch server_priced_repairs.json makes the repair handler
//  0x53FE00 charge its own price (1.3.5's repair hooks are gone with it). The
//  two sides' lists must still hold the same values for the sheet to show what
//  combat uses.
//
//  Both passes keep going by queueing their next tick from the current one. Up
//  to 1.3.3 a queue call that did not succeed ended that chain, and only the
//  next LocalPlayerReady (the next game) started it again. That side's lists
//  then stood still while the other side's kept following durability (Durability
//  Defense, Unbreakable Gear's pool) and every other input. Now:
//    - every tick records when it ran;
//    - the client tick starts a new host chain when the host chain has ended or
//      has not run for HostStallMs (a remote TCP/IP client has no host chain and
//      stops trying once the thread service says Unavailable);
//    - the host tick starts a new client chain when the client chain has ended;
//    - act and level changes start any chain that is not running.
//  A new chain takes a new id, and an older tick still queued stops at its next
//  run, so two chains never both run passes.
//
//  Console "blacksmith sync", in a game this process hosts, compares the host
//  and client copies of the local character's equipped gear: per item, every
//  entry of its full stats (what the repair price reads), its base stats, its
//  stat lists and this plugin's bonus list. The host copy is read on the game
//  thread and the client copy on the UI thread, never across threads. Every
//  difference goes to the console and the plugin log as stat:layer=value.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  ITEM TYPES
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  ITEMS_CheckItemTypeId 0x373890 (item, type) tests the item's two items.txt
//  types (+12Eh, +130h) in the itemtypes equivalence table of its data
//  context: row = 0x367220(tables + 1368h, type * dword [tables + 1360h]),
//  bit target. Relic Hunter only has the item class, so it runs the same test
//  on the class's record. itemtypes.txt codes are resolved against the live
//  table (tables + 1348h, count + 1350h, rows of E8h bytes, code at +0).
//
//  Console command "blacksmith" shows the settings, what is installed and
//  counters. Settings: d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.blacksmith.toml
// =============================================================================

// The cube output routine 0x5269C0, for Brilliant Quality on cube outputs:
// the item request setup holding relay 8's site (rbp+20h is the request,
// player at +00h, game at +10h), the build call that passes rbp+20h on,
// the recipe mod loop (each mod through 0x3D3420) with relay 9's site and the
// item reload after it, the skip branch into that site, and the output's
// "mod" flag and type reads.
// 52752B..5275F3 (200 bytes)

// 0x43EF10 giving an item its quest-difficulty list: 2F5B80 find, 34A330 unit id,
// 2F7300 alloc (40h, 0, 4, id), 2F2CB0 attach (item, list, 1), 2F7C00 set (context, list, 356, value, 0)
// 43F407..43F487 (128 bytes)

// DATATBLS_GetItemStatCostRecord (context, stat): records at tables +1258h,
// count +1260h, stride 144h (imul at 2D97AB)
// 2D9730..2D97C7 (151 bytes)

// GenerateMagicAffixes 0x442C60: 58AE00 (item, 1, must pick, apply, prefix, forced) for the prefix
// and the suffix, stored by 36DB40 / 36DBE0 (item, id, slot)
// 442C9A..442D3A (160 bytes)

// the expansion rare generator applying its affixes: 36CD50 / 36CDE0 (item, slot) -> id,
// 314250 (context, id) -> record, 3D3670 (0, 0, item, record, 0, 0)
// 58BE29..58BE94 (107 bytes)

// the gamble purchase in 0x53D850 (game, player, vendor, ...): 541AB0 (game, vendor, class, item level,
// seed), the player at [rsp+58h]
// 53EB22..53EB45 (35 bytes)

// the melee hit's durability step 0x4526C0: the attacker's weapon (rax from 0x4242B0)
// goes to 0x441B10 (game r13, attacker rbx, weapon)
// 4526FE..452711 (19 bytes)

// the same step: the defender's randomly picked armor piece (r8) goes to 0x441B10
// (game r13, defender r12, item)
// 4528C7..4528D2 (11 bytes)

// a skill's own durability loss: 0x5590C0 (game rsi, unit rdi, item r13, chance ebx,
// amount [rsp+20h])
// 55880B..558820 (21 bytes)

// UNITS_GetMeleeRange's weapon: INVENTORY_GetEquippedWeapon 0x385D20 (inventory,
// &item, &body location, &flag), then the item's rangeadder 0x371F10 (items.txt +115h)
// 34A76C..34A7B4 (72 bytes)

// INVENTORY_GetEquippedWeapon 0x385D20: the right hand (4) if it holds a weapon, else
// the left hand (5)
// 385D20..385D34 (20 bytes)

// D2RCore!VisitWideUnitStats (1.3.1), the 78 bytes that read the full stats
// array: ex = unit +88h; extended (+1Ch < 0) -> array +A8h, count +B0h;
// entries of 10h bytes.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relays (assembled with keystone, decoded back with capstone; every rip
// relative operand lands on its own qword slot after the code)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relay 1, entered by the call at 4410C7 in place of 0x444680. rcx, rdx, r8
// and r9 pass through; the two stack arguments are copied and the killer
// [rbp-80h] of the drop roll's frame is added as a seventh.
//   mov rax,[rbp-80h] / sub rsp,48h / mov r10,[rsp+70h] / mov [rsp+20h],r10
//   mov r10,[rsp+78h] / mov [rsp+28h],r10 / mov [rsp+30h],rax
//   call [slot SpawnWithKiller] / add rsp,48h / ret

// Relay 2, entered by the jump at 442429 with ebx = the unique range.
//   sub rsp,20h / mov ecx,ebx / mov rdx,[rsp+0B0h] (killer) / mov r8d,[rsp+0C0h] (class)
//   call [slot AdjustUniqueRange] / add rsp,20h / mov ebx,eax / test ebx,ebx
//   jle hit / jmp [slot 442431] / hit: jmp [slot 44264B]

// Relay 3, entered by the call at 4434DB with eax = rand(100), rdi = the item
// and rbp = the drop request.
//   push rax / sub rsp,20h / mov rcx,rdi / mov rdx,rbp / call [slot EtherealThreshold]
//   mov edx,eax / add rsp,20h / pop rax / xor ecx,ecx / cmp eax,edx / ret

// Relay 5, entered by the two gamble purchase calls in place of 0x541AB0. rcx,
// rdx, r8 and r9 pass through, the seed is copied and the purchase's player
// [rsp+58h] is added as a sixth argument.
//   mov rax,[rsp+60h] / sub rsp,38h / mov r10,[rsp+60h] / mov [rsp+20h],r10
//   mov [rsp+28h],rax / call [slot GambleWithPlayer] / add rsp,38h / ret

// Relay 4, the target of the two requirement percent calls. Their arguments
// are exactly WeightlessMastery's (unit, item, 0, 6), so it only jumps.
//   jmp [slot WeightlessMastery]

// Relay 6, the target of the two combat durability calls (45270C, 4528CD) and
// relay 7, the target of the skill durability call (55881B). Their arguments
// are exactly the hook's, so each only jumps.
//   jmp [slot KeepCombatDurability] / jmp [slot KeepSkillDurability]

// Relay 8, entered by the jmp at 5275DF in the cube's item request setup.
// Replays the store, keeps every volatile register and records the request.
//   mov qword ptr [rbp+44h],4 / push rax,rcx,rdx,r8,r9,r10,r11 / sub rsp,88h
//   movdqu [rsp+20h..70h],xmm0..xmm5 / lea rcx,[rbp+20h] / mov rdx,[rbp-80h]
//   call [slot MarkCubeBuild] / movdqu xmm0..xmm5,[rsp+20h..70h] / add rsp,88h
//   pop r11,r10,r9,r8,rdx,rcx,rax / jmp [slot 5275E7]

// Relay 9, entered by the jmp at 527A08 after the recipe mod loop. Keeps
// every volatile register but eax, which the replayed mov sets.
//   push rcx,rdx,r8,r9,r10,r11 / sub rsp,80h / movdqu [rsp+20h..70h],xmm0..xmm5
//   movsxd rax,[rsp+104h] (the output index [rsp+54h]) / mov r8,[rbp+rax*8+120h]
//   mov rdx,r15 / mov rcx,[rbp-80h] / call [slot CubeOutputReady]
//   movdqu xmm0..xmm5,[rsp+20h..70h] / add rsp,80h / pop r11,r10,r9,r8,rdx,rcx
//   mov eax,200h / jmp [slot 527A0D]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Brilliant Quality on cube outputs: the roll waits for the recipe's own
// properties (relays 8 and 9)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The item request the cube output routine is setting up, recorded by relay 8.

// The plugin's list is only ever touched when it is still in one of the
// item's two child chains (statlist ex at +88h, chains at +90h and +98h, next
// at +68h; the D2RCore attach picks the chain by the list's 8h flag).

// A list this plugin (or another plugin) made: flag 10000h and none of the
// game's item list flags (40h modifiers and runeword, 2000h set, 80000000h
// extended: a socketed item's list).

// The list's own stats (+30h, count +38h, 16-byte entries) are exactly the
// values last written to it: every entry that is not 0 is one of them with the
// same value, and every value that is not 0 is there. An entry at 0 (a stat
// the engine keeps at 0) is no difference.

// What an item's stat lists hold, per (stat, layer) pair, read where D2RCore's
// VisitWideUnitStats reads a list (its body is compared at load; 16-byte
// entries: layer +0, stat id +4, value +8): every list in the active child
// chain of the item's statlist ex (+88h, extended flag in +1Ch; chain at +90h,
// next at +68h), a plain list's own stats at +30h (count +38h), and for an item
// socketed in it (an extended list in the chain) its full stats at +A8h (count
// +B0h), which is what the engine adds into the host for it. That is the sum
// the engine evaluates a stat from before its ops (D2MOO sub_6FDB5830), and it
// is always current, unlike the item's own full stats array (see TEMPERED
// STEEL AND MITHRIL WEAVING). The plugin's own list is included.

// 0x3D3510 left each base at 3 * base / 2; ceil(2b/3) gives the items.txt
// base back exactly. Raise it to base * (150 + perk) / 100, with the
// weapon's enhanced damage on the raise as the engine puts it on a base.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

// The cube output routine's sites for Brilliant Quality's recipe properties.
// A mismatch only turns that part off: the perk then rolls in the hook as
// before, and the console line says why.

// Bleed
//
// Physical hits make the target bleed: it loses life every frame for a
// while, carried by negative life regeneration on a timed state. The item
// tooltip shows the three bleed stats as one composed line.
//
// Port of the ESR D2R 2.4 bleed system to D2R 3.3 on D2RLoader 1.3.0. The
// 2.4 memory patches were:
//   326772 -> cave 386E83   bleed on hit, in the per-hit life total
//   1C709E -> cave 386D20   tooltip accumulator, reads the bleed stats
//   1C81E9 -> cave 386DC9   tooltip composer, one line for the three stats
// Everything below was read out of the live D2RLoader.exe image and
// D2RCore.dll and disassembled before being relied on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What the 2.4 cave did, and what this plugin does the same way
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   On every hit whose life total is computed:
//     physical = post-resistance, post-absorb physical damage of the hit
//     skip if physical <= 0, no attacker, or no attacker stat list
//     min = attacker min stat, skip if <= 0      (total over every source)
//     max = attacker max stat
//     len = attacker length stat, skip if <= 0   (total over every source)
//     N   = number of stat lists attached to the attacker whose own
//           length stat is above 0, at least 1
//     avg = len / N, skip if <= 0
//     seed: low = low * 0x6AC690C5 + high, only the low half is written
//     roll = max >= min ? min + low % (max - min + 1) : min
//     perFrame = (roll * physical) / (avg * 100), 64-bit, clamped to int32,
//                skip if <= 0
//     apply the timed state: level = perFrame, duration = avg,
//                            stat 74 = -perFrame, callback = default
//   The arithmetic below is the 2.4 cave instruction for instruction,
//   including the 32-bit avg * 100 and the low-half-only seed write.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Part 1: the hit, SUNITDMG_CalculateTotalDamage 0x44DF10 (2.4 sub_140325AF0)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   void (game, attacker, defender, D2Damage*)
//     44DF3D  4C 8B EA              mov  r13, rdx            ; attacker
//     44DF54  4D 8B F9              mov  r15, r9             ; D2Damage
//     44DF5E  49 8B F0              mov  rsi, r8             ; defender
//   A full linear sweep of the function (899 instructions) finds no other
//   write to r13, r15 or rsi before the epilogue.
//
//   Physical is D2Damage+18h: the resistance loop's first record points at
//   it and uses damageresist (stat 36):
//     44E142  49 8D 47 18           lea  rax, [r15+18h]
//     44E149  48 89 45 80           mov  [rbp-80h], rax
//     44E14D  C7 45 88 24 00 00 00  mov  dword [rbp-78h], 24h
//
//   The absorb event (11) fires at 44EB3A, then the life total is summed:
//     44EC71  41 8B 47 30 ...       Magc + Cold + Phys + Ligt + Pois + Fire
//     44EC99  41 89 87 34 01 00 00  mov  [r15+134h], eax
//     44ECA0  4C 89 7C 24 20        mov  [rsp+20h], r15
//     44ECA5  E8 C6 E8 FF FF        call 44D570              ; event 16
//     44ECAA  45 85 E4              test r12d, r12d
//   The only branch into 44EC71..44ECAA lands on 44EC71, so reaching 44ECA5
//   means the life total was just summed, which is exactly where the 2.4
//   hook sat. Nothing between the two touches D2Damage+18h.
//
//   The poison immunity plugin patches 44EC8C and pins 44EC71..44ECA4, so
//   this plugin changes only the rel32 of the call at 44ECA5. It lands in a
//   near relay that jumps to HookedLifeTotalEvent, which receives the call's
//   own arguments (game, 16, defender, attacker, D2Damage), applies bleed,
//   then makes the original call to D2GAME_ApplyUnitStatEvent 0x44D570.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Stats and the stat list chain in D2RLoader 1.3.0
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   D2RLoader owns stat storage. The game image entries are thunks:
//     2F6CD0  FF 25 / 90 x4         D2RCore!ReadWideEffectiveStat
//             int32 (dataContext, statList, statId, layer): full stats for
//             an extended list, base stats otherwise. This is the 2.4 reader
//             sub_1401E2E70 the cave used for the totals and for each list.
//     2F5DF0  FF 25 / 90 x4         D2RCore!ReadWideListStat
//             int32 (dataContext, statList, statId, layer): base stats. This
//             is the 2.4 reader sub_1401E2E00 the tooltip accumulator used.
//   Data context is Unit+1BDh (34A0E0), the stat list is Unit+88h (34B870).
//
//   Child lists: 2.4 walked [list+68h] then [node+48h]. 3.3 moved both:
//     2F59BB  48 8B 87 90 00 00 00  mov  rax, [rdi+90h]      ; first child
//     2F59C7  39 58 20              cmp  [rax+20h], ebx      ; state id
//     2F59CC  48 8B 40 68           mov  rax, [rax+68h]      ; next child
//   D2RCore's attach links merged lists into +90h and lists flagged 2000h
//   (never merged into the parent) into +98h, both through +68h. 2.4 walked
//   only the merged chain, so this plugin walks only +90h.
//
//   The seed pair is Unit+28h (UNITS_GetSeed 34A1E0).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The timed state, 0x433D20 (2.4 sub_140295240)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Thunk to D2RCore!ApplyWideTimedStatEffect, StatList* (ApplyArgs*).
//   The native Open Wounds callback 584170 fills the same 30h record:
//     +00 source  +08 target  +10 skill 0  +14 level 1  +18 duration 200
//     +1C stat 74  +20 value -damage  +24 state 62  +28 callback 0
//   D2RCore's implementation looks the state up on the target: an equal
//   level only refreshes the duration, a lower level is refused, a higher
//   level replaces the list. 2.4 put perFrame in the level field for exactly
//   that highest-per-frame-wins rule, and so does this plugin.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Part 2: the tooltip
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   D2RLoader replaced the item stat list renderer 2DA760 with
//   D2RCore!RenderWideItemStatList, which still calls the game image through
//   its import slots: the accumulator 2DAED0 once per tooltip (which calls
//   2DB370 first) and the composer 2DB800 once per stat entry, in
//   description order. Both are hooked at their entries.
//
//   2DB370 (unit, statList, accumulator): clears 118h bytes, then reads the
//   special stats with ReadWideListStat(context, statList, stat, 0). The hook
//   runs it, then reads the three bleed stats the same way. The flag is
//   min > 0 && max > 0, as in 2.4. The values are kept per accumulator on the
//   calling thread, never written into the game's accumulator.
//
//   2DB800 (accumulator, statId, output, capacity, chronicle):
//     2DB84B  83 C3 EF              add  ebx, -11h
//     2DB84E  83 FB 2A              cmp  ebx, 2Ah
//     2DB851  0F 87 62 06 00 00     ja   2DBEB9              ; return 0
//   Stats 17..59 go to the native switch first, as the 2.4 cave did. For the
//   min stat with the flag set the hook formats the line into a 256-byte
//   buffer from (text, min, max, seconds), the argument order of the native
//   poison range line, then runs the native tail: the chronicle bullet when
//   requested, the line, and the newline key, each through the composer's
//   own string resolver and 12276A0. The max and length stats return the flag.
//
//   The line is formatted by the plugin itself rather than by the game's
//   formatter 847F0, so the text may show the seconds with decimals.
//   FormatBleedLine fills the text one placeholder at a time: placeholder n,
//   numbered (%3$.2f) or in order (%.2f), takes value n. A floating
//   placeholder gets the exact seconds, length / 25.0, without the trailing
//   zeros of its decimals (3.20 shows as 3.2, 10.00 as 10); an integer one
//   gets the whole seconds, length / 25. A text still written with %d keeps
//   working and never reads a double as an int.

using ComposeFn           = std::uint64_t(__fastcall*)(std::int32_t* accumulator,
                                std::uint64_t statId, char* output, std::uint64_t capacity,
                                std::uint64_t chronicle) noexcept;

// Follows the E8 at rva (inside a pinned witness) and requires the target to
// be a loader thunk. The resolver thunk sits in D2RLoader's own section past
// the retail image, so it is read directly rather than through the loader's
// byte check.

// Blood Boil
//
// Two server-side changes to skills.txt srvdofunc 160 (WarDoBloodBoil). Both
// live inside Blood Boil's per-demon step and nowhere else.
//
//   1. sksrc() in the calcs Blood Boil evaluates on a demon reads the caster.
//      calc1/calc2/calc3 (the sacrifice percent) and aurarangecalc (the radius)
//      are evaluated with the demon as the formula unit. stat(), skill(),
//      rand() and every other function in them keep reading the demon. Only
//      sksrc() is pointed at the Warlock who cast Blood Boil, read at the moment
//      the skill hits.
//
//   2. Lethal sacrifice. Vanilla skips a demon completely (no sacrifice, no area
//      damage) when the sacrifice would kill it. When the caster has a positive
//      value in a configurable stat (default 523) that skip is removed: the
//      demon takes the whole sacrifice, dies if it runs out of life, and its
//      area damage still goes off.
//
// Everything below was read out of the live D2R 3.3 D2RLoader image (base
// 0x140000000) and disassembled before anything here relies on it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Blood Boil, 3.3
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   srvdofunc table 0x238EA00, slot 160 at 0x238EF00 -> 0x520F80. The handler
//   returns unless the caster is a player, counts the demons (of pettype when
//   that column is set, pooled pets included), picks calc1/calc2/calc3 for
//   1/2/3 demons and calc1 otherwise, and walks the demons with the per-demon
//   callback 0x521320 and the block {skills.txt record, skill id, level, calc
//   index}.
//
//   0x521320(game, caster, demon, block) keeps game in r15, the caster in r12,
//   the demon in rdi and the block in rbx for its whole body:
//
//     52138D  call 2F4D20              demon max life -> rsi
//     52139F  call 2F5020              demon life, stat 6 -> r14d
//     5213C1  mov  r8d,[rdx+r8*4+190h] calc1/2/3 pool offset
//     5213C9  mov  rdx,rdi             the demon is the formula unit
//     5213CC  call 3B5160              site A: the sacrifice percent
//             sacrifice = max life * percent / 100 (three overflow branches)
//     521495  lea  eax,[rdx+1]
//     521498  cmp  r14d,eax
//     52149B  jle  52171F              site C: "would die", skip everything
//     5214AA  call 51EAD0              the sacrifice, r9 = percent
//     52154C  call 3B5160, rdx = r12   calc4 (next hit delay), on the caster
//     521606  mov  rdx,rdi
//     521614  mov  r8d,[rcx+84h]       aurarangecalc
//     521623  call 3B5160              site B: the radius, on the demon
//     521694  call 4327D0              area damage around the demon
//
//   The percent is the only thing calc1-3 produce. The life it is applied to
//   is always read from the demon by 2F4D20, whatever unit the calc runs on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why sksrc() read the demon
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Formulas are compiled bytecode run by the interpreter 0xA234B0 against a
//   context with seven unit slots. Which slot each function reads, from the
//   nine callbacks at 0x1D09C50 and the token resolver 0x3B61D0:
//
//     +08 rand() seed   +10 stat()    +18 skill(), sklvl(), lvl/clc tokens
//     +20 callback 8    +28 sksrc()   +30 miss()    +38 callback 8
//
//   The skills.txt evaluator 0x3B5160 writes the one unit it is given into all
//   seven, so in a skills.txt calc sksrc() always reads the same unit as
//   skill(). Blood Boil hands it the demon. The only evaluator that takes a
//   second, caller-supplied unit is 0x3B4D10, and it puts that unit in +28
//   (and +38): that is the "source" the sksrc documentation describes for
//   monster-scope formulas. Blood Boil simply never uses it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Sites A and B: the call is redirected, the evaluator is reproduced
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Both 5-byte calls to 3B5160 become calls into a relay on a page this
//   plugin allocates within rel32 reach:
//
//     +00  4C 89 64 24 30       mov  [rsp+30h],r12   ; 6th argument = caster
//     +05  0F 1F 44 00 00       nop
//     +0A  FF 25 00 00 00 00    jmp  [rip+0]
//     +10  dq                   EvaluateWithCasterSource
//
//   [rsp+30h] at the relay is the callback's own [rsp+28h], the sixth-argument
//   slot of its outgoing area (the callback passes eleven arguments at
//   521694), which holds nothing across a call. The tail jump leaves the
//   callback's return address on top, so EvaluateWithCasterSource runs as if
//   the callback had called it with one more argument.
//
//   EvaluateWithCasterSource reproduces 3B5160 line for line, with the single
//   difference that slot +28 holds the caster. It calls the same interpreter
//   with the same resolver, callback table, count and context vtable, so
//   every function and token, and any plugin hook on them (playermode-
//   exposing hooks the stat callback 0x3B33F0), behaves as in a native call:
//
//     3B5188  call 300A90              GetDataTables(dataContext)
//     3B5192  cmp  rbx,[rax+140h]      offset >= pool size -> return 0
//     3B51A2  lea  rax,[1D0AEB8]       context vtable at +00
//     3B51A9  mov  byte [rsp+0A4h],0   +44
//     3B51BD  mov  [rsp+0ACh],eax      +4C skill level
//     3B51C4  mov  [rsp+0A0h],eax      +40 skill level
//     3B51CB  mov  [rsp+0A8h],r14d     +48 skill id
//     3B51D3  [rsp+68h..98h] = rdi     +08..+38 the unit, seven times
//     3B5202  length = pool size - offset, asserts unless 0..INT32_MAX
//     3B526C  add  rdx,[rdi]           code = pool base (+138h) + offset
//     3B5296  call A234B0              (ctx, code, length, 3B61D0, 1D09C50, 9, &context)
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Site C: the lethal sacrifice
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   52149B, 6 bytes, 0F 8E 7E 02 00 00 -> 0F 8E <rel32>: the same jle, aimed at
//   a relay, so the relay only runs when vanilla would skip the demon. It asks
//   LetSacrificeKill(game, caster, demon) and continues at 5214A1 (sacrifice,
//   then area damage) or at 52171F (the vanilla skip):
//
//     +00  41 51                push r9              ; percent, live into 5214AA
//     +02  48 83 EC 28          sub  rsp,28h         ; rsp is 16-aligned at 52149B
//     +06  4C 89 F9             mov  rcx,r15         ; game
//     +09  4C 89 E2             mov  rdx,r12         ; caster
//     +0C  49 89 F8             mov  r8,rdi          ; demon
//     +0F  48 B8 imm64          mov  rax,LetSacrificeKill
//     +19  FF D0                call rax
//     +1B  48 83 C4 28          add  rsp,28h
//     +1F  41 59                pop  r9
//     +21  84 C0                test al,al
//     +23  74 0E                je   +33h
//     +25  FF 25 00 00 00 00    jmp  [rip+0]         ; 5214A1
//     +33  FF 25 00 00 00 00    jmp  [rip+0]         ; 52171F
//
//   rax, rcx, rdx, r8, r10 and r11 are dead at both targets; r9 is the only
//   live volatile register and is restored. No branch in the callback lands
//   inside any of the three patched windows.
//
//   Why a demon may die inside the walk:
//     - The sacrifice 0x51EAD0 is an ordinary damage packet (HitFlags 0x1000,
//       ResultFlags 0x21, life damage at +12Ch) run through 0x44CE80 and
//       0x44A9B0, the same two calls the holy auras use for their damage, so a
//       lethal sacrifice kills through the normal death path.
//     - Both demon walks (inline in 0x4FFA70, and 0x5014C0) load the next demon
//       before they call the callback, so the current demon dying cannot break
//       the walk. Both loops are fingerprinted below.
//     - A demon that is already dead (0x34C2C0) keeps the vanilla skip, so a
//       corpse still in the pet list never fires the area damage.
//
//   The stat is read on the caster with 0x2F5020 (STATLIST_GetUnitStat, the
//   loader thunk to ReadWideUnitStat), which is what stat('x'.accr) reads, and
//   is bounded by the itemstatcost row count at DataTables+0x1260, the same
//   bound the stat() callback applies at 0x3B344B.
//
// Neighbours: no other CelestialRayOne plugin patches 0x520F80..0x521750 or
// 0x3B5160.

// 0x3B5160..0x3B52B8: the whole skills.txt evaluator this plugin reproduces.
// Its rel32 and RIP-relative operands pin 0x300A90, 0x1D0AEB8, 0x3B61D0,
// 0x1D09C50 and 0xA234B0.

constexpr std::uint8_t SourceStub[]{
    0x4C, 0x89, 0x64, 0x24, 0x30,                   // mov  [rsp+30h], r12
    0x0F, 0x1F, 0x44, 0x00, 0x00,                   // nop
    0xFF, 0x25, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,             // jmp  [rip+0]
    0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, // dq   target
};

using BbeInterpreterFn    = std::int32_t(__fastcall*)(std::uint8_t dataContext, const std::uint8_t* code,
                                std::int32_t length, std::uintptr_t resolver, std::uintptr_t callbacks,
                                std::int32_t callbackCount, void* context) noexcept;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// celestialrayone.blood-magic
//
// While a player has state 114 (blood_mana), skill and aura costs are paid
// from life only. A cost above current life is refused, mana is never used,
// and a payment never ends the state. See celestialrayone.blood-magic.toml.
//
// Target: D2RLoader 1.3.1 (Diablo II: Resurrected 3.3.93847), PluginSDK 0.3.0.
// Game addresses are RVAs in the D2RLoader.exe image (base 0x140000000).
// D2RCore.dll addresses are RVAs in that DLL as shipped with 1.3.1
// (SHA-256 2A868D013D2E0830BD2D9E04B918B19E46A73CF726C833E70D089B948FDEB5A2).
//
// The engine, for a player with state 114:
//   cast gate   00436750  loader jump into D2RCore CanAffordWideUsedSkill:
//                         life >= cost. Correct, left alone.
//   skill cost  00436830  loader jump into D2RCore ConsumeWideSkillResource,
//                         whose body (003DD6E0) jumps to D2RCore 003DDB50.
//   aura cost   00436A80  native periodic payment, called by four aura skill
//                         functions (00563650, 00563CE1, 00564224, 00564578).
//                         For state 114 it jumps to native 00584E80.
// Both life payments (003DDB50 and 00584E80) still run the vanilla Blood Mana
// curse rules:
//   life < cost  expire and free the state 114 stat list (its remove callback
//                turns the state off), set life to 256 (1 life), return 0.
//   paid         take cost from life; if life is now below the granting
//                skill's dword [record+1C8h] << 8, expire and free the list.
// With the state off, the gate and both payments charge mana again: life
// below the cost, the skill still casts, mana is taken.
// 00584E80 also faults when state 114 is set but its stat list is gone: it
// hands the null lookup to 002F56A0, which reads [rbx+28h] at 002F56C1. A
// granting skill id without a Skills.txt record faults at 00584EF7. Neither
// was ever dead code: the aura payment still reaches it.
//
// The fix replaces both life payments with one rule:
//   life < cost  return 0. Nothing is taken, the state stays.
//   otherwise    take cost from life, return 1. The state stays.
// The callers are unchanged, so non-players, item charges, players without
// state 114 and the cast gate run exactly as before.
//
// Sites:
//   D2RCore 003DDB50  reached from the export ConsumeWideSkillResource: stub
//                     call -> payment body -> the body's tail jump for state
//                     114. Stub, body and routine are compared byte for byte,
//                     leaving out rip-relative displacements and rel32s to
//                     other functions; the D2RCore slots they call through
//                     must name the expected game functions. The first 14
//                     bytes become an absolute jump into this DLL and are
//                     restored on unload.
//   game 00584E80     inline hook through the loader, after the whole routine
//                     and the aura payment's jump to it are compared.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct ByteGap {
	std::size_t offset;
	std::size_t size;
};

// Vanilla blood mana life payment, (unit, cost) -> 1 paid / 0 refused.
//   +014h mov edx, 72h / +019h call 002F5940     state 114 stat list, no null test
//   +021h call 002F56A0                          granting skill id (faults on null)
//   +032h call 002F5020                          life
//   +03Ch jl  +0CCh                              life < cost
//   +04Eh call 002F34F0                          life -= cost
//   +077h mov ecx, [rbx+1C8h] / shl ecx, 8       threshold from the granting skill
//   +09Fh call 002F7920 / +0B2h call 002F4180    expire and free the stat list
//   +0FFh mov r8d, 100h / +10Ch call 002F7D10    life = 256 on the refuse path

// Periodic payment, (unit, cost) -> int. Its only route into the routine
// above: state 114 -> jmp 00584E80.
//   +01Eh call 003351B0 with edx = 72h (lea edx, [rax+72h] at +018h)
//   +036h jmp 00584E80

// 00436830: the game's skill payment entry, a loader jump into D2RCore
// ConsumeWideSkillResource. Only checked, to confirm skill casts reach the
// D2RCore routine this plugin replaces.

// Payment body, RVA 003DD6E0, 527 bytes. (context, unit, skill id, level).
//   +140h call [slot 0033AA00]                   the skill's cost
//   +161h mov edx, 72h / +166h call [slot 003351B0]
//   +199h jmp 003DDB50                           state 114: life payment

// Blood mana life payment, RVA 003DDB50, 473 bytes. (context, unit, cost).
// The context is never read.
//   +01Fh mov edx, 72h / +024h call [slot 002F5940]
//   +036h call [slot 002F56A0]                   granting skill id
//   +0CCh mov r9d, 100h                          life = 256 on the refuse path
//   +13Dh call [slot 00097790]                   granting skill's record
//   +148h mov edi, [rax+1C8h] / shl edi, 8       threshold

// A call qword ptr [rip+disp32] whose slot is D2RCore's entry for the game
// function at `rva`. Each entry is { address, rva, address }; the address is
// zero until D2RCore resolves it.

// Boss Set Unique Drop
//
// Drops a named unique (uniqueitems.txt index) when the last monster of a
// configured set of monster ids dies, and replaces the game's hardcoded uber
// Tristram drop, which gives the last of the three uber bosses a RANDOM unique
// large charm. Vanilla has a single unique large charm, so that random pick is
// always the Hellfire Torch; a mod with several is not.
//
// Everything below was read out of the live D2RLoader 1.3.1 image (D2R
// 3.3.93847) in IDA before being relied on, and every byte the plugin checks
// was copied from that image. The four function entries that the
// RuffnecKk-D2RLoader-Suite corpus also documents match it byte for byte.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The vanilla drop: QUESTSFX_UberPrimeEvil at 0x5A8450
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   monumod.txt questcomplete (umod 22) has a mode-change callback, 0x4A1480,
//   in the umod table at 0x2396210 (row 22, column 1, through the dispatcher
//   0x498F80). On a death mode change it switches on the monster class;
//   704, 705 and 709 go to 0x5A8450. That routine sets one flag per boss in
//   the game (704 -> +1A5FC, 705 -> +1A5F8, 709 -> +1A5F4), and once all three
//   are set it runs, with rbx = game and rsi = the boss that died:
//
//     5A84C2  83 BB FC A5 01 00 00   cmp  dword [rbx+1A5FCh], 0 (+F8h, +F4h)
//     5A84E9  BA 63 6D 32 20         mov  edx, 'cm2 '
//     5A84EE  48 89 6C 24 58         mov  [rsp+58h], rbp
//     5A84F3  48 8B CE               mov  rcx, rsi
//     5A84F6  E8 -> 34F390           set the boss's drop item code
//     5A84FB  33 ED                  xor  ebp, ebp
//     5A84FD  4C 8D 4C 24 50         lea  r9, [rsp+50h]          ; &item level
//     ...                            stack args from rbp (0) and -1
//     5A8519  44 8D 45 07            lea  r8d, [rbp+7]           ; quality 7
//     5A851D  E8 -> 43FBF0           drop an item at the boss
//     5A852A  E8 -> 46F310           RefillWideItemCharges(game, 0, item)
//     5A852F  BA 73 74 64 20         mov  edx, 'std '
//     5A853C  8B 83 40 01 00 00      mov  eax, [rbx+140h]        ; client count
//     5A8561  41 B8 02 00 00 00      mov  r8d, 2                 ; normal
//     5A8572  E8 -> 43FBF0           one Standard of Heroes per client
//
//   The unique is never named. 0x43FBF0 hands quality 7 and the base code to
//   the item generator, and the unique pick 0x443910 rolls among every
//   eligible unique of that base, weighted by rarity.
//
//   The patch: the two bytes at 5A84FD become EB 30, jmp 5A852F. The xor at
//   5A84FB has already run (the Standard loop stores rbp as its zero stack
//   arguments), the drop code is overwritten with 'std ' right after, and the
//   Standard of Heroes drops are untouched. Only the random unique and its
//   recharge call are skipped. The bytes go back on unload.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Kill detection: the monster death-mode callback
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Every monster death enters mode 0 through the monster mode table at
//   0x1D1A200: 0x20 bytes per mode, start callback first, so slot 0 holds
//   MONSTERMODE_DeathModeCallback 0x444F50(game, modeChange) next to 0x445970,
//   and mode 1 starts with 0x445A80 and 0x444F30. Nothing else references
//   0x444F50. At 444F75 it loads the dying unit from modeChange+08h, and at
//   444FBE it calls D2RCore (j_D2RCore_1370), which runs the normal drop and
//   raises D2RLoader's monster-death event. D2RCore builds that event from the
//   same two pointers (game id = word game+08h, class = unit+04h, GUID =
//   unit+08h) but hands plugins only the numbers, and a GUID alone does not
//   lead back to the unit: SERVER_GetUnitByIdAndType 0x9A5A0 found nothing
//   for the dead uber Baal. So the plugin takes the pointers where D2RCore
//   does: slot 0 is swapped (compare-exchange) for a wrapper that calls what
//   the slot held, 0x444F50 or an entry hook another plugin put on it, and
//   then counts the kill. Any monster id works, not only the three with the
//   questcomplete monumod.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The replacement drop: 0x43FBF0 step for step, plus the unique row
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   0x43FBF0 (game, unit, quality, int* itemLevel, out request, a6, a7):
//     item level   monster: stat 12 (level) through 0x2F5020, at least 1
//     base         0x313EF0(game+106h data context, drop code), -1 = unknown
//     origin       0x34A040(unit, &coords), x low dword, y high dword
//     room         UNITS_GetRoom 0x34B440
//     position     (x+2, y+3) when 0x38DFB0(room, x+2, y+3) finds it, else (x, y)
//     free spot    0x3632F0(room, &position, &origin, game+124h ? 3 : 1,
//                  3E01h, 801h, 1) returns the drop room, position in/out
//     request      0xC8 bytes, zero from +44h:
//                  +00 unit  +08 seed 0  +10 game  +18 item level
//                  +20 item id  +24 spawn type 3  +28 x  +2C y  +30 room
//                  +38 word 1  +3A word game+128h  +3C force 0  +40 quality
//     create       0x43CD50(game, &request, 0)
//
//   The one addition is the field a treasureclass entry naming a unique sets:
//   request+50h = uniqueitems row + 1. In 0x443910 every row is tested on its
//   code (+28h against the base's items code), version (+24h), flags word
//   (+2Ch), ladder seasons (+8Ch, +8Eh) and lvl (signed word +38h, against the
//   item level). When request+50h - 1 is the row being tested and it passed,
//   the row is taken at once, without the rarity roll and without the
//   spawnable bit or dropconditioncalc that random candidates also need. Then
//   the once-per-game rule: a row already marked in the game's bitfield at
//   game+3638h fails the pick (the item falls back to rare or magic), and a
//   row without nolimit (flags bit 1) is marked. A row that does not pass the
//   tests is skipped, and the pick rolls among the other uniques of the base,
//   so the plugin raises the item level to the unique's lvl when it is lower.
//
//   Afterwards the plugin calls 0x46F310 (RefillWideItemCharges) with (game,
//   null, item), the same call 5A852A makes, and checks the result with
//   ITEMS_GetItemQuality 0x36CF60 and the file index getter 0x36CE70
//   (ItemData+34h), both of which 0x443910 calls. 0x2F5020 and 0x46F310 are
//   the game entries D2RCore routes to ReadWideUnitStat and
//   RefillWideItemCharges, so the plugin calls exactly what the game calls.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// uniqueitems.txt, compiled by 0x31F1D0
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Records of 348 bytes: +00 word row, +02 index (31 chars + NUL), +22 string
//   id, +24 version, +28 code, +2C flags (spawnable bit 0, nolimit 1,
//   disabled 2, disablechronicle 3), +34 rarity, +38 lvl, +3A lvl req,
//   +8C/+8E first/last ladder season, +90 dropconditioncalc, properties from
//   +98. The live rows are at DataTables+13C8h with a qword count at +13D0h,
//   DataTables from GetDataTablesForContext 0x300A90(game+106h), exactly as
//   0x443910 reads them. The plugin finds the configured name there at drop
//   time, so the row always comes from the table the game is using.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Verification before anything is installed
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Exact bytes of the live image, compared through CheckExpectedBytes:
//   - 5A84C2..5A8577, the whole flag check and drop block listed above;
//   - in 0x43FBF0 the level read at 43FC88, the base lookup at 43FDDA and the
//     position, request and create block 43FE10..43FF3B;
//   - in 0x443910 the table read at 443A70 and the lvl and row test at 443C5D;
//   - the entries of 0x34B440, 0x36CE70 and 0x36CF60;
//   - the mode table slot and its neighbours, and 444F75..444FBE in 0x444F50.
//   Every function the plugin calls is pinned by one of these, by its entry
//   or by the exact call the game itself makes to it, so a moved function is
//   caught before it is ever called. Any failure leaves the game untouched
//   and says why in the plugin log.

// 43FE10..43FF3B: 34A040 coords, 34B440 room, game+124h search size,
// 38DFB0 at (x+2, y+3), 3632F0(room, &pos, &origin, size, 3E01h, 801h, 1),
// the 0xC8-byte request and call 43CD50(game, &request, 0).

// 444F75..444FBE in 0x444F50: mov rdi, [rdx+8] (the dying unit), mov rbx, rdx,
// mov rdx, rcx, mov rsi, rcx, the game calls 48FF40, 491130 and 448A30, then
// rcx = game and rdx = modeChange for D2RCore's handler at 444FBE.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::size_t         size;
    const char*         label;
};

struct Pointer {
    std::size_t   offset;
    std::uint64_t rva;
};

// celestialrayone.cast-on-cast
//
// Port of ESR's D2R 2.4 cast-on-cast patch set to D2R 3.2. Every RVA is an
// offset into the D2RLoader process image and was read from that image. Each
// site, and every layout fact the handlers rely on, is fingerprinted before a
// single byte is written; on any mismatch the plugin installs nothing.
//
// 2.4 entry                                       3.2 equivalent
// CastOnCast_doactive_hook 298A12 + cave 2C0810   relay at 43AF1D, skill do-handler sub_14043ACB0
// SrvmissileProc_skip_a6a7_block 36538D           byte patch at 589C6F, item-effect caster sub_140589930
// CltCastExec_skip_itemproc_coordblock 1732AB     byte patch at 216DBB, client cast executor sub_140216D20
// CltProcCast_reassert_target 1792FC + 2C0A00     relay at 2312E3, client item-effect cast sub_1402310B0
// CltMissile_tickrewind 1716E7 + 2C0878           relay at 213F28, client missile builder sub_140213D80
//
// The 2.4 caves lived in .text. Here the relay code lives in one page the
// plugin allocates within rel32 reach of the image, and the game sites jump
// into it. Relays never move rsp, so each relay reuses the unwind data of the
// game function it was entered from and stack walks pass through it cleanly.
//
// The cast gate reads the cast animation per unit type. Players and monsters
// number their modes separately: a player casts in mode 10 and a monster,
// hirelings included, in mode 7. The 2.4 gate only knew the player number, so
// a mercenary's cast was never recognised as one.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Layout facts (3.2), each proven from this image
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Unit+0x00 type, +0x0C animation mode, +0x38 path: the engine's own inline
// code reads them this way (sub_140213D80, sub_140216D20 and sub_14043ACB0 all
// test *unit == 2 || *unit - 4 < 2 before reading the path at +0x38).

// Cast animation per unit type. Player SC is mode 10, monster SC is mode 7
// (monster 10 is S3). Hirelings are monsters (type 1) and take the mode of
// each skill from the Mode columns of hireling.txt.

// Monster mode callback table: 16 records {start, update, end, flag} of 0x20
// bytes, indexed by monster mode. A1 (4), A2 (5), SC (7), S1 (8) and S2 (9)
// share the attack-or-skill pair, start sub_140445F10 and update
// sub_140446000, whose action frame runs the used skill through
// sub_140436B10, the do-handler call with consumeResources 1. BL (6) has the
// block pair, and S3 (10) has its own update sub_140445C00, which runs no
// skill. That is the DT..RN order of monmode.txt, so the table proves
// MonsterCastMode. It holds absolute pointers, so records 4..10 are compared
// against image base + RVA at load. On a mismatch only mercenary and monster
// casts stay off; players are unaffected.

// DynamicPath: precision X/Y dwords at +0x00/+0x04 (PATH_GetX/GetY return
// their high words), first point words at +0x10/+0x12 and target unit at +0x70
// (the set-target-point and get-target-unit accessors), per-tick step at
// +0x96/+0x9A (sub_140380FD0 stores (direction * velocity) >> 8 there, with
// velocity at +0xA0). Identical to 2.4.

// skills.txt row flags start at +0x24 in the same bit order as 2.4: the
// do-handler tests bit 0 (decquant) and bit 1 (lob) of that byte, so the aura
// flag is still bit 5.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t size;
    const char* what;
};

// Item-effect caster sub_140589930 (2.4 PROC_FireSkill): the 12th argument is
// loaded from [rsp+0xF8] and stored as argument 7 (itemEffect) of the call to
// the do-handler at 589C93.

// Client item-effect cast sub_1402310B0(unit, skillId, level, targetUnit,
// targetX, targetY, itemProc). 7 pushes + sub rsp,70h put the packet target
// at [rsp+0xD0]/[rsp+0xD8] and itemProc at [rsp+0xE0].

// D2RLoader 1.3.1 accepts a jmp-rel32 patch only when its target is inside
// D2R.exe, and the relay page is not. Each hook site therefore jumps to a
// 5-byte "jmp relay" trampoline in int3 padding between two game functions,
// and the trampoline jumps on to the relay block. A jmp changes no register,
// flag or stack slot, so every relay sees exactly what its hook site saw. Each
// window is the ret that ends the function before the padding plus the whole
// int3 run, so the padding is proved unused before a byte is written.
//   server         43ACA1  int3 run 43ACA1..43ACAF, after the ret at 43ACA0
//   client target  231363  int3 run 231363..23136F, after the ret at 231362
//   client rewind  21401B  int3 run 21401B..21401F, after the ret at 21401A
// All three sit above LowestHookRva, so the relay page is in reach of them.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relay stubs (keystone-assembled, capstone and unicorn verified)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Server, entered at 43AF1D with rdi game, rbx caster, rsi skills.txt row:
//   mov rcx,rdi / mov rdx,rbx / mov r8,rsi / mov r9d,[rsp+E0h]
//   call [OnServerSkillDo] / movzx r14d,byte [rsi+24h] / xor bpl,bpl / jmp [43AF25]
// Client target, entered at 2312E3 with rbx caster:
//   mov rcx,rbx / mov edx,[rsp+D0h] / mov r8d,[rsp+D8h] / call [OnClientProcCastTarget]
//   movzx r9d,word [rsp+E0h] / jmp [2312EC]
// Client rewind, entered at 213F28 with rcx command, edx 0:
//   call [sub_1401B7760] / mov [rsp+20h],rax / mov rcx,rax / call [OnClientMissileCreated]
//   mov rax,[rsp+20h] / jmp [213F2D]
// None of them moves rsp. The callees use the host frame's home space, which is
// scratch at all three sites; [rsp+20h] in the rewind host is its outgoing
// argument slot, unused after the creator call.

// 2.4 cave 2C0810: fire doactive for a real cast. Gate as in 2.4: cast
// animation, not an aura, and a cast that pays its cost (item procs call the
// do-handler with consumeResources 0, which is what stops recursion). The cast
// animation is read per unit type, so a mercenary qualifies in monster SC.
// Monster casts reach this site through the monster attack-or-skill updater,
// the same used-skill executor as players. The other unit is the caster's
// path target, so EventFunc20 casts on that unit, or at the path's first
// point when there is none.

// 2.4 cave 2C0A00: the client start function overwrites the path's first point
// with the caster's facing before the executor runs. Write the packet's target
// point back right before the executor call.

// 2.4 cave 2C0878: step the new missile back by exactly one movement tick. The
// step vector was stored by the path setup during creation, so the rewind
// scales with the missile's own speed and direction.

// Each relay block is described with the unwind data of the game function the
// relay is entered from. Code starts 0x100 into its block, past any prolog
// length, so the whole host frame is always unwound.

