// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Charge impact  -  D2RLoader plugin (D2R 3.x)
//
// Port of the three 2.4 static patches:
//     "Charge Missile-on-Hit Hook"   (2.4 RVA 386020)
//     "Charge Missile-on-Hit Cave"   (2.4 RVA 149B82C, 578 bytes)
//     "Charge srvdofunc - damage conversion uses Calc5 instead of Calc4"
//                                    (2.4 RVA 385F13)
//
// Behaviour, identical to the 2.4 caves:
//     When Charge (srvdofunc 67) connects and the engine queues the combat
//     record for that hit, the plugin
//         * evaluates one skills.txt calc column of the charging skill and
//           spawns that many server missiles,
//         * spawns them ON the target and aims them through it: the
//           destination is 2*target - caster, so they carry on in the charge
//           direction,
//         * then runs the impact skill once. The 2.4 cave called
//           SrvDo150_Smite(pGame, pUnit, 525, chargeLevel); here the handler
//           is taken from the impact skill's own skills.txt srvdofunc column,
//           so retuning that row in the txt keeps working.
//     The missile id is the one the engine itself would pick for the skill:
//     srvmissilea for a non progressive skill, srvmissilea/b/c by charge
//     count for a progressive one.
//     Only hits on monsters spawn missiles, which is the 2.4 cave's opening
//     test (cmp dword ptr [r12], 1).
//
// What is configurable
//     Every calc column this touches is a config value in the range 1..10.
//     skills.txt in 3.x really does carry calc1 through calc10: the column
//     names sit at 0x1CF9CD0..0x1CF9D18 and the txt loader at 0x302380 binds
//     them to record offsets 0x190, 0x194 ... 0x1B4, four bytes each.
//
// How it is implemented on 3.x
//     The 2.4 version replaced an instruction in the middle of the Charge
//     handler with a jmp into a code cave. A mid function hook cannot be
//     expressed as a C function (the detour would be entered without a return
//     address on the stack), so this plugin hooks the ENTRY of two functions:
//
//       0x5622C0  srvdofunc 67, Charge. Wrapping it marks "a charge is
//                 running on this thread" and carries the skill id and level.
//                 Verified through the srvdofunc table at 0x238EA00 slot 67,
//                 which must point at this exact address.
//       0x44B600  SUNITDMG_PrepareAndQueueCombatRecord
//                 (game, attacker, defender, D2Damage*, flag). The Charge
//                 handler calls it exactly once per landed impact, then
//                 drains durability (0x44B2B0) and sets overlay 147
//                 (0x349020). That call is the 2.4 hook site, so firing right
//                 after the trampoline reproduces the old insertion point
//                 exactly: damage first, then missiles, then the impact
//                 skill. Charge's melee range test (0x348650) only measures
//                 distance, so no other tick of the skill reaches this call.
//     Both hooks are inert for everyone else: the damage hook does one bool
//     test and forwards to the trampoline.
//
// Game functions used (RVAs into the running main module, D2RLoader.exe):
//     0x097790  DATATBLS_GetSkillsTxtRecordForContext(context, skillId)
//     0x3B5160  SKILLS_EvaluateSkillFormula(context, unit, code, skill, lvl)
//     0x571700  SKILLS_GetProgressiveSkillMissileId(unit, skillId)
//     0x1B89A0  missiles.txt row count for a context, used for the same bound
//               check the engine performs before creating a missile
//     0x5371A0  MISSILES_CreateMissileFromParams(game, params)
//     0x341A20  path X, movzx eax, word ptr [rcx+2]
//     0x341A30  path Y, movzx eax, word ptr [rcx+6]
//
// Missile params struct, 0x98 bytes, confirmed from two native builders
// (srvdofunc 11 at 0x5562B0 and the skill start wrapper at 0x4333F0):
//     +0x00 flags 0x21   +0x08 owner       +0x20 missile id
//     +0x24 nX           +0x28 nY          +0x2C nTargetX   +0x30 nTargetY
//     +0x34 skill id     +0x40 skill level
// Everything else stays zero, which is what the corrected 2.4 cave did.
// Missiles built this way do not carry the attack rating stamp themselves;
// the attack-rating-overhaul plugin's missile fix covers that at creation.
//
// unit:  +0x00 unit type (1 = monster),  +0x38 path pointer
// path:  unit types 2, 4 and 5 keep the position as dwords at +0x10/+0x14,
//        every other type as words at +0x02/+0x06. That is what the engine
//        does inline at each of these sites.
//
// Runtime settings live in
// d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.charge-impact.toml, created with defaults
// on first load. The values below are only the fallbacks used when a key is
// missing or the file cannot be read.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The two calc reads inside the Charge handler, both `mov r8d, [r13+disp32]`
// feeding SKILLS_EvaluateSkillFormula. The displacement is the skills.txt
// record offset of the calc column, so repointing them is a four byte write.
//     0x5627CB  calc1 -> the charge's own damage
//     0x5627F8  calc4 -> the elemental conversion percentage

// From here on the plugin never returns false: the first detour is live,
// and returning false makes the loader unload this DLL with a hook still
// pointing into it.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Charge Pursuit  -  D2RLoader plugin (D2R 3.3, D2RLoader process image)
//
// Makes monster Charge keep going until it hits an enemy instead of stopping
// a few frames after it starts.
//
// Monster Charge is skills.txt srvstfunc 31 (the launch) plus srvdofunc 67
// (the per tick body). A monster whose monstats.txt skill mode for the charge
// names a sequence (seq_...) runs it in monster mode 14, and in 3.3 that mode
// ends a charge early in five independent ways:
//
//   1. The sequence runs out. Mode 14 ends when the sequence's remaining
//      length (unit+0x64) reaches 0. srvdofunc 67 tries to keep the charge
//      alive by "rewinding" its animation, but the rewind (0x4914E0) only
//      resets the plain-mode animation fields (unit+0x5C/+0x60); it never
//      touches a sequence. A sequence charge therefore lasts exactly one pass
//      of its monseq.txt rows: a 5 row sequence moves the monster 5 ticks.
//   2. The per tick body only runs on tagged rows. Mode 14 starts with a
//      recurring tick event, but the first time srvdofunc 67 runs it deletes
//      every tick event and books a single one for the next frame, which only
//      it re-books. The mode 14 handler (0x446770) only calls it when the
//      current row carries event 1, 2 or 4, so the first untagged tick (or
//      any tick a slowed animation spends on the same row) leaves nothing
//      booked and the monster freezes on the server.
//   3. Packmates block. The charge steps with the monster's walking mask,
//      which treats the "unit present" bit (0x1000) that every monster and
//      player stamps on its own cell as a wall.
//   4. The distance budget runs out. The launch hardcodes 20 steps (engine cap
//      77); each subtile moved costs one.
//   5. The hit never lands on short sequences. On contact srvdofunc 67 defers
//      the hit to "the next skill tick", which it expects its rewind to book
//      from the rows after frame 8. A sequence with no tagged row there books
//      nothing, so the mode ends without the hit.
//
// What this plugin changes
//   keep_sequence_alive  while charging, the sequence's remaining length is
//                        topped up every tick, so the sequence loops (its row
//                        index already wraps on its own).
//   skill_every_tick     the mode 14 handler's read of "this tick's action
//                        event" (call at 0x44680C) answers "action" for a
//                        charge in progress, so srvdofunc 67 runs every tick
//                        and keeps re-booking it, the way the attack/skill
//                        mode handler already treats a charge.
//   impact_on_contact    the two calls from the mode 14 handler into the skill
//                        body (0x446802 path ended, 0x44683B action tick) go
//                        through a wrapper: when a charge has just made
//                        contact and the engine has no follow-up tick booked,
//                        the deferred hit is delivered on the same tick.
//   pass_through_allies  while a hostile monster charges, its movement uses
//                        the walking mask minus 0x1000 plus the player bit
//                        0x80 (movement 0x3812D0, launch 0x564DC0).
//   unlimited_distance   while charging, the budget is refilled every tick.
//   client_mirror        the movement rules and the sequence top-up also apply
//                        to the client's predicted copy of the monster.
//
// Hook sites, all verified byte for byte before anything is written:
//   0x564DC0  srvstfunc 31 entry (inline hook)
//   0x3812D0  per tick path update entry, shared by server and client
//             (inline hook). Callers: server 0x491AC0, client 0x0F9AEA and
//             0x1B5512. Second argument is a float in xmm1.
//   0x44680C  call 0x349E30 (action event getter) inside the mode 14 handler
//   0x446802  call 0x436B10 (skill perform) inside it, path ended branch
//   0x44683B  call 0x436B10 inside it, action tick branch
//   The three call sites are retargeted through a relay page; the skill
//   perform entry itself is NOT hooked (dynamic-etype owns it), it is called,
//   so its hook still sees every perform. srvdofunc 67 (0x5622C0) is not
//   touched: charge-aoe-hits owns its entry and dynamic-etype patches inside.
//
// Functions called: 0x34BA40 UNITS_GetUsedSkill, 0x436B10 skill perform
// (game, unit), 0x48B230 earliest booked frame of a unit's events
// (game, unit, type, customId), 0 when nothing is booked.
//
// Layouts used, each read from the 3.3 binary:
//   unit   +0x00 type (1 monster), +0x04 class id, +0x0C mode, +0x38 path,
//          +0x40 sequence rows, +0x48 sequence length (x256), +0x54 sequence
//          speed, +0x64 sequence remaining (decremented by 0x3476B0),
//          +0x6A action event (byte), +0x100 skill list, +0x128 bit 27
//          animation finished
//   path   +0x02/+0x06 position, +0x10/+0x12 target, +0x30 point index,
//          +0x34 point count, +0x48 flags (0x10 client copy), +0x50 path type
//          (7 straight, set by the launch), +0x5C collision pattern, +0x64
//          move test mask, +0x68 collided-with mask, +0xBC distance budget
//   skill  +0x00 skills.txt record, +0x14 flags (bit 0 charging),
//          +0x2C/+0x30 target unit type / id (type 6 = none)
//   record +0x4E srvdofunc (word)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Server: as ServerChargeSkill, in an action mode.
// Client: the client keeps no charge flags, so it is a monster on that
// straight path, in an action mode, whose used skill (when the client knows
// one) is a srvdofunc 67 row.

// Keeps a sequence from running out: the advance (0x3476B0) subtracts the
// sequence speed from the remaining length every frame and ends the mode at 0,
// while the row index wraps by itself. One more full pass is added whenever
// fewer than "one pass plus two frames" remain.

// Replaces the mode 14 handler's call to the action event getter (0x44680C).
// Returns the same byte the getter returns (unit+0x6A), except that a charge
// in progress always gets an action event, so the handler runs srvdofunc 67
// on every frame. The handler still applies its own "path still moving" and
// "skill body not already run" tests to the answer.

// mov rax, imm64 / jmp rax. Entered by the retargeted call, so the return
// address the handler pushed is still on the stack and the C++ target returns
// straight to the handler. rax is the return value register anyway.

// =============================================================================
//  Charm Dedup  -  celestialrayone.charm-dedup
//
//  Groups of charms, matched by item code, of which only one charm can be
//  active at a time. The other charms of the group stay where they are, give
//  no bonuses, and are drawn with the red background of an unusable item.
//
//  Built for D2R 3.3 under D2RLoader 1.3.1. Every address below was read out
//  of the dumped D2RLoader 1.3.1 image or D2RCore.dll 1.3.1 and disassembled
//  before being relied on. 1.3.1 moved both thunks, their slots and the two
//  D2RCore exports; the export bodies are the 1.3.0 code, rebuilt (the only
//  change is where D2RCore keeps its own inactive-charm tint flag, which the
//  plugin never touches). Byte witnesses guard all of them, so a different
//  build refuses cleanly and the game runs stock.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHERE A CHARM IS SWITCHED ON AND OFF
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  D2RLoader moved the charm predicate into D2RCore.dll. All 12 game callers
//  of the stock ITEMS_IsCharmUsable (0x36AE00, now dead) call the import thunk
//  at 0x3E2B61C (jmp [0x3E2ACA0]), which lands on D2RCore!IsCharmUsable:
//
//      IsCharmUsable(item, owner) = item is valid
//                                && item type 13 (charm)
//                                && its page is allowed by the Charm
//                                   Inventory policy (0 and/or custom page 6)
//                                && item requirements are met
//
//  Nothing inside D2RCore calls it directly (no call, no RIP reference), so
//  the thunk is the only door.
//
//  The game attaches a stored charm's stat list to its owner only when that
//  predicate says yes and the stat list is not attached yet:
//      server  0x470CA0 (inventory refresh, run after every item transaction)
//      client  0x1C9610 (client inventory refresh), and the item packet
//              handlers 0x1C37F0, 0x1C4300, 0x1C5060
//  and the client detaches a charm it removes only when the predicate still
//  says yes (0x1C60A0). Stats, skills and item auras all follow the stat list.
//
//  THE RULE, answered for a charm whose code is in a group:
//    1. its stat list is attached to its owner   -> stock answer (stays on)
//    2. another charm of the group is attached   -> no
//    3. an eligible charm of the group has a
//       lower unit id                            -> no
//    4. otherwise                                -> stock answer
//  "Eligible" is D2RCore's own answer for that charm, so a charm in the stash,
//  the cube, on the cursor or with unmet requirements never blocks another.
//
//  Rule 1 means the predicate never turns an active charm off. The game never
//  has to detach anything outside its own paths, and the client removal path
//  above still detaches every charm it attached. No write of any kind is made
//  to an item, a unit or a stat list: the engine does all of that itself.
//  Rule 3 is order independent (unit ids are the same on client and server),
//  so when nothing is active both sides pick the same charm.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  RED BACKGROUND
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The inventory item renderer 0x2C3570 calls the thunk at 0x3E2B298
//  (jmp [0x3E2A808]) -> D2RCore!CheckInventoryItemRequirementsForDisplay at
//  0x2C3774 and takes its red, requirements-not-met path at 0x2C377B when the
//  result is 0. It is the only caller. The plugin returns 0 there for a
//  grouped charm that is eligible but switched off by the rule. D2RCore's own
//  inactive-charm tint flag is left untouched, so the stock red is drawn.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  HOW THE THUNKS ARE TAKEN
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Each 6-byte thunk (FF 25 rel32) becomes jmp rel32 + nop to a jump stub in a
//  page the plugin allocates within reach of the image; the stub jumps to the
//  hook. The hooks call the D2RCore exports resolved by name, after checking
//  that each thunk slot holds exactly that export and that the export bodies
//  are the 1.3.1 bodies. Nothing in D2RCore.dll is modified.
//
//  Console command "charmdedup" shows the settings, what is installed and
//  counters. Settings: d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.charm-dedup.toml
// =============================================================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The rule for a charm whose code is in a group and that D2RCore already
//  considers usable. Env supplies the native queries, so the rule is testable.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct ThunkPatch {
    std::uint64_t               rva;
    const std::uint8_t*         stock;
    std::array<std::uint8_t, 6> written;
    bool                        applied;
};

// =============================================================================
//  Chronicle Tracker  -  celestialrayone.chronicle-tracker
//
//  1. A Set item, Unique item or Runeword completes its Chronicle entry the
//     moment a character comes into possession of it already identified:
//     picked up from the ground, or handed over by a Horadric Cube transmute.
//  2. A Set item, Unique item or Runeword on the ground whose entry the local
//     character has not completed gets a "Not in Chronicle" line under its
//     label (Alt labels and the hover name).
//
//  Everything below was read out of the dumped D2RLoader 1.3.1 image (md5
//  8af7a47c...). Every site is checked byte for byte at load; a part whose
//  check fails installs nothing and the game runs that part stock.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHY A TRANSMUTED UNIQUE NEVER COMPLETED ITS ENTRY
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The item builder 0x43CD50 (every drop and every cube output) marks a Set or
//  Unique item "Chronicle pending" when the game's eligibility check passes:
//      43CF6E  feature check 0x3B06C0(context, 4)
//      43CF7E  quality 0x36CF60: 5 set, 7 unique; a unique also needs its
//              items.txt record byte +13Ah clear and 0x374870(item) == 0
//      43CFBB  row record 0x3E2B2E6(context, row) (D2RCore thunk), then
//              0x375F80(record + 2Ch)
//      43CFDD  0x36C7B0 attaches the item's Chronicle record (ItemData+0xD0)
//      43CFF0  0x36D8F0(item, 0x10000000, 1), the pending flag
//  The server writes a Chronicle entry only through 0x4247D0(client, item),
//  which accepts a pending item or a runeword (flags 0x10000000 | 0x4000000):
//      46EA4C  identifying an item (0x46E8C0), pending items only
//      46EEA3  a runeword completed by socketing (0x46EB00)
//      424970  identifying for every player in the game, pending items only
//  The cube generator 0x5269C0 places each output (527E1B), sets it identified
//  itself (527E52) and sends it to the client (527E93). Identification never
//  runs, so nothing ever writes the entry: the output stays pending forever.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  1. COMPLETING ENTRIES: the item updates that hand an item to a player
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  0x535F60(game, player, item, ...) sends a placed item to its player. Three
//  of its calls are the moments an item comes into a player's possession:
//      471E69  pickup 0x471950 (packet 0x16), after placement on the cursor,
//              in the inventory, the belt or an equip slot
//      527E93  cube generator, each output after placement and identification
//      5280F6  cube generator, each socketed item a recipe took out
//  Each call goes through a slot on a stub page next to the image. The hook
//  runs the native update first, so the client already knows the item, then:
//      - unidentified: nothing, the game completes it when it is identified
//      - its entry already in the player's Chronicle (0x3D87D0 on 0x347440):
//        nothing
//      - pending or a runeword: 0x4247D0(client 0x48FDE0(player), item), the
//        game's own entry write, exactly as identification calls it
//      - otherwise, only if the builder's own eligibility check above passes:
//        the item gets the record and flag the builder gives a fresh drop
//        (0x36C7B0, 0x36D8F0, dropped-by 27229 through 0x36C480 as the builder
//        writes for an item without a source), then 0x4247D0
//  0x4247D0 writes the entry, sends packet 0x87 to the client and releases the
//  item's pending record, as it does after identification.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  2. THE GROUND LABEL LINE
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//      1FAA18  ground label builder 0x1FA9F0 calls the label text 0xCBEB0
//              (item, text, 128, color); the text is measured after it, so a
//              second line makes the label two lines tall
//      0C08C5  hovered unit name 0xC0420 calls the GetItemName thunk
//              0x3E2B634 (item, 1024-byte text), colours it at 0C08D4 and
//              copies 128 bytes at 0C08EC
//  Both calls go through the stub page. The hooks call the original targets,
//  so the loot filter's hooks on 0xCBEB0 and on the thunk keep working, then
//  append "\n" + colour + text when the item needs it:
//      - an item on the ground (mode 3 or 5)
//      - a set or unique (ItemData quality 5 or 7, row at +0x34) or a runeword
//        (row from the game's own Chronicle record builder 0x425F60)
//      - not in the local player's Chronicle (0x8B2D0, 0x9A480, 0x347440,
//        0x3D87D0)
//      - pending, a runeword, or passing the builder's eligibility check with
//        the item's own data context 0x34A0E0
//  The text is the string Key ChronicleTrackerLabel; the colour marker is
//  the one 0xCD6E0 writes, read once from it as the loot filter does.
//
//  Console command "chronicletracker" shows the state and the counts.
//  Settings: d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.chronicle-tracker.toml
// =============================================================================

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         what;
};

