// Finds D2RCore's handler for event function 20 through RegisterWideSkillEffect,
// checks the export and the handler byte for byte, and binds the handler as the
// function the hook calls through.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Native contract, D2RLoader 1.3.0 (D2R 3.3 image at base 0x140000000 plus
// D2RCore.dll). Re-derived from both binaries.
//
// Crushing Blow lives in D2RCore now. In 1.2.x the game image held the core
// (0x581BD0) and its two callers. In 1.3.0:
//
//   - 0x581BD0 is a thunk to D2RCore!ApplyWideCrushingBlow, and neither of
//     its game-image callers is reached any more: EventFunc16 at 0x583150 is
//     no longer registered, and 0x51D950 is dead code behind the Mirrored
//     Blades thunk at 0x51D7B0.
//     The event registrar 0x438230 is a thunk to D2RCore!RegisterWideSkillEffect,
//     which stores D2RCore's own handler for every event function (its table
//     entry 16 is D2RCore's Crushing Blow handler). The game image's handler
//     table is only read by dead code left behind the loader's thunks.
//
//   - The live paths are all inside D2RCore and all end in one internal
//     function, the Crushing Blow core:
//         D2RCore Crushing Blow event handler  (4 / 10 / 10, ranged = event 6)
//         D2RCore Mirrored Blades effect       (skill-calc divisors)
//         D2RCore!ApplyWideCrushingBlow export (the game-image thunk)
//     The first two call the core directly, so a hook on the game image
//     never sees them. This plugin therefore hooks the D2RCore core itself.
//     The loader's plugin hooks only address the game image, so this one hook
//     is installed by the plugin: the core is located through the export,
//     whose stub ends in `call core`, and both are byte-checked first.
//
// The core keeps the vanilla ABI and params block:
//     int32 __fastcall (context, pAttacker, pTarget, CrushingBlowParams*)
// and does, in order:
//     player                     -> divisor = params.playerDivisor
//     monster, hireling          -> divisor = params.hirelingDivisor
//     monster, monstats boss     -> divisor = params.monsterDivisor * 2
//        or MonTypeFlag 0x02
//     monster, anything else     -> divisor = params.monsterDivisor
//     monster (non hireling)     -> divisor += divisor * playersBonus / 100
//     params.rangedEvent         -> divisor *= 2
//     damage  = currentLife / divisor
//     damage -= damage * min(damageresist, 100) / 100
//     life    = max(life - damage, 0)
//
// This plugin only rewrites the params block before handing the call to the
// original. Every later step, including the boss doubling, the /players
// scaling, physical damage reduction, the kill flag and overlay 147, stays
// native. Those steps are multiplicative, so the result keeps exactly the
// configured ratio against vanilla.
//
// The classification helpers below are native game-image functions; the
// D2RCore core calls the same three monster queries.
//
// Crushing Blow effectiveness stat (1.2.0). [effectiveness] stat_id names an
// itemstatcost row. On every Crushing Blow the hook reads it from pAttacker
// with 0x2F5020, the game-image thunk to D2RCore!ReadWideUnitStat, called as
// (unit, stat, layer 0): the same total stat('x'.accr) reads. It takes the
// layer as a full 32-bit register and answers 0 for stat ids from 0x8000 up.
//
// The stat cannot go into the divisor: the core divides current life by an
// integer, so 8 * 100 / 125 = 6.4 would round to 6 and turn +25 % into +33 %
// against a monster at 50. The core therefore runs at the class divisor
// exactly as before, and the stat then adds its share of the life the core
// actually removed (after damage reduction), in 64 bits:
//     extra = removed * value / 100,  life = max(life - extra, 0)
// written with 0x2F7D10, the thunk to D2RCore!SetWideUnitStat
// (unit, stat, value, layer), on stat 6 hitpoints layer 0, the stat and
// writer the core itself uses. The core's own end steps are kept: life floors
// at 0, and the will-die bit (0x0002 in the D2DamageStrc result flags at
// +0x04, the word the critical-strike-damage plugin's verified 0x44C3C8 block
// also writes) is set when the stat's share kills, or put back to its state
// before the core when a negative value leaves alive a target the core had
// killed. Overlay 147 and the kill credit stay the core's.
//
// pAttacker is the unit that rolled the Crushing Blow chance. Both game-image
// paths D2RCore reimplements show it, read on the 1.3.1 image:
//   EventFunc16 0x583150: the chance is read at 0x583194 with
//     0x2F5C60(rdi, ...), and the core is called at 0x583257 with rdx = rdi.
//   Mirrored Blades 0x51D7B0: the chance is read at 0x51D846 with
//     0x2F5C60(rdi, ...), and the core is called at 0x51D950 with rdx = rdi.
// A missile hit fires the damage event on the missile's owner (D2MOO
// MissMode.cpp: SUNITDMG_ExecuteEvents(pGame, pOwner, ...)), so ranged
// Crushing Blow reads the shooter's value.
//
// Re-checked on D2RLoader 1.3.1: the four helpers, the 0x581BD0 thunk and
// the 0x2F5020 reader match the game image byte for byte. The D2RCore core is
// still checked at load, as before.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// D2RCore!ApplyWideCrushingBlow, 64 bytes:
//   +00 sub rsp,38h / +04 mov rax,[rip+cookie] / +0B xor rax,rsp /
//   +0E mov [rsp+30h],rax / +13 null checks of rcx, r8, r9 / +2F jne +40 /
//   +31 mov [rsp+28h],rcx / +36 lea rcx,[rsp+28h] / +3B call core
// The cookie displacement (+07) and the call rel32 (+3C) move between loader
// builds and are not pinned; the call target is decoded from them.

// The core's first 22 bytes: push r15 / push r14 / push r13 / push r12 /
// push rsi / push rdi / push rbp / push rbx / sub rsp,68h / mov r14,r9 /
// mov rsi,r8. The first 16 are position independent and are the ones the
// hook replaces with jmp qword ptr [rip+0] and two nops.

// The params block as the core reads it, each instruction byte-checked:
//   +025 mov ebx,[r9+8]                       monster divisor
//   +039 mov ebx,[r14+0Ch]                    player divisor
//   +052 mov ebx,[r14+10h]                    hireling divisor
//   +108 movzx ecx,byte [r14+14h] / shl ebx,cl ranged doubling
//   +1D7 mov rax,[r14]                        damage, receives the kill flag

struct CoreWitness {
    std::size_t                 offset;
    std::array<std::uint8_t, 7> bytes;
    std::size_t                 size;
};

// 0x2F5020 (unit in rcx, stat id in edx, layer in r8d), a loader import thunk:
//   2F5020  FF 25 xx xx xx xx   jmp [rip+disp32]
//   2F5026  90 90 90 90         nop x4
//   2F502A  48 89 74 24 20 ...  the untouched remainder of the old body
// disp32 locates the loader's import slot and moves between loader builds,
// so it is not pinned. Only checked while the stat is on.

// 0x2F7D10, STATLIST_SetUnitStat, the same 10-byte loader thunk shape
// (jmp [rip+disp32] / nop x4) into D2RCore!SetWideUnitStat. Only checked
// while the stat is on.

struct CrushingBlowParams {
    void*        damage;          // +0x00 D2DamageStrc, receives the kill flag
    std::int32_t monsterDivisor;  // +0x08 vanilla 4
    std::int32_t playerDivisor;   // +0x0C vanilla 10
    std::int32_t hirelingDivisor; // +0x10 vanilla 10
    std::uint8_t rangedEvent;     // +0x14 set when the event is domissiledamage
};

// base is the divisor the engine would have started from. The returned value
// leaves every later native multiplier intact, so the final ratio against
// vanilla is exactly targetPercent * rangedPercent.

// Core entry -> HookApplyCrushingBlowCore. The trampoline replays the 16
// replaced prologue bytes and jumps to core + 16, so OriginalCore is the
// untouched core.

//
// Extra Curses: the decision the plugin hands back to the timed-state builder.
//
// No Windows or SDK dependencies, so the same code is unit-tested against
// fake stat-list chains. plugin.cpp proves every offset below against the
// game and D2RCore images before it uses this.
//
// What the builder does with the answer (D2RCore 1.3.1 +0x3E77B9 onward):
//   nullptr            create the new curse, remove nothing
//   same state         stock refresh, or stock refusal of a lower-level recast
//   another curse      remove that curse the stock way, then create the new one
//

// statListEx is the target's extended stat list (unit + 0x88). The walk is the
// stock FindStatListByFlags(target, 0x20) walk: chain at +0x90, next at +0x68,
// a list matches when its flags have 0x20.
//
// The chain is newest first (PostStatList inserts at the head), so the walk
// meets the oldest curse last.

//
// Extra Curses, client side: when a new overlay command would delete another
// state's cast overlay before it finished, that cast hands over to its own
// follow-up overlays instead of vanishing.
//
// No Windows or SDK dependencies, so it is unit-tested on its own. plugin.cpp
// proves every offset below against the game image before using it.
//
// Overlay node (0xB8 bytes, built by the dispatcher at 0x150423..0x150514):
//   +00h mode, +04h overlay id, +08h follow-up 1, +0Ch follow-up 2,
//   +10h state (modes 8 and 9), +B0h next node.
//

// True when the dispatcher is about to delete `node` only because the new
// command uses the same cast overlay, while the node is still playing that
// cast for a DIFFERENT state's visuals (different follow-ups). Two states that
// share a cast overlay, every curse with the stock data, hit this whenever the
// second is applied before the first one's cast animation ended.
//
// Every other match keeps the stock behaviour:
//   - the same follow-ups: the same visuals again, the old one is replaced;
//   - a match through the node's follow-ups: the very overlay is re-added;
//   - a new command that is not a cast, or a node with nothing to hand over.

// Extra Curses
//
// Every point of a configurable stat lets its owner keep one more curse on the
// same target:
//
//   curses a caster can keep on one target = 1 + the caster's total of the stat
//
// With 0 points nothing changes: a new curse replaces the target's curse, as
// the game always did.
//
// WHERE THE ONE-CURSE RULE LIVES (D2RLoader 1.3.1, D2R 3.3)
// Every curse, buff and timed debuff is built by the game's timed-state
// builder. D2RLoader 1.3 replaced it: the game's 0x433D20 is now
// jmp [import] into D2RCore.dll's ApplyWideTimedStatEffect (export +0x8339D0),
// whose body at +0x3E7650 is a faithful port of the retail builder that also
// carries wide (16-bit) stat ids. When the new state is a curse for the
// target's unit type (IsCurseState, game 0x335A40, asked at +0x3E76E9), the
// builder looks for ANY curse already on the target:
//
//   3E7795  test eax,eax              ; eax = "is a curse"
//   3E779B  mov edx,20h / cmove edx,[rsi+24h]   ; curse: flag 0x20, else the state
//   3E77A4  lea rax,[slot +0x6FED10]  ; {pointer, RVA 0x2F5810} FindStatListByFlags
//   3E77AB  lea r8,[slot +0x6FEC08]   ; {pointer, RVA 0x2F5940} GetStatListFromUnitAndState
//   3E77B2  cmovne r8,rax
//   3E77B6  call qword ptr [r8]       ; FindStatListByFlags(target, 0x20)
//   3E77B9                            ; <- the only return address this plugin acts on
//
// and acts on what the lookup returns:
//
//   nothing                         3E7854  create the new curse, remove nothing
//   same state, skill and level     3E77DC  refresh: new expire frame and removal event
//   same state and skill, lower lvl 3E7816  refuse the new curse
//   anything else                   3E781C  remove that list, then create the new curse.
//                                           The removal is D2RCore's unlink (+0x3DA6E0),
//                                           which ends by tail-calling the list's remove
//                                           callback (+0x3DA86E) exactly as an expiring
//                                           curse does, then the list is freed (game
//                                           0x2F9EB0). An extended list (bit 31 of its
//                                           flags) is never removed (3E7820 js).
//
// So the whole rule is "what that lookup returns". D2RCore reaches the GAME's
// own FindStatListByFlags through its slot (the slot's RVA field reads 0x2F5810
// and the live pointer is checked to be Base + 0x2F5810), so an ordinary inline
// hook on the game function, acting only when its caller is that one call site,
// decides it:
//
//   - the target's existing curse of the same state, if it has one
//       -> stock refresh or stock refusal, never a slot of its own
//   - nothing, while the target holds fewer other curses than the caster's cap
//       -> the curse is added
//   - otherwise the curse picked by the replace policy
//       -> stock removal of that one curse, then the new one is added
//
// A landing curse therefore never removes more than one curse, like vanilla.
// Everything the builder checks first is untouched: the monster checks
// (+0x3E769F..+0x3E76E1), the target's stat 109 shortening the curse by that
// percentage and refusing it at 100 or more (+0x3E76F5..+0x3E774C), and the
// refusal when the target has state 57 (+0x3E774F..+0x3E7760).
//
// THE CURSE RECORD
// The builder keeps its 0x30-byte curse record in rsi from +0x3E7660
// (mov rsi,rdx) to the lookup, with no write to rsi in between, and its context
// in rdi (+0x3E7663, mov rdi,rcx). The pinned window below proves both. The
// hook recovers the builder's rsi and rdi by unwinding its own frames with
// RtlCaptureContext / RtlVirtualUnwind, which restore each caller's
// nonvolatile registers from the callee's unwind data, so no assembly and no
// generated code is involved. record+8 (target) is cross-checked against the
// lookup's own unit argument, which the builder loads from [rsi+8] at
// +0x3E7797. Layout, read off the window:
//   +00h caster  +08h target  +10h skill  +14h level  +18h duration
//   +1Ch stat id (-1 none)  +20h stat value  +24h state  +28h remove callback
//   context (rdi): +00h data context byte, +08h game; game frame at game+170h.
//
// STAT LISTS (game image, checked byte for byte at load)
//   0x2F5810  FindStatListByFlags(unit, flags): GetStatListEx (0x34B870 ->
//             [unit+88h]); needs the extended flag (bit 31 of [ex+1Ch]); walks
//             [ex+90h] (or [ex+98h] when flags has 0x2000) through next [+68h];
//             returns the first list whose flags share a bit with `flags`.
//   0x2F7AB0  SetStatListExpireFrame: frame > 0 sets flag 0x2 and stores
//             (float)frame at [list+24h].
//   0x2F5020  STATLIST_GetUnitStat, the loader's thunk to D2RCore's
//             ReadWideUnitStat: the unit's total for (stat, layer), 0 for a stat
//             id of 0x8000 or more.
//   D2RCore's PostStatList (+0x3DA4F0) links a list at the HEAD of [ex+90h]
//   (unless it has flag 0x2000), so the chain is newest first. State at
//   [list+20h] is written by the builder at +0x3E7959 and compared at +0x3E77C2.
//
// OTHER USERS OF THE CURSE LOOKUP (audited, left alone by design)
//   0x5C372D, 0x5C3CAF  monster AI: casts a curse only at an uncursed target.
//   Every other game caller passes another flag (1, 2, 8, 0x80, 0x800,
//   0x4000, 0x10000), or sits in a retail body the loader bypasses, or is the
//   debug stat-list inspector. D2RCore's other users of the slot pass 0x40,
//   0x80, 0x10000 or walk every flag bit for its stat inspector. None of them
//   returns to +0x3E77B9, so all of them get the stock answer.
//
// CLIENT: EVERY CURSE KEEPS ITS OWN LOOK (1.1.0)
// What the client does with a state's visuals (game image):
//   0x21A880  state on: a state the unit did not have gets its visuals from
//             0x21B640 (call at 0x21A9C1).
//   0x21B640  castoverlay ([rec+0Ah]) set: ONE node,
//             dispatch(unit, castoverlay, 4 (9 for some states), 0,
//                      overlay1 [rec+2], overlay2 [rec+4], 0 (or the state));
//             no castoverlay: overlay1 and overlay2 as looping nodes (mode 3/8).
//   0x150280  dispatcher: before adding, deletes the first node of the unit
//             with the same id, or a cast node (mode 4/9) whose follow-ups
//             include the id (0x1503BD..0x1503FF -> 0x155CB0, returns 0x150404).
//   0x153300  overlay tick: when a cast node's animation ends it is deleted and
//             its follow-ups are dispatched as looping nodes, mode 4 -> mode 3
//             (0x153551..0x1535B9), mode 9 -> mode 8 while the state is on
//             (0x1535BE..0x15363C).
//   0x21AC50  state off: plays removerlay, then removes overlay1 and overlay2
//             (every size variant, 0x21B400 -> 0x152090). It never removes by
//             the cast overlay.
// The curses share one cast overlay, so a curse that lands while another
// curse's cast animation still plays deletes that cast node before it hands
// over, and the earlier curse's look never appears although the curse is on.
// With one curse at a time the earlier curse was gone anyway.
//
// The fix hooks the node removal (0x155CB0) and acts only on the call from
// the dispatcher's collision check (return address 0x150404). When the node is
// a cast for a DIFFERENT state's visuals (same cast overlay, other follow-ups),
// it hands over exactly as the tick would at the end of the animation: the
// cast node is deleted and its follow-ups are dispatched (mode 3, or mode 8
// when the state is still on). Then the dispatcher adds the new cast as usual.
// Every other removal is stock. State-off removal is by each state's own
// overlay ids, so it keeps working per curse.
//
// The dispatcher's new command is read from its frame: id in edi
// (0x150295 mov edi,edx), mode in r15d (0x150292 mov r15d,r8d), follow-ups
// as its fifth and sixth arguments at [rsp+180h] / [rsp+188h] (five pushes and
// sub rsp,130h). Registers come from unwinding this plugin's own frames, as
// on the server side.

// FindStatListByFlags, 2F5810..2F58B4:
//   2F5810  mov [rsp+10h],rbx / push rdi / sub rsp,20h / mov edi,edx /
//           mov rbx,rcx / test rcx,rcx                        <- hooked (18 bytes)
//   2F583A  call 0x34B870 (GetStatListEx)
//   2F5861  cmp dword [rbx+1Ch],0 / jge -> null               extended only
//   2F5867  bt edi,0Dh / [rbx+98h] or [rbx+90h]                chain head
//   2F5882  mov eax,[rcx+1Ch] / and eax,edi / test eax,0FFFFDFFFh / jne found
//   2F588E  mov rcx,[rcx+68h] / test / jne loop                next
//
// GetStatListEx, 34B870..34B8AB: returns [unit+88h] (both paths).
// SetStatListExpireFrame, 2F7AB0..2F7AC9: frame > 0 -> or [rcx+1Ch],2;
//   cvtdq2ps; movss [rcx+24h],xmm0.
// STATLIST_GetUnitStat as D2RLoader 1.3.x leaves it: FF 25 thunk to
//   D2RCore!ReadWideUnitStat, four NOPs, then the untouched start of the old
//   body (the same witness Summon Resist Cap checks).

// Client overlays: dispatcher 0x150280..0x150404 (prologue through the
// collision call), the tick's cast hand-over 0x153551..0x15363C, the removal
// entry (hooked, four register saves) and STATES_CheckState's entry.

// D2RCore.dll (D2RLoader 1.3.1), SHA-256 2A868D01...5A2. The window above is
// the builder from its first instruction (+0x3E7650) through the removal of a
// mismatching list (+0x3E784E call, ends +0x3E7854). No base relocation falls
// inside it, so the loaded bytes equal the file's.

// The dispatcher's fifth and sixth arguments, from its rsp at the collision
// call: five pushes + sub rsp,130h = 158h, then the return address, the
// 20h home area and the two stack arguments.

// Walks back to the frame whose rip is `returnAddress` and returns that
// frame's rsp, rsi, rdi and r15. RtlVirtualUnwind restores each caller's
// nonvolatile registers from the callee's unwind data, so the values are the
// caller's own whatever this plugin's code did with the registers.

// damage_overflow_fix.cpp
//
// D2R 3.3 damage overflow fix. Port of the Eastern Sun Resurrected 2.4 set.
//
// D2R computes "value += value * percent / 100" (and "value * SrcDam / 128")
// through a compiler-emitted branch chain that does the multiply in 32 bits
// whenever both operands look small. Past INT32_MAX the product wraps negative,
// the negative result is folded back into the damage component, and the clamps
// further down the pipeline turn the hit into zero damage.
//
// Each site below is replaced by a jump into a stub generated at load time that
// does the same arithmetic in 64 bits and saturates to the int32 range. Because
// the game's own next instruction is usually "value = value + edx", the stubs
// saturate the SUM and hand back the delta that lands the game's add exactly on
// the saturated total. Saturating the delta alone is not enough: the 32-bit add
// of base plus a saturated delta wraps again.
//
// 2.4.0: missile_percent, the damage percent a missile applies to its own
// physical damage on impact (sub_140465420, 0x465A13) in 32 bits.
//
// 2.3.0: debug_log, a text file with every stage of every player hit and
// every player missile (stats, rolls, sites that ran, packets, resistance
// step, target), and the dmgoverflow log console command.
//
// 2.2.0: the melee kill check, the physical-to-element conversion add, and
// dmgoverflow hit, a per-hit report of every damage type at the resistance step.
//
// 2.1.0 (D2RLoader 1.3.1): the stat op group moved to D2RCore.dll, where the
// loader runs the stat engine (the game-image copy 2.0.2 patched is never
// called), and three groups were added: the resistance step, the hit's total
// life damage, and life/mana steal. See the TOML for every site.
//
// Every stub also records what it saw into a per-site trace slot, readable in
// game with the console command:
//
//   dmgoverflow            site list and state
//   dmgoverflow trace      last values observed at each site
//   dmgoverflow verify     ask D2RLoader's diagnostics service who owns each
//                          patched range, so a silent clobber by another
//                          plugin shows up instead of being guessed at
//
// Author: CelestialRayOne

// Every stub records these four values, in this order.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// D2RCore.dll sites
//
// D2RLoader 1.3.x moved the whole statlist engine into D2RCore.dll for its wide
// stats. The game image keeps its own op processor (sub_1402F8AD0) and the
// evaluate-and-update routine (0x2FA430), but nothing calls them any more: the
// statlist entries are jumps into D2RCore, and D2RCore stores entries as 16
// bytes where the game code walks 8. Up to 2.0.2 this plugin patched that dead
// copy. The live one is D2RCore 0x3DC2D0: (ctx, statList, key) -> value.
//
// D2RCore already does the arithmetic in 64-bit with an int32 cap, but only for
// stats 6..11 (life, max life, mana, max mana, stamina, max stamina). Every
// branch is gated on "cmp r15w, 6" with r15w = stat id - 6, and every other stat
// takes Blizzard's 32-bit code:
//   3DC4A0  add r14d, r11d          the stat summed over the child statlists
//   3DCA6D  imul eax, ecx / shl 6   ops 8 and 9
//   3DCA8F  imul eax, ecx           ops 1, 11 and 13 (also 3DCC32, 3DCDFA)
//   3DCAB5  imul eax, r14d          op 7 (also 3DCD2A, 3DCC52)
//   3DCCD8  imul eax, ebp / sar cl  ops 2 to 5
//   3DCD10  add r14d, eax           the op contribution added
// The six gates below send every stat down the life/mana branches. Those are
// the same formulas widened: identical results while nothing overflows, capped
// at the int32 limit when something would. The sum loop is replaced instead of
// gated: the life/mana loop caps after every child list, which would break a
// stat whose partial sum overflows and a later negative value cancels. The
// replacement keeps the loop and its binary search byte for byte, sums in
// 64-bit and caps once after the last child list.
//
// The whole function is checked against D2RLoader 1.3.1 before anything is
// written, every window again on its own, and the bytes are put back on unload.
// enhanced-damage-fix only reads 3DC710, 3DC834..3DC86D and 3DC90F..3DC97A of
// this function; none of the sites below touch those bytes.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct CoreSite {
	const char* id;
	const char* group;
	const char* doc;
	uint32_t    rva;           // D2RCore RVA of the first byte checked and written
	const char* expectedHex;   // bytes there before patching
	const char* patchHex;      // same-length replacement, nullptr for a stub site
	const char* stubHex;       // stub template for a stub site, trace slots zero
	uint32_t    traceAt[4];
	uint32_t    exitRva;       // D2RCore RVA the stub resumes at
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// stat write watch
//
// Hook at the entry of sub_1402F3030, the statlist stat setter. Every absolute
// value written to any stat on any unit passes through it, and the caller's
// return address is still at [rsp]. For stat ids 48..58 we record the value,
// the type of unit that owns the statlist, and who wrote it.
//
// D2RLoader 1.3.1 moved the setter into D2RCore (SetWideListStat): 0x2F3030 is
// now jmp [0x3E2A230] and four nops. The site takes those ten bytes and the
// stub rejoins at 0x2F7C00, the loader's other thunk through the same slot,
// so the write still reaches D2RCore with every register as the caller left
// it. The stub no longer replays a prologue: there is none left to replay.
//
// The unit type is the part that matters: the same stat id holds whole damage
// units on a player and 256ths on a missile, so a number that looks impossible
// on one is correct on the other.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// D2RCore patching. The loader only tracks and reverts patches in the game
// image, so these are checked, written, read back and put back by hand.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The whole of D2RCore 0x3DC2D0 must be the D2RLoader 1.3.1 code before any of
// its bytes are touched: the gates only make sense with the branches they send
// every stat to, and the sum stub replays the original loop.

// The trampoline blocks are left mapped on purpose. D2RLoader reverts the
// game image patches itself and the D2RCore windows are put back here;
// freeing pages a thread might still be executing inside is far more
// dangerous than leaking two small reservations.

// Dodge Avoid Evade Cap
//
// Caps the chance to dodge (passive_dodge, stat 338), avoid (passive_avoid,
// stat 339) and evade (passive_evade, stat 340) at a configurable percent,
// 75 by default. The server-side roll and the Advanced Stats panel are both
// capped, so the panel never shows a chance the roll will not honour.
//
// Port of the ESR D2R 2.4 memory patches (six roll hooks into a clamp cave
// at 3870F5, four display hooks into a stat-scoped clamp cave at 35C2F0) to
// D2R 3.3. Everything below was read out of the live D2RLoader.exe image and
// disassembled before being relied on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The getter
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   STATLIST_GetUnitStat @ 0x2F5020, int32 (unit, statId, layer).
//
//   D2RLoader 1.3.0 widened the stat store and forwards this entry to
//   D2RCore's ReadWideUnitStat. Its first two instructions (10 bytes) are
//   replaced by an import thunk; the rest of the old body is still in place,
//   unreachable:
//
//     2F5020  FF 25 xx xx xx xx   jmp   [rip+disp32]   ; D2RCore!ReadWideUnitStat
//     2F5026  90 90 90 90         nop x4
//     2F502A  48 89 74 24 20      mov   [rsp+20h], rsi ; old body from here on
//     ...
//     2F5034  41 0F B7 E8         movzx ebp, r8w       ; layer, 16 bits
//     2F5038  8B FA               mov   edi, edx       ; stat id, raw
//     2F503A  48 8B D9            mov   rbx, rcx       ; unit
//
//   A jump through a pointer changes no register and no stack slot, so
//   ReadWideUnitStat receives exactly what the caller passed. The game's own
//   callers, the five sites below included, still call 0x2F5020 with this
//   ABI, and the relays call the same entry. Capped and uncapped reads both
//   run the loader's implementation.
//
//   disp32 locates the loader's import slot for ReadWideUnitStat. That slot
//   belongs to the loader and moves between loader builds, and this plugin
//   never reads it, so the check does not pin it. It pins the thunk shape and
//   the untouched remainder of the old body, which identifies the function.
//
//   ReadWideUnitStat builds its lookup key from the full 32-bit r8d (key =
//   stat << 32 | r8d), where the vanilla getter only used r8w. Every call site
//   below passes xor r8d,r8d, and the relayed reads forward the layer as 32
//   bits, so no stale upper half can reach the loader.
//
//   2.4 passed a packed key (statId << 16) in edx. In 3.3 edx carries the raw
//   stat id, so the stat check below compares it directly.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Server: the avoidance roll, sub_14044EF70 (attacker, defender, bMissile)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Reached from the block roll sub_14044B930, which the melee resolver
//   sub_14044B720 runs after the to-hit roll sub_14044BA60 succeeds.
//   A defender that is not walking or running (player modes 2/3, monster
//   modes 2/15) gets a block check, then avoid for missiles or dodge for
//   melee. A moving defender rolls evade. Every roll is seed % 100 < chance
//   and returns 2 (avoid), 4 (dodge) or 8 (evade).
//
//   The 3.3 compiler merged the player and monster branches, so each stat is
//   read by exactly one call. 2.4 still had two calls per stat.
//
//     44F072  BA 53 01 00 00      mov   edx, 153h      ; passive_avoid
//     44F077  E8 A4 5F EA FF      call  2F5020         ; <- hook
//     44F07C  8B E8               mov   ebp, eax
//
//     44F1CE  BA 52 01 00 00      mov   edx, 152h      ; passive_dodge
//     44F1D3  E8 48 5E EA FF      call  2F5020         ; <- hook
//     44F1D8  8B E8               mov   ebp, eax
//
//     44F251  BA 54 01 00 00      mov   edx, 154h      ; passive_evade
//     44F256  E8 C5 5D EA FF      call  2F5020         ; <- hook
//     44F25B  44 8B F0            mov   r14d, eax
//
//   Branch targets around them are 44F072 (from 44F1C8), 44F1CE (from
//   44F06C), 44F24B (from 44F0FB and 44F104) and 44F24E (from 44EFAE). None
//   lands inside a replaced call.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Client: the Advanced Stats panel (D2Client/src/UI/Widgets/AdvancedStatsPanel.cpp)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Every panel row reads its stat through one of two helpers. The stat id
//   comes from the row definition, so these calls read EVERY stat on the
//   panel and the clamp has to stay gated on 338..340. The first 2.4 display
//   cave was not gated and capped IAS, FRW, FHR, FCR and the rest at 75.
//
//     sub_1414EB1B0  row value, returned as value >> ItemStatCost ValShift
//       14EB1D6  0F BF 16               movsx edx, word ptr [rsi]   ; row stat id
//       14EB1D9  45 33 C0               xor   r8d, r8d
//       14EB1DC  48 8B 8F 68 01 00 00   mov   rcx, [rdi+168h]       ; panel unit
//       14EB1E3  48 8B D8               mov   rbx, rax
//       14EB1E6  E8 35 9E E0 FE         call  2F5020                ; <- hook
//
//     sub_1414EB250  row text, value handed to the formatter 2D97E0
//       14EB276  48 8B 89 68 01 00 00   mov   rcx, [rcx+168h]       ; panel unit
//       14EB28B  45 33 C0               xor   r8d, r8d
//       14EB28E  41 0F BF 16            movsx edx, word ptr [r14]   ; row stat id
//       14EB292  E8 89 9D E0 FE         call  2F5020                ; <- hook
//       14EB297  8B D0                  mov   edx, eax
//
//   Their callers are 14EAD50, 14EAF40, 14EC800 and 14ED3A0. There is no
//   other call to STATLIST_GetUnitStat (2F5020) or STATLIST_UnitGetStatValue
//   (2F5C60) anywhere in 14EA000..14EE000.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Hook shape
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Each site keeps its five-byte call and only the rel32 changes, to a near
//   relay (jmp qword ptr [rip+0]) that lands in a C++ function with the
//   getter's own ABI. That function calls the real getter and clamps the
//   result. To the call site it is an ordinary callee: same arguments, same
//   return register, and only volatile registers change, exactly as the
//   original getter is allowed to change them.
//
//   If the plugin unloads, or a later site fails to patch, both relays are
//   first pointed straight at the vanilla getter. A call site that could not
//   be restored then still reaches native code, uncapped, instead of code in
//   an unloaded DLL.

// Each witness covers the call and the instructions that prove its
// arguments: the stat id immediate at the roll sites, the row id load and
// panel unit load at the panel sites.

// 14EB276  mov rcx,[rcx+168h] / mov rdi,rdx / mov [rsp+570h],r14 /
//          mov r14,r8 / xor r8d,r8d / movsx edx,word ptr [r14] /
//          call 2F5020 / mov edx,eax

struct Site {
    const char*         name;
    SiteKind            kind;
    std::uint64_t       witnessRva;
    const std::uint8_t* witness;
    std::uint32_t       witnessSize;
    std::uint64_t       callRva;
};

// In-image trampolines (D2RLoader 1.3.1). The loader now accepts a rel32
// call only when its target is inside D2R.exe, and the relay page is not.
// Each site therefore calls a 5-byte "jmp relay stub" written into int3
// padding, and the relay stub continues to the read hook exactly as before.
// A jmp changes no register and no stack slot, so the hook still sees the
// getter's ABI and returns straight to the call site.
//
//   0x44CE75  11 x int3 between the ret at 0x44CE74 and the next function at
//             0x44CE80. Gameplay trampoline at 0x44CE75, display at 0x44CE7A.

// The relay page is deliberately kept: a thread may be inside a jump
// through it right now, and any site that could not be restored still
// needs it to reach the vanilla getter.

// =============================================================================
// Dynamic EType (celestialrayone.dynamic-etype)
// =============================================================================
//
// A skills.txt calc column decides a skill's EType once per cast. Everything
// that reads EType during that cast sees the decided element: elemental
// damage and its mastery, physical to elemental conversion, missile damage,
// D2RCore's "+X to <element> Skills" level bonus, and the missiles.txt EType of
// every missile the cast creates that has elemental damage of its own.
//
// Everything below was read out of the live D2RLoader 1.3.1 image (D2R
// 3.3.93847, base 0x140000000) and D2RCore.dll. Every byte the plugin writes
// or relies on is checked at load; nothing is installed when one differs.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The column
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   skills.txt binder 0x302380 stores "EType" as type 13 (elemtypes.txt code
//   to row number) at record +0x284 (0x306624 / 0x30662F), one byte. So at
//   runtime EType is a number like SrcDam, and a calc can drive it. calc1 to
//   calc10 are dwords at record +0x190 to +0x1B4; a blank one is 0xFFFFFFFF.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Who reads EType
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Readers take the byte straight out of the shared skills.txt row, inline,
//   in dozens of places:
//     0x338340  elemental max getter, reads +0x284 at 0x338495 and hands it
//               to the mastery function 0x337F40 (the min getter 0x3387D0 too)
//     0x3BF9B0  missile damage builder, reads the linked skill's +0x284 at
//               0x3BFF5F and switches on it (1 fire, 2 ltng, 3 mag, 4/12 cold,
//               5 pois, 6 life, 7 mana, 8 stam, 9 stun, 11 burn)
//     0x5622C0  Charge, conversion element: cmp byte [r13+284h],0 at 0x5627EA,
//               movzx eax,byte [r13+284h] at 0x56281F (every melee skill
//               function that converts physical damage does the same)
//     D2RCore   the skill bonus level behind the ReadWideSkillLevel export
//               (D2RCore+0x832ED0 -> +0x3DDD80) reads +0x284 at +0x3DDF08 and
//               adds item_elemskill (stat 126) layered by it. It is the only
//               EType reader in D2RCore and nothing is cached.
//   Hooking every reader is not possible within reason, so the plugin binds
//   the element to the row itself, but only while the server runs code for
//   that cast, and puts the row's own value back the moment it returns.
//
//   missiles.txt has its own EType (record +0x10C, one byte). It is read:
//     0x3BF9B0  at creation, for a missile with no Skill and no MissileSkill
//               flag (3BFB5C): EMin/EMax and that row's ELen go under it.
//               D2RCore's AssignWideMissileDamage then copies every element
//               field to the missile's stats as it is, with no EType check.
//     0x3BB060  (dataContext, missile id) -> row+0x10C, on every hit, by the
//               functions that pick by the missile's own element: pSrvDmgFunc
//               1's conversion target (0x465160, also used by 5 and 12), the
//               per-element damage reader 0x465D60 (pSrvHitFunc 1, 9, 13, 31
//               and pSrvDmgFunc 5, 12, 15: the explosion reads only that
//               element's damage stats and length), pSrvDmgFunc 12 and 15 and
//               pSrvHitFunc 13 directly. All of them run inside the missile
//               hit resolution 0x4639A0; missile do functions that deal damage
//               call 0x4639A0 too. The standard hit (0x465420) reads every
//               element and needs none of this.
//   So a missile whose damage was built cold but whose row still says fire
//   would look for fire damage and fire length on hit and find neither: its
//   explosions deal nothing, conversion goes to fire, and cold never chills.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One element per cast
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Every cast has an id at unit+0x12C. The decision is made once and kept
//   under (game, unit, skill, cast id):
//     skill start    0x436480 (game, unit). Mode start of every player and
//                    monster skill, before the start function. Evaluates the
//                    calc with the level the row's own element gives, stores
//                    the decision, then runs the start with the row bound, so
//                    the level the game computes inside already carries the
//                    new element's "+X to <element> Skills".
//     skill perform  0x436B10 (game, unit). Every do event of the cast (each
//                    hit of a sequence too). Reuses the start's decision for
//                    the same cast id; the level it computes, the do-handler
//                    it calls and the aura timer it arms all see the element.
//   Code that runs on its own, without a start, decides on each run and keeps
//   the decision for the rest of that game frame, so a proc's start function
//   and its do-handler agree:
//     start dispatcher 0x43B3E0 (game, unit, skill, level, flag)
//     do-handler       0x43ACB0, see below (procs, aura and item aura pulses)
//     indexed do       0x435490 (game, unit, function, skill, level, cast id),
//                      skill functions that missile functions run
//   Missiles keep the decision under (game, missile cast id, skill row), so a
//   sub-missile created seconds later gets the element of the cast it belongs
//   to. A sub-missile takes its parent's cast id (missile creator 0x537A17)
//   and its parent's skill (missile data +0xC, every missile function copies
//   it). The row is the linked skills.txt row, or for a missile with its own
//   damage, the skill it was created for.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Sites
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Inline hooks (D2RLoader, function entries, nothing relative inside):
//     0x436480  48 89 5C 24 08 55 56 57 41 56 41 57 48 83 EC 50
//     0x436B10  48 89 5C 24 10 48 89 6C 24 18 56 48 83 EC 40
//     0x43B3E0  48 89 5C 24 10 48 89 6C 24 18 48 89 74 24 20 57
//
//   Do-handler 0x43ACB0 (game, unit, skill, level, consume, itemCast,
//   itemEffect). Its entry belongs to srcdam-calc-field and cast-on-cast
//   verifies its first 33 bytes, so the scope sits inside the body, where
//   rdi = game, rbx = unit, r13d = skill id and the level is at [rsp+0D8h]:
//     0x43ACFC  E8 8F CA C5 FF        call 97790, the record fetch. Becomes a
//               call to stub A, which makes that call, then opens the scope.
//     0x43AE1A  48 8B 9C 24 C8 00 00 00   mov rbx,[rsp+0C8h], the first
//               instruction of the one epilogue every exit reaches (early
//               exits fall through 0x43AE18, the normal one jumps from
//               0x43B2A7). Becomes a call to stub B plus 3 NOPs; stub B closes
//               the scope, keeps rax, replays the mov and returns to the NOPs.
//   rsp is the same at both sites, so the host rsp names the scope: stub B
//   only closes a scope opened by stub A in the same do-handler call.
//
//   Missile damage builder 0x3BF9B0 has exactly one caller:
//     0x537A6D  E8 3E 7F E8 FF  in the missile creator 0x5371A0, after the new
//               missile got its cast id. Becomes a call to a thunk that jumps
//               to HookedMissileDamage, which has the builder's own signature.
//   Indexed do 0x435490 starts with cmp r8d,0BEh / ja rel32, so its two
//   callers are redirected instead, the same way:
//     0x453466  E8 25 20 FE FF  in missile function 0x4533C0
//     0x459340  E8 4B C1 FD FF  in missile function 0x4592A0
//
//   Missile hit resolution 0x4639A0 (game, missile, target, forced). Its entry
//   belongs to crossbow-charges, so the scope sits inside the body, where
//   r12 = game and r14 = missile. The whole body (4639A0..4640E2) was
//   disassembled: one ret, no jump out, rsp only moves in prologue/epilogue.
//     0x4639FE  E8 3D 26 D0 FF        call 166040, the missiles.txt record
//               fetch, straight-line from the entry. Becomes a call to stub C,
//               which makes that call, then opens the scope on the row's
//               EType byte.
//     0x463AEB  48 8B 8D 10 01 00 00  mov rcx,[rbp+110h], the first
//               instruction of the one epilogue every exit reaches (463AE6
//               falls through, 463D73 and 4640DD jump). Becomes a call to stub
//               D plus 2 NOPs; stub D closes the scope, keeps rax, replays the
//               mov and returns to the NOPs before xor rcx,rsp.
//
//   The four stubs have UNWIND_INFO registered for them, so a stack walk
//   through any of them reaches the game frame. The thunks are a single jmp
//   and need none (an address with no entry is unwound as a leaf).
//
//   Not covered: 0x435520, the other table dispatcher, has no caller and no
//   reference in this build or in D2RCore.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Other plugins
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Untouched: srcdam-calc-field's hook on 0x43ACB0's entry and cast-on-cast's
//   33-byte witness there, cast-on-cast's 0x43AF06..0x43AF25 and 0x43B0EC,
//   crossbow-charges' 0x43B18D and its 0x5379F5..0x537A22 window,
//   damage-overflow-fix and srcdam-calc-field inside 0x3BF9B0,
//   prgstack-calc-field's 36-byte witness on 0x56C3E0 (the progressive release
//   is only called by skill functions, so it already runs inside a scope),
//   crossbow-charges' hook on 0x4639A0's entry (16 bytes), and the witnesses
//   Ruff's doll-explosion keeps on 0x4639A0..0x4639BE and 0x4640D8.
//
// Console: "dynamic-etype" for status and counters, "dynamic-etype log on|off"
// for the decision log. Settings: d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.dynamic-etype.toml
// =============================================================================

// 43ACEC: movzx ecx,byte [rcx+106h] / xor r15d,r15d / mov edx,r8d /
// mov r12d,r15d / call 97790 / mov rsi,rax / test rax,rax / jz 43AE18 /
// mov rcx,rbx.

// 537A53: mov [rsp+30h],al / mov r9,r15 / movzx eax,byte [rsp+58h] /
// mov r8,rbx / mov [rsp+28h],al / mov rdx,rsi / mov [rsp+20h],edi /
// call 3BF9B0 / mov r9d,edi.

// 4639B0: lea rbp,[rsp-120h] / sub rsp,220h. After these the hit resolution
// never moves rsp until its epilogue (the whole body 4639A0..4640E2 was
// disassembled: no push, no pop, no other rsp write), so its rsp names it.

// 4639D0: mov r14,rdx / mov [rsp+38h],r9d / mov r15,r8 / lea rdx,[source file] /
// mov r12,rcx / mov r8d,1F31h / mov rcx,r14 / call 349860 /
// movzx ecx,byte [r12+106h] / mov edx,eax / call 166040 / mov rdx,r14 /
// mov rcx,r12 / mov r13,rax. Straight-line code from the entry: every hit
// passes the fetch, with r12 = game and r14 = missile.

// 463AE6: mov eax,1 / mov rcx,[rbp+110h] / xor rcx,rsp / call cookie check /
// mov rbx,[rsp+278h] / add rsp,220h / pop r15..rbp / ret. The function's only
// ret; every exit reaches 463AEB (falling through 463AE6, or jmp from 463D73
// and 4640DD).

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         what;
};

// Evaluates the configured calc of the row in the given unit's context. The
// result is an elemtypes.txt row number; 0 or anything outside 1..12 keeps the
// row's own EType.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scopes
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   A scope binds one row's EType byte for as long as the game runs code for
//   one cast: a skills.txt row (+0x284) while the cast's own code runs, or a
//   missile's missiles.txt row (+0x10C) while that missile's damage is built
//   or while it hits. Scopes nest strictly (every opener's code returns before
//   the opener does) and each one keeps the value it found and puts it back,
//   so an inner scope on the same byte restores the outer one's element. The
//   stack is per thread; only server threads ever open one.

struct Scope {
    const void*    contextUnit;  // the unit the decision belongs to (a missile's owner)
    std::int32_t   skillId;      // the skills.txt row the decision belongs to
    std::int32_t   element;      // the decision; Keep = the byte's own value
    std::uint8_t*  etype;
    std::uint8_t   saved;
    bool           wrote;
    ScopeKind      kind;
    std::uintptr_t frame;        // host rsp for the stub-opened scopes, 0 otherwise
};

// The skills.txt row the damage builder 0x3BF9B0 reads EType from, or -1 when
// it reads the missile's own missiles.txt EType. Mirrors 3BFACE..3BFE46:
// Skill > 0 links the row; the MissileSkill flag prefers the skill the missile
// was created for, falling back to Skill.

// The skills.txt row whose decision a missile follows: its linked row when the
// builder takes the element from a skills.txt row, otherwise the skill it was
// created for (its element then comes from its own missiles.txt EType).

// In place of the creator's call to the damage builder 0x3BF9B0, same
// signature. The new missile already carries its cast id, skill and level.
//   Linked missile: the builder reads the skills.txt row's EType (3BFF5F),
//   so that row is bound, unless the cast's own code already bound it.
//   Missile with its own damage: the builder reads the missile's missiles.txt
//   EType (3BFB5C) and puts EMin/EMax and its ELen under that element, so that
//   byte is bound to the element of the cast the missile was created for.
//   A blank missiles.txt EType has no element to follow and stays blank.

// Stub C, after the hit resolution 0x4639A0 fetched the hitting missile's
// missiles.txt row. frame = the hit resolution's rsp. For the whole hit, the
// missile's own EType carries its cast's element: the hit and damage
// functions that read it (3BB060) then look for the damage, the length and
// the conversion target where the creation put them.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Stub region
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   One allocation within rel32 reach of every site, never freed (a thread may
//   be inside it when the plugin unloads):
//     +000  zero, so no RUNTIME_FUNCTION has UnwindData 0
//     +010  UNWIND_INFO, alloc 38h (stubs A and C)
//     +018  UNWIND_INFO, alloc 28h (stubs B and D)
//     +020  RUNTIME_FUNCTION table, 4 entries (to +050)
//     +060  thunk: jmp HookedMissileDamage     +080  thunk: jmp HookedIndexedDo
//     +100  stub A (69 bytes), slots +148 skills.txt record getter, +150 DoScopeOpen
//     +180  stub B (38 bytes), slot +1A8 DoScopeClose
//     +200  stub C (50 bytes), slots +248 missiles.txt record getter, +250 HitScopeOpen
//     +280  stub D (37 bytes), slot +2A8 HitScopeClose
//
//   All four stubs were assembled with keystone, decoded back with capstone
//   and emulated with unicorn: arguments, 16-byte alignment at every call,
//   the record and return value kept, rbx replayed from [host rsp+0C8h] (B),
//   rcx replayed from [rbp+110h] (D), every non-volatile register untouched,
//   and the null-record paths skipping the scope.

constexpr std::uint8_t StubB[]{
    0x48, 0x83, 0xEC, 0x28,                    // sub  rsp, 28h
    0x48, 0x89, 0x44, 0x24, 0x20,              // mov  [rsp+20h], rax     ; the do-handler's result
    0x48, 0x8D, 0x4C, 0x24, 0x30,              // lea  rcx, [rsp+30h]     ; the do-handler's rsp
    0xFF, 0x15, 0x14, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,        // call [rip+14h]          ; +28: DoScopeClose
    0x48, 0x8B, 0x44, 0x24, 0x20,              // mov  rax, [rsp+20h]
    0x48, 0x8B, 0x9C, 0x24, 0xF8, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,  // mov rbx, [rsp+0F8h]  ; replays mov rbx,[rsp+0C8h]
    0x48, 0x83, 0xC4, 0x28,                    // add  rsp, 28h
    0xC3,                                      // ret
};

constexpr std::uint8_t StubD[]{
    0x48, 0x83, 0xEC, 0x28,                    // sub  rsp, 28h
    0x48, 0x89, 0x44, 0x24, 0x20,              // mov  [rsp+20h], rax     ; the hit result
    0x48, 0x8D, 0x4C, 0x24, 0x30,              // lea  rcx, [rsp+30h]     ; the hit resolution's rsp
    0xFF, 0x15, 0x14, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,        // call [rip+14h]          ; +28: HitScopeClose
    0x48, 0x8B, 0x44, 0x24, 0x20,              // mov  rax, [rsp+20h]
    0x48, 0x8B, 0x8D, 0x10, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00,  // mov  rcx, [rbp+110h]    ; replays the patched instruction
    0x48, 0x83, 0xC4, 0x28,                    // add  rsp, 28h
    0xC3,                                      // ret
};

// Order matters: each epilogue (closer) goes in before its record fetch
// (opener), so no scope can ever be opened that nothing closes; removal runs
// the other way round.

// Soaring Strike (pSrvHitFunc 34) return-trip changes.
//
// See celestialrayone.soaring-strike.toml for the behaviour write-up. In short:
//
//   1. RE-HIT ON THE WAY BACK
//      Slot 34 gives the missile a fresh cast id when the return trip starts,
//      which resets next-hit delays, but it never touches the LastCollide slot
//      ([pMissile+0x10], type at +0x20, id at +0x24). That slot holds ONE pair,
//      the most recent target, so only the last monster struck outbound stays
//      blocked on the way home. This hook re-stamps it when the turn happens.
//
//   2. UNCAPPED RETURN PATH
//      sub_1403BA560 computes the return lifetime from min(distance, 50). The 50
//      is a literal `mov edi, 32h` at RVA 3BA6B1. The value is fed through
//      `shl edi, 10h`, so 0x7FFF is a hard ceiling: anything larger goes negative
//      and the missile expires on the spot.
//
// Built against D2R 3.3 (module 140000000.D2RLoader.exe,
// md5 baf085b077a4f9605bfddd977cfd9207) and PluginSDK v4. Re-checked against
// D2RLoader 1.3.1 and PluginSDK 0.3.0: every site is unchanged. 1.0.1 only
// writes its documented config file when it is missing.

// MISSILE_SetLastCollide(pMissile, pTarget). Writes the target type to [pMD+0x20]
// and the target id to [pMD+0x24]; with a null target it writes -1 into the id
// instead. Internally gated on the missiles.txt LastCollide bit (missile record
// byte 36), so it is a no-op for missiles that do not use that column.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// engine-stability
//
// Vanilla engine bug fixes, each behind its own switch.
//
// A fix corrects a vanilla engine bug that is not a crash. Fixes do change
// gameplay: they make the engine apply what its own data already says.
//
// Target: D2RLoader 1.3.1, which runs Diablo II: Resurrected 3.3 inside
// D2RLoader.exe and moves part of the engine into D2RCore.dll.
//
// Retired in 0.11.0, because D2RLoader 1.3.1 now does the same thing itself.
// Each was confirmed in the 1.3.1 image or D2RCore.dll before it was removed:
//   client_unit_lookup_tombstone      0009F270 answers null for unit id -1
//                                     before walking the bucket
//   lifesteal_while_dead              D2RCore's leech routine returns early for
//                                     a player in mode 0 (death) or 17 (dead)
//   event_handler_recursion           D2RCore's event dispatcher skips a
//                                     handler whose running bit is already set
//   skill_attack_rating_on_missiles   00537B23 gives a player missile created
//                                     without a ToHit its skill's SkillToHit
