// Max Life One
//
// Adds a configurable itemstatcost stat (default id 512). While a player or
// monster has that stat, its maximum life is 1 and nothing can raise or lower
// it. Damage still kills it.
//
// Everything below was read out of D2RLoader 1.3.0 and disassembled before
// being relied on: the D2RLoader.exe process dump (md5
// afdf8d05662885e48af48b8208892178) and D2RCore.dll (SHA-256
// ae1ea9b7f97af5b89a550281e6a6c6b6e9c74e73ac8759e6558b40e751428cd0).
// D2RCore addresses are RVAs into that D2RCore.dll.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where maximum life lives in 1.3.0
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   D2RLoader 1.3.0 moved the whole stat store into D2RCore. The game's stat
//   functions are 10-byte import thunks (jmp [rip+slot] / nop x4) into D2RCore
//   exports:
//
//     0x2F4D20  max life of a unit        -> ReadWideMaxLife
//     0x2F5020  STATLIST_GetUnitStat      -> ReadWideUnitStat
//     0x2F5C60  STATLIST_UnitGetStatValue -> ReadWideItemEventStat
//     0x2F5170  (?, unit, stat)           -> ReadWideUnitStatCallback
//     0x2F6CD0  (context, list, stat, layer) -> ReadWideEffectiveStat
//     0x2F5D90  (context, list, stat)     -> ReadWideEffectiveStatZero
//     0x2F7D10  STATLIST_SetUnitStat      -> SetWideUnitStat
//     0x46FDD0  server stat callback      -> OnWideServerStatChanged
//
//   The total of maxhp is computed inside D2RCore (evaluate 0x3E6540, commit
//   0x3E6EF0). No game code computes maximum life any more, so the rule sits
//   on the D2RCore side of those thunks.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the hooks sit on the D2RCore exports, not on the game thunks
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   dodge-cap, critical-strike-damage and smite-hit-cap verify the thunk at
//   0x2F5020 byte for byte when they load (FF 25 / nop x4 / old body). An
//   inline hook on that thunk rewrites its first five bytes, so whichever of
//   them loads after this plugin would refuse to install.
//
//   This plugin hooks the exports inside D2RCore.dll, resolved by name with
//   GetProcAddress. It uses no game RVA. Every caller of the thunks (the game,
//   D2RCore features that call the game entry points, other plugins) still
//   goes through the untouched thunks and reaches the hook.
//
//   D2RLoader 1.3.1's hook installer only accepts hook sites inside D2R.exe
//   ("patch range is outside D2R.exe"), so the plugin installs these hooks
//   itself. For each export it checks the first instructions byte for byte
//   against D2RCore.dll 1.3.1, copies them into a trampoline that ends with an
//   absolute jump back past them (rel32 calls and jumps and the RIP-relative
//   cookie load re-aimed at their original targets), and turns the export
//   entry into jmp rel32 to a relay that jumps to the hook. Relays and
//   trampolines share one page allocated within rel32 reach of D2RCore. The
//   entries are restored on unload and when a later hook fails to install.
//
//   Every export's first bytes relocate cleanly (checked per export against
//   D2RCore.dll 1.3.1, including that nothing branches back into them): sub rsp / call rel32 (ReadWideUnitStat, ReadWideEffectiveStat),
//   jmp rel32 (ReadWideItemEventStat, ReadWideMaxLife), sub rsp / mov / xor
//   (ReadWideUnitStatCallback, ReadWideEffectiveStatZero), sub rsp / mov
//   rax,[rip+cookie] (OnWideServerStatChanged), push x4 (SetWideUnitStat,
//   AddWideUnitStat). None of them branches back into its first five bytes.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ABIs (x64, from the export bodies)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   ReadWideMaxLife          (unit) -> int32
//   ReadWideUnitStat         (unit, stat, layer) -> int32
//   ReadWideItemEventStat    (unit, stat, layer) -> int32   (same body)
//   ReadWideUnitStatCallback (unused, unit, stat) -> int32  (layer 0)
//   ReadWideEffectiveStat    (context byte, list, stat, layer) -> int32
//   ReadWideEffectiveStatZero(context byte, list, stat) -> int32 (layer 0)
//     reads the list's totals when the list is extended ([list+1Ch] < 0),
//     its own base values otherwise.
//   SetWideUnitStat          (unit, stat, value, layer) -> bool
//   AddWideUnitStat          (unit, stat, delta, layer) -> void
//   OnWideServerStatChanged  (game, owner, unit, key, old, new) -> void
//     key = stat << 32 | layer.
//
//   Fields the rule reads, each read the same way by D2RCore's own
//   ReadWideUnitStat (0x3E2E40):
//     unit+00h   dword  unit type (0 player, 1 monster)
//     unit+88h   qword  stat list
//     list+1Ch   dword  flags, sign bit = extended list
//     list+A0h   qword  owner unit of an extended list
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What the engine does when maximum life changes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   When the total of a stat whose itemstatcost row has fCallback changes,
//   D2RCore calls the unit's stat callback (0x3E5E00 / 0x3E6EF0:
//   [list+2AF0h] = callback, [list+2AF8h] = game, [list+0A0h] = owner,
//   test byte [isc+5],4 = fCallback). The callback installed on server units
//   is the game's 0x46FDD0 (loaded at unit creation by 0x4252C0 and
//   0x543120), the thunk to OnWideServerStatChanged. Its branch for maxhp,
//   maxmana and maxstamina (0x3D6CB9):
//
//     if (old > 0 && old != new && current > 0)
//         current = clamp(current * new / max(old, 256), 1, new)
//     then, for a monster's life, damage regeneration is rebuilt from new.
//
//   maxhp has fCallback in vanilla, hitpoints does not. The client callback
//   (OnWideClientStatChanged, 0x3D73E0) has no life logic: the client's life
//   value comes from the server.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The rule
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   "Has the stat": total of the configured stat, layer 0, is non-zero, on a
//   player or monster. Life is stored in 256ths, so 1 life = 256, the same
//   value D2RCore itself writes as the life floor after a life cost (0x3E7B32:
//   mov r9d,100h for stat 6).
//
//   Reads. ReadWideMaxLife, and stat 7 layer 0 through ReadWideUnitStat,
//   ReadWideItemEventStat, ReadWideUnitStatCallback, ReadWideEffectiveStat and
//   ReadWideEffectiveStatZero (the last two only on the unit's own extended
//   list) answer 256. Base reads (ReadWideUnitBaseStat, ReadWideListStat) are
//   left alone: level-up builds the new base maxhp from them, and overriding
//   them would destroy the character's real base life.
//
//   Callback. The configured stat appearing is forwarded to the engine as
//   maxhp going from its real total to 256, and disappearing as 256 back to
//   the real total, so the engine's own rescale moves current life. A real
//   maxhp change while the stat is present is forwarded as 256 -> 256, which
//   the engine treats as no change. The configured stat's row needs fCallback
//   or the engine never reports it; the plugin checks every itemstatcost bank
//   after each table load and stays off while a bank has the row without it.
//
//   Writes. SetWideUnitStat / AddWideUnitStat on stat 6 layer 0 are held at
//   256 while the stat is present. Monster spawn and unique-modifier code
//   write life directly, not through a maximum life read, possibly after the
//   stat is already on the unit. D2RCore's own internal life writes were all
//   checked (0x3E3E60 call sites): the maxhp rescale above, Crushing Blow
//   (0x3D91AD, life minus damage, floored at 0) and blood mana running out
//   (0x3E7B32, life set to exactly 256) are the only ones, and none of them
//   can go above 1 life.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Client display
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The client reads maximum life through the same hooked exports, from its
//   own copy of the unit:
//
//     0x14F3959  character screen life line  j_ReadWideMaxLife -> "%d / %d"
//     0x266A8D   HUD life check              j_ReadWideMaxLife
//
//   So the client shows 1 only when its copy of the unit carries the stat.
//   The server copy gets it wherever the stat comes from; the client copy
//   gets it only through the engine's own sync, which each itemstatcost
//   column gates:
//
//     states (auras, curses, skill states): the 0xA8 state stat packet
//       (D2RCore 0x3EFD51) skips every stat whose Send Bits is 0.
//     stats set on a player: only rows with Saved (flags bit 11) enter the
//       player's changed-stat list (D2RCore 0x3E3A64), which is what
//       VisitWideChangedStats (0x7AA370) hands the game for the player's own
//       client.
//     items: the item writer skips every stat whose Save Bits is 0
//       (0x37F158: cmp byte [row+15h],0). In this image the same writer
//       also clamps the stat id to 1FFh in 9 bits (0x37F186 .. 0x37F1A1).
//
//   The plugin prints those three columns after each table load, and logs
//   once per player when the server copy has had the stat for 3 seconds but
//   the client copy still does not.

// The rule runs only while every hook is installed and the itemstatcost
// check passed. Until then every hook forwards untouched. Either can happen
// first, so both paths arm.

// One instruction of an export's entry. dispOffset is 0 for an instruction
// copied as is, else the offset of its rel32 or RIP disp32, which the
// trampoline re-aims at the same absolute target.

struct HookSpec {
    const char*                             exportName;
    void*                                   target;
    void**                                  original;
    std::uint8_t                            patchSize;
    std::array<std::uint8_t, MaxPatchSize>  expected;      // D2RCore.dll 1.3.1
    std::array<Instruction, 2>              instructions;  // together patchSize bytes
    bool                                    installed;
    std::uint8_t*                           entry;
    std::array<std::uint8_t, MaxPatchSize>  written;
};

// chain-limit - caps the number of simultaneously active chain missiles.
//
// WHAT IT DOES
//   Intercepts missiles.txt pSrvDoFunc 45 (the per-frame step of "miasmachains")
//   by replacing its entry in the server do-func table. On every tick it works out
//   how many newer chains the same caster owns; once that reaches the limit, the
//   oldest chain is destroyed. Net effect: at most N chains alive per caster, and
//   casting past the cap drops the oldest instead of failing the cast.
//
//   The limit N comes from skills.txt calc4 on the skill that fired the missile,
//   evaluated per cast against the caster at the missile's skill level, exactly
//   like any other skill formula. calc4 blank or <= 0 means unlimited, so this
//   plugin is inert for every other missile that happens to use pSrvDoFunc 45.
//
// WHY A TABLE SWAP AND NOT A CODE HOOK
//   sub_140457B10 has no direct callers in the image. Its only inbound reference
//   is the table slot, so replacing the slot intercepts 100% of calls without
//   modifying a single instruction. Fully reversible on unload.
//
// VERIFIED AGAINST D2R 3.3 (image base 0x140000000)
//   2390E80  pSrvDoFunc table, entry N at base + 8N, count at 2391400 (= 53)
//   2390FE8  slot 45, must contain 457B10
//   457B10   sub_140457B10, psrvdofunc 45, called (pGame, pMissile); return 2 destroys
//   490300   SUNIT_GetOwner(pGame, pMissile)
//   3BC450   MISSILE_GetSkill(pMissile)
//   3BB470   MISSILE_GetLevel(pMissile)
//   97790    SKILLS_GetSkillsTxtRecord(gameVersion, skillId)   stride 748
//   3B5160   SKILLS_EvaluateFormula(gameVersion, pUnit, poolOffset, skillId, level)
//   +412     skills.txt calc4 in the skill record (calc1 400, calc2 404, calc3 408)
//   +262     gameVersion byte on pGame
//   +368     dwGameFrame on pGame

// Misc Extra Stats  (celestialrayone.misc-extra-stats)
//
// Adds stat4/calc4, stat5/calc5 and stat6/calc6 to misc.txt, next to the stock
// stat1..3 / calc1..3, so a usable item's pSpell can apply up to six stats.
// Nothing in the save format changes.
//
// Target: D2RLoader 1.3.1 running D2R 3.3. Every RVA and byte below was read
// from the dumped D2RLoader.exe image (base 0x140000000) and decoded before
// being relied on. On any build whose bytes differ, the plugin installs
// nothing, logs why, and stays loaded so its console command can explain.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Why a plugin
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The item record (448 bytes, shared by weapons, armor and misc) is fully
//   packed: stat1..3 are words at +0x9E/+0xA0/+0xA2, calc1..3 dwords at
//   +0xA4/+0xA8/+0xAC, len at +0xB0. The item loader 0x315FD0 hands a fixed
//   column list to the txt parser, which skips any other column, and D2RLoader
//   has no way to add columns to a stock table. The pSpell handlers hardcode a
//   loop of three.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. The loops that read the pairs (all verified byte for byte)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   pSpell 3  0x57ECB0  index rdi, record rbp, record slot [rsp+68h]
//   pSpell 4  0x57F280  index rax ([rsp+60h]), record rdi, slot [rsp+58h]
//   pSpell 5  0x57F790  index r12, record rsi, slot [rsp+50h]
//   pSpell 9  0x57FD80  rbx walks the stat words, r12 = end, r13 = record,
//                       calc index = (r15 + rbx) >> 1 with r15 = -0x9E - r13
//   can-use   0x580A80  rbx walks the stat words, rsi = current entry,
//                       rdi = record (shared pre-use check of pSpell 3/4/5)
//   pSpell 6 (0x5807B0) runs pSpell 9's code. pSpell 8, 10, 11, 12 and 15 do
//   not loop over the pairs. Inside each loop the record is read only for the
//   stat word [rec + i*2 + 0x9E] and the calc [rec + i*4 + 0xA4].
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. How six slots work
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Each row with extra stats gets a 24-byte extension block E:
//     E+0, E+2, E+4    stat4..6 (word, -1 = empty)
//     E+12, E+16, E+20 calc4..6 (dword formula id, -1 = empty)
//   and a virtual record base B = E - 164. For loop index i = 3..5,
//   B + i*2 + 0x9E = E + (i-3)*2 and B + i*4 + 0xA4 = E + 12 + (i-3)*4, so the
//   game's own loop body reads stat4..6 / calc4..6 at index 3..5.
//
//   Each loop's advance-and-test is replaced by a jmp to a relay on a page
//   this plugin allocates within rel32 reach. The relay replays the stolen
//   instructions; when the stock loop would end at 3 it asks LookupVirtualBase
//   for the row, swaps the record register and its stack slot to B and keeps
//   counting 3, 4, 5. The index is never reset, so code the game runs only on
//   the first slot (the timed-state packet in pSpell 3/4) still runs once.
//   The walks (pSpell 9, can-use) are re-seeded onto E the same way.
//
//   The relay was assembled with keystone, decoded back with capstone, and run
//   under unicorn against a callee that clobbers every volatile register and
//   xmm0..5: every path, register, stack slot and the 16-byte call alignment
//   were checked.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. calc4..6
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Compiled with the game's own compiler, FORMULA_Compile 0xA24290, with the
//   exact resolvers the item compiler 0x3B47C0 passes (0x3B58A0, 0x3B57A0,
//   0x3B54E0), into buffers this plugin owns: the game's formula pool is never
//   touched. The record carries id 0x7E000000 | n. An inline hook on the item
//   formula evaluator 0x3B4880 runs ids with that tag through the game's
//   interpreter 0xA234B0 with exactly the context 0x3B4880 builds:
//     +00 vtable 0x1D0AEC8, +08..+38 the unit (item at +20), +40 dword 0,
//     +44 byte 1, +48 the item; token callback 0x79100, functions 0x1D09C50,
//     count 9.
//   Every other id goes to the stock evaluator untouched.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Reading the columns
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   On D2RLoader's tables-loaded event (game thread) the plugin reads the same
//   misc.txt the game loaded, from the mod folder: data\global\excel\ for the
//   RotW bank, data\global\excel\base\ for Classic and LoD. The bank of each
//   loaded Items table is found by matching the data-table service's Items
//   rows pointer. Rows are matched by item code against the game's compiled
//   records (code dword at +0x80), stat names are resolved through the game's
//   own itemstatcost registry (data tables +0x1270, lookup 0xA121F0).

struct LoopSite {
    std::uint64_t rva;
    std::uint64_t head;
    std::uint64_t done;
    std::size_t   relayEntry;
    std::size_t   headSlot;
    std::size_t   doneSlot;
    const char*   name;
};

using InterpretFn      = std::int32_t (*)(std::uint8_t bank, const std::uint8_t* code, std::int32_t length,
                                          const void* tokens, const void* functions, std::int32_t functionCount,
                                          void* context) noexcept;

// Monster affix rework (monumod.txt unique monster mods).
//
// See celestialrayone.monster-affixes.toml for the behaviour write-up.
//
// Since 1.4.0 the plugin no longer writes the affix pointer tables. Every
// change is made in code: inline hooks on the affix dispatchers and spawn
// routines, and one-byte `ret` patches on the vanilla routines it removes.
//
//   1. SKILLS CAST WHEN STRUCK (Fire / Cold / Lightning Enchanted, Teleportation,
//      Stone Skin). SUNITDMG_FinalizeDamage 0x44A9B0 calls the "struck" affix
//      dispatcher 0x498EE0 after the get-hit mode change (0x44AD08) and on hits
//      that do not flinch or are soft hits (0x44AD39). It is hooked: the vanilla
//      dispatch runs first, then the plugin casts the configured skills for the
//      struck monster's affixes, through the native item-skill casters the game
//      uses for "chance to cast when struck" (EventFunc21 0x5837F0). Lightning's
//      vanilla bolt routine 0x4A0020 is patched to return at once when a
//      lightning skill is set.
//
//   2. FIRE / COLD ENCHANTED DEATH EFFECTS: the corpse explosion 0x49F650 and the
//      cold nova 0x49F5B0 are only reachable through their death event; both
//      start with `ret` now.
//
//   3. SPECTRAL HIT: the random-element routine 0x4A11E0 and its missile copy
//      0x4A0F80 start with `ret`. The mode-change dispatcher 0x498F80 (called by
//      D2GAME_ModeChange 0x4475C0 after the new mode is set) is hooked; on A1,
//      A2, SC and S1-S4 the plugin casts one of up to four skills, each with its
//      own localdelay lockout. A skill that is locked is not picked.
//
//   4. CURSED: the "landed a hit" dispatcher 0x4990C0 (called by
//      SUNITDMG_ExecuteEvents 0x44CE80) is replaced by the same loop, except that
//      Cursed is handled by the plugin: a random skill from the pool, cast at the
//      unit that was hit, minions included. Monsters with rank flag 800h still go
//      to whatever routine the affix table holds for Cursed (D2RLoader's own).
//
//   5. SPAWN BONUSES: Extra Strong 0x496180 is replaced, Extra Fast 0x496300 is
//      wrapped (vanilla walk speed, then attack and cast rate), Mana Burn's spawn
//      routine 0x496CE0 starts with `ret` (the drain happens per hit in
//      FinalizeDamage instead, at most once per cooldown for each player).
//
//   6. LOCKOUT: after a cast the affix is locked for the cast skill's localdelay,
//      a calc at skill record +0x238 evaluated through 0x3B5160, or for the
//      configured frame count (Teleportation). Item-style casts never apply
//      localdelay on their own (the do handler 0x43ACB0 only applies it when
//      spending resources).
//
//   7. RESISTANCE: every affix resist bonus lives in one routine, 0x38E430 (spawn
//      entry of affixes 8, 27 and 28, tail-called by the spawn entries of 9, 17,
//      18, 23 and 25). It is replaced: only Magic Resistant does anything, with
//      the new rules. Ghostly's own physical resist (0x4957F2) is untouched.
//
//   8. MAGIC RESISTANT ON MINIONS: SpawnMinions 0x49EAD0 copies the owner's
//      xfer affixes into each minion and sets rank flag 10h through 0x38E710 at
//      0x49ED68, but never runs their spawn entries. The plugin hooks 0x38E710
//      and, for that one call site (return address 0x49ED6D), runs Magic
//      Resistant on the new minion. A minion that went inactive is rebuilt by
//      0x499210, which does run the spawn entries; the resist routine accepts
//      minions there too.
//
//   9. AURA ENCHANTED: with aura_enchanted_selection = "3.1" in d2rloader.toml,
//      D2RCore!ApplyAuraEnchanted runs the game's own routine 0x495CD0 (unit,
//      mod, flag). Once any aura key is in the TOML that routine is replaced
//      by the same steps, with the TOML's skills:
//        - monstats row 704 (Uber Mephisto): skill 123 at level 20 and nothing
//          else (0x495D3E);
//        - superuniques row 37 (Lord De Seis): record 5 of the main list
//          (0x495DCF);
//        - every other monster rolls the main list on its name seed (0x38E370):
//          state (seed, 666), one step seed * 6AC690C5h + 666, the low 32 bits
//          modulo the total weight of the records at or under its level. A
//          monster without a name seed (0, prime evils for one) would get the
//          same record every time, so it takes the same step on D2RCore's
//          per-monster value instead (game+12Ch xor unit id, see 9b);
//        - the skill goes in at (mlvl + offset) * multiplier / divisor, kept
//          in 1..99 (0x433910, mode 1) and becomes the right-hand skill
//          (0x438A70), which starts the aura;
//        - a herald (rank flag 200h) rolls the herald list the same way and
//          gets that skill as stat 151 with the skill as layer, in its stat
//          list flagged 10000h (0x2F5810, else 0x2F7300 and 0x2F2CB0; the write
//          is 0x2F7C00).
//      The lists are 24-byte records (min level, weight, level offset,
//      multiplier, divisor, skill): main at 0x1D1DE70 (7), herald at 0x1D1DF20
//      (4). A list the TOML does not set is read from the game's table at roll
//      time, so a static patch on it still counts. The lists in use are
//      published as the "aura-enchanted" service for Monster Display.
//      The helpers the replacement calls are identified by the calls to them
//      inside 0x495CD0 (each call's target is checked), never by their first
//      bytes: other plugins hook some of them (curse-cap hooks 0x2F5810), and a
//      hooked helper is still the one the game itself calls.
//
//  9b. HERALD ENCOUNTER AURAS: D2RCore!ApplyAuraEnchanted (D2RCore.dll 1.3.1,
//      SHA-256 2a868d01...) first asks D2RLoader's encounter rules about the
//      monster (+0x820046 calls +0x40C790, rule record at rbp-30h). A monster
//      with a rule (record +14h set: terror zone heralds and the bosses given
//      herald modifiers) never reaches 0x495CD0: D2RCore rolls 0 to 2 auras
//      itself from its own list (98, 368, 108, 365, 123, 122, 369, 113, 115,
//      428 at +0x62C6F8, divisors at +0x62C6D0), gives the first as skill and
//      right-hand aura, the second as stat 151, and logs "Encounter auras".
//      Only its pick (+0x820056..+0x82032F) is replaced: a 15-byte absolute
//      jump at +0x820056 to a stub that calls EncounterPick(rule, unit, pairs)
//      and resumes at +0x820330 with r13 = unit, where D2RCore's own code gives
//      both auras and logs them. EncounterPick keeps D2RCore's rules: how many
//      (rule +28h min, +2Ch max, never more than the slots allowed in +24h,
//      slot 40h only from monster level 20), the level cap (+30h, 0 = 99) and
//      the random value ((game+12Ch xor unit id) * 6AC690C5h + 29Ah, one step
//      per aura). The first aura comes from the main list, the second from the
//      herald list. Summon Enhancements' detour on the export's first 19
//      bytes is a different place and is not touched.
//
//  10. HIGH LEVEL MONSTERS: past high_level_start, every monster level (stat
//      12) raises Extra Strong's two bonuses and Magic Resistant's +50 by a
//      percent of their own value and shortens the Mana Burn cooldown by a
//      percent. Extra Strong and Magic Resistant read the level at spawn,
//      Mana Burn reads the burning monster's level at the hit.
//
//  11. EXTRA FAST FRAMES: at spawn an Extra Fast monster past
//      high_level_start gets fast_frames_percent_per_level percent per level
//      (at most 100) in the itemstatcost.txt stat fast_frames_stat, which is
//      sent to clients (Send Other). UNITS_UpdateAnimRateAndVelocity 0x350B40
//      ends every path by handing the unit to 0x980E0 (the call at 0x350C87,
//      after the 0x350C73 call frenzy-fpa redirects). 0x980E0 is hooked and
//      acts only on the call that returns to 0x350C8C. For a monster with the
//      stat in GH, A1, A2, BL, SC, S1-S4 or SQ the action gets that percent
//      fewer frames, never fewer than fast_frames_minimum: the rate word at
//      unit+68h (and the sequence rate at +54h when it holds the same value)
//      becomes the slowest rate that reaches that many frames. Frames are
//      ceil(256 * frames per direction / rate) - 1, frames per direction from
//      the animation record (unit+70h, +8). SQ uses the sequence model
//      (frenzy-fpa: length unit+48h, ceil(length / rate) frames) and keeps
//      every hit of the sequence. 0x350B40 is shared code, so server units
//      (timing, hits) and client units (animation) follow the same rule from
//      the same stat; that is why the plugin is Shared.
//
// Built against D2R 3.3 in the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image (base 0x140000000),
// PluginSDK 0.3.0. Every hook site and patch site is checked byte for byte before
// anything is written; nothing is installed for a feature whose check fails.

// The three affix dispatchers the plugin hooks, all (game, unit) and all with
// the same prologue: mov [rsp+20h],rbp / push rsi / push rdi / push r14 /
// sub rsp 20h, thirteen bytes.
//   0x498F80 every mode change (slot 1), from D2GAME_ModeChange
//   0x4990C0 the monster landed a hit (slot 3), from ExecuteEvents
//   0x498EE0 the monster was struck (slot 4), from FinalizeDamage

// Spawn routines. Extra Strong: push rbp / push rsi / sub rsp 38h /
// mov ebp,r8d / mov rsi,rcx, thirteen bytes. Extra Fast: push rdi /
// sub rsp 20h / mov rdi,rcx, nine bytes.

struct ReturnPatch {
	std::uint64_t       rva;
	const std::uint8_t* expected;
	const std::uint8_t* replacement;
	std::uint32_t       size;
	const char*         name;
};

// The end of UNITS_UpdateAnimRateAndVelocity 0x350B40 (unit in rsi), past the
// 0x350C73 call frenzy-fpa redirects: lea rdx,[rbp+18h] / mov [rbp+18h],rsi /
// lea rcx,[rax+168h] / call 0x980E0. 0x980E0 gets the updated unit by address
// in rdx. Its entry, push r12 / push r15 / sub rsp,0A8h: eleven bytes, no
// relative operands. Only the call returning to 0x350C8C is acted on.

// The game's Aura Enchanted routine (unit, mod, flag), reached from
// D2RCore!ApplyAuraEnchanted at aura_enchanted_selection "3.1". push rbp /
// push rbx / push rdi / mov rbp,rsp / sub rsp,70h / mov ebx,r8d / mov rdi,rcx:
// seventeen bytes, no relative operands.

// D2RCore!ApplyAuraEnchanted (D2RCore.dll 1.3.1), offsets from its export
// (+0x81FFC0 in the DLL). Checked in memory, not through the loader.
// +7Ch..+ACh: the encounter rule lookup and the first instructions of the pick.
//   lea rcx,[rbp-30h] / mov rdx,rax / mov r8,rsi / call +0x40C790 / nop /
//   cmp byte [rbp-1Ch],0 / je (no rule) / mov r12d,[rbp-0Ch] (allowed slots) /
//   mov r14d,[rbp-8] (min) / mov r15d,[rbp-4] (max) / mov ebx,[rbp] (level cap)
//   / mov r13,[rbp+0B0h] (unit)

// +96h: the first byte of the pick. Its 15 bytes (mov r12d / mov r14d /
// mov r15d / mov ebx, above) become jmp qword [rip] + the stub + nop. No branch
// in the function lands inside them.

// +370h..+4C2h: gives the two auras from [rbp+0A0h..0ACh] (skill, level, skill,
// level; -1 = none) with r13 = unit: the first through set skill and right-hand
// skill, the second as stat 151 in the stat list flagged 10000h; then logs
// "Encounter auras" and jumps to the epilogue. Nothing else is live here.

// The stub (keystone-assembled, capstone-checked):
//   lea rcx,[rbp-30h] / mov rdx,[rbp+0B0h] / lea r8,[rbp+0A0h] /
//   mov rax,EncounterPick / call rax / mov r13,[rbp+0B0h] / jmp qword [rip]
//   followed by the address of +370h. rsp is 16-byte aligned at +96h (eight
//   pushes, sub rsp,148h) and [rsp..rsp+20h) is the frame's own home area.

// Casts skillId for the monster the way EventFunc21 casts a "when struck" item
// skill: flag 10000000h on, unit target when there is one, path point when
// there is not, flag off. The lockout starts with the cast and lasts the
// skill's localdelay (evaluated for this monster at the cast level) unless a
// fixed frame count is given. lockoutSub is WholeAffix, or the skill id for a
// per-skill lockout.

// Cursed, when the monster lands a hit. Monsters with rank flag 800h go to the
// routine the affix table holds (D2RLoader's own Amplify Damage routine).
// Everyone else, minions included, follows that routine's rules (chance from
// monumod.txt constants row 34, level = monster level / 5 + 1) but casts a
// random skill from the pool at the unit that was hit. Units with rank flag
// 400h are skipped, as vanilla skips them.

// SQ: the sequence model, only for a rate the attack or cast path stored
// (the dword at +54h equals the word at +68h) and a sequence that has just
// started (position + countdown == length).

// Replaces the affix resist routine. Only Magic Resistant does anything;
// Stone Skin no longer doubles defense. Vanilla skipped units with rank flag
// 10h (minion) or 400h. 400h stays skipped; minions get Magic Resistant too
// once the minion spawn hook is in place (otherwise they stay excluded, so
// minions are never treated two different ways). A missing MonsterData
// passes, as it does in the vanilla routine.

// Replaces D2RCore's encounter pick (+0x820056..+0x82032F, see 9b). pairs is
// [rbp+0A0h]: skill and level of the first aura, then of the second, -1 for
// none. D2RCore gives them itself from +0x820330.

struct Witness {
	std::uint64_t       rva;
	const std::uint8_t* bytes;
	std::uint32_t       size;
	const char*         name;
};

struct AuraWitness {
	std::uint64_t       rva;
	const std::uint8_t* bytes;
	std::uint32_t       size;
	const char*         name;
};

// The helpers are the functions the routine itself calls: each call above
// (already byte-checked) must reach the expected one. Their own first bytes
// are not checked, other plugins hook some of them (curse-cap: 0x2F5810).

// Monster Display
//
// Replaces what the stock monster health panel shows with, top to bottom:
//   - the monster name (stock)
//   - a framed health bar, twice as wide for prime evils, with exact life and
//     a smaller percentage after max life
//   - "Level X <type>" (the stock type / description line with the level in
//     front of it)
//   - the unique affixes, with Aura Enchanted on its own row showing the aura
//     name and its final level (both auras for Heralds)
//   - the resistances the hovering player's hits actually meet, including the
//     Summon Resist Cap for allied monsters
//   - the immunity line, built the way the game builds it but from the same
//     values as the resistance row, so the Summon Resist Cap is respected
//
// 1.0.18: the nameplate's Name and NameBold move with the shrunk bar's top
//   edge, so they keep their layout distance above the bar instead of
//   floating half the stock bar height higher.
//
// 1.0.17: the controller nameplate's frame now shrinks with its bar. The frame
//   art is drawn at its own size times the widget's scale, so a smaller
//   rectangle left it at the stock height; the frame's scale now follows the
//   bar's height and its rectangle is divided by the same ratio, so its caps
//   still sit on the bar's edges. The shrunk bar keeps the stock bar's bottom
//   edge instead of its centre (a quarter of the stock height lower at 50%).
//   A NameBold text box in TargetAttached gets the name's text and colour on
//   every update, which draws the name bold. width_percent default 85.
//
// 1.0.16: the controller nameplate (the TargetAttached container, drawn over
//   the hovered monster's head) gets its bar and frames shrunk around their
//   centre to a configured share of their stock size: [nameplate]
//   width_percent of the width the game gives the bar (the width of the
//   name) and height_percent of the bar's layout height, 75 and 50 by
//   default. Its content (name, fill, frames) stays stock.
//
// 1.0.15: a bound demon is shown as an ally. It keeps the class id of the
//   hostile monster it was bound from, whose monstats.txt row has Align 0, so
//   the ally test treated it as an enemy and the effective view subtracted the
//   hovering player's sunder and -enemy resistance from its resistances (17%
//   summon physical resistance showed as 2% for an owner with -15% enemy
//   physical resistance). The ally test now reads Align from the row Bound
//   Demon Scaling gives a bound demon through its "stats-row" service, the
//   same row Summon Resist Cap decides its cap by. Until a bound demon's
//   server data arrives, its client copy's bind_demon state stands in for
//   that answer, so its uncapped stock immunity line is not shown meanwhile.
//
// 1.0.14: a monster's panel data is read on the game thread and kept for
//   RefreshMs (120 ms), so right after Bind Demon the panel went on showing
//   the monster's numbers from before the bind for up to that long. Bound
//   Demon Scaling (celestialrayone.bound-demon-scaling 1.0.3 and later)
//   publishes the "demon-bound" event on the game thread as soon as a demon
//   is bound; a demon the panel holds data for is read again right there, so
//   its new life shows in the same frame.
//
// Everything below was read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image
// (D2R 3.3.93847). Every entry, call site and layout used here is checked byte
// for byte at load; nothing is installed when a check fails.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. How the stock panel works
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   0CEA50 (unit, position, attached) builds the panel data for the hovered
//     monster every frame (single caller 0C0C8D): name, colour, health as a
//     percentage, the "type / description" line, the unique-affix line and
//     the immunity line, and posts it as message MonsterHealth:SetInfo.
//     The type is the string key "UndeadDescriptX" or "DemonID" (0CED50
//     window: 3AF190 is-demon, 3AF2C0 is-undead); the description is the
//     monstats.txt DescStr string.
//   1F84E0 (panel, message, container, attached) applies it to one container:
//     message+88h is the message hash, message+110h the 64-byte payload:
//       +00 name   +08 colour (16 bytes)   +18 health percent (int)
//       +20 unique-affix line, or the type line when there are no affixes
//       +28 immunity line   +30 position   +38 unique flag (byte)
//     It sizes "Health" to the width of the name text (1F8784..1F87F9: 8460F0
//     ui scale, 903C20 measure(text, style +B4h, scale in xmm2), 1E6750 widget
//     scale, 857520 set width, 857570 set x), copies that rectangle onto
//     UniqueMonsterBackground / NormalMonsterBackground and shows one of them.
//     The pc layout is updated once (container = panel), the controller one
//     once per ScreenAnchored / TargetAttached container.
//   This plugin runs after the stock update and fills only containers that
//   hold its own widgets. The one other container it touches is the
//   controller nameplate: the TargetAttached container (the panel's child of
//   that name, updated with attached = 1; the pc layout passes the panel
//   itself), when it holds none of the plugin's widgets. Its content stays
//   stock. Only its bar and both frames are resized: the stock update has
//   just made the bar as wide as the name, centred it, set the fill and
//   copied the bar's rectangle onto both frames, and it never sets the bar's
//   y or height. The plugin shrinks the bar to [nameplate] width_percent of
//   that width (around its centre) and height_percent of the bar's layout
//   height (remembered once; the bar keeps the stock bar's bottom edge), and
//   sets the fill again for the new rectangle. It does so only when the
//   container has its Name text box, which is exactly when the stock update
//   sized the bar, so the width is never shrunk twice.
//   The frames follow the bar on screen. GrowableImageWidget::Draw (8DCE80)
//   draws the frame art at its own size times the widget's scale (+80h, read
//   at 8DCED5), and its caps are child images anchored at the frame's left
//   and right middle (8DD6FF: (0, 0.5) and (1, 0.5)), whose offsets the
//   screen position walk (8562F0) multiplies by the frame's scale (8563B8).
//   A rectangle alone therefore moves the caps but never changes the art's
//   height. With r = the bar's new height over its layout height, each frame
//   gets scale r (times its own scale before the plugin changed it, normally
//   1) and the bar's rectangle with width and height divided by r: its caps
//   land on the bar's left and right edges, centred on it, and all of its art
//   is r times the stock size. At 100% the frames get the bar's rectangle and
//   their own scale back, as stock.
//   Name and NameBold keep their layout distance above the bar: the stock
//   update never sets their rectangles, and the plugin moves both by as much
//   as the bar's top edge moved (their layout y remembered once, like the
//   bar's).
//   The name stays the stock Name text box. When TargetAttached also has a
//   text box named NameBold, the plugin gives it the name's text and colour
//   on every update, exactly as the stock update fills Name (1F8731: payload
//   +08, 16 bytes, to the text box at +BCh, then the text from payload +00),
//   and clears it with Name. Placed a pixel or two beside Name in the layout,
//   the two copies draw the name bold; the game's text styles have no bold
//   option and its only bold font is an italic one.
//   Widgets: rect x,y,w,h at +70h (8562A0 reads the 16 bytes), fitToParent
//   byte at +52h, SetVisible is vtable +50h, text set through 86DD50.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Exact life, level, resistances and aura levels
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The client only knows life as a percentage. The exact values live on the
//   server unit, so they are computed on the authoritative game thread
//   (ThreadService::runOnGameThread) and cached for the panel:
//     single player / TCP host  : the server unit is found with 09A5A0
//                                 (id, type) and read directly.
//     joined TCP/IP client      : the client asks the host through the plugin
//                                 network channel; the host computes the same
//                                 data for that player and answers.
//   Life is stat 6 / 7 (in 256ths), level stat 12. A monster with life bars
//   reports its total life through the Monster Life Bars plugin's
//   "life-totals" service (D2RLoader plugin communication: provider
//   celestialrayone.monster-life-bars, version 1), asked on the game thread
//   with the server unit: bars left x bar size + current bar, out of bars
//   total x bar size, 64-bit. Every other monster reads stat 6 / 7 as before.
//
//   Resistances follow SUNITDMG_ApplyResistancesAndAbsorb 4523E0 exactly. For
//   a monster: resist stat -> sunder 44F6D0 (item_pierce_*_immunity) ->
//   -enemy resistance 44F8B0 (passive_*_pierce, ignored against immunity),
//   both called with the hovering player as attacker; then a positive result
//   is uncapped, a non-positive one is floored at the engine's floor
//   (mov ecx,-100 at 4524C4). Physical resistance is 0 against undead while
//   the attacker has state 47. The switch 092A10 that would let -enemy
//   resistance reduce immunities returns 0 in this build.
//   Allied monsters (monstats.txt Align 1: summons, hirelings, town NPCs)
//   always show their own resistances, without the hovering player's sunder or
//   -enemy resistance: those steps describe hits the player cannot land on
//   them, and subtracting them made e.g. a summon's 40% physical show as 25%
//   for an owner with -15% enemy physical resistance.
//   A bound demon keeps the class id of the hostile monster it was bound from,
//   and that row has Align 0. It is decided by the row Bound Demon Scaling
//   gives it instead: that plugin's "stats-row" service (D2RLoader plugin
//   communication: provider celestialrayone.bound-demon-scaling, version 1),
//   asked on the game thread with the server unit, returns the pseudo row a
//   bound demon takes its stats from (-1 for every other unit), and Align is
//   read from that row. Summon Resist Cap decides the cap by the same row.
//   Without that plugin, or while it names no pseudo row, a bound demon is
//   decided by its own row.
//   Which monsters are capped is asked from the Summon Resist Cap plugin
//   itself, through its "cap-decision" service (D2RLoader plugin
//   communication: provider celestialrayone.summon-resist-cap, version 1),
//   on the game thread with the server unit. That one answer covers its
//   Align column, its extra_monster_ids and its uncap pairs: a summon whose
//   owner has a paired stat is reported uncapped and can show 100 or more,
//   exactly as the engine resolves it. Nothing of that plugin's config is
//   copied here. A capped monster gets the player cap while the cap plugin's
//   jump at the 45249C gate is live: 75 + the max-resist stat, ceiling 95
//   (4524DE), physical 50 (4524D6), floor -100 (4524E7). No difficulty
//   penalty, as in the engine.
//
//   Aura Enchanted (MonUMod 30, callback 495CD0) gives one skill through the
//   skill list; Heralds also get one more as stat 151 (item_aura) with the
//   skill id as its layer. The skills to look for come from Monster Affixes'
//   "aura-enchanted" service (its TOML lists) when that plugin publishes it,
//   else from the game's tables at 1D1DE70 (7 records) and 1D1DF20 (4), both
//   24-byte records with the skill id at +14h. A bound demon rolls its aura
//   from Summon Enhancements' own list (its auras key), which that plugin
//   publishes under the same service name and shape; those skills are looked
//   for as well. Levels are read back with 33DCD0 / 33D1E0 and stat 151.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Text
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Affix names come from the game's own builder 1DFAE0 (the one that makes
//   the stock affix line), called with the unit's MonUMod list minus Aura
//   Enchanted, so they read exactly as stock. Colours use the "ColorCode"
//   string plus '0' + colour id (the game's 0CD6E0). Type names come from the
//   localization service ("UndeadDescriptX", "DemonID"). Skill names are the
//   game's own: the skills.txt record (097790) holds its skilldesc.txt row at
//   +23Ch, 088850 returns that row, and its "str name" string id sits at +8
//   (the skilldesc.txt column table, 3025A5); the localization service turns the
//   id into text. A skill without a skilldesc row falls back to
//   "skillname<id>".
//
//   Minions carry in their own MonUMod list every affix their boss passes on
//   (monumod.txt xfer = 1), also the ones that do nothing for a minion. The
//   affixes in minion_hidden_affixes are left out for minions. A minion is
//   what the stock builder treats as one before it writes its minion line:
//   type flag 10h set and none of 0Fh (0CED9A 38E0E0, 0CF05D 38E870 ->
//   1DF830). 38E870 (unit, mask) tests mask against the type flag word at
//   monster data +1Ah. The stock line for a minion is that minion line, never
//   an affix line, so a minion left with nothing to show takes it as its type
//   line, like a minion without affixes.
//
//   Bosses are monstats.txt primeevil: flags at record offset 60, bit 7 (the
//   3.3 column parser 39DB30, descriptor slot rbp+480h).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Immunity line (1.0.10)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The stock builder 0CEA50 makes the line on the client from the client
//   copy of the monster's resist stats (0CEDCC..0CF7B1). Per element, in the
//   order Physical 36, Magic 37, Fire 39, Lightning 41, Cold 43, Poison 45:
//   value = resist stat; if value >= 100 it subtracts the local player's
//   item_pierce_*_immunity (sunder: 192, 193, 189, 190, 187, 191; the -enemy
//   resistance branch behind 092A10 is off in this build); value >= 100 adds
//   the element. Class ids 704..709 never get a line (0CEDB8: sub 2C0h,
//   cmp 5, jbe). It cannot know about the Summon Resist Cap, which exists only
//   on the server, so a capped summon past 100 showed "Immune to X" although
//   its capped value tops out at 95 (physical 50).
//
//   The plugin builds the row itself, the same way:
//     text      d2r strings strScaryMonDesc5, 6, 1, 3, 2, 4, or the
//               PhysicalImmunityIcon .. PoisonImmunityIcon set while 077E10
//               (a display setting, read by the stock code at 0CEDEA..) is on
//     colours   4, 8, 1, 9, 3, 2 through the game's colour function 0CD6E0
//     separator TargetHealthBarTextSeparator coloured 5 while 846210 is on,
//               otherwise KeyComma followed by space
//   but an element counts as immune only when the value the damage code
//   uses for the hovering player's hit is 100 or more: ResolveResist with the
//   hovering player as attacker (sunder applied as stock does, Summon Resist
//   Cap applied), on the server, in both resistance modes. Until the server
//   data arrives the stock line is shown, except for allied monsters
//   (monstats.txt Align 1), which may be capped and show nothing yet. A
//   bound demon counts as one of those until then: the client cannot ask
//   Bound Demon Scaling, so the client copy's bind_demon state (207) stands
//   in, the state the game's own demon save tests (533CE0, at 533D60:
//   mov edx,0CFh / call 3351B0). That test is checked at load; when it does
//   not match, a bound demon shows the stock line until its data arrives.

// 1F8731, the stock update: movups xmm0,[r14+8] / movups [r12+0BCh],xmm0 /
// mov rcx,[r14]: the name's colour (payload +08, 16 bytes) goes to the Name
// text box at +BCh, and its text is payload +00.

// 8563B8, the screen position walk 8562F0: movups xmm0,[rbx+70h] /
// movups [rsp+30h],xmm0 / mov ecx,[rsp+38h] / movss xmm1,[rbx+80h]: a child's
// offset is multiplied by its parent's own scale.

// 8DCED5, GrowableImageWidget::Draw: mulss xmm2,[rdi+80h] / lea r14,[rdi+140h]:
// the frame art is drawn at its own size times the widget's scale; the left
// cap is the image widget at +140h.

// 8DD6FF, GrowableImageWidget setup: the left cap (+140h) is anchored at
// (0, 0.5) of the frame (+188h / +18Ch) and the right cap (+280h) at (1, 0.5)
// (+2C8h / +2CCh).

// Stat ids as the game's own tables use them (0CEDCC..0CEF7F builds the same
// {resist, sunder, pierce} triples; 4523E0 reads +8 resist, +C max, +10 pierce,
// +14 sunder from its per-type records).

struct StatsRowService {
    using StatsRowFn = std::int32_t(__cdecl*)(void* unit) noexcept;

    std::uint32_t serviceSize;
    std::uint32_t serviceVersion;
    // Game thread, server unit: the monstats.txt row a bound demon takes its
    // stats from, -1 for every other unit (and while that plugin is off or
    // names no pseudo row).
    StatsRowFn    statsRow;
};

// Game thread, server unit: the monstats.txt row a bound demon takes its
// stats from, -1 for every other unit (and while that plugin is off or
// names no pseudo row).

// The monstats.txt row whose Align decides whether a server monster is shown
// as an ally: a bound demon's pseudo row when Bound Demon Scaling names one,
// the monster's own row otherwise. Game thread only.

// The two steps read only these fields: context +0 game (its byte at
// +106h is the data context), +10h attacker, +18h defender, +24h
// "uncapped" (non-zero for monsters); record +8 resist stat, +C max
// stat, +10h pierce stat, +14h sunder stat.

// Runs on the authoritative game thread only. The server copy of a unit is
// found the way the game's own hover path finds it (0CF859 -> 429110):
// current server game 405790, then SUNIT_GetServerUnit 48FE80 (game, type, id).

// Game thread, right after the demon was bound (the publisher calls it from
// its bind relay): when the panel holds data for it, read it again now
// instead of RefreshMs later.

// Colours through the game's own function (0CD6E0), the one that colours the
// stock monster and immunity texts: it prepends the game's "ColorCode" string
// and '0' + colour to a 1024-byte buffer. A prefix built from the localization
// service's copy of that string was drawn literally in game ("\xC3\xBF" "c1-1").

// The name the game gives the skill: its skilldesc.txt "str name". Skills
// without a skilldesc row (several of ESR's "Old" rows) fall back to the
// "skillname<id>" key.

// The game's own affix-name builder (the stock affix line without its type
// prefix). Its output string is {pointer, length, capacity}; bit 63 of the
// capacity marks inline storage, otherwise the block belongs to the game's
// allocator (0CF6B1 / 1DF7DE free it the same way).

// Growable line geometry, matching the shipped resist_bar_* art: each end cap
// is 48 wide (a faded stub, the end diamond 20 in from the end, then the ramp
// to full strength) and drawn outside the widget's rect (placeCapsOutsideRect).
// The whole bar reaches LinePadding past the text on each side, which puts the
// end diamonds 26 past it, and is never shorter than LineMinimumWidth so the
