// The immunity row from the server mask, written the way 0CEA50 writes the
// stock line: Physical, Magic, Fire, Lightning, Cold, Poison, each in its own
// colour, joined by the stock separator. Empty text when a string is missing,
// so the caller can fall back.

// The bar. The stock update sized it to the width of the name and placed
// it around an x it stored at layout load (panel +168h / +16Ch), which is
// not the centre of these layouts; the plugin centres it in its container
// and gives it the configured width. HealthBarWidget builds its fill
// rectangle when its value is set (2AC890 -> 85A190 writes +ACh from the
// rect at that moment), so the value is set again after the resize,
// exactly as the stock update does after its own sizing.
// Prime evils also get a taller bar, grown evenly about the layout's
// centre line, but only when the layout has the boss frame whose opening
// is built for that height.

// Stack the rows from LevelType's layout position, skipping empty ones,
// with row_gap between two shown rows. A row's line images keep their
// layout offset from it and share its visibility.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

// Monster Life Cap
//
// Keeps the life of every monster, summon and bound demon inside the 32-bit
// life stat. Life is stored in 256ths (hitpoints 6, maxhp 7), so the most a
// life stat can hold is 2147483647 = 8,388,607.99 life. A computation that goes
// past that wraps around to a garbage or negative number and the monster ends
// up with nonsense life or dies on the spot. This plugin finds every place that
// can do that and holds the result at the cap instead. Below the cap every
// result is exactly what the game computed before.
//
// Everything below was read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image (D2R
// 3.3.93847, md5 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d) and D2RCore.dll (SHA-256
// 2a868d013d2e0830bd2d9e04b918b19e46a73cf726c833e70d089b948fdeb5a2). Every
// patched byte is checked before it is written; a part whose bytes differ (a
// different build, or another plugin already owns them) stays off and says so
// in the log and in the "lifecap" console command.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Already held at the cap (left alone)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Spawn (543E90): rolled life + player-count bonus, cmp edi,800000h /
//     cmovge 7FFFFFh at 544284, then shl 8.
//   Summons (55EF80): D2RLoader's own cave at 55F367 computes max + max *
//     calc1 / 100 in 64 bits and saturates it to 32 bits.
//   Heralds (D2RCore 40EC00): lowers the requested boost so max life stays
//     under 32 bits: allowed = (7FFFFFFFh - max) * 100 / max.
//   Bonuses (D2RCore 3DC2D0, a stat's full value): every list value and every
//     op result (64-bit imul, /100, clamped) is added with a saturating add
//     (sar 31 / add 80000000h / cmovo), so Battle Orders, Oak Sage, item and
//     aura max life percent and flat bonuses top out at 7FFFFFFFh.
//   Life percent (34A220 for the AI, 588CE0 for the health bar): both shift
//     life down to whole life before multiplying (by 100 / 128), no overflow.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Fixed here
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   1. Monster-mod life (49BD50: unit, percent): champion, unique, minion,
//      Possessed and Herald bonuses. max = ReadWideMaxLife, max * percent /
//      100 with three 32-bit branches, then stats 7 and 6 = max + that, all in
//      32 bits. Replaced through D2RLoader's inline hook: the same three
//      branches in 64 bits, the sum held at 7FFFFFFFh.
//   2. Level scaling (3A26C0: data context, monstats row, column set,
//      difficulty, level, field flags, out int32[], extra): for flag 1 the
//      monstats MinHP / MaxHP WORD (signed) times monlvl.txt HP percent, a
//      32-bit product, / 100, into out[0] / out[1] (whole life). Hooked: after
//      the original, the same math in 64 bits; only when it differs from what
//      the game wrote (its product wrapped) the value is replaced, held at
//      8,388,607, the spawn cap.
//   3. Bind Demon (D2RCore 8316F0, ApplyBindDemonBoundStats): each bound stat
//      value from its calc is shifted left 8 for stats 6..11 and 216/217 at
//      83188A..8318A3 (26 bytes, only inbound branch 831892 from inside), then
//      set at 8318B0. The span jumps to a stub: life and max life shifted in 64
//      bits and held at 7FFFFFFFh; the other shifted stats exactly as before.
//   4. Life gained (D2RCore 3D9EE0, the stat add behind AddWideUnitStat): the
//      new value is add eax,r9d at 3D9FD7, 32 bits, no clamp, so a heal, leech
//      or any gain on a monster near the cap wrapped it negative. The 15 bytes
//      3D9FD7..3D9FE5 (add eax,r9d / mov qword [rsp+20h],0 / mov rsi,r8; no
//      branch lands inside) jump to a stub: for hitpoints and maxhp the add is
//      done in 64 bits and held to 32 bits; every other stat adds as before.
//   5. Regeneration tick (448C00): life + regen is add esi,ebx at 448D19, then
//      cmp esi,r14d (max) / jle 448D35, else 448D20 stops the regen event and
//      takes max. A wrapped sum is below max, so it skipped the clamp, became
//      0 at 448D35 and the monster died. 448D19..448D1F (7 bytes, the whole
//      function is branch-audited, nothing lands inside) jump to a stub that
//      does the add and the compare in 64 bits.
//   6. Max life change (D2RCore 3CC660, on a change of stat 7 / 9 / 11): the
//      current stat is rescaled in single-precision floats, current * newMax /
//      max(oldMax, 100h) at 3CC9C2..3CCA12, and a float result at or above
//      2^31 gives 1. Near the cap a float that large is reached easily (float
//      steps are 128 there), so a monster near the cap whose max life changed
//      (an aura, Battle Orders) dropped to 1 life. The span (no branch and no
//      jump-table target lands inside) jumps to a stub calling RescaleValue:
//      the game's float math step by step, except that for life a result at
//      or above 2^31 keeps the exact 64-bit share instead of 1. Mana and
//      stamina behave exactly as before.
//   7. Life set from a sum (D2RCore 3D9BF0, the stat set behind
//      SetWideUnitStat: context, unit, key, value). Dozens of skill, missile
//      and item functions heal by setting life to life + heal (or max life to
//      a computed sum) in 32 bits instead of adding; the same sum near the
//      cap wraps negative and the unit dies. A damage result cannot get
//      there: damage is at most 2^31 - 1, so life - damage is at least
//      old - (2^31 - 1), while old + heal wrapped is at most old - 2^31 - 1.
//      So a negative new value below old - (2^31 - 1) is always a wrapped
//      sum and never damage (kills set 0, not a negative value). The
//      entry (16 relocatable bytes: 8 pushes and sub rsp,58h) jumps to
//      HookCoreSet, which then sets life to max life (a heal stops at max) or
//      max life to the cap; every other set goes through unchanged.

// cvtsi2ss xmm0,r13d / cmp edi,101h / mov ecx,100h / cmovae ecx,edi / cvtsi2ss xmm1,ecx /
// cvtsi2ss xmm2,eax / divss xmm0,xmm1 / mulss xmm2,xmm0 / cvttss2si edi,xmm2 / cmp edi,2 /
// mov eax,1 / cmovl edi,eax / movss xmm0,[2^31] / ucomiss xmm0,xmm2 / cmovbe edi,eax /
// ucomiss xmm2,[-2^31] / cmovb edi,eax / cmp edi,r13d / cmovge edi,r13d

using LevelStatsFn     = std::uint64_t(__fastcall*)(std::uint64_t dataContext, std::uint64_t row,
                             std::uint64_t columnSet, std::uint64_t difficulty, std::int32_t level,
                             std::uint64_t fields, std::int32_t* out, std::int32_t extra);

// 3CC9C2..3CCA12: eax = current value of stat r12d (6 / 8 / 10), edi = old
// max, r13d = new max; 3CCA13 sets the stat to edi. The function body keeps
// rsp 16-byte aligned (8 pushes + sub rsp,98h), xmm0..xmm5 are not read after
// the span.

// Monster Regen Start
//
// A monster starts regenerating life as soon as it gains hpregen, whatever
// gave it, instead of only after the next hit.
//
// Everything below was read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process dump (md5
// 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d) with IDA, and out of D2RCore.dll 1.3.1
// (SHA-256 2a868d013d2e0830bd2d9e04b918b19e46a73cf726c833e70d089b948fdeb5a2).
// Every game address the plugin uses is guarded by a byte witness at load.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The bug
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   A monster regenerates only while its stat-regen timer (event type 3) runs.
//   The timer's handler, the monster regen tick at 0x448C00, reschedules
//   itself every frame, but deletes itself when hpregen is 0 and when life
//   would pass maximum life. The game starts it again in two places only:
//     0x44D2C7  the damage commit: life damage of the hit (+0x134) is not 0,
//               the defender is a monster, it survived, and it already has
//               hpregen -> EVENT_Delete + EVENT_SetEvent(type 3, frame + 1)
//     0x4519F9  the poison applier, for a monster
//   Nothing else starts it. hpregen added by a skill state (aurastat), a
//   curse or an item therefore does nothing until the monster takes life
//   damage again. Under an absorb shield (auraeventfunc 40, Bone Armor,
//   Cyclone Armor) every hit deals 0 life damage, so it never starts, and the
//   hit that breaks the shield removes the state, and its hpregen, before the
//   leftover damage lands.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The fix
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   When the total of a stat whose itemstatcost row has fCallback changes,
//   D2RCore calls the unit's stat callback (D2RCore 0x3DBDC7 and 0x3DD054:
//   call [list+2AF0h] after test byte [isc+5],4). Every monster gets the
//   game's 0x46FDD0 as that callback at creation (0x5432E6: lea r8,0x46FDD0,
//   passed to the stat-list allocator 0x2F65D0, which stores it at
//   list+2AF0h). 0x46FDD0 is D2RLoader's stub jmp [0x3E2A3F0], whose pointer
//   leads to D2RCore's OnWideServerStatChanged.
//
//   The plugin swaps that one pointer for its own handler, which calls the
//   original first and then, when hpregen (layer 0) changes to a value other
//   than 0 on a live monster, restarts the timer the same way the damage
//   commit does. No game code is patched. Max Life One hooks the D2RCore
//   function the pointer leads to; the two chain, in either load order.
//
//   Live monster: unit type 1, still registered in the game
//   (SUNIT_GetServerUnit returns the same unit), in a room, and life above 0.
//   Stat lists are freed while a unit is removed from the game; without this
//   check a timer could be scheduled on a unit that is about to be freed.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ABIs (x64)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   stat callback        (game, owner, unit, key = stat << 32 | layer,
//                         old total, new total) -> void
//   EVENT_SetEvent       0x48B720 (game, unit, type, frame, customId,
//                         customParam, arg7) -> void
//   EVENT_Delete         0x48B890 (game, unit, type, customId) -> void
//   SUNIT_GetServerUnit  0x48FE80 (game, type, id) -> unit
//   UNITS_GetRoom        0x34B440 (unit) -> room
//   read unit stat       0x2F5020 (unit, stat, layer) -> int32
//   game frame           dword at game+170h
//   unit type, unit id   dwords at unit+00h, unit+08h
//
//   D2RCore's own symbol table names 0x48B720, 0x48B890, 0x48FE80, 0x34B440
//   and 0x46FDD0 for this build, and calls them with these arguments.

// 0x448C72, regen tick: mov r9d,[rbp+170h] / inc r9d / three zero stack
// arguments / r8d = 3 / call 0x48B720 (EVENT_SetEvent), then r9d = 0 /
// r8d = 3 / call 0x48B890 (EVENT_Delete).

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

// =============================================================================
//  Move Only Skill  -  celestialrayone.move-only-skill
//
//  D2R 3.3 under D2RLoader 1.3.1. A skill that makes the mouse button it sits
//  on move only. A click with it walks or runs, talks to NPCs, picks up items
//  and uses objects, portals and waypoints, the way a click with no skill
//  does, and the skill itself is never cast.
//
//  Every address below was read out of the dumped D2RLoader 1.3.1 image
//  (md5 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d) and disassembled before being relied
//  on. Byte witnesses guard every instruction the plugin depends on, so a
//  different build refuses cleanly.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  HOW THE GAME HANDLES A CLICK IN THE WORLD (client)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  0x0FE3B0  the world click dispatcher builds a click command on its stack and
//            calls the click handler with it. A left press fills the skill
//            slot from the left skill (0x34A540) right before the call at
//            0x0FE69F; the left hold handler 0x1013B0 does the same before its
//            call at 0x101555. Command layout: +00h flags (1 left, 2 right,
//            4 press, 20h Shift, 400h controller), +08h player, +10h target
//            unit, +18h/+1Ch x/y, +20h mode of a move to a spot, +24h message
//            of a move toward a unit, +28h skill.
//  0x102490  the click handler(command). mov rdi,rcx at 0x102496; rdi stays the
//            command for the whole function.
//  0x101020  the handler's entry check, called first at 0x102499 with the real
//            skill. It sets +20h/+24h from the run setting, refuses a skill
//            that may not be used in town for Shift, right button and
//            controller clicks, settles the target, and returns 0 for a
//            command that has no skill, which drops the click entirely. That
//            is why the skill is cleared after this check and never before.
//  0x1025B9  call 0x33F790(player, skill), the skill's range class. r14 holds
//            &command.skill (0x102599). Right after the call the handler reads
//            the skill again from [r14] (0x1025BE), and every later read goes
//            through r14. With the skill cleared there, the handler takes its
//            no-skill paths:
//      ground, no Shift  0x101EF0 moves to the spot, as for every skill.
//      ground, Shift     0x1029AB..0x1029BA: no skill, the click ends.
//      unit              0x102753 asks 0x33F770, which returns 0 for no skill
//                        (Left Click Auras' relay there passes the same null
//                        skill on), then 0x1028F7 handles the click without a
//                        skill: Shift ends it, a unit that is not a monster
//                        goes to 0x101910 (talk, pick up, use), a monster
//                        press moves toward it through 0x0FABE0 with the +24h
//                        message, and an NPC is talked to.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  THE CHANGE (client)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  0x1025B9  call relay. The stub adds the command (rdi) as a third argument.
//            When the command's skill is the move only skill, the bridge clears
//            command.skill and returns 0, the range class of no skill. Every
//            other click calls 0x33F790 untouched.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  FINDING THE SKILL BY NAME
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  charstats.txt Skill 1 to Skill 10 hold skills.txt names. The charstats
//  loader 0x30C560 resolves them through the skills.txt name table at
//  DataTables+0x11C8: 0x30C5F7 loads DataTables into rsi, 0x30C90A
//  lea rax,[rsi+11C8h] is the name table of StartSkill and Skill 1 to Skill 4,
//  and nothing in between writes rsi. The plugin asks the same table:
//      0x300A90  DataTables of a data context
//      0xA121F0  name -> row, the name lowercased first, -1 when missing
//      0xA12420  row -> name, used to check the answer
//      0x097790  the skills.txt record, whose id word must equal the row
//  The context is the player's (0x34A0E0, byte [unit+1BDh]). The answer is
//  kept until the data tables load again or the name table moves.
//
//  Console command "moveonlyskill" shows what is installed, the resolved skill
//  and the counter.
//  Settings: d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.move-only-skill.toml
// =============================================================================

// The game tests a skills.txt flag as byte & mask, the masks being a table of
// dwords 1 << n at 0x1D996D0: n 0 InTown, 2 InGame (byte 25h), 4 passive,
// 5 aura (byte 24h), 6 leftskill (byte 27h), 7 rightskill (byte 28h). The
// bits match the skills.txt loader 0x302380 (InTown bit 8, InGame bit 10,
// leftskill bit 30, rightskill bit 39 of the flags at +24h).

// 0x102595..0x1025E7: player from [rdi+8], r14 = rdi+28h, rdx = [r14],
// r15 = target [rdi+10h], ebx = flags [rdi], the call 0x1025B9 -> 0x33F790,
// then mov rcx,[r14] and the record lookup 0x33CBC0 on it.

// 0x1028F7..0x102992, a unit click with no skill: Shift ends it, a unit that
// is not a monster goes to 0x101910, a monster press checks the monstats NPC
// flag (talk, 0x101910) or moves toward it (0x0FABE0, message [rdi+24h]).

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Relay page. One page the plugin allocates within rel32 reach of the image;
//  nothing inside D2R's own code is used as a cave.
//
//      +0x000  UNWIND_INFO, no frame (the stub leaves rsp untouched)
//      +0x010  RUNTIME_FUNCTION of the stub
//      +0x100  mov r8,rdi / mov rax,<bridge> / jmp rax
//
//  The stub is reached by the redirected call, so the return address is on
//  the stack and rcx/rdx are the stock arguments; the bridge is entered as if
//  the game had called it, with the command in r8. r8 and rax are volatile
//  and 0x33F790 takes two arguments.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Op 1 Item Fix (celestialrayone.op1-item-fix)
//
// An itemstatcost.txt op 1 stat on an item stops reaching the character as
// soon as it changes while the item also carries the stat it raises. In
// Eastern Sun Resurrected that is passive_summ_mastery_total (op 1, op stat1
// passive_summ_mastery): a Jeweler perk bonus on it, or a socketed item's part
// of it, never reaches the character's summon damage.
//
// Every address and byte below was read out of D2RCore.dll 1.3.1 (sha256
// 2a868d01...): the witnesses are the ones the enhanced-damage-fix plugin
// verified there, copied byte for byte, and the op 1 table entry is checked at
// load against the value the switch's own bounds check proves for the
// default arm.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. The engine rule that loses it
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   When a stat with an op changes on a statlist, the engine re-evaluates the
//   stats it raises (its op stat1..3) and then decides whether the stat's own
//   total may be committed to that statlist, which is what carries it up to
//   the socket host and then to the character. That is the evaluate-and-update
//   routine (D2MOO sub_6FDB64A0): for each target whose re-evaluated total is
//   not 0 it switches on the stat's `op`, and op 1 falls into the default arm,
//   which withholds the commit on every statlist.
//
//   op 1 itself is applied by the evaluator (D2MOO sub_6FDB5830 case 1, D2RCore
//   3DC2D0): on a statlist owned by an item it multiplies the ITEM'S OWN BASE
//   value of the target (3DC834: "ops 1 and 13 on an item owner take the base
//   Stats path"), on a character or monster the accumulated total. So on an
//   item without a base value of the target op 1 multiplied nothing, yet the
//   commit is withheld because the target's total, affixes included, is not 0.
//   The item's total of the op 1 stat keeps whatever was committed before, the
//   character's op 1 reads that stale total, and the change is lost. The
//   character's own statlist is right to withhold: op 1 applied there.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Where it runs under D2RLoader 1.3.1: D2RCore.dll
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//     D2RCore 3DBEB0  evaluate and update (ctx, statList, key, record, unit)
//       3DBF0E  mov [rsp+40h],r15 / mov al,1 / mov [rsp+34h],eax  ; commit = 1
//       3DBF25  and al,[rsp+34h] / mov [rsp+34h],eax              ; <- resume
//       3DBF30  next target, at most 3
//       3DBF3D  movzx r12d, word [rsi+r15*2+54h]                  ; op stat N
//       3DBF8E  call 3DBEB0                                        ; re-evaluate it
//       3DBFAF  call 3DCE90                                        ; commit it
//       3DBFB4  test ebp,ebp / jz next                             ; total 0: no switch
//       3DBFBC  movzx eax, byte [rsi+50h]                          ; the stat's `op`
//       3DBFC0  cmp rax,0Dh / ja 3DC123                            ; default arm
//       3DBFE0  lea rcx,[rip+254445h]                              ; table 63042C
//       3DBFE7  movsxd rax,dword [rcx+rax*4] / add rax,rcx / jmp rax
//
//   Entry N of the table at 63042C is the arm for op N as an offset from the
//   table. Entry 13 is 3DC117 (the op 13 arm the enhanced-damage-fix plugin
//   repairs). The bounds check sends every op above 13 to 3DC123, the default
//   arm; op 1 has no case of its own, so its entry holds 3DC123 too. That is
//   checked at load (F7 BC DA FF), and nothing is installed otherwise.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. The fix
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Entry 1 is pointed at a 60-byte stub in a page this plugin allocates next
//   to D2RCore. The stub asks AllowCommit for al and resumes at 3DBF25, so the
//   engine's own and/mov records the answer exactly as for any other arm:
//
//     native  al = 0 (withhold), for every statlist
//     fixed   a statlist owned by an item (statlist +08h is 4): withhold only
//               when the owning item (statlist +A0h) has a base value of the
//               target at layer 0 (D2RCore ReadWideUnitBaseStat, the read op 1
//               itself makes there); with none, or no owning item, commit
//             every other statlist: native
//
//   Registers at the arm: rdi = statList, rsi = the op 1 stat's record (stat
//   id at +0), r15 = target index 0..2, ebp = the target's non-zero total, the
//   commit flag at [rsp+34h]; rsp is 16-aligned there (8 pushes + sub rsp,48h
//   at 3DBEB0), so the stub takes 20h of shadow space, calls, and gives it
//   back. Nothing volatile is live at 3DBF25: the next iteration and the exit
//   path reload rcx, rdx and r8..r11 before use.
//
//   Only op 1 goes through the stub: the default arm and every other entry are
//   untouched, and so is the enhanced-damage-fix plugin's op 13 arm.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Verified against
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   D2MOO D2StatList.cpp (sub_6FDB5830 case 1, sub_6FDB64A0 commit switch,
//   sub_6FDB6C10 propagation, PostStatToStatList); the enhanced-damage-fix
//   plugin's D2RCore 1.3.1 witnesses. The stub was assembled with keystone,
//   decoded with capstone and run under unicorn through D2RCore's real dispatch
//   bytes (3DBFBC..3DBFF0) with the table entry pointing at it: arguments
//   (statList, target, op 1 stat), 16-byte alignment at the call, every
//   non-volatile register kept, the commit flag 1 or 0 as answered, the next
//   target reached at 3DBF3D, and after unload the stub's native tail landing
//   on the default arm at 3DC123.
//
//   No other plugin in this repo touches the op switch table at 63042C.

// 3DBF0E..3DBFF0: commit flag at [rsp+34h], the resume block at +17h, the loop
// index in r15, the target id read at +2Fh, the recursive re-evaluation, the
// target commit, the zero-total skip, the switch on byte [rsi+50h] and its
// jump through the table at 63042C.

struct CoreWitness {
    std::uintptr_t      rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::size_t         size;
    const char*         name;
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The stub
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// +00  48 83 EC 20              sub   rsp, 20h            ; shadow, stays aligned
// +04  48 89 F9                 mov   rcx, rdi            ; statList
// +07  42 0F B7 54 7E 54        movzx edx, word [rsi+r15*2+54h] ; target stat id
// +0D  44 0F B7 06              movzx r8d, word [rsi]     ; the op 1 stat's id
// +11  48 B8 imm64              mov   rax, AllowCommit
// +1B  FF D0                    call  rax
// +1D  48 83 C4 20              add   rsp, 20h
// +21  0F B6 C0                 movzx eax, al
// +24  FF 25 00 00 00 00        jmp   qword ptr [rip+0]
// +2A  dq                       D2RCore+3DBF25
// +32  FF 25 00 00 00 00        jmp   qword ptr [rip+0]   ; the native arm,
// +38  dq                       D2RCore+3DC123            ; used after unload

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The corrected decision
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Called from the stub with the engine's own statList (rdi), the op 1 target
// being decided (word [rsi+r15*2+54h]) and the op 1 stat's id (word [rsi]).
// Returns the al the engine expects at 3DBF25: 1 lets the op 1 stat be
// committed to this statlist, and from there reach the socket host and the
// character; 0 withholds it.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Performance improvements  -  D2RLoader plugin (D2R 3.3, D2RLoader 1.3.1)
//
// Every missile-performance fix in one plugin. Each fix removes work the game
// did in a way that grew with the square of the number of missiles alive, and
// each leaves gameplay exactly as it was: the game gets the same results, it
// just stops redoing the same search thousands of times per frame. Each fix
// can be switched off on its own in the config file.
//
//   room index            Server and client room unit lists (every missile is
//                         linked into its room's list). A side index next to
//                         each list lets a unit leave its room without a walk
//                         over the whole list, and lets missile collision
//                         checks look at players and monsters only, for the
//                         collide types that can only ever hit those
//                         (missiles.txt CollideType 1, 2, 3, 5, 8).
//                         Hooks 0x38EC90, 0x38EFE0, 0x38F270, 0x324BC0.
//   room add check        Skips a debug-only duplicate check that walked a
//                         room's whole unit list every time a unit was added.
//                         Byte patch at 0x38ED8B (replaces the patch file
//                         skip-room-add-duplicate-check.json, and detects it).
//   translation queue     HD layer, PreprocessMessageQueue: drops superseded
//                         unit messages with hash lookups instead of comparing
//                         every message with every earlier one. Hook 0x775A70.
//   weak instances        HD layer, EntityContainerDefinition::
//                         RemoveWeakInstance: a destroyed missile visual leaves
//                         its type's instance list directly instead of a walk
//                         over every other visual of that type (the main cost
//                         of many missiles dying or colliding at once).
//                         Hooks 0x955E90, 0x94A7A0.
//   client unit loop      Client per-type unit loop: skips the per-unit
//                         re-lookup whose result the game only uses for
//                         monsters and objects. Hook 0x09F470.
//   entity pool           The HD entity registry's allocator: one fixed 70 MB
//                         TLSF pool that runs out around 16k missiles (the
//                         "Unable to allocate for entity pool" crash). Raised
//                         to entity_pool_mb, by the game's own setup code at
//                         0xD714E0 when it has not run yet, or as a second
//                         TLSF pool when it has.
//   unit entity lookup    HD layer: finds a removed unit's HD entity by
//                         scanning the key array instead of walking every
//                         entity with a lookup and a call each (the second
//                         cost of mass missile deaths). Hook 0x9725E0.
//
// Every hooked function is checked byte for byte against this build before
// anything is installed; a part whose code does not match, or whose hook site
// is already taken by another plugin, stays off and says so in the log, and
// the other parts carry on.
//
// Console
//     perf           state of every part and its counters
//     perf reset     zero the counters
//     perf verify    check the room index against the game's room lists
//     perf memory    memory held by this plugin, the game's RAM and commit,
//                    system headroom, and the game's video memory per GPU
//
// Runtime settings live in
// d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.performance-improvements.toml, created
// with defaults on first load.
//
// Replaces the separate plugins room-unit-index (formerly in this folder),
// translation-queue, client-unit-loop, weak-instances and unit-entity-lookup.
// The sampling profiler stays a plugin of its own.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct Site {
	std::uint64_t       rva;
	const std::uint8_t* bytes;
	std::uint32_t       size;
	const char*         what;
};

// ===========================================================================
// Entity pool (HD entity registry allocator)
//
// Every component storage of D2R's HD entity registry (one set of components
// per missile visual, among everything else) comes from one TLSF heap with a
// fixed 70 MB pool, created at startup by 0xD714E0 ("Entity System Pool").
// Around 16k missiles the pool is full: the allocator returns null, the game
// asserts "Unable to allocate for entity pool" (entt_config.h:31) and then
// writes through the null pointer and crashes.
//
// This part gives that heap more room, the game's own way:
//   - when the plugin loads before the pool exists, the four copies of the
//     pool size in 0xD714E0 are raised and the game creates the bigger pool
//     itself;
//   - when the pool already exists, a second pool is added to the same TLSF
//     heap with the exact steps tlsf_create_with_pool (0x1215A30) uses for its
//     first pool: first block marked free, the game's block_insert
//     (0x1215650), end sentinel. It is taken from the same parent allocator,
//     under the allocator's own lock, and registered with the game's memory
//     tracker under the same name. The allocator's allocate, free, resize and
//     size calls (0x82DAD0, 0x82DC60, 0x82DBF0, 0x82DCB0) never look at which
//     pool a block is in.
// ===========================================================================

struct SizeSite {
	std::uint64_t rva;
	std::uint8_t  bytes[8];
	std::uint32_t size;
};

// Allocator object: +0x08 parent allocator, +0x10 memory, +0x18 size,
// +0x20 TLSF heap, +0x28 SRW lock (0xD714E0 writes the first four,
// 0x82DAD0 takes the lock).

// Each part installs on its own; one that fails stays off and the rest
// carry on. A part never returns with a half-installed state that changes
// behaviour: at worst an installed hook passes straight through.

// ===========================================================================
// Pet Max Lock (celestialrayone.petmax-lock)
// ===========================================================================
//
// A skill whose skills.txt petmax formula works out to 0 or less cannot be
// cast at all. It gets the game's own wrong-weapon treatment, the one a
// throwing skill gets while a sword is equipped: the skill button shows the
// invalid look, the client refuses the cast and the character says
// "Impossible", and the server refuses the command. Skills with a blank petmax
// column are never touched. Players only.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Why petmax 0 still gives one pet
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The summon code raises the evaluated petmax to at least 1 before it counts
//   pets (D2MOO PlayerPets.cpp: std::max(petmax, 1); Skills.cpp: "if (nPetMax
//   < 1) nPetMax = 1"). The plugin does not touch the summon code: it stops
//   the cast before it starts, through the check the game already uses for
//   a weapon that cannot use the skill.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. The skill use check, 33F360 (unit, skill) -> state
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   D2Common's "can this unit use this skill now" check. 0 means usable.
//   2 is the weapon state: 33F1F0 matches the equipped weapons against the
//   skill's item type requirements (record words +38h / +44h, checked through
//   3406B0) and a failure returns 2. 1 is the mana state, 8 the cooldown gate
//   3404A0; other values are other reasons (no level, aura, passive...).
//   Its callers, and what they do with a non-zero state:
//     client  21B170, which feeds
//               - the skill button refresh 239380 (switch at 23948B): states
//                 2, 3, 4, 7 and 9 draw the layout's invalidTint (button
//                 +3036); state 1 would draw notEnoughManaTint (+2988)
//               - the input paths, e.g. 100760: any state but 0 and 5 refuses
//                 the cast and hands the feedback id from the table at 22A1F98
//                 (state 1 -> 22, states 2..4 -> 20, state 9 -> 21) to
//                 106B70, which plays the class voice line for it; id 20 is
//                 slot 2 of the class's sound table 22A5920, "Impossible"
//     server  4FDCA0 / 4FDEF0, the player skill command validation: any
//             non-zero state refuses the command
//   Both sides turn states 1, 2 and 4 on a skill flagged AttackNoMana (record
//   byte +27h, bit 10h) into a normal attack.
//   The hook runs the native check first and only turns a 0 into a 2, so
//   every native reason keeps its own state.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Reading petmax
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The skill keyword switch 33B3B0 (param, dispatch at 33B3EA through the
//   table at 33C8A0) evaluates petmax as case 71: 33BFBD loads the formula at
//   skills record +114h and calls the evaluator 3B5160. Its case 58 loads
//   calc4 at +19Ch, the calc4 offset already proven in game, so the case
//   numbering is D2MOO's (71 petmax, 72 skpoints). A blank formula column
//   compiles to FFFFFFFFh, outside the formula pool, and is skipped.
//     skill -> record   [skill+0]; skill id = the record's first word (33E080)
//     level             33D1E0 (unit, skill, 1, 0), D2RCore ReadWideSkillLevel
//     context           34A0E0 (unit)
//     formula           3B5160 (context, unit, calc, skillId, level)
//     unit type         34B9D0 (unit), 0 = player
//   petmax is evaluated on every check, so a formula that depends on level,
//   synergies or stats locks and unlocks the skill as those change.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Other plugins
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Nothing else in this repo hooks 33F360. crossbow-charges hooks the
//   cooldown gate 3404A0, which 33F360 calls; the two stack without contact.
//
// ===========================================================================

// 33F360: push rsi / push rdi / sub rsp,28h / mov rdi,rdx / mov rsi,rcx /
// test rdx,rdx. The inline hook's expected bytes.
// 33B3EA: cmp ebx,7Eh / ja / lea rdx,[image base] / movsxd rax,ebx /
// mov ecx,[rdx+rax*4+33C8A0h] / add rcx,rdx / jmp rcx.
// 33C9BC: the jump table entry for param 71 = 33BFBD.
// 33BFBD: case 71, r8d = [r11+114h] (petmax), call 3B5160.
// 33BDDC: case 58, r8d = [r11+19Ch] (calc4), call 3B5160.
// 33E080: test rcx,rcx / jnz / xor eax,eax / ret / mov rax,[rcx] /
// movsx eax,word [rax] / ret.
// 22A1F98: the client feedback id per state, one dword each for states 0..9:
// 0, 22, 20, 20, 20, 0, 0, 0, 0, 21. State 2 -> 20, the "Impossible" line.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

// 33F360. The native check runs first; only a usable skill (state 0) whose
// petmax formula is filled and works out to 0 or less is turned into the
// weapon state.

// No Monster Collision
//
// While a player carries a chosen stat (ItemStatCost id 530 by default) with a
// value above 0, its walking and running pass through monsters, the same way
// Whirlwind carries it through a pack. Walls, doors, objects and blocked ground
// still stop it.
//
// Built for D2R 3.3.93847 as hosted by D2RLoader 1.3.1. Every address below is
// an RVA against the executable base, read out of the live D2RLoader.exe image
// and disassembled before being relied on. The plugin checks all of them byte
// for byte at load and installs nothing on any mismatch.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// How movement collision works
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Every unit standing on the map writes a presence flag, 1000h (D2MOO
//   COLLIDE_NO_PATH), into the collision grid under itself: monsters, town
//   NPCs and players alike. Hirelings and summons write 2000h (COLLIDE_PET)
//   instead, which a player's walk never tests.
//
//   Each unit's path holds its move-test mask at path+64h. The path planner and
//   every movement step test the grid against it. A player's path is created
//   with 1C09h: wall 1, no-player ground 8, object 400h, door 800h and the
//   presence flag 1000h, so any unit standing in the way stops the player.
//
//     sub_140342C30 (path allocator), player branch (unit type 0):
//       342EB6  BA 07 00 00 00          mov edx, 7             ; straight path
//       342EBB  C7 46 60 80 00 00 00    mov [rsi+60h], 80h     ; footprint: player
//       342EC2  48 8B CE                mov rcx, rsi
//       342EC5  C7 46 64 09 1C 00 00    mov [rsi+64h], 1C09h   ; move-test mask
//       342ECC  E8 9F FB FF FF          call 342A70            ; set path type
//       342ED1  66 C7 86 BD 00 00 00 49 46  mov word [rsi+0BDh], 4649h
//                                            ; max distance 73, A* score 70
//     The monster branch of the same function writes 3C01h or 3401h there,
//     both with the presence flag, so monsters stay blocked by players.
//
//   Whirlwind's server start sub_14056A420 plans and moves with masks that
//   leave the presence flag out, which is exactly why it passes through:
//       56A540  BA 01 0C 00 00          mov edx, 0C01h         ; plan mask
//       56A548  E8 F3 81 DD FF          call 342740            ; set move-test mask
//       56A54D  BF 09 1C 00 00          mov edi, 1C09h         ; kept to restore
//       56A5A0  E8 CB 68 DD FF          call 340E70            ; plan the path
//       56A5CC  BA 01 04 00 00          mov edx, 401h          ; movement mask
//       56A5D4  E8 67 81 DD FF          call 342740
//     342740 is the whole setter:  89 51 64 C3  mov [rcx+64h], edx / ret.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The change: one inline hook on the path planner
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   sub_140340E70 (path, unit, a3, a4) plans every path in the game. Client
//   and server both use it (60+ call sites; the client reaches it through its
//   wrapper 215EF0, which calls it as (path, unit, 0, 1)), so one hook covers
//   both sides:
//
//     340E70  48 89 5C 24 08          mov [rsp+8], rbx       ; <- hook, 16 bytes,
//     340E75  48 89 74 24 10          mov [rsp+10h], rsi     ;    no RIP-relative
//     340E7A  48 89 7C 24 18          mov [rsp+18h], rdi     ;    code, ends on an
//     340E7F  55                      push rbp               ;    instruction edge
//     ...
//     340E95  4C 8B F2                mov r14, rdx           ; unit
//     340E98  48 8B D9                mov rbx, rcx           ; path
//     ...
//     340F58  8B 43 64                mov eax, [rbx+64h]     ; move-test mask,
//     340F5B  89 45 F4                mov [rbp-0Ch], eax     ; into the planner
//
//   Before the planner runs, for a player whose path holds the stock walking
//   mask 1C09h and whose stat is above 0, the mask becomes 0C09h: 1C09h
//   without the presence flag and nothing else, so walls, no-player ground,
//   objects and doors keep blocking. When the stat is gone, 0C09h goes back to
//   1C09h. Every other value is a skill's own temporary rule (Whirlwind's 0C01h
//   and 401h, for one) and is never touched. The planner and every following
//   movement step read the field, so the whole move honours it.
//
//   The switch lands on the player's next planned move after the stat appears
//   or disappears. Anything that resets the mask to 1C09h (the end of
//   Whirlwind, the client's position reassign handler at 12AE3C) is re-applied
//   the same way on the next move.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Other anchors
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Unit type is the dword at unit+0, 0 = player:
//     UNITS_GetUnitType 34B9D0 ... 34B9F6  8B 01  mov eax, [rcx]
//
//   STATLIST_GetUnitStat 2F5020 (unit, statId, layer) -> int32 is the
//   D2RLoader thunk to D2RCore ReadWideUnitStat: jmp [rip+disp32], four nops,
//   then the untouched old body. The loader-owned disp32 is not pinned; the
//   thunk shape and the old body identify the function (same check as
//   dodge-cap). Layer 0 is passed.
//
//   Server units carry 4000000h in unit+128h. The server unit allocator sets
//   it right after writing type, class and game (4906A4..4906D4) through
//   34E140, whose body is  mov eax,[rcx+128h] / test r8d,r8d / je / or eax,edx
//   / mov [rcx+128h],eax. Only the diagnostics use it, to report the server
//   and the client separately.

// 340E80  push r12 / push r13 / push r14 / push r15 / mov rbp,rsp /
//         sub rsp,70h / mov r12d,r9d / mov r15d,r8d / mov r14,rdx /
//         mov rbx,rcx / test rcx,rcx

// 340F48  mov eax,[rbx+58h] / lea rcx,[rbp-40h] / mov [rbp-14h],eax /
//         mov eax,[rbx+5Ch] / mov [rbp-10h],eax /
//         mov eax,[rbx+64h] / mov [rbp-0Ch],eax

// 4906A4  mov [rax],r14d / mov rcx,rdi / mov [rax+4],r13d /
//         mov [rax+0D8h],r15 / movzx edx,byte [r15+106h] / call 34E1E0 /
//         mov edx,4000000h / mov r8d,1 / mov rcx,rdi / call 34E140

struct Witness {
    const char*         what;
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// playermode-stat
//
// Exposes a unit's animation mode to softcode formulas as one virtual stat id
// (410 by default, configurable). Every other stat id is untouched and goes
// down the stock path.
//
// Target: Diablo II: Resurrected 3.3.93847 (also 3.2.92777 and Steam
// 3.3.93787 -- those builds share an executable identity for patching).
//
// Safety model: the RVA below is against image base 0x140000000, and nothing
// is installed unless the bytes already there match byte for byte. On a build
// this plugin does not recognise it installs nothing, logs loudly and stays
// loaded so the console command can explain why. An unrecognised build is a
// no-op, never a mis-patch.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where the hook goes, and how that was established
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// .txt formulas are compiled to bytecode by the shared expression engine
// (D2Common\src\DataTbls\SharedBBE.cpp) and evaluated by a generic evaluator
// that is wired with a per-keyword callback table. The table lives at RVA
// 0x1D09C50 and is NINE 16-byte records of {callback VA : qword, arity : dword,
// padding : dword} -- not a plain array of function pointers:
//
//   record 0  0x3B3200  arity 2
//   record 1  0x3B3210  arity 2
//   record 2  0x3B3220  arity 2
//   record 3  0x3B32F0  arity 2
//   record 4  0x3B33A0  arity 2
//   record 5  0x3B33F0  arity 2   <- stat(), this is the one
//   record 6  0x3B3500  arity 3
//   record 7  0x3B3590  arity 2
//   record 8  0x3B3640  arity -1
//
// Read straight out of the image and byte-identical to the runtime capture
// published in the D2RLoader Suite research ledger (known-rvas.json,
// DATATBLS_BBEEvaluatorCallbackTable), which also pins the keyword string
// "stat" for record 5.
//
// sub_1403B33F0 disassembled in full (0x3B33F0..0x3B34F8, size 0x109):
//
//   RCX = game version byte, EDX = stat id, R8D = field code,
//   R9  = unused here,       [rsp+0x28] = evaluation context
//
//   0x3B33FF  mov rsi,[rsp+0x50]      ; = [entry rsp + 0x28] = 5th arg, context
//   0x3B3434  mov rsi,[rsi+0x10]      ; CONTEXT + 0x10 = the unit
//   0x3B343D  test edi,edi / js       ; negative stat id -> 0
//   0x3B3446  call 0x300A90           ; GetGameData(version)
//   0x3B344B  mov rbp,[rax+0x1260]    ; itemstatcost row count
//   0x3B3472  cmp edi,ebp / jge       ; id past the last row -> 0
//   0x3B347B  cmp edi,0x13 / jne      ; stat 19 (tohit) -> tail 0x3483C0
//   field 0 -> tail 0x2F5020  STATLIST_GetUnitStat      (.accr)
//   field 1 -> tail 0x2F48C0  STATLIST_GetUnitBaseStat  (.base)
//   field 2 -> tail 0x2F5C60  STATLIST_UnitGetStatValue (.mod)
//
// Two things follow from that and both matter here.
//
// First, the unit is at CONTEXT + 0x10 on this build. The legacy ESR byte
// patch for this feature read it at CONTEXT + 0 -- that was correct for the
// build it was written against and is wrong here. This is not a detail that
// can be carried over on faith.
//
// Second, hooking the entry puts the answer BEFORE the row-count guard at
// 0x3B3472 and before the field dispatch, so the virtual id does not need a
// real itemstatcost row and .base / .mod / .accr all return the same value.
//
// The mode itself is the 32-bit field at Unit + 0x0C. Confirmed against
// UNITS_GetUnitMode (RVA 0x34AB60), which is exactly
// `return unit ? *(uint32*)(unit + 12) : 0`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Verified before anything is installed. This covers the entry, the null
// context path, and the CONTEXT+0x10 unit read this plugin depends on, so the
// plugin also refuses on a build where the context layout moved rather than
// silently reading the wrong field. 77 bytes, 0x3B33F0..0x3B343C.

// The bytes the inline hook displaces: mov [rsp+10h], rbx. One instruction,
// position independent, no rip-relative operand and no branch. Every branch
// inside the function was decoded and none targets 0x3B33F0..0x3B33F4, so
// nothing can land in the middle of the displaced region.

using StatFieldResolverFn = std::int64_t(__fastcall*)(
	std::uint64_t gameVersion,
	std::int32_t  statId,
	std::int32_t  fieldCode,
	std::uint64_t reserved,
	std::uint64_t context) noexcept;

// Read once at load, never written afterwards, so the hook needs no ordering
// beyond a relaxed load. Kept separate from Config so the hot path touches one
// cache line and no struct offsets.

// An installed inline hook cannot be withdrawn, and the trampoline lives in
// this module, so there is deliberately nothing to undo here. The loader is
// expected to keep the DLL resident for the life of the process.

// Converted Poison
//
// Poison that a hit gets from converted weapon damage lasts the poison length
// of the skill that converted it, and nothing else: no averaging with the
// other poison sources on the hit and no minimum. Everything else about poison
// is untouched. With states.txt canstack 0 a target still holds one poison,
// and any poison with an equal or higher damage per frame replaces it.
//
// Built for D2R 3.3 on D2RLoader 1.3.1 (PluginSDK 0.3.0). Everything below was
// read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.1 dump (md5 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d)
// and D2RCore.dll 1.3.1 (SHA-256 2a868d013d2e0830bd2d9e04b918b19e46a73cf726c8
// 33e70d089b948fdeb5a2). Every game address is guarded by a byte witness at
// load.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// How a hit's poison gets its length
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   A hit (D2Damage) carries poison as a rate per frame (+38h, 256ths of life)
//   and a length in frames (+3Ch). The damage commit SUNITDMG_ExecuteEvents
//   0x44CE80 applies it through the poison applier 0x4518C0. With canstack 0
//   that finds the target's one poison list (0x2F5940) and replaces it when
//   the new rate is equal or higher.
//
//   Missile hits. MISSILES_ServerHitResolution 0x4639A0 fills the hit from the
//   missile's stats (0x465420): rate from poisonmindam/poisonmaxdam, length =
//   poisonlength / poison_count when poison_count is above 1 (0x46571C ..
//   0x465739). The missile got those stats when it was made (0x3BF9B0 and
//   0x3C08A0): the skill's own poison length plus the owner's item poison
//   lengths, and only item poison properties add to poison_count. Then
//   0x4639A0 calls the missiles.txt pSrvDmgFunc (table 0x2391270, call at
//   0x463F1D). pSrvDmgFunc 1 (0x453490) moves DmgCalc1 percent (at most 100)
//   of the physical damage into the missile's element through 0x465160, which
//   for poison adds it to the rate (+38h) and leaves the length alone.
//
//   Melee hits. SUNITDMG_FillDamageValues 0x44C030 builds the poison the same
//   way, then runs the skills.txt calc4 conversion the skill put on the hit
//   (+149h element, +14Ch percent): ebx = the converted physical damage
//   (0x44CBBC .. 0x44CC8B), r10b = the element, after element 10 (random)
//   rolled 1..5 (0x44CC9B .. 0x44CCDC). Element 5 adds ebx / 8 to the rate
//   and raises the length to at least 50 frames (0x44CD2E .. 0x44CD4D).
//
//   So the converted damage keeps hitting for whatever length the other
//   poison sources produced: a 3-frame skill poison next to a 250-frame item
//   poison runs for 253 frames with one poison item and (3+250+250)/2 with
//   two.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What the plugin does
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Right after a conversion adds poison to a hit, the plugin sets the hit's
//   poison length to the converting skill's length from the game's own
//   SKILLS_GetElementalLength 0x337DA0 (context, unit, skill, level): ELen +
//   ELevLen1/2/3 by level + ELenSymPerCalc percent, the value the skill's own
//   poison gets. That happens before resistances, so poison length reduction
//   and everything after treat it like any other length. The hit still
//   carries one poison and is applied and replaced exactly as before. A hit
//   that carries converted poison runs all of its poison for that length.
//
//   Missiles: inline hook on pSrvDmgFunc 1 (0x453490). A higher rate after the
//   original means poison was converted. Skill, the way 0x3BF9B0 picks it:
//   when the missiles.txt row is skill driven (+25h & bit 7), the missile's
//   own skill (missile data +0Ch) if above 0, else the row's Skill column
//   (+1B6h); a row that is not skill driven and has no Skill gets no change.
//   Level: the missile's stat 12, which the game sets to the skill level when
//   it makes the missile. Synergies: the missile's owner (0x490300).
//
//   Melee: the call at 0x44CD88, right after the conversion switch, goes
//   through a 20-byte relay that packs r10b (element) and ebx (converted
//   physical damage) into the call's fourth argument. The original callee
//   0x5889E0 takes three. Checked against the stock switch and against the
//   damage-overflow-fix plugin's replacement of 0x44CCDF .. 0x44CD7F, which
//   reads both registers and writes neither. Skill: the one the attacker is
//   using ([[unit+100h]+18h]), level through the loader thunk 0x33D1E0.
//
//   canstack 1: the commit applies each poison item as its own poison and the
//   rest (the skill's poison and the converted damage) with length = hit
//   length minus the items' lengths (0x44D1CF .. 0x44D262). There the plugin
//   sets the hit length to the skill length plus the items' lengths
//   (+40h list, +48h count, 12-byte entries, length at +8), so the rest runs
//   for the skill length.

// 453490: mov [rsp+18h],rbp / push rsi / push rdi / push r14 / sub rsp,30h /
// mov rsi,r9 / mov r14,r8 / mov rdi,rdx / mov rbp,rcx. No relative operand;
// the next instruction (test rdx,rdx at 4534A9) starts on the boundary.

// 44CBA3 .. 44CCDE: movzx r10d,byte [rdi+149h] (element), the converted
// physical damage into ebx along all three overflow-safe branches, physical
// minus that, and the element 10 roll that leaves 1..5 in r10b. Ends right
// before 44CCDF, where the damage-overflow-fix plugin's window starts.

// 41 89 D9          mov   r9d, ebx      converted physical damage
// 41 0F B6 C2       movzx eax, r10b     element
// 48 C1 E0 20       shl   rax, 32
// 49 09 C1          or    r9, rax
// FF 25 00 00 00 00 jmp   [rip]         MeleeConversionHook, slot right after
// rcx, rdx and r8 still hold the original call's three arguments.

// Poison Immunity
//
// Any player or monster that has a configurable state (ESR: state 25,
// "poisonimmunity" in states.txt) is immune to poison:
//   - the poisoned state is never applied to it, and
//   - the poison part of a hit is left out of the life that hit removes.
// Physical, fire, lightning, cold and magic damage from the same hit still
// land normally.
//
// Port of the ESR D2R 2.4 memory patches (state gate hook 321D9D into cave
// 3633D8, life-total hook 32677E into cave 363410) to D2R 3.3 on
// D2RLoader 1.3.0. Everything below was read out of the live D2RLoader.exe
// image and disassembled before being relied on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the 2.4 caves cannot be copied
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Both 2.4 caves tested the state inline, [statlist+0AC8h] & 2000000h.
//   D2RLoader 1.3.0 owns state storage (32,768 states), so this plugin asks
//   the game's own predicate instead. 3.3 makes the same call itself inside
//   the damage calculation, for states 133 and 131:
//
//     44EBA3  45 39 77 3C           cmp  [r15+3Ch], r14d      ; poison length
//     44EBA7  7E 15                 jle  44EBBE
//     44EBA9  BA 85 00 00 00        mov  edx, 85h             ; state 133
//     44EBAE  48 8B CE              mov  rcx, rsi             ; defender
//     44EBB1  E8 FA 65 EE FF        call 3351B0               ; STATES_CheckState
//
//   STATES_CheckState @ 0x3351B0, int32 (unit, stateId), bounds the id first:
//
//     3351DF  48 8B CE              mov  rcx, rsi
//     3351E2  E8 F9 4E 01 00        call 34A0E0               ; data context, unit+1BDh
//     3351E7  0F B6 C8              movzx ecx, al
//     3351EA  E8 A1 B8 FC FF        call 300A90               ; data tables
//     3351EF  48 8B B8 98 02 00 00  mov  rdi, [rax+298h]      ; states count
//     ...                           cmp ebx, edi / jl ok, else assert
//     335281  E9 9A 0F FC FF        jmp  2F6220               ; the state getter
//
//   An id at or past the states count raises an assert inside D2RCore, so the
//   plugin bounds the configured id against that same count before calling
//   the predicate. The 2.4 cave had the same count gate.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Part 1: the poison state, sub_1404518C0 (2.4 sub_140321D70)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   void (attacker, defender, damage, length, source5, source6, source7)
//
//   Called from the damage resolver SUNITDMG_ExecuteEvents 0x44CE80, at
//   44D21A, 44D262 and 44D380, with r8d = [D2Damage+38h] (poison damage) and
//   r9d = [D2Damage+3Ch] (poison length). The hook sits on the entry, so it
//   covers any other caller as well.
//
//     4518C0  45 85 C9              test r9d, r9d             ; length
//     4518C3  0F 8E 61 03 00 00     jle  451C2A               ; the bare ret
//     4518C9  48 8B C4              mov  rax, rsp
//     ...                           push rbp/rsi/r14/r15, sub rsp,68h
//     4518DC  48 8B F2              mov  rsi, rdx             ; defender
//     4518E2  45 85 C0              test r8d, r8d             ; damage
//     4518E5  0F 8E 35 03 00 00     jle  451C20               ; epilogue
//     451903  ...                   GetDataTables(game context)+298h > 2
//     451A35  8D 53 02 48 8B CE 44 8D 43 01 E8 7C 3A EE FF
//                                   STATES_ToggleState(defender, 2, 1)
//     ...                           stat 74 = -damage on the new poison list
//     451C20  48 83 C4 68           add  rsp, 68h
//     451C24  41 5F 41 5E 5E 5D     pop  r15 / r14 / rsi / rbp
//     451C2A  C3                    ret
//
//   The 2.4 gate sat after both early-outs and left through the epilogue, so
//   for every caller it is the same as returning at the entry when damage > 0,
//   length > 0 and the defender has the state. No branch in the function
//   lands inside its first 9 bytes.
//
//   Those 9 bytes start with a relative jle, so they are not handed to
//   InstallInlineHook. They become a jmp to a stub that enters
//   HookedApplyPoison with the function's own ABI. The original is reached
//   through a second stub that replays test r9d,r9d / jle with absolute
//   targets, 451C2A and 4518C9.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Part 2: the life a hit removes, SUNITDMG_CalculateTotalDamage 0x44DF10
// (2.4 sub_140325AF0)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   void (game, attacker, defender, D2Damage*)
//   r15 = D2Damage (44DF54 mov r15,r9) and rsi = defender (44DF5E mov rsi,r8)
//   for the whole function; neither is written again before the epilogue.
//   Component offsets, from the function's own labels:
//     +18h Phys  +20h Fire  +2Ch Ligt  +30h Magc  +34h Cold  +38h Pois  +3Ch Plen
//
//     44EC71  41 8B 47 30           mov  eax, [r15+30h]       ; Magc
//     44EC75  4D 8B CD              mov  r9, r13              ; event 16 args
//     44EC78  41 03 47 34           add  eax, [r15+34h]       ; Cold
//     44EC7C  4C 8B C6              mov  r8, rsi
//     44EC7F  41 03 47 18           add  eax, [r15+18h]       ; Phys
//     44EC83  BA 10 00 00 00        mov  edx, 10h
//     44EC88  41 03 47 2C           add  eax, [r15+2Ch]       ; Ligt
//     44EC8C  41 03 47 38           add  eax, [r15+38h]       ; Pois  <- 8 byte hook
//     44EC90  41 03 47 20           add  eax, [r15+20h]       ; Fire
//     44EC94  48 8B 4C 24 30        mov  rcx, [rsp+30h]       ; resume
//     44EC99  41 89 87 34 01 00 00  mov  [r15+134h], eax      ; life damage total
//
//   SUNITDMG_ExecuteEvents subtracts [D2Damage+134h] from life with a floor
//   of 0 (44D062..44D093), so the poison part of a hit can kill.
//
//   With no poison on the hit, the stub replays the two original instructions
//   verbatim. Otherwise it saves every volatile register, the flags and
//   xmm0-5, asks PoisonHitpointTerm, adds the answer into the saved eax,
//   restores everything and replays add eax,[r15+20h], so rax and the flags
//   on exit match the original instructions exactly. edx, r8 and r9 already
//   hold the event 16 arguments here and come back unchanged. No branch in
//   the function lands inside the window.
//
//   Both stubs were assembled with keystone at their page offsets, decoded
//   back with capstone, and emulated against the original instructions with
//   randomized registers, flags, xmm state and stack alignment.

// 451903: movzx ecx,[rax+106h] / call 300A90 / mov rbx,rax /
// cmp qword [rax+298h],2. Live proof that the states count sits at +298h of
// the data tables.

// +00  45 85 C9                 test r9d, r9d
// +03  7E 0E                    jle  +13h
// +05  FF 25 00 00 00 00        jmp  qword ptr [rip+0]
// +0B  dq                       4518C9
// +13  FF 25 00 00 00 00        jmp  qword ptr [rip+0]
// +19  dq                       451C2A

// +00  FF 25 00 00 00 00        jmp  qword ptr [rip+0]
// +06  dq                       check path, or the plain path
// +0E  41 03 47 38              add  eax, [r15+38h]            ; plain path
// +12  41 03 47 20              add  eax, [r15+20h]
// +16  FF 25 00 00 00 00        jmp  qword ptr [rip+0]
// +1C  dq                       44EC94
// +24  41 83 7F 38 00           cmp  dword ptr [r15+38h], 0    ; check path
// +29  74 E3                    je   +0Eh
// +2B  push rax, rcx, rdx, r8, r9, r10, r11 / pushfq / push rbp
// +38  48 89 E5                 mov  rbp, rsp
// +3B  48 83 E4 F0              and  rsp, -16
// +3F  48 81 EC 80 00 00 00     sub  rsp, 80h
// +46  movaps [rsp+20h..70h], xmm0..xmm5
// +64  48 89 F1                 mov  rcx, rsi                  ; defender
// +67  41 8B 57 38              mov  edx, [r15+38h]            ; poison damage
// +6B  48 B8 dq                 mov  rax, PoisonHitpointTerm
// +75  FF D0                    call rax
// +77  01 45 40                 add  dword ptr [rbp+40h], eax  ; saved eax
// +7A  movaps xmm0..xmm5, [rsp+20h..70h]
// +98  48 89 EC                 mov  rsp, rbp
// +9B  pop rbp / popfq / pop r11, r10, r9, r8, rdx, rcx, rax
// +A8  41 03 47 20              add  eax, [r15+20h]
// +AC  FF 25 00 00 00 00        jmp  qword ptr [rip+0]
// +B2  dq                       44EC94

using ApplyPoisonFn    = void(__fastcall*)(std::uint64_t attacker, std::uint64_t defender,
    std::uint64_t damage, std::uint64_t length, std::uint64_t source5,
    std::uint64_t source6, std::uint64_t source7);

// Reached from the entry stub with the poison applier's own ABI. Every
// argument is carried as a full 64-bit value so the forwarded call is
// bit-identical to the one the resolver made.

// Progressive Stack Calc
//
// Ports the 2.4 "make prgstack work off calc3 instead" pair of memory
// overrides to D2R 3.3, as a plugin with the calc column configurable.
//
// What the original edit actually did (recovered by disassembling the 2.4
// caves rather than trusting their description):
//
//   The hook did NOT replace the prgstack test. It retargeted the existing
//   conditional jump, so the cave ran only on the branch where the prgstack
//   bit was CLEAR. The cave then evaluated the skill's calc3 column and, if
//   the result was > 0, jumped to the fallthrough, i.e. behaved as though
//   prgstack were set. So the real rule is:
//
//       stack the lower stages  <=>  prgstack == 1  OR  calcN > 0
//
//   That is reproduced here exactly. A row that already has prgstack set in
//   skills.txt keeps vanilla behaviour and never reaches this code.
//
// Native background, all of it read out of the live 3.3 image (the dumped
// 140000000.D2RLoader.exe) and disassembled before being relied on here:
//
//   prgstack is a bit column. From the skills.txt binder sub_140302380 its
//   descriptor is type 0x1D (bit), record offset 0x24, bit index 7, and the
//   mask global 0x141D996EC is entry 7 of the power-of-two table at
//   0x141D996D0, so the test is [skillRecord+0x24] & 0x80.
//
//   SERVER, sub_14056C3E0 (progressive release; dispatches srvprgfunc1..3
//   from the word array at skillRecord+80 through the srvdofunc table at
//   0x238EA00):
//     0x56C78D  movzx ecx,byte [rdx+0x24]
//     0x56C791  test  byte [rip+0x182CF55],cl        ; mask 0x80 = prgstack
//     0x56C797  je    0x56C8A7                       ; <- the six bytes moved
//     0x56C79D  fallthrough: the loop that fires stages 1..N-1
//   Live state at 0x56C797: rdx = skills.txt record, rbp+0x30 = game,
//   rbp+0x38 = unit, [rsp+0x5C] = skill id, [rsp+0x4C] = skill level.
//   rbp is a real frame pointer here (prologue: mov rax,rsp / mov [rax+8],rcx
//   / mov [rax+0x10],rdx / push rbp / lea rbp,[rax-0x28]), so rbp+0x30 and
//   rbp+0x38 are the home slots of the game and unit arguments.
//
//   CLIENT, sub_140216380 (the mirror; dispatches cltprgfunc1..3 from the
//   word array at skillRecord+332 through the table at 0x14235F7D0):
//     0x2165D0  movzx ecx,byte [rsi+0x24]
//     0x2165E2  test  byte [rip+0x1B83104],cl
//     0x2165F2  je    0x2166B6                       ; <- the six bytes moved
//     0x2165F8  fallthrough: the loop that fires stages 1..N-1
//   Live state at 0x2165F2: rsi = skills.txt record, rdi = unit,
//   r15b = data context (set at 0x2163EB from sub_14034A0E0, before every
//   path into the gate), [rsp+0xC0] = skill id, [rsp+0xC8] = skill level.
//
//   Both vanilla branch destinations begin with a flag-setting or
//   flag-indifferent instruction (0x56C79D mov ecx,[rsp+0x44]; 0x56C8A7
//   xor edi,edi; 0x2165F8 movsxd r14,r13d; 0x2166B6 movsxd rax,r13d), so
//   restoring the incoming flags on both paths is safe.
//
//   SKILLS_EvaluateFormula @ 0x3B5160, ABI
//     int __fastcall(uint8 dataContext, void* unit, uint32 poolOffset,
//                    int skillId, int skillLevel)
//   It bounds-checks poolOffset against the compiled formula pool itself and
//   returns 0 for anything out of range, so a blank column can never misfire.
//   The stub still tests for 0xFFFFFFFF first and skips the call outright,
//   because a blank column is the common case on this path.
//
//   skills.txt calc column offsets, read from the same binder:
//     calc1 400 (0x190), calc2 404, calc3 408, calc4 412, calc5 416,
//     calc6 420, calc7 424, calc8 428, calc9 432, calc10 436 (0x1B4)
//   Corroborated by Static Field (sub_1405546B0), which passes record+400
//   and record+404 straight to the evaluator.
//
// The six bytes of each "je rel32" are rewritten to a "je rel32" aimed at a
// relay stub allocated within rel32 reach. The stub preserves every general
// purpose register, the stack pointer, the flags and xmm0-5, evaluates the
// configured column, and returns into either the vanilla jump target or the
// vanilla fallthrough by way of a destination slot reserved below its saves.
// The 2.4 cave did not do that: it clobbered ebx on the server side and r11d
// on the client side on the assumption that both were dead.

// 0x56C785, 24 bytes, ending on the gate itself.
//   mov rdx,[rsp+0x50] / mov r15d,eax / movzx ecx,byte [rdx+0x24]
//   test byte [rip+0x182CF55],cl / je 0x56C8A7

// 0x216380 entry, 24 bytes. sub_140216380 prologue.
//   mov rax,rsp / mov [rax+0x20],r9d / mov [rax+0x18],r8d
//   push rbx / push rbp / push rdi / push r13 / sub rsp,0x88

// Builds the relay a retargeted "je" lands in.
//
// Entry contract, proven by the bytes immediately before each gate: on the
// server rdx is the skills.txt record and rbp is the host frame pointer; on
// the client rsi is the record, rdi the unit and r15b the data context. The
// gate is reached with the flags the host produced, and every register in the
// host frame is live, so nothing may be left disturbed.
//
// Shape:
//   reserve a destination slot, save the volatiles, the flags and xmm0-5,
//   read the five evaluator inputs out of the host frame, evaluate the calc
//   column, pick the vanilla fallthrough when the result is > 0 and the
//   vanilla jump target otherwise, write it into the slot, restore
//   everything, and RET into it.
//
// The slot is reserved with LEA rather than SUB on purpose: SUB writes the
// flags, and at that point the host's flags have not been captured yet.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Sampling profiler  -  D2RLoader plugin (diagnostic, touches no game code)
//
// Shows where the game's time actually goes, with no external tool.
//
// While recording, a background thread pauses the game's main thread about
// once a millisecond for a few microseconds, reads where it is, walks its
// call stack, and lets it continue. It does the same for the busiest other
// threads (the renderer, when the renderer is what holds a frame back).
// When the recording ends it writes the report in one go to its own file,
// d2rloader/logs/celestialrayone.sampling-profiler.report.txt (replaced on
// every recording), and puts one line in this plugin's log saying so:
//   * CPU time per thread during the recording, with the thread names;
//   * per sampled thread: the functions found on the stack most often
//     ("inclusive", i.e. that function plus everything it called), the
//     innermost functions ("self", the code that was actually running), and
//     the most common complete call stacks.
// Addresses are printed as module+RVA. D2RLoader.exe RVAs are the same as
// in the IDB, so every line maps straight to a function there.
//
// Cost while recording: each sample holds a thread for a few tens of
// microseconds, a few percent of speed in total. Nothing is sampled when no
// recording is running, and no game code is patched or hooked.
//
// Console
//     profile              record for the default time (15 s), or show the
//                          progress when a recording is running
//     profile <seconds>    record for that many seconds (1 to 120)
//     profile stop         stop early and write the report
//     profile last         the headline of the last report
//
// The report used to go to the plugin log line by line. D2RLoader's logger
// takes about 13 ms per line, so a 10 s recording kept the log growing for
// another 40 s while its 3,000 lines trickled in. One file write instead
// makes the report complete a moment after the recording ends.
//
// Runtime settings live in
// d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.sampling-profiler.toml, created with
// defaults on first load.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Death Penalty Safe Levels
//
// Dying inside a listed level costs nothing: no experience loss and no gold
// loss. Every other level behaves exactly like vanilla. The list is empty by
// default, so installing this plugin without editing its config changes
// nothing at all.
//
// Port of the ESR D2R 2.4 memory patch pair "Skip Exp Loss in Protected
// Levels" (hook 28D9A0 -> 149B5BC cave, 32-byte level bitmap) to D2R 3.3.
// Everything below was read out of the live D2RLoader.exe image and
// disassembled before being relied on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The death penalty, sub_140424AC0 @ RVA 0x424AC0
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   D2Game\src\Player\Player.cpp, asserts on line 978. Windows x64 ABI
//   (Game* rcx, Unit* dyingPlayer rdx, Unit* killer r8) -> int32.
//
//     424AC0  40 53                push  rbx
//     424AC2  56                   push  rsi
//     424AC3  57                   push  rdi
//     424AC4  48 83 EC 40          sub   rsp, 40h        ; 8-byte prologue
//     424AC8  49 8B D8             mov   rbx, r8         ; killer
//     424ACB  48 8B F2             mov   rsi, rdx        ; dying player
//     424ACE  48 8B F9             mov   rdi, rcx        ; game
//     424AD1  E8 DA 11 00 00       call  425CB0          ; the gold stage
//
//   It runs two stages. The gold stage is a separate function in 3.3 (it was
//   inlined in the 2.4 build, which is why sub_14028D9A0 was 555 bytes and
//   this one is 343). The experience stage is the rest of the body:
//
//     - a killer check. GetUnitType(killer) and, for a minion,
//       GetUnitType(GetOwner(killer)): a player kill costs no experience.
//     - difficulty record through 0x300830, DeathExpPenalty at record + 8.
//     - stat 12 (level), and the experience thresholds for level-1 and level
//       through 0x300920, giving the span of the current level.
//     - loss = span * DeathExpPenalty / 100, floored so the player can never
//       drop below the start of the current level.
//     - the write that makes it real:
//
//         424BEE  45 33 C9         xor   r9d, r9d
//         424BF1  44 8B C7         mov   r8d, edi        ; new experience
//         424BF4  48 8B CE         mov   rcx, rsi        ; dying player
//         424BF7  41 8D 51 0D      lea   edx, [r9+0Dh]   ; stat 13, experience
//         424BFB  E8 10 31 ED FF   call  2F7D10          ; STATLIST_SetUnitStat
//
//   Exactly one caller, at 0x42A441, and it discards the return value: the
//   next instruction is xor r8d,r8d for the following call. Returning early
//   is therefore free, which is what the 2.4 cave did with its bare RET.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The gold stage, sub_140425CB0 @ RVA 0x425CB0
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Same three arguments. Reads stat 12 (level), 14 (gold) and 15 (goldbank),
//   applies the classic "lose 20%, capped at level * 500" rule and writes
//   stats 14, 15 and 175. Exactly one caller: the function above.
//
//   The 2.4 patch replaced the whole penalty function with a RET, so it
//   already skipped gold as well as experience. Now that the stages are
//   separate functions the two are separate switches, and gold is left alone
//   by default: dying in a safe level costs experience nothing and gold the
//   usual amount. Set protect_gold to reproduce the 2.4 behaviour exactly.
//
//   When experience is protected and gold is not, the hook cannot simply let
//   the original run, because the original is what takes the experience. It
//   calls the gold stage itself and then returns.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Which level the dying player is in
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Identical chain to 2.4, and each step is verified against the accessor
//   that performs it rather than trusted from a note:
//
//     UNITS_GetRoom            0x34B440   (unit) -> ActiveRoom*
//     DUNGEON_GetLevelIdFromRoom 0x2EFC10  mov rcx,[rcx+18h]; jmp 360FC0
//     DRLGROOM_GetLevelId      0x360FC0   mov rax,[rcx+90h]; mov eax,[rax+1F8h]
//
//   So ActiveRoom + 0x18 -> DrlgRoom, DrlgRoom + 0x90 -> Level, Level + 0x1F8
//   -> level id. Those last two bodies are byte-checked at load, which means
//   the three offsets below are verified against the image, not assumed.
//
//   The walk is done inline with a null check at every step, exactly like the
//   cave did, rather than calling 0x2EFC10: DRLGROOM_GetLevelId dereferences
//   its DrlgRoom and its Level without checking either.
//
//   The 2.4 bitmap was 32 bytes because level ids were treated as 0..255.
//   In 3.3 the Levels.txt record-count guard at 0x330446 compares against
//   0x400, so ids run 0..1023 and the set here is sized for that.

// The experience write itself. This is what proves 0x424AC0 is the function
// that takes experience away, and that it takes it from argument two.
// xor r9d,r9d / mov r8d,edi / mov rcx,rsi / lea edx,[r9+0Dh] / call 2F7D10

// mov [rsp+10h],rbx / push rbp / push rsi / push rdi / push r12 / push r13 /
// push r14 / push r15 / sub rsp,20h / mov rdi,rdx / mov r12,r8 /
// xor r8d,r8d / mov r13,rbp

// Installed only when protect_gold is on. It is reached only when the
// experience hook let the original run, since that hook returns before the
// gold stage is ever called.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// psrvhitfunc-29-fixes
//
// Engine fixes for Missiles.txt pSrvHitFunc 29, the burst Frozen Orb and
// everything built on it uses, each behind its own switch:
//
//   frozen_orb_burst_on_wall      a missile that dies on a wall bursts
//   burst_missile_survives_miss   a missile that misses its to-hit roll flies
//                                 on instead of being deleted
//
// They correct engine bugs that are not crashes, so they do change gameplay:
// they make the engine apply what its own data already says.
//
// Target: D2RLoader 1.3.1, which runs Diablo II: Resurrected 3.3 inside
// D2RLoader.exe and moves part of the engine into D2RCore.dll.
//
// Split out of engine-stability 0.12.0, which also carried a skill-freeze AI
// resume fix. That one is not carried over: D2RLoader handles it.
//
// Safety model: every game address below is an RVA against image base
// 0x140000000, and nothing is installed unless the bytes already at that RVA
// match byte for byte. On any build the plugin does not recognise it installs
// nothing, logs loudly and stays loaded so the console command can explain why.
// An unrecognised build is therefore a no-op, never a mis-patch.
//
// Configuration: every fix has an on/off switch in
// <scope>\d2rloader\config\celestialrayone.psrvhitfunc-29-fixes.toml, all true
// by default. The file is created with documented defaults on first run. The
// config is read once at load, so edits need a game restart. A fix that the
// config turned off and a fix the build refused are reported differently by
// the console command -- they are not the same thing and must never look it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// True when `prefix` is exactly the leading bytes of `body`. Used to prove at
// compile time that the bytes an inline hook displaces really are the first
// bytes of the body the plugin verified.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Fix 1: the burst hit function never runs when a missile dies on a wall
//        (port of the 2.4 fix at 3D1DCC)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   sub_14045FB50 is missiles.txt pSrvHitFunc 29, the burst Frozen Orb and
//   everything built on it uses: it spawns a ring of the missile's
//   HitSubMissile around the missile. Being a hit function, the engine calls it
//   for every collision the missile has, and the burst is only meant to happen
//   once, at the end of the missile's life. It tells those apart by the frame
//   counter:
//
//     0045FB91  call 00166040             missiles.txt record
//     0045FBA2  cmp  word ptr [rax+46h], 0  HitSubMissile
//     0045FBA7  jl   0045FD00             none: nothing to spawn, return 1
//     0045FBAD  mov  rcx, rbx             the missile
//     0045FBB0  call 003BB1E0             frames left = ceil(countdown / 40)
//     0045FBB5  test eax, eax
//     0045FBB7  je   0045FBC3             none left: burst
//     0045FBB9  mov  eax, 2               still flying: refuse
//     0045FBBE  jmp  0045FD05             and leave
//
//   The missile step 00463100 decrements that counter every update and only
//   resolves the hit once it has run out, so a missile that lives out its Range
//   arrives here with zero frames left and bursts. A missile stopped by a wall
//   does not: the step sees the collision and resolves the hit with frames
//   still on the clock, so the counter test refuses. The missile dies silently
//   against the wall and the whole ring, with all the damage it carries, is
//   lost.
//
//   The counter is not the right question. The step already says what happened,
//   in the third argument it hands to the hit path: a unit for a collision with
//   a unit, null for a wall and for running out of range.
//
//     004631F0  ResolveHit(game, missile, 0, 1)      out of frames
//     004633D5  ResolveHit(game, missile, 0, 1)      wall
//     004633F2  ResolveHit(game, missile, unit, 0)   unit found in the cell
//
//   ResolveHit 004639A0 passes that argument straight through to the hit
//   function in r8, at both of its dispatch sites and at the third one in
//   00464AC0. So what the frame counter is really being asked is "is this a
//   passing hit on a unit", and the argument answers it exactly.
//
//   The fix: refuse the burst only when there are frames left AND the hit is
//   against a unit. A mid-flight unit collision behaves exactly as before, the
//   end of Range behaves exactly as before, and a wall now bursts.
//
//   r8 does not survive to the counter test: 0045FB7D loads it with an assert
//   line number. So it is spilled first, into [rsp+20h]. That slot is the head
//   of the missile creation block this function builds later; nothing reads or
//   writes it until 0045FBEC zeroes it, which is past both patched sites, and
//   the three calls in between can only touch the shadow space at
//   [rsp+00h..1Fh].
//
//   The spill site is rewritten in place, 6 bytes:
//
//     0045FB6D  E9 <rel32>                jmp  relay page + B0h
//     0045FB72  90                        never executed
//
//   and its relay stub does the two displaced moves, the spill, and rejoins:
//
//     +00  48 8B DA                       mov  rbx, rdx            ; stock
//     +03  48 8B E9                       mov  rbp, rcx            ; stock
//     +06  4C 89 44 24 20                 mov  [rsp+20h], r8       ; the hit unit
//     +0B  FF 25 00 00 00 00 <abs64>      jmp  0045FB73
//
//   The counter test is rewritten in place, 14 bytes:
//
//     0045FBB5  E9 <rel32>                jmp  relay page + D0h
//     0045FBBA  90 x9                     never executed
//
//   and its relay stub adds the second test:
//
//     +00  85 C0                          test eax, eax
//     +02  74 1B                          je   +1Fh                ; no frames left: burst
//     +04  48 83 7C 24 20 00              cmp  qword ptr [rsp+20h], 0
//     +0A  74 13                          je   +1Fh                ; no unit: wall or expiry, burst
//     +0C  B8 02 00 00 00                 mov  eax, 2              ; stock refuse value
//     +11  FF 25 00 00 00 00 <abs64>      jmp  0045FD05            ; stock refuse exit
//     +1F  FF 25 00 00 00 00 <abs64>      jmp  0045FBC3            ; burst
//
//   The refuse path jumps to the function's own epilogue rather than to
//   0045FBB9, because 0045FBB9 is inside the replaced region and is a NOP after
//   the patch; landing there would slide through the NOPs into the burst. Stock
//   0045FBB9 is mov eax,2 / jmp 0045FD05, so setting eax in the stub and going
//   straight to 0045FD05 is the same thing.
//
//   eax is dead on the burst path: stock reaches 0045FBC3 with eax zero from
//   the counter, the fix can reach it with the counter still set, and 0045FBC3
//   is mov rdx,rbx / mov rcx,rbp / call, which rewrites it. The flags are dead
//   at both destinations. Neither stub touches rsp, so the call inside the
//   function is made on exactly the alignment it had before.
//
//   The whole function, 0045FB50..0045FD1F, is verified before patching, which
//   also proves the counter call, both stubs' destinations and the refuse
//   value. Decoded end to end: the only branch targets inside it are 0045FBC3,
//   0045FC90, 0045FCDD, 0045FD00 and 0045FD05, so nothing lands in either
//   replaced region, and 0045FBC3's only source is the je being replaced.

// Where the hit unit is parked between the two sites. Third home slot of the
// shadow space this function reserves for its own calls, and the first dword of
// the missile creation block it fills from 0045FBEC.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Fix 2: a pSrvHitFunc 29 missile is deleted when its to-hit roll misses
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Missile hit resolution 004639A0 rolls to hit before anything else for a
//   row with ToHit. On a miss it never reads CollideKill: the missile is
//   spent, the way a missed arrow is spent, unless the row has AlwaysExplode
//   and the hit function, run once for the miss, answers bit 4 or bit 8:
//
//     00463C2C  cmp  byte ptr [r13+1B3h], dil   ToHit
//     00463C5F  call 0044BA60                   the to-hit roll
//     00463C66  jne  00463D8F                   hit: the normal path
//     00463D1C  cmp  byte ptr [r13+1B4h], dil   miss: AlwaysExplode
//     00463D52  call r9                         the row's pSrvHitFunc
//     00463D55  test al, 0Ch                    4 or 8: keep the missile
//     00463D59  mov  dword ptr [rsp+48h], 2     else return 2
//
//   and the missile dispatcher 00466CE0 deletes a missile whose step returns
//   2. A hit reads the same answer bit by bit instead:
//
//     00463E4E  test al, 4      absorbed: no damage, keep the missile
//     00463E8A  test dil, 2     deal the damage
//     0046400F  test dil, 1     kill the missile
//
//   Bit 8 is read by the miss branch only, so it means "keep me after a miss"
//   and nothing else.
//
//   pSrvHitFunc 29 answers a mid-flight contact with a unit with 2: no burst
//   yet, deal the damage, fly on. A hit reads that as fly on; a miss finds
//   neither 4 nor 8 and deletes the missile. So a burst missile that misses
//   dies on the spot with no damage and no burst, while the same missile
//   hitting flies on and bursts at the end of its Range. Frozen Orb never
//   rolls to hit, which is why vanilla never showed it.
//
//   The fix: the mid-flight refuse answers 0Ah (2 plus 8) instead of 2. A hit
//   never reads bit 8, so it is exactly as before. A miss now keeps the
//   missile, and it bursts where it would have anyway. The burst answer (3)
//   and the nothing-to-spawn answer (1) are untouched, so a miss on the
//   missile's last frame still bursts and ends it. The hit resolution itself
//   is not touched, and the miss has already stamped LastCollide (00463ABD)
//   before the roll, so with LastCollide set the missed unit is not rolled
//   again by the same missile.
//
//   Where the refuse lives decides where the value is written:
//     burst-on-wall fix installed      the relay gate stub, +0Ch mov eax, 2,
//                                      written when the relay page is built
//     burst-on-wall fix not installed  stock 0045FBB9 B8 02 00 00 00
//   0045FBB9 sits inside the 14 bytes the burst-on-wall fix replaces, so the
//   two are never written at the same time.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relay page: the frozen orb fix
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Both frozen orb sites sit in the middle of pSrvHitFunc 29, where the loader's
// inline hook cannot go: the bytes they replace include a branch or call, which
// does not relocate. Each site is rewritten in place to reach one page
// allocated within rel32 reach of the image:
//
//   +00h  frozen orb hit-unit spill stub, native code only
//   +40h  frozen orb burst gate stub, native code only
//
// The page is never freed. A thread may be jumping through it at any time, and
// the native stubs need nothing from this DLL.

// Superior Affixes (celestialrayone.superior-affixes)
//
// Replaces the stock superior affix picker (qualityitems.txt) with one that
// picks by the row's frequency, removes its 10-row limit, and adds optional
// frequency, itype and etype columns to qualityitems.txt. Nothing in the save
// format changes.
//
// Target: D2RLoader 1.3.1 running D2R 3.3. Every address and byte below was
// read out of the dumped D2RLoader.exe image (md5 8af7a47c...) and decoded
// before being relied on. RVAs are against image base 0x140000000.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. The stock picker, 0x58B9E0 .. 0x58BBA0 (only caller: the wrapper 0x58A5A0,
//    which tail-jumps into it; it is the D2MOO 1.10 sub_6FC54690)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   58BA09  call 34A0E0                 ; item -> data context (bank)
//   58BA15  call 313DE0                 ; bank -> qualityitems table {rows, count}
//   58BA2D  call 349860                 ; item class id
//   58BA38  call 314110                 ; weapons/armor/misc record
//   58BA52  mov  rbx, [rbx+8]           ; count = qualityitems row count
//   58BA5B  mov  [rsp+38h], rax / mov [rsp+40h], ax   ; the "tried" list: 10 bytes
//   58BA65  call 374710                 ; itemtypes.txt Throwable of the item's own type
//   58BA6E  cmp  [rdi+122h], al         ; items record nodurability
//   58BA76  mov  ebx, 4                 ; either one: only rows 0..3
//   58BAA0  call 36CC80                 ; item seed {low, high}
//   58BAB5  imul r8, rax, 6AC690C5h / add r8, rcx ; low*0x6AC690C5 + high
//   58BAD8  div  ebx                    ; row = low % count
//   58BAF8  cmp  byte [rsp+38h+row], 0 / jnz reroll
//   58BB03  call 313D70                 ; bank, row -> record (44 bytes)
//   58BB16  call 3D40D0                 ; may this item use the record?
//   58BB21  mov  byte [rsp+38h+row], 1  ; mark tried, reroll
//   58BB6F  call 36DC80                 ; store the row number on the item
//   58BB89  call 3D3670 (1, 0, item, record, 0, 0)    ; apply mod1, then mod2
//   58BB94  call 58A050 (item, drop)    ; itemtypes.txt StaffMods
//
//   The tried list is 10 bytes at [rsp+38h]; [rsp+48h] is the stack cookie and
//   [rsp+50h] up the saved r15, r14, r13, r12, rdi, rsi, rbp and the return
//   address. Row 10 and up therefore reads uninitialised padding (rows 10-15),
//   the cookie (16-23) and the saved registers (24+): a non-zero byte makes
//   the row unpickable, and a rejected row writes 1 into the cookie (fast-fail
//   crash on return) or into a caller's saved register.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Why no save change is needed
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   - 313D70 (row -> record) and 313DE0 (table) have exactly one caller each,
//     this picker; their other references are .pdata unwind entries. So no
//     code turns a stored row number back into affixes.
//   - 3D3670 type 1 writes the rolled values into the item's own magic stat
//     list (state 0, flag 0x40); the item serializer 37D140 saves that list
//     with every other item stat (loop at 37EF05, writer call 37F093).
//   - The row number itself is written as 3 bits clamped to 7 (37DB7B..37DBA0,
//     shared with low quality) and read back as 3 bits. It is a label nothing
//     reads, so rows past 7 keep their affixes through any save and reload.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Stock eligibility, 3D40D0 (unchanged, called as is)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   own type = items record type, 372C90
//   weapon column: CheckItemType(item, 45) and own type not 24..27 or 35
//   armor column:  CheckItemType(item, 50) and own type not 2, 15, 16, 19
//   then exact own type: 2 shield, 24 scepter, 25 wand, 26 staff, 27 and 35
//   bow, 15 boots, 16 gloves, 19 belt. It reads only the item and the record.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. frequency / itype / etype columns
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   CheckItemType is 373890 (item, itemtypes index): true when the item's
//   items.txt type (record+302) or type2 (record+304) has the index set in the
//   itemtypes inheritance matrix (data tables +4968, stride +4960, count
//   +4944). That is the test magic affixes use for their itype/etype columns
//   (descriptor type 15 resolved through the itemtypes linker at data tables
//   +0x1340, see the rareprefix/raresuffix loader 31B230).
//
//   The stock qualityitems loader 315300 passes a fixed descriptor list to the
//   txt parser (j_LoadExcelTable, record size 44) and the parser skips every
//   column not in that list. So the extra columns are read by this plugin, on
//   D2RLoader's tables-loaded event (game thread), from the same
//   qualityitems.txt the game loaded:
//     - candidates are <mod>\data\global\excel\[base\]qualityitems.txt and the
//       same under <mod>\<mod>.mpq\, from the context's mod and scope folders;
//     - a candidate is used for a table bank only when it matches what the game
//       compiled for that bank row by row: same row count, the ten item-class
//       bytes (record 0..9) and mod1/mod2 param, min, max (record 16, 20, 24,
//       32, 36, 40), each parsed like the game parses them;
//     - codes are resolved with the data table service's ItemTypes lookup in
//       that bank;
//     - the result is bound to that bank's compiled rows pointer, which is the
//       table 313DE0 hands the picker, so the right set is used per item.
//   Anything that does not match is reported and simply not used.
//
//   The frequency column (named after, and working like, the column of
//   magicprefix.txt and magicsuffix.txt) is read from that same matched file,
//   row by row, so a frequency can only ever land on the row it was written
//   on. Empty or 0 means the row never rolls. A bank with no matched file, or
//   a matched file with no frequency column, gives every row the same odds
//   (stock).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. The hook
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The whole body is verified byte for byte (it pins every callee below),
//   then an inline hook on its first instruction (5 bytes, mov [rsp+18h],rbx)
//   sends every call to PickSuperiorAffix, which never calls the original.
//   PickSuperiorAffix does what the stock body does, in the same order and
//   through the same native calls, except for the row choice:
//     - no tried list: two passes over the rows instead, the first summing
//       the frequencies of every row the item may use, the second walking to
//       the rolled one; eligibility only reads, so asking twice changes
//       nothing;
//     - one seed roll per Superior item (the stock picker rolls once per
//       attempt), with the stock generator: low*0x6AC690C5 + high, then
//       low % total;
//     - when no row is usable the stock picker returns 0 without applying
//       anything, and so does this one.
//   Frequencies are 0..65535 and rows at most 65535, so the total fits 32 bits.

// CheckItemType 373890: mov [rsp+10h],rbx / mov [rsp+18h],rbp / mov [rsp+20h],rsi /
// push rdi, r14, r15 / sub rsp,20h / movsxd rsi,edx / mov rdi,rcx / test rcx,rcx /
// jz ... (the item null check).

using AssignPropertiesFn      = std::uint8_t(*)(std::int32_t mode, void* unit, void* item,
                                    const void* record, std::uint32_t propertySet,
                                    std::int32_t applyType);

// The odds of `row` in this table: its frequency cell, or the same for every
// row when no qualityitems.txt matched the table or the matched file has no
// frequency column (stock odds).

// Rerolling Orb Storing
//
// A hotkey that feeds matching rings, amulets, jewels, charms and quivers into
// a Rerolling Orb through the Horadric Cube, one transmute per item. 3.3 port
// of the ESR-utils 2.4 rerolling orb pass. On a controller, an Autostore
// button on the inventory panel starts the same run. Nothing is clicked: the
// plugin sends the game's own item and transmute requests, and the server
// checks every one of them.
//
// Everything below was read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image (D2R
// 3.3, base 0x140000000) and every function used is checked byte for byte at
// load. Nothing is installed when a check fails. The plugin installs no hooks
// and writes no game memory.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Requests used (client builders, server handlers from the table 1D2A790)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   0x19 pick up      Send17(0x19, item, x, y, page)     server 4AA100
//                     a stored item of the player onto the cursor
//   0x18 put down     Send17(0x18, item, x, y, page)     server 4BFF30
//                     the cursor item into a player page at an exact cell
//   0x46 shared take  Send13(0x46, item, owner, xy)      server 4C6480
//   0x45 shared put   Send13(0x45, item, owner, xy)      server 4C5570
//                     the same two for a shared stash tab; owner is the tab's
//                     auxiliary player (state 186, owned by the local player)
//   0x20 transmute    ED120(cube, cubePage, cubeXY, records, end)
//                     server 4C1590 -> 4F2490
//   Send17 = 0EC7D0 (17 bytes, opcode + 4 dwords), Send13 = 0EC780 (13 bytes,
//   opcode + 3 dwords). The native callers are 2C7080 (pick up: 0x19, or 0x46
//   with the selected tab's owner) and 15F8B0 (put down: 0x18, or 0x45).
//   Here the owner is always the owner the item actually lives in, so no stash
//   tab has to be selected.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Why one item at a time
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The server transmute (4F2490) refuses unless the cursor is empty and the
//   request lists exactly the items in the cube, each at its cell, sorted by
//   row then column (strictly increasing x | y << 16). It then does not
//   transmute on the spot: it returns 4 and 4C1590 queues the work on a game
//   job (vtable 1D24730, run 4ADAD0) that re-runs the same check later. Moving
//   item 2 into the cube before item 1's job ran would break both checks, so
//   each item waits until the client sees it consumed. Per item the plugin
//   sends pick up + put down + transmute together; the transmute lists the
//   orb and the item at the cell the put down used.
//   Record format read from ED120: qword = guid | x << 32 | y << 48, sent as
//   guid + one byte (x & 0xF) | y << 4.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Finding items
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Player items: 34A360 (inventory) -> 388C10 / 38ABA0, page from 36CFE0
//   (0 inventory, 3 cube, 4 personal stash). Shared stash tabs: the player's
//   inventory keeps a list of auxiliary owners (388E00, next 38CD70, active
//   byte 38CD50, owner id 2EF880, unit from 09A5D0 with type 0); 15F580 says
//   whether one is a shared stash tab owner of the local player, and 46DA50
//   names the Advanced Stash owner, which is left out. Their items sit on
//   page 4.
//   Quality 36CF60 (ItemData +0), base code 36EF50 ("box " is the cube), type
//   check 373890 (item, itemtypes row) with the game's equivalence table, so
//   child types match. Rows come from the SDK data-table service by code.
//   Free cube cell: 3865B0 (inventory, item, grid 34A410(player, 3,
//   08D3B0() == 0), &x, &y, 3), as 15F8B0 does. Cell of an item: 34A110,
//   x | y << 16. Cursor item: 388A70.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Threads
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The hotkey is caught on the game window (a window procedure subclass, as
//   ESR-utils did in 2.4) and only queues work. Item type rows are looked up on
//   the game thread (the data-table service requires it), then the run itself
//   steps once per UI update on the UI thread, where the client units and the
//   request builders live. The controller button's click (section 5) queues
//   the same work as the hotkey.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. The controller button
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   An Autostore button in the controller inventory. The button itself is
//   part of the mod's controller inventory layout (autostore_button), so its
//   place, size and d-pad links are set there. The plugin hands D2RCore the
//   button's art as plugin resources: the Rerolling Orb (ESR's reroll1
//   inventory art) in the frame of the Transfer to Stash controller button,
//   4 frames of 82x82 (41x41 lowend), frame 0 normal, 1 pressed, 2 disabled,
//   3 hovered.
//
//   A press. A on the focused button sends its click message; the plugin's
//   UI message listener consumes it and starts the same run as the hotkey.
//   When a service is missing or a registration fails, the hotkey still
//   works.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

// The next step runs on the next UI update. A callback cannot queue itself on
// its own thread, so each step hands over to the game thread, which queues the
// next UI step (the same relay Stance Dance uses).

// Respec Counter
//
// [d2rcore.player] enable_respec turns on the game's own respec shortcut:
// Alt + click a stat button resets every stat and skill point. This plugin
// puts a counter on that shortcut, saved on the character, and shows the
// count in the stat button's tooltip.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The counter
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The character's own value (base value, layer 0) of itemstatcost row
//   stat_id (237 by default). A new character starts with starting_respecs
//   (5). Each shortcut respec costs 1; at 0 the shortcut does nothing. The
//   plugin never adds to it: the Token of Absolution's misc.txt pSpell does.
//
//   D2RLoader's extended character save (D2RCore WritePlayerStats and
//   ReadPlayerStats) writes every own stat whose CSvBits is 1 to 32 with a
//   16-bit stat id and reads it back, so the counter persists when its row
//   has CSvBits above 0.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The shortcut (host)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The client sends opcode 39h: one opcode byte, then a dword NPC unit id.
//   The server callback 4B2530 (game, player, packet, size) copies the packet,
//   and when the id is -1 and the game flags at game+5658h have bit 800000h
//   (what enable_respec sets) it skips every NPC and quest check, calls the
//   respec 580F20 and returns 0. Any other id is Akara's quest respec, which
//   this plugin leaves alone. With the flag off, -1 fails the NPC lookup and
//   the callback returns 1 without a respec.
//
//   The hook reads the counter before the original runs. 0 or less: refused,
//   the original never runs and nothing changes. Otherwise the original runs
//   and, when it returns 0, the counter is written back one lower. The respec
//   only resets skills (refunded to stat 5) and stats 0 to 3 (refunded to
//   stat 4), so it never touches the counter.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// New characters (host)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   52D770 (game, player, act) writes a brand-new character's starting stats
//   from charstats.txt. Its only two callers, 420070 and 5355B0, allocate a
//   new player unit and then hand out the starting stats, items and skill.
//   The hook runs the original, then writes starting_respecs.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The tooltip (client)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The stat button is a CharacterStatLevelUpButtonWidget (vtable 1FDD938,
//   stored by its factory at 14EE1C8). Its tooltip is the layout's
//   tooltipString, resolved once at load into a game string inside the
//   widget: text pointer at +88h, length at +90h. 875980 draws a hovered
//   widget's tooltip by handing that pointer to the tooltip dispatcher
//   (875A44), which copies the text. For this widget only, the hook swaps in
//   a copy with the first %d replaced by the local player's counter for the
//   length of the call, then puts the original pointer and length back.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Native pieces, D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image. Every one is checked byte for
// byte at load, before any hook is installed.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   4B2530  opcode 39h server callback, hooked
//   4B259F  its shortcut test: cmp eax, -1 / test dword [rdi+5658h], 800000h
//   52D770  new character starting stats, hooked
//   875980  hovered widget tooltip draw, hooked
//   875A44  mov rcx, [rdi+88h]: the tooltip text it hands on
//   14EE19A mov edx, 0B90h: CharacterStatLevelUpButtonWidget object size
//   14EE1C8 lea rax, [1FDD938] / mov dword [rbx+0B88h], 0 / mov [rbx], rax
//   2F48C0  own value read (unit, stat, layer), loader thunk into D2RCore
//   2F7D10  own value write (unit, stat, value, layer), loader thunk
//   8B2D0   local player index        9A480   local player from that index

// 4B2530 prologue, the bytes the hook displaces: push rbp / push rbx /
// push rsi / push rdi / push r14 / lea rbp, [rsp-37h] / sub rsp, 0C0h.
// Nothing relative; the next instruction is the rip-relative cookie load.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

// The two host hooks go in together: a gate without the starting value would
// lock every new character out, a starting value without the gate would be
// meaningless. The tooltip hook is separate.

// Restrict State Block (celestialrayone.restrict-state-block)
//
// Adds a fourth value to the skills.txt "restrict" column:
//
//   restrict 4   the skill cannot be used while the unit has any of the states
//                named in State1, State2 or State3. Empty State columns are
//                skipped. At every other time the skill is usable, exactly as
//                with restrict 1. The blocking states do not need the
//                states.txt restrict flag, so nothing else in the game changes
//                when they are applied, and value 4 never strips a state.
//
// Value 4 was inert before this plugin: every reader of the column treats any
// value other than 0 and 2 as "no check", and the only state-stripping reader
// compares against 3 exactly. Everything below was read out of the live
// D2RLoader.exe image (D2R 3.3) and disassembled before being relied on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The column
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   skills.txt loader 0x302380 builds its column descriptors inline:
//     30692B / 306936   "restrict"  type 4 (byte)          record +2D0h
//     306948 / 306953   "state1"    type 16h (state name)  record +2D2h
//     306965 / 306970   "state2"    type 16h               record +2D4h
//     306982 / 30698D   "state3"    type 16h               record +2D6h
//   states.txt loader 0x3074D0 makes the states.txt "restrict" column flag
//   bit 18 (3079CA); its group mask is DataTables+358h, the mask 0x336250
//   ("unit has any restrict-flagged state") tests.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Readers of the column, and what value 4 does in each today
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   0x33F360  SKILLS_GetUseState(unit, skill) -> use state. Used by the client
//             (skill button tint, cast refusal and "Impossible" voice line)
//             and the server (player skill command validation). The restrict
//             block returns use state 4 on refusal:
//
//     33F496  48 8B 1F              mov   rbx, [rdi]            ; skills record
//     33F499  48 85 DB              test  rbx, rbx
//     33F49C  74 52                 je    33F4F0
//     33F49E  0F B6 8B D0 02 00 00  movzx ecx, byte [rbx+2D0h]  ; restrict
//     33F4A5  85 C9                 test  ecx, ecx
//     33F4A7  74 51                 je    33F4FA                ; 0: any restrict state refuses
//     33F4A9  83 E9 01              sub   ecx, 1                ; <- hook window, 10 bytes
//     33F4AC  74 58                 je    33F506                ; 1: carry on
//     33F4AE  83 F9 01              cmp   ecx, 1
//     33F4B1  75 53                 jne   33F506                ; 3, 4, 5...: carry on
//     33F4B3  48 8B CE              mov   rcx, rsi              ; 2: the State1-3 whitelist
//     ...                                                       ;    loop, calls 3351B0
//     33F4F0  B8 04 00 00 00        mov   eax, 4                ; refused
//     33F4F5  E9 28 FF FF FF        jmp   33F422                ; epilogue
//     33F4FA  ...                                               ; restrict 0
//     33F506  48 8B CE              mov   rcx, rsi              ; carry on with the next gates
//
//   0x33F6A0  the same check by skill id, (unit, skillId) -> bool. Its only
//             caller is SKILLITEM_HandleItemEffectSkill 0x589930 (item procs),
//             at 589A1C, and only when the row has ItemUseRestrict = 1:
//
//     33F6D0  0F B6 90 D0 02 00 00  movzx edx, byte [rax+2D0h]  ; restrict
//     33F6D7  85 D2                 test  edx, edx
//     33F6D9  74 69                 je    33F744                ; 0
//     33F6DB  83 EA 01              sub   edx, 1                ; <- hook window, 10 bytes
//     33F6DE  74 52                 je    33F732                ; 1: usable
//     33F6E0  83 EA 01              sub   edx, 1
//     33F6E3  75 4D                 jne   33F732                ; 3, 4, 5...: usable
//     33F6E5  48 8B CE              mov   rcx, rsi              ; 2: whitelist loop
//     33F720  32 C0 ...             xor   al, al / epilogue     ; refused
//     33F732  B0 01 ...             mov   al, 1 / epilogue      ; usable
//
//   0x436480 (server skill start, 4365FE) and 0x218030 (client, 218085) strip
//             every restrict-flagged state only when the byte is exactly 3.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The hooks
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Each 10-byte window becomes a jmp into a stub in a private region next to
//   the image, the rest NOPed. The window starts and ends on instruction
//   boundaries, is reached only by falling through from the je above it, and
//   no branch in either function lands inside it.
//
//   A stub starts with a dispatch through an 8-aligned qword:
//     armed     value 4 runs the new check, 2 goes to the stock whitelist, any
//               other value carries on, as stock;
//     vanilla   exactly the replaced instructions: 2 goes to the whitelist,
//               anything else carries on. The plugin points the dispatch here
//               on unload, with one atomic store, before restoring the window.
//   The value-4 check walks State1-3, skips a negative (empty) entry, and calls
//   the game's own STATES_CheckState 0x3351B0(unit, state), the function the
//   stock whitelist loop calls, so states above 511 work through whatever
//   relocation is installed. A state the unit has leaves through the stock
//   refusal (33F4F0 / 33F720); none leaves through the stock carry-on
//   (33F506 / 33F732).
//
//   Register use, checked against both functions:
//     use check   rsi = unit, rdi = skill, rbx = record, r14 = 0 (must stay 0,
//                 it is read at 33F515 and 33F58A). The stub uses ebp as the
//                 index and keeps rbx; the stock whitelist loop clobbers both,
//                 and both are dead on every exit (reloaded at 33F569/33F582/
//                 33F659 or restored by the epilogue). ecx and the flags are
//                 dead on every exit.
//     item check  rsi = unit, rdi = record, rbx = the skill id argument, dead
//                 here (the stock loop uses it as its index too). edx and the
//                 flags are dead on every exit.
//   Both functions keep rsp 16-aligned with 20h bytes of home space below
//   their saved registers, and call 3351B0 from exactly that frame, so the
//   stubs call it from the same frame without touching rsp.
//
//   Unwind data: the stubs run inside the game function's frame, so the region
//   registers UNWIND_INFO that describes that frame (the same codes as the
//   game's own chained entries 33F438-33F68E -> 33F360, and 33F6A0), and a
//   stack walk through a stub reaches the game function's caller correctly.
//
//   The stubs were assembled with keystone, decoded back with capstone, and
//   emulated with unicorn over the original 826 and 193 bytes: restrict 0-5
//   and 255, every State1-3 combination of {empty, 0, 139, 140, 794}, with and
//   without a restrict-flagged state, against six unit state sets (10,500
//   cases, 63,000 runs). Vanilla matches the stock code exactly in exit, call
//   sequence and registers for every value; armed matches it for every value
//   but 4, and for 4 refuses exactly when a listed state is present, with the
//   stack aligned at every call and rsi, rdi, rsp and r12-r15 preserved.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Other plugins
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   petmax-fix hooks the entry of 0x33F360 and crossbow-charges the cooldown
//   gate 0x3404A0 it calls. Neither touches these windows, and no witness here
//   covers the entry of 0x33F360.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Hook region
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   One allocation within rel32 reach of both windows, never freed:
//     +0000  code page, PAGE_EXECUTE_READ
//              +010  UNWIND_INFO for the use check stub
//              +028  UNWIND_INFO for the item check stub
//              +040  RUNTIME_FUNCTION table, 2 entries
//              +100  use check stub
//              +200  item check stub
//     +1000  counters page, PAGE_READWRITE, written by the stubs
//   The stubs read and write nothing but the game and this region, so they
//   never depend on this DLL being loaded.

// The use check stub, at region +100 (offsets below are from the stub start):
//   +00  66 90                    nop, puts the dispatch qword on an 8-byte boundary
//   +02  FF 25 00 00 00 00        jmp  qword [rip+0]
//   +08  dq                       dispatch: +21 armed, +10 vanilla
//   +10  83 F9 02                 cmp  ecx, 2                    ; vanilla
//   +13  74 06                    je   +1B
//   +15  FF 25 ..                 jmp  qword [+80]               ; 33F506 carry on
//   +1B  FF 25 ..                 jmp  qword [+88]               ; 33F4B3 whitelist
//   +21  83 F9 04                 cmp  ecx, 4                    ; armed
//   +24  74 07                    je   +2D
//   +26  83 F9 02                 cmp  ecx, 2
//   +29  74 F0                    je   +1B
//   +2B  EB E8                    jmp  +15
//   +2D  F0 48 FF 05 ..           lock inc qword [counters+00]
//   +35  31 ED                    xor  ebp, ebp
//   +37  0F BF 94 6B D2 02 00 00  movsx edx, word [rbx+rbp*2+2D2h] ; State1+i
//   +3F  85 D2                    test edx, edx
//   +41  78 0D                    js   +50                       ; empty: skip
//   +43  48 89 F1                 mov  rcx, rsi                  ; unit
//   +46  FF 15 ..                 call qword [+98]               ; 3351B0
//   +4C  85 C0                    test eax, eax
//   +4E  75 09                    jne  +59
//   +50  FF C5                    inc  ebp
//   +52  83 FD 03                 cmp  ebp, 3
//   +55  72 E0                    jb   +37
//   +57  EB BC                    jmp  +15                       ; none present: carry on
//   +59  F0 48 FF 05 ..           lock inc qword [counters+08]
//   +61  0F BF 03                 movsx eax, word [rbx]          ; skill id
//   +64  89 05 ..                 mov  [counters+20], eax
//   +6A  0F BF 84 6B D2 02 00 00  movsx eax, word [rbx+rbp*2+2D2h]
//   +72  89 05 ..                 mov  [counters+24], eax
//   +78  FF 25 ..                 jmp  qword [+90]               ; 33F4F0 refused
//   +7E  CC CC
//   +80  dq 33F506 / +88 dq 33F4B3 / +90 dq 33F4F0 / +98 dq 3351B0
//
// The item check stub, at region +200, is the same with edx for ecx, rdi as
// the record, ebx as the index, the item counters, and the exits 33F732
// (usable), 33F6E5 (whitelist) and 33F720 (refused).

// UNWIND_INFO for code running inside 0x33F360's frame after its prologue and
// home saves: the codes of the game's own entries 33F438-33F68E (chained to
// 33F360), which the stub's position inside that frame requires.

constexpr std::uint8_t UseCheckUnwind[]{
    0x01, 0x00, 0x09, 0x00,   // version 1, no flags, prolog 0, 9 slots, no frame register
    0x00, 0xE4, 0x04, 0x00,   // SAVE_NONVOL r14, [rsp+20h]
    0x00, 0x54, 0x0A, 0x00,   // SAVE_NONVOL rbp, [rsp+50h]
    0x00, 0x34, 0x08, 0x00,   // SAVE_NONVOL rbx, [rsp+40h]
    0x00, 0x42,               // ALLOC_SMALL 28h
    0x00, 0x70,               // PUSH_NONVOL rdi
    0x00, 0x60,               // PUSH_NONVOL rsi
    0x00, 0x00,               // pads the slot count to even
};

constexpr std::uint8_t ItemCheckUnwind[]{
    0x01, 0x00, 0x06, 0x00,   // version 1, no flags, prolog 0, 6 slots, no frame register
    0x00, 0x64, 0x07, 0x00,   // SAVE_NONVOL rsi, [rsp+38h]
    0x00, 0x34, 0x06, 0x00,   // SAVE_NONVOL rbx, [rsp+30h]
    0x00, 0x32,               // ALLOC_SMALL 20h
    0x00, 0x70,               // PUSH_NONVOL rdi
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// sequence-skill-stat
//
// Exposes one virtual stat id (configurable, no default) to .txt formulas.
// While the unit a formula is evaluated for is in a sequence (player mode 18,
// monster mode 14), the stat reads as the skills.txt id of the skill running
// that sequence. Otherwise it reads 0. Every other stat id is untouched.
//
// Target: the D2RLoader 1.3.1 image of D2R 3.3 (image base 0x140000000).
// Nothing is installed unless every byte this plugin depends on matches. On a
// build it does not recognise it installs nothing, logs which check failed
// and stays loaded so the console command can say why.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where the stat is answered, and why there
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// .txt formulas are compiled to bytecode and run by the generic evaluator
// 0xA234B0. A function token calls through a nine-record callback table at
// 0x1D09C50, records of {callback VA : qword, arity : dword, pad : dword}.
// Record 5 (slot 0x1D09CA0) is stat(), callback 0x3B33F0, arity 2. The
// evaluator reads the slot on every call:
//
//   call [table + 16 * index] (bankByte, arg1 = stat id, arg2 = field, 0, context)
//
// and every table-wired evaluator call site passes this same table (census in
// the RuffnecKk research ledger, SKILLS_BBEEvaluatorCallbackTableWiringWitness).
// Apart from unwind data, that slot is the only reference to 0x3B33F0.
//
// playermode-exposing already owns an inline hook on the first 5 bytes of
// 0x3B33F0, and D2RLoader refuses a patch that overlaps a tracked one. So this
// plugin redirects the table slot instead: the slot points at the handler
// below, which answers its own id and hands everything else to the stock
// callback. The stock callback is wherever playermode-exposing's hook sits, so
// both plugins chain in either load order. The same slot-swap through
// PatchWriteU64 is what RuffnecKk's auto-pickup does to the server packet
// table at 0x1D2A790, in the same read-only data region.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the used skill is the right answer
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The game picks a running sequence from the unit's used skill:
//
//   0x3CB890 (sequence resolver)  call 0x34BA40 UNITS_GetUsedSkill
//                                 call 0x33DBC0 seqnum from that skill
//   0x34C880 (mode start)         player mode 18 -> 0x34D900 SetSequence
//                                 monster mode 14 -> 0x34D900 SetSequence
//
// UNITS_GetUsedSkill is [[unit+0x100]+0x18] (0x34BA40 -> 0x33E090), and a
// skill's id is the signed word at the start of its skills.txt record
// (0x33E080: mov rax,[rcx] / movsx eax,word [rax]). So "in a sequence and the
// used skill is X" is exactly "running X's sequence".
//
// Procs do not move it. The central server do-handler 0x43ACB0, which every
// chance-to-cast goes through, never installs the skill it casts: its only
// UNITS_SetUsedSkill (0x34F430) call clears the slot on its abort path. No
// caller of UNITS_SetUsedSkill sits in the item-skill casters (0x589930,
// 0x5896E0, 0x589820), and none of the 172 srvdofunc slots at 0x238EA00 (the
// functions a proc runs, ItemEffect included) is one of its callers. The one
// event function that calls it, 0x584480, sets it on a monster it has just
// spawned, not on the attacker.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// stat() callback from +5, so the 5 bytes playermode-exposing displaces are
// left out. Proves the context is the 5th argument (push rdi / sub rsp,20h /
// mov rsi,[rsp+50h]) and the unit sits at context+0x10 (mov rsi,[rsi+10h]).

struct Witness {
	std::uint64_t       rva;
	const std::uint8_t* bytes;
	std::uint32_t       size;
	const char*         what;
};

using StatCallbackFn = std::int64_t(__fastcall*)(
	std::uint64_t bankByte,
	std::int32_t  statId,
	std::int32_t  fieldCode,
	std::uint64_t reserved,
	std::uint64_t context) noexcept;

// Shared, not Client or Server: .txt formulas are evaluated on both sides
// and the two must agree on what the virtual stat returns. NativeHooks
// because the game calls straight into this module through the slot.

// Cow Set Drops
//
// Replaces D2R's hardcoded Cow King's Leathers drop rule with a configurable
// one: a list of monsters (monstats.txt hcIdx) and a list of restricted sets
// (sets.txt rows). The hardcoded row 29 check is removed completely; only the
// rows in the config are restricted, and only drops from the listed monsters
// can roll them. Defaults: monsters 391 and 735 (the vanilla pair), no
// restricted rows.
//
// Built for D2R 3.3 on D2RLoader. Every byte below was read out of the live
// D2RLoader.exe image and disassembled before being relied on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The vanilla rule, part 1: the drop gets tagged (0x444680)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   0x444680 is the TC item-spawn helper. Its only caller is the TC core
//   0x4404F0, once per item the treasure class produces. It fills the drop
//   request on its stack (request = rsp+60h, request+0C0h = [rbp+20h]) and
//   hands it to the item creator 0x43CD50. rdi is the dropping unit and ebx
//   is 1 from 4446E2 on; nothing between there and 444852 writes ebx.
//
//     44480E  E8 BD 71 F0 FF          call 34B9D0              ; unit type
//     444813  3B C3                   cmp  eax, ebx            ; 1 = monster
//     444815  75 38                   jne  44484F              ; not a monster: no tag
//     444817  41 B8 55 0F 00 00       mov  r8d, 0F55h
//     44481D  48 8D 15 9C 54 8D 01    lea  rdx, <source file name>
//     444824  48 8B CF                mov  rcx, rdi
//     444827  E8 34 50 F0 FF          call 349860              ; eax = unit+4, the monstats id
//     44482C  3D 87 01 00 00          cmp  eax, 187h           ; 391 hellbovine
//     444831  74 1F                   je   444852              ; tag
//     444833  41 B8 55 0F 00 00       mov  r8d, 0F55h          ; the same lookup again
//     444839  48 8D 15 80 54 8D 01    lea  rdx, <source file name>
//     444840  48 8B CF                mov  rcx, rdi
//     444843  E8 18 50 F0 FF          call 349860
//     444848  3D DF 02 00 00          cmp  eax, 2DFh           ; 735 cowking
//     44484D  74 03                   je   444852              ; tag
//     44484F  8B 5D 20                mov  ebx, [rbp+20h]      ; no tag: the request flags (zeroed)
//     444852  0B 9D B8 00 00 00       or   ebx, [rbp+0B8h]     ; tag: ebx is 1. Plus the TC's own flags
//     444858  48 8D 54 24 60          lea  rdx, [rsp+60h]      ; the request
//     44485D  45 33 C0                xor  r8d, r8d
//     444860  89 5D 20                mov  [rbp+20h], ebx      ; request+0C0h, bit 0 = the tag
//     444863  48 8B CE                mov  rcx, rsi            ; game
//     444866  E8 E5 84 FF FF          call 43CD50              ; create the item
//
//   0x349860 only returns unit+4 (or -1 with an assert for a null unit).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The vanilla rule, part 2: the set item picker (0x58C2F0)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Reached from the set-quality branch of 0x442D60 through 0x58ACD0 for every
//   item whose format is not 0. r14 is the drop request (mov r14, r8 at
//   58C321). rbx walks the setitems.txt records at record+22h, 1CCh apart
//   (add rbx, 22h at 58C3B4; add rbx, 1CCh at 58C4AA). record+30h is the
//   `set` column: the setitems loader 0x31C2D0 stores it at offset 48 after
//   resolving the set's name against the sets.txt list, so it holds the
//   sets.txt row number. record+2 holds the set item's own `index` name.
//   By 58C411 the row has already passed the code, version, flag, ladder and
//   level tests.
//
//     58C411  66 83 7B 0E 1D          cmp  word [rbx+0Eh], 1Dh   ; set row 29?
//     58C416  75 0A                   jne  58C422                ; other sets: carry on
//     58C418  41 F6 86 C0 00 00 00 01 test byte [r14+0C0h], 1    ; the tag
//     58C420  74 7C                   je   58C49E                ; untagged: skip the row
//     58C422  41 8B 46 50             mov  eax, [r14+50h]        ; forced row, then pooling
//     ...
//     58C49E  0F B7 74 24 30          movzx esi, word [rsp+30h]  ; skip: next row
//
//   The test runs before the forced-row check at 58C422, so a treasure class
//   that names a set item directly does not get around it either.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The hooks
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Tag site, 35 bytes at 44482C (both class-id compares): E9 rel32 into the
//   tag stub, the rest NOPed. eax already holds the monstats id from the
//   lookup at 444827. The stub tags (exit 444852, ebx still 1) when the id is
//   0..32767 and its bit is set in the monster bitmap, otherwise it leaves
//   through 44484F.
//
//   Picker site, 17 bytes at 58C411 (the row 29 test): E9 rel32 into the
//   picker stub, the rest NOPed. When the record's set row has its bit set in
//   the set bitmap, the stub requires the tag and leaves through 58C49E
//   without it. Every other row leaves through 58C422.
//
//   Why the windows are safe:
//     - both start and end on instruction boundaries and nothing branches
//       into them except onto their first byte (the jne at 444815 targets
//       44484F, the end of the tag window);
//     - the stubs write only rax, rcx, rdx and r8, and those are dead on
//       every exit: 444852 and 44484F reload rdx, r8 and rcx before the
//       3-argument call to 43CD50; 58C422 reloads eax, then writes ecx, r8
//       and rdx before reading them on every path; 58C49E reloads esi and
//       eax and the loop writes rcx, rdx and r8 before its calls;
//     - the flags are dead on every exit, and the stubs never use the stack;
//     - rbx, rsi, rdi, rbp, r9 to r15 and rsp are never touched.
//
//   The stubs were assembled with keystone and decoded back with capstone.
//   Both were emulated with unicorn against the original bytes mapped at
//   their real addresses: 1,507 monstats ids (0..1499, the 32767/32768
//   boundary, 65535, 65536, 7FFFFFFFh and -1) for the tag stub and all 65,536
//   set rows with four tag-byte values for the picker stub, under several
//   lists. The vanilla paths and the default monster list match the original
//   exactly, a [29] row list matches the original picker exactly, the
//   preserved registers come out unchanged and the counters are exact.

// 444680: mov r11, rsp / push rbp, rbx, rsi, rdi, r14 / lea rbp, [rsp-60h] /
// sub rsp, 160h / cookie / mov r14d, r8d / mov rdi, rdx / mov rsi, rcx / ... /
// mov ebx, 1 at 4446E2.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Hook region
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   One allocation within rel32 reach of the image, never freed:
//     +0000  code page        PAGE_EXECUTE_READ  tag stub at +000, picker stub at +100
//     +1000  counters page    PAGE_READWRITE     written by the stubs
//     +2000  monster bitmap   PAGE_READONLY      4 KB, bit n = monstats id n may tag
//     +3000  set row bitmap   PAGE_READONLY      8 KB, bit n = sets.txt row n restricted
//   The set bitmap covers every 16-bit value, so the picker stub needs no
//   bounds check; rows above 32767 are never set. The stubs read and write
//   nothing but the game and this region, so they never depend on this DLL.

struct DropCounters {
    std::uint64_t tagged;         // +00  drops tagged by monster_ids
    std::uint64_t allowed;        // +08  restricted rows kept, the drop was tagged
    std::uint64_t denied;         // +10  restricted rows skipped, the drop was not tagged
    std::uint32_t lastTaggedId;   // +18  monstats id of the last tagged drop
    std::uint32_t lastRow;        // +1C  set row of the last restricted row checked
    char          lastItem[32];   // +20  its setitems.txt index (record+2)
    std::uint8_t  lastAllowed;    // +40  1 kept, 0 skipped
};

