// Shadow Warrior AI
//
// See celestialrayone.shadow-warrior-ai.toml for the behaviour write-up.
//
// Shadow warriors (every monster whose monstats.txt AI is ShadowWarrior) follow
// the same two skill rules as a player: skills.txt localdelay and globaldelay,
// and the skill's weapon types (itypea1-3, itypeb1-3, etypea1-2, etypeb1-2). A
// skill that breaks a rule is turned down, and the AI falls back exactly as it
// does for any skill it turns down.
//
// Written for D2R 3.3 on D2RLoader 1.3.1, PluginSDK 0.3.0. Every address and
// byte below was read out of the live D2RLoader.exe process image
// (md5 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d) and disassembled before being relied
// on. Every site is checked byte for byte at load; nothing is installed when a
// check fails, and the game runs stock.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why shadows ignored both rules
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Cooldowns are player-only in three places:
//     0x43ACB0  the skill do-handler (game, unit, skillId, level, consume,
//               itemCast, itemEffect). Its cooldown block (globaldelay calc at
//               skill record +234h, localdelay calc at +238h) only runs when the
//               byte at [rsp+0C0h] is set, and 0x43AD17 sets that byte from
//               UnitType == player.
//     0x3404A0  the "is this skill on cooldown" gate answers "free" at once for
//               every non-player.
//     0x339D70  the global delay setter refuses every non-player.
//   The Shadow Warrior AI 0x5CEEA0 (AI table 0x2396E90, row 105) asks none of
//   them. It keeps a pacing timer of its own (AI control +44h): after every
//   skill it turns down every skill but Attack for globaldelay / 3 + 18 frames,
//   and it never reads localdelay. That timer is left as it is.
//
//   Weapon types: players are checked by the use-state routine 0x33F360, whose
//   weapon part is 0x33F1F0 (unit, skill). The AI never calls either. 0x33F1F0
//   reads the unit's own inventory and both hands, so it works for a shadow as
//   it is.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What is changed
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   1. 0x5CF420  the AI's skill check (game, unit, owner, skill, skillId,
//                combat, tick), called only by 0x5CEEA0. Inline hook: a skill
//                that breaks a rule is refused before the native checks run, so
//                no random roll is spent and the AI's skill fatigue counter (AI
//                control +48h) does not move. The AI then tries its other
//                skill, then Attack.
//   2. 0x4A7BC0  AITACTICS_UseSkill (game, unit, mode, skillId, target, x, y,
//                flag). Inline hook, shadows only. The AI uses its Attack
//                fallback without asking 0x5CF420, so the final pick is checked
//                here as well. A refused skill gets what the game does when its
//                own mode change refuses a skill (0x4A7CC2): 10 frames standing
//                in neutral through 0x4A6D10, then 0.
//   3. 0x43B152  the do-handler's player test, cmp byte [rsp+0C0h],0 /
//                je 0x43B1C4. Replaced by a jump through a trampoline in the
//                int3 padding at 0x4A37C6 to a relay. Players continue at
//                0x43B15C into the untouched native block. Every other unit
//                calls OnSkillDo(game rdi, unit rbx, skill r13d, level
//                [rsp+0D8h]) and continues at 0x43B1C4, where the native je
//                went. OnSkillDo starts the two cooldowns for shadows only, at
//                the level the do-handler was given, with the same calls in the
//                same order as the native player block. That covers every path
//                a player gets a cooldown from: casts (0x436B10), periodic
//                pulses (0x437460) and aura starts (0x43B680).
//
//   Shadow cooldowns are kept by the plugin, keyed by game and unit GUID the
//   way monster-affixes keeps its lockouts. A different game or a frame counter
//   that went backwards drops an entry. The check mirrors 0x3404A0: a running
//   localdelay blocks its own skill; a running globaldelay blocks every skill
//   whose globaldelay or localdelay evaluates above 0. A newer globaldelay
//   replaces the running one, as 0x2F7AB0 does for players.
//
//   charstats.txt MinimumCastingDelay is not applied: the native setters look
//   it up by class id (0x0FCFC0, charstats.txt row +50h), which only finds a row
//   for player classes.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Unit, game and record fields read here
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   unit +00h type (1 = monster), +04h class id (monstats.txt hcIdx),
//   +08h GUID (0x34A330 is mov eax,[rcx+8]), +10h MonsterData.
//   MonsterData +00h -> monstats record, record +52h AI index (int16): the
//   game's own AI table lookup 0x4A36C0 reads exactly these.
//   game +106h data context (byte), +170h frame counter.
//   skills.txt record +234h globaldelay calc, +238h localdelay calc.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Coexistence (checked against every plugin in d2rloader_plugins)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   crossbow-charges witnesses 0x43B163..0x43B19B and redirects the call at
//   0x43B18D. srcdam-calc-field hooks the entry of 0x43ACB0. dynamic-etype
//   redirects the call at 0x43ACFC. cast-on-cast patches 0x43AF1D and uses the
//   padding at 0x43ACA1. None of them writes 0x43B152..0x43B15B, the Shadow
//   Warrior AI, AITACTICS_UseSkill or the padding at 0x4A37C6, and no witness
//   here covers a byte any of them writes.

// 0x5CF420  mov [rsp+8],rbx / mov [rsp+10h],rbp / mov [rsp+18h],rsi /
//           push rdi, r12, r13, r14, r15 / sub rsp,30h. No branch, no
//           RIP-relative operand.

// 0x4A7CC2  mov r8d,0Ah / mov rdx,rdi / mov rcx,rsi / call 0x4A6D10 /
//           mov rbp,[rsp+68h] / xor eax,eax. Read only: the refusal the hook
//           copies.

// 0x43AD0D  mov rcx,rbx / call 0x34B9D0 (UnitType) / test eax,eax /
//           sete byte [rsp+0C0h]. Read only: the byte tested at 0x43B152 is
//           "the unit is a player".

// 0x43B15C..0x43B18C  the native block the relay hands players back to:
//   mov ebp,[rsp+0D8h] (level) / mov r9d,r13d (skill id) /
//   mov r8d,[rsi+234h] (globaldelay calc) / mov rdx,rbx (unit) /
//   movzx ecx,byte [rdi+106h] (data context of the game in rdi) /
//   mov [rsp+20h],ebp / call 0x3B5160 / test eax,eax / jle /
//   mov r8d,eax / mov rdx,rbx / mov rcx,rdi
// Read only. It stops right before crossbow-charges' call at 0x43B18D.

// 0x43B192..0x43B1BE  its localdelay half: mov r8d,[rsi+238h] / mov r9d,r13d /
//   movzx ecx,byte [rdi+106h] / mov rdx,rbx / mov [rsp+20h],ebp /
//   call 0x3B5160 / test eax,eax / jle 0x43B1C4 / mov r9d,r13d / mov r8d,eax /
//   mov rdx,rbx / mov rcx,rdi. Read only; stops before the call at 0x43B1BF.

// 0x43B1C4  mov rcx,rbx / call [rip+39EEFE3h]: where the native je lands. It
// overwrites rcx and calls at once, so no scratch register is live there and
// the relay's own call cannot disturb it. Read only.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relay (keystone-assembled, capstone and unicorn verified)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Entered from 0x43B152 through the trampoline, with the do-handler's frame and
// registers untouched (two jmps):
//   +00h  cmp byte [rsp+0C0h],0          ; the native player test
//   +08h  jne +27h                       ; player: back to 0x43B15C
//   +0Ah  mov rcx,rdi                    ; game
//   +0Dh  mov rdx,rbx                    ; unit
//   +10h  mov r8d,r13d                   ; skill id
//   +13h  mov r9d,[rsp+0D8h]             ; level
//   +1Bh  call [rip+0Fh]                 ; OnSkillDo, slot +30h
//   +21h  jmp [rip+11h]                  ; 0x43B1C4, slot +38h
//   +27h  jmp [rip+13h]                  ; 0x43B15C, slot +40h
// rsp is never moved: the do-handler body keeps it 16-byte aligned, so the
// call enters OnSkillDo exactly as a normal call would, and OnSkillDo's home
// space is the do-handler's outgoing home area, scratch at this point.

// The relay is described with the do-handler's own unwind data. Its code starts
// 100h into the block, past any prolog length, so the unwinder always treats it
// as the do-handler's body and unwinds the whole host frame.

// 3. Reached from the relay for every non-player do-event that spends
//    resources: the moment the native block would start a player's cooldowns.
//    Same calls, same order, shadows only.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         what;
    bool                cooldownsOnly;
};

// Shrine Rework
//
// One plugin, three parts with their own switches in the config:
//   Removal  takes a configurable list of shrine types (shrines.txt Code) out
//            of the game. Shrine objects draw from the same pools minus the
//            removed shrines, so the remaining shrines keep their relative
//            odds, and pools that removal empties fall back to another
//            effectclass (Refilling by default).
//   Effects  Armor: a percent less damage taken. Combat: configurable stats.
//            Resists: maximum resistance on top. Skill: +all skills by
//            difficulty. Mana Recharge: percent of max life and mana per
//            second.
//   Sharing  a clicked shrine also reaches every other player and every
//            hireling and summon within a configurable radius of it, 40
//            yards by default.
//
// D2R 3.3 on D2RLoader 1.3.1. Everything below was read out of the live
// D2RLoader.exe image and disassembled before being relied on. Every game
// address the plugin calls, hooks or patches is fingerprinted at load; a part
// whose fingerprints do not match is left out and the other part still runs.
//
// ===========================================================================
// Part 1: Removal
// ===========================================================================
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// shrines.txt as the 3.3 loader compiles it (0x391E60, record 0x1C bytes)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   +00 Code (byte)          +04 Arg0            +08 Arg1
//   +0C Duration in frames   +10 reset time in minutes (byte)
//   +12 StringName           +14 StringPhrase    +16 effectclass (byte)
//   +18 LevelMin
//   The loader has no rarity column. Code selects the effect: the shrine
//   operate function (objects.txt OperateFn 2, 0x58F2A0) indexes the 24-slot
//   chart at 0x1D3F1D0 with it. So Code is what identifies a shrine type.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where the type is chosen: objects.txt InitFn 1 = 0x50E450 (hooked)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   50E463  8B B8 34 01 00 00     mov  edi, [rax+134h]       ; objects.txt Parm0
//   50E469  E8 A2 17 DE FF        call 2EFC10                ; level id of the room
//
//   Parm0 == 0, the whole table (50E4E8..50E58E):
//     up to 8 draws of row = 1 + roll(count - 1) on the object region seed
//     [ctx+18h]. It stops at the first row whose LevelMin [rec+18h] is not
//     above the level id (unsigned, 50E584 cmp / jae), otherwise it keeps
//     the 8th draw.
//
//   Parm0 != 0, an effectclass pool (50E47A..50E4E3):
//     Parm0 1 -> class 2, Parm0 2 -> class 3, any other value -> class 4,
//     or class 1 when a region-seed roll is a multiple of 10 (50E4A8..50E4BD).
//     The picker 0x512050 then draws up to 8 times from that class list on
//     the object control seed with the same LevelMin rule. A list entry of 0
//     reads as row 1 (512119 cmove ebx, ebp).
//
//   Then (50E59A..50E5C3) the drawn row is remapped: everything becomes
//   row 17 while the byte at 0x2AAF4B8 is set (only toggled by the 15-byte
//   0x510AC0), otherwise 4 -> 2, 5 -> 3, 16 -> 18. That remap is why the
//   Health/Mana Exchange and Enirhs shrines never appear. The final row goes
//   to ObjectData.InteractType (0x34E9D0) and its record to ObjectData
//   (0x38FE40, tail jump to 0x34EE70).
//
//   The class lists are built per game by 0x5125A0 from effectclass
//   [rec+16h]: every row with effectclass 0..7 is appended to
//   list[effectclass] in row order. Object control is [Game+21D0h]: seed
//   pair at +0, int32 counts[8] at +8, int32* lists[8] at +28h. 0x512BE0
//   returns the object control (its seed) and 0x512DA0 returns +8.
//
//   The picker's only other caller is the healing well spawner 0x50CF10.
//   When a level still needs a healing well and the shrine it just placed
//   is not row 2, it turns the object into a HealingWell, calls the picker
//   for class 2 only for its seed side effect, and hard-sets row 2:
//     50D10B  48 8B CE              mov  rcx, rsi              ; game
//     50D10E  41 B8 01 00 00 00     mov  r8d, 1
//     50D114  B2 02                 mov  dl, 2
//     50D116  E8 35 4F 00 00        call 512050                ; result unused
//     50D11B  B2 02                 mov  dl, 2
//     50D11D  48 8B CB              mov  rcx, rbx              ; the object
//     50D120  E8 AB 18 E4 FF        call 34E9D0                ; InteractType = 2
//     50D125  0F B6 8E 06 01 00 00  movzx ecx, byte [rsi+106h]
//     50D12C  BA 02 00 00 00        mov  edx, 2
//     50D131  E8 0A 2D E8 FF        call 38FE40                ; record of row 2
//     50D136  48 8B D0              mov  rdx, rax
//     50D139  48 8B CB              mov  rcx, rbx
//     50D13C  E8 2F 1D E4 FF        call 34EE70
//     50D141  8B D5                 mov  edx, ebp              ; healing count
//   50D11B..50D140 (38 bytes) becomes mov rcx, rsi / mov rdx, rbx / call
//   into a near relay to ForcedHealingWell, then NOPs. rbx, rsi, rbp and
//   r14 are nonvolatile and 50D141 reads only those, and no branch lands
//   inside the window. ForcedHealingWell keeps row 2 unless its shrine is
//   removed, and otherwise draws from class 2 with the same rules as above,
//   so a healing well never carries a removed shrine.
//
//   When removal empties the pool an object draws from (Health and Mana
//   wells draw only from effectclass 2 and 3), the object draws from the
//   fallback effectclass instead (default 1, Refilling).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The hook
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   0x50E450 is replaced by SelectRow plus the original tail. SelectRow is
//   that function with one difference: a pool only holds rows whose FINAL
//   shrine (after the remap above) keeps its Code. The draw count, the seeds
//   and how much of them each step consumes, the LevelMin rule, the remap
//   and the three native calls at the end are those of the original. With
//   nothing removed it returns the same row and leaves both seeds in the
//   same state as the original machine code (checked offline: the original
//   0x50E450 and 0x512050 bytes run under emulation against this code).
//
//   The kept rows are counted and indexed in place, so pools have no size
//   limit. When removal empties an effectclass pool, the draw moves to the
//   fallback effectclass. When nothing is left there either, that object
//   gets the original draw over the full pool (same seeds, same result as
//   without the plugin) and the plugin log says so. When the game's own pool
//   is already empty, the result is the original's (row 1).
//
//   The whole 0x50E450 body and the whole picker body are fingerprinted
//   before anything is installed, so a build where either differs is left
//   untouched.
//
// ===========================================================================
// Part 2: Effects
// ===========================================================================
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// How a shrine applies its effect
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   objects.txt OperateFn 2 = 0x58F2A0 calls chart[Code].handler through the
//   24-slot chart at 0x1D3F1D0 (16 bytes: handler, stat id, state id), with
//   (dataContext, operate, shrine record). operate: +00 game, +08 the shrine
//   object, +10 the player. Record: +00 Code, +04 Arg0, +08 Arg1, +0C Duration
//   in frames.
//
//   Chart (stat, state): 6 Armor (171 skill_armor_percent, 128), 7 Combat
//   (-1, 129), 8 Fire (39, 131), 9 Cold (43, 132), 10 Lightning (41, 130),
//   11 Poison (45, 133), 12 Skill (11, 134), 13 Mana Recharge (27, 135).
//
//   Handlers:
//     0x591E20  Armor, the four resists, Mana Recharge, Experience: value =
//               0x594DA0(context, record, stat, Arg0, player), then one timed
//               state with that stat.
//     0x591F70  Combat: damagepercent 25 = Arg1, then tohit 19 = Arg0 % of
//               the AR snapshot from 0x593910.
//     0x591F00  Skill: state 134 with a dummy stat (11 = 0) and the remove
//               callback 0x594FB0, then the skill refresh 0x438970. The +2
//               itself is in the skill-level code, which D2RLoader replaced
//               (0x33D1E0 / 0x3400A0 jump to D2RCore), keyed on state 134.
//
//   The timed state is 0x433D20, a thunk to D2RCore!ApplyWideTimedStatEffect,
//   StatList* (args). The 30h args record, as the handlers fill it:
//     +00 source (object)  +08 target (player)  +10 skill 0  +14 level 0
//     +18 duration  +1C stat (-1 = none)  +20 value  +24 state  +28 remove cb
//   Extra stats go on the returned list through 0x2F3030, a thunk to
//   SetWideListStat (context, list, stat, value, layer), as the Combat
//   handler does for tohit.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Armor: damage taken (SUNITDMG_CalculateTotalDamage 0x44DF10)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The function first applies the DifficultyLevels damage percent to
//   physical, fire, lightning, cold, magic, poison and the life, mana and
//   stamina drains, then fires absorbdamage (event 11), then resistances,
//   then sums the life total:
//     44EB25  48 8B 4C 24 30        mov  rcx, [rsp+30h]      ; game
//     44EB2A  4D 8B CD              mov  r9, r13             ; attacker
//     44EB2D  4C 8B C6              mov  r8, rsi             ; defender
//     44EB30  4C 89 7C 24 20        mov  [rsp+20h], r15      ; D2Damage
//     44EB35  BA 0B 00 00 00        mov  edx, 0Bh
//     44EB3A  E8 31 EA FF FF        call 44D570              ; event 11
//   Only the rel32 of that call changes. It lands in a near relay that jumps
//   to HookedUnitEvent, which scales the hit when the defender has the Armor
//   state and then makes the original call. So the reduction sits next to
//   the game's own all-damage percent, before absorbs and resistances.
//
//   D2Damage: +18 Phys, +20 Fire, +24 Burn, +28 Burn length, +2C Ligt,
//   +30 Magc, +34 Cold, +38 Pois, +3C Pois length, +40/+48 per-source poison
//   (12-byte entries: id, damage per frame, length; 44D1CF), +120/+124/+128
//   Life/Mana/Stam drain, +134 life total. The commit (44D145..) applies each
//   source, then the rest as Pois - sum(sources), so both are scaled.
//   Poison, burning and bleed (event 16, after resistances) are all derived
//   from these values, so they are reduced as well. The poison immunity and
//   bleed plugins touch 44DF10, 44EC8C and 44ECA5, none of which this plugin
//   writes.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Mana Recharge: regeneration
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Player stat regen 0x42E600 runs every frame (reschedules at frame+1):
//   life += hpregen (stat 74); mana (0x42E350) += manarecovery (stat 26) +
//   the charstats base. Both are 1/256 of a point per frame, so a percent of
//   the maximum per second is max * percent / 2500 per frame.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The hooks
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   0x591E20, 0x591F00 and 0x591F70 are replaced by the functions below,
//   which build the same args record the originals build and call the same
//   natives, plus the extra stats. A shrine or a part that is off calls the
//   original. Every handler body is fingerprinted whole before installing.
//   The natives they call are proven through those verified bodies (the
//   call, jmp or lea that reaches them), never by their own entry bytes,
//   because other plugins may hook those: damage-overflow-fix's
//   stat_write_watch rewrites the 0x2F3030 thunk, and the first version of
//   this plugin refused to load its effects because of it. Each part checks
//   only what it uses, so one mismatch leaves the other parts running.
//
// ===========================================================================
// Part 3: Sharing
// ===========================================================================
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where a shrine is used: objects.txt OperateFn 2 = 0x58F2A0 (hooked)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Both object dispatchers (0x593630 for a click or Telekinesis, 0x592C40)
//   build the same operate record and pass it in rcx:
//     +00 Game*  +08 the shrine  +10 the player  +18 object control
//     +20 objects.txt row (int32)
//   0x58F2A0 reads it as:
//     58F2BC  call 34AB60             ; shrine mode, not 0: return 0 (used)
//     58F3AD  call 34B520             ; rsi = the shrine's shrines.txt record
//     58F432  movzx eax, byte [rsi]   ; Code
//     58F435  cmp al, 18h / jae 58F461
//     58F43C  lea r9, [1D3F1D0] / mov r9, [r9+rax*8] (rax = Code*2)
//     58F447  test r9, r9 / je 58F461
//     58F44F  r8 = shrines.txt record, rdx = operate record,
//             ecx = byte [Game+106h]
//     58F45C  call r9                 ; the effect, on +10 only
//     58F461  no handler: life += max life - life, mana += max mana - mana
//             (2F5020, 2F4D20, 2F34F0, then 2F5020, 2F4E20, 2F34F0)
//     58F540  mov eax, 1
//   The hook runs the original and, when it returned 1, calls the same chart
//   handler once more for every other unit in range, with a copy of the
//   record whose +10 is that unit. Codes without a handler share through the
//   Refilling handler 0x590DB0, which makes the same six calls on the same
//   stats (proven at compile time below). Because the calls go through the
//   chart, the Effects hooks on 0x591E20 / 0x591F00 / 0x591F70 apply to
//   everyone exactly as they do to the clicker.
//   The hook displaces 58F2A0..58F2A5 (push rdi / sub rsp,70h); the next
//   instruction is the RIP-relative security cookie load.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Who is in range
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Players: 0x490D00 (game, callback, arg) walks the 128 player buckets at
//   Game+2230h (next at unit+158h) and calls callback(game, player, arg) for
//   every player without state 7 (playerbody) or 186 (shared stash proxy).
//   Hirelings and summons: 0x4FF990 (game, player, callback, arg, all) walks
//   the pet lists of a player (qword +98h of what 0x34B240 returns for it,
//   pettype 1 and up) through 0x5014C0, which resolves each pet with
//   SUNIT_GetServerUnit 0x48FE80 (game, 1, guid) and calls
//   callback(game, player, pet, arg). all = 0 skips list nodes with flag 1,
//   as the healing well does when it heals pets (call at 0x594B37).
//   A unit is in range when it is alive (0x34C2C0), in the shrine's act
//   (room 0x34B440, level 0x2EFC10, act 0x326710) and within the radius.
//   Positions are read the way the Fire shrine reads them (59127C..5912F5):
//   unit+38h is the path; objects, items and tiles (unit types 2, 4, 5) keep
//   x and y at path+10h / +14h, players and monsters go through PATH_GetX
//   0x341A20 / PATH_GetY 0x341A30 (words at path+2 / +6). The radius test
//   has the game's own shape, dx*dx + dy*dy against radius*radius in game
//   units, as the area scan 0x4398B0 applies it through 0x3412C0.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Yards
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   A yard is 1.5 game units (subtiles). skilldesc.txt prints radii in yards
//   as game units * 2 / 3: Inner Sight's "Radius: %d yards" line (descline
//   36, StrSkill18) is rng*2 over 3, rng being its aurarangecalc. The test
//   4 * (dx*dx + dy*dy) <= 9 * yards * yards is exactly that, unrounded.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Who gets which shrine
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Everyone (players, hirelings, summons): Codes 1, 2, 3, 6 to 13, 15 and
//   the Codes without a handler. Their handlers only touch the unit at +10:
//   its stats, a timed state through ApplyWideTimedStatEffect, or the skill
//   refresh 0x438970, which checks the unit type (438983) before its
//   player-only pet step.
//   Players only: 4 and 5 trade the unit's own life and mana, 14 is stamina,
//   16 (Enirhs) rewrites the PlayerData name, 18 (Gem) reads the backpack
//   and drops a gem next to the unit.
//   The clicker only: 17 Portal, 19 Fire, 20 Monster, 21 Exploding and
//   22 Poison act on the area around the shrine, not on the unit at +10.
//   The 24 handler slots of the chart are checked against these functions
//   at load; a different chart leaves this part out.
//
// ===========================================================================
// Shared
// ===========================================================================
//   Both patched call sites (the healing well window at 0x50D11B and the
//   absorbdamage call at 0x44EB3A) need a rel32 target, so both land in one
//   page allocated next to the image holding two FF 25 jumps, one into
//   ForcedHealingWell and one into HookedUnitEvent. The page is written once,
//   made read-execute and never freed; D2RLoader restores both sites and
//   removes the inline hooks when the plugin unloads.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The natives the effects call, proven by the verified code that calls them
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Their own entry bytes are NOT fingerprinted: other plugins may hook them,
//   and calling through such a hook is exactly what the game's own call does
//   (damage-overflow-fix's stat_write_watch, on by default, rewrites the
//   0x2F3030 thunk). Instead each RVA is checked at compile time against a
//   call, jmp or lea inside a body that is fingerprinted whole at load.

// 4FF9B1 pet lists at +98h, 4FF9D8 'all' is the fifth argument, 4FFA45
// call 5014C0; 501508 type 1 to 48FE80, 501526 call r14 with
// (game, player, pet, arg).

// One step of the seed pair, exactly as 50E48C..50E4A5, 50E530..50E566 and
// 5120C9..5120FC store it: next = low * 6AC690C5h + high, written back as
// low/high halves. Returns the new low half.

// Smite
//
// Three independent Smite features, each switchable on its own in the TOML.
// A disabled feature installs nothing at all.
//
//   1. Multi-hit                   one cast lands N times, N from a
//                                  skills.txt calc column          (server)
//   2. Holy Shield display fix     the damage line reads a configurable
//                                  skill instead of vanilla 117    (client)
//   3. item_normaldamage display   the damage line includes the
//      fix                         "adds X damage" stat            (client)
//
// Features 2 and 3 are display only and change no damage. Because the plugin
// touches both the server do-func and the client description handler, its role
// is Shared.
//
// ===========================================================================
// NATIVE BACKGROUND
// ===========================================================================
// All of it read out of the live D2R 3.3 image under D2RLoader and
// disassembled before being relied on here.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Server: the Smite do-func
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   0x238EA00  pSrvDoFunc table, entry N at base + 8N, index bound 190.
//              Dispatched by the skill do-handler at 0x43ACB0 through the
//              skills.txt srvdofunc column at skill-record offset 78.
//   0x564930   slot 150, Smite. __fastcall(game, unit, skillId, skillLevel)
//              -> 1 on a landed hit, 0 when nothing happened.
//
//              Identified by content, not by slot number alone: it resolves
//              the equipped shield, reads the shield's min and max damage
//              bytes at item-record offsets 271 and 272 and shifts them left
//              by 8 into 256ths, adds the Holy Shield bonus by reading state
//              101 off the caster and taking that state's own source skill
//              and level, evaluates calc1 as the damage percent and calc2 as
//              bonus damage, then applies:
//
//                0x564CDB  call 0x44B600   apply damage    (game, att, tgt, &dmg, 0x80)
//                0x564CF1  call 0x44B2B0   durability drain and the melee
//                                          event pair, domeleeattack and
//                                          attackedinmelee (game, att, tgt, 0, 1)
//                0x564CF6  mov r13d, 1     the success return
//
//   0x97790    SKILLS_GetSkillsTxtRecord(gameVersion, skillId), stride 748.
//              Its first call is GetDataTables at 0x300A90.
//   0x3B5160   SKILLS_EvaluateFormula(gameVersion, unit, poolOffset, skillId,
//              level), the Skills-pool evaluator. A blank .txt formula column
//              holds 0xFFFFFFFF, and the engine tests for that before calling
//              in (see the ToHit evaluator at 0x339040), so this plugin does
//              the same.
//   game+262   the game version byte, read the same way Smite itself reads it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// skills.txt calc column offsets, D2R 3.3
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Rebuilt from the .txt column binder 0x302380, which hands its descriptor
//   array to LoadExcelTable with a record size of 748. Each descriptor is 32
//   bytes: name at +0, type at +8, record offset at +16.
//
//     calc1  400    calc6  420
//     calc2  404    calc7  424
//     calc3  408    calc8  428
//     calc4  412    calc9  432
//     calc5  416    calc10 436
//
//   Cross-checked three ways rather than trusted from the binder alone: the
//   ToHit evaluator 0x339040 reads record[144..146], which is 0x240/0x244/
//   0x248, and the same reconstruction puts ToHit, LevToHit and ToHitCalc at
//   exactly those offsets; the reconstruction lands cltstopfunc at 0x2E8,
//   flush against the 0x2EC record size; and it puts seqnum at +0x33, which
//   is where the native sequence selector reads it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the whole do-func is repeated for multi-hit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   The 2.4 build looped only the two calls at the tail and replayed one saved
//   copy of the damage struct. That cannot be carried across: the 3.3 damage
//   struct is about 0x180 bytes and carries two embedded small-buffer arenas,
//   a tagged pointer with a size, a capacity of 0x8000000000000010 and a
//   0xC0-byte inline buffer, which the function's own epilogue destructs.
//   Snapshotting and restoring across those would leak the moment an arena
//   spilled to the heap.
//
//   Repeating the do-func is the same feature with none of that exposure. It
//   needs no stack layout, no struct copy and no assembly. Every hit runs the
//   full path, so each one applies damage, drains durability and fires
//   domeleeattack and attackedinmelee. One behavioural difference is worth
//   knowing: each hit rolls its own damage between the shield's min and max
//   instead of replaying a single roll. The mean is unchanged, and criticals
//   were already rolled per hit in 2.4 because they happen inside the applier.
//   If a hit returns 0 the target is gone or out of reach and the loop stops
//   there rather than hammering a corpse.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Client: finding the descdam=10 display handler
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   D2R compiles .txt formulas into one pool per table and gives each pool its
//   own evaluator wrapper off GetDataTablesForContext (0x300A90):
//
//       0x3B5160   Skills pool      DataTables +0x138 / +0x140
//       0x3B5000   SkillDesc pool   DataTables +0x150 / +0x158
//
//   Every caller of 0x3B5000 is a skilldesc.txt descdam or descatt handler,
//   which bounds that family to 0x280720..0x28F6E0. Inside it, 0x283A80 is the
//   only one that resolves the equipped shield, and its body matches the 2.4
//   descdam=10 handler line for line. That is the Smite damage line.
//
//   skilldesc.txt itself: 300-byte (0x12C) records, descdam at +16, descatt at
//   +18, ddam calc1 at +20, ddam calc2 at +24. Taken from the same binder,
//   whose LoadExcelTable call for skilldesc passes record size 0x12C, and
//   confirmed by the last column landing at +0x12A.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The block the display fixes touch, at 0x283B26..0x283BBB
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Register contract:
//       rsi  = the unit whose sheet is being drawn
//       dil  = the data context byte
//       rax  = the shield's compiled ItemsTxt record
//       r12d = displayed min damage, r13d = displayed max damage
//
//     0x283B26  mov   edx,117                     \
//     0x283B2B  mov   r8d,-1                       |
//     0x283B31  mov   rcx,rsi                      |
//     0x283B34  movzx r12d,byte ptr [rax+0x10F]    |  shield min damage
//     0x283B3C  movzx r13d,byte ptr [rax+0x110]    |  shield max damage
//     0x283B44  call  0x33DCD0                     |  SKILLS_GetSkillById(unit,117,-1)
//     0x283B51  mov   edx,101                      |  state 101, Holy Shield
//     0x283B59  call  0x3351B0                     |  has-state
//     0x283B6F  call  0x33D1E0                     |  SKILLS_GetBaseLevel
//     0x283B7F  mov   r8d,117                      |  skill min damage
//     0x283B8E  call  0x3385B0                     |  >> 8, added to r12d
//     0x283B99  mov   r8d,117                      |  skill max damage
//     0x283BB1  call  0x338160                    /   >> 8, added to r13d
//
//   The vanilla skill id is three plain imm32 loads, which is the one place
//   3.3 is easier than 2.4: 2.4 hid it in a statlist walk comparing a word
//   against 117, and 546 did not fit the imm8 encoding, so it needed a cave.
//   Here it is three dword writes and nothing moves.
//
//   The state the display tests for stays 101. That is deliberate: the server
//   side also keys off state 101 and reads the source skill from the state, so
//   a replacement Holy Shield has to apply state 101 through its skills.txt
//   aurastate column either way.
//
//   After this block the handler folds in the skill's own param3 and param4,
//   the shield's strength and dexterity bonuses, then damagepercent (stat 25),
//   item_mindamage_percent (17) and item_maxdamage_percent (18), and scales
//   the shield damage by the accumulated percent before formatting the line.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where item_normaldamage goes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   2.4 computed the stat-111 contribution on the side, with its own
//   100 + strength + damagepercent factor, and added it to the finished min
//   and max. That double-counted the flat min/max percent term and missed the
//   shield's own strength and dexterity bonuses.
//
//   3.3 gets it for free: adding the stat to r12d and r13d at the point the
//   shield damage is read sends it through the engine's own scaling, which is
//   exactly the treatment the real damage calculation gives it. The displayed
//   number is the scaled sum rather than two separately scaled halves.
//
//   0x2F5020   GetUnitStat(unit, statId, layer) -> int32.

// D2RLoader 1.3.1 accepts a jmp-rel32 patch only when its target is inside
// D2R.exe, and the relay page is not. The read at 0x283B34 therefore jumps to
// a 5-byte "jmp relay" trampoline in the int3 run after the handler's ret at
// 0x283DB4 (0x283DB5..0x283DBF, the next function starts at 0x283DC0), and the
// trampoline jumps on to the relay. A jmp changes no register, flag or stack
// slot, so the relay sees exactly what the read site saw.

// 0x564930 entry, 26 bytes, through the frame setup.
//   push rbp / push rbx / push rsi / push rdi / push r12 / push r14 / push r15
//   lea rbp,[rsp-0x100] / sub rsp,0x200

// 0x2F5020, the unit stat getter (unit in rcx, statId in edx, layer in r8d). In
// D2RLoader 1.3.0 its first ten bytes are a thunk to D2RCore!ReadWideUnitStat:
//   2F5020  FF 25 disp32   jmp [rip+disp32]   disp32 locates the loader's import
//                                             slot and is not compared
//   2F5026  90 90 90 90    nop x4
//   2F502A  ...            the untouched remainder of the old body
// ReadWideUnitStat keys its lookup on the full 32-bit layer register.

// 0x283B34, 16 bytes:
//   movzx r12d, byte ptr [rax+0x10F]
//   movzx r13d, byte ptr [rax+0x110]

// The unit is the one whose sheet is being drawn, and it carries the stat, so
// no owner resolution is needed. A missing stat reads as 0 and the relay's two
// adds become no-ops, which leaves the vanilla line untouched.

// Builds the stub that replaces the 16-byte shield-damage read.
//
// Entry contract, proven by the very bytes being replaced and by the block
// around them: rax is the shield's ItemsTxt record, rsi is the unit, r12d and
// r13d are about to receive the shield's min and max damage, and rcx, rdx and
// r8 already hold the arguments for the call at the resume address.
//
// The two stolen instructions run first, while rax is still the record. Every
// volatile register, the flags and xmm0-5 are then saved, because the replaced
// block contained no call and the surrounding code assumes they survive. The
// bonus is folded into r12d and r13d before the restores, since those two are
// callee-saved and therefore cross the callback untouched. pushfq happens
// before the adds and popfq after them, so the flags the resume point sees are
// the ones it would have seen in vanilla.

// Snapshot Sounds Fix
//
// See celestialrayone.snapshot-sounds-fix.toml for the write-up. In short:
//
//   D2RLoader keeps a "base compiler snapshot" of the unmodded game tables in
//   d2rloader/data/compiler. When it has to build that snapshot (first launch on
//   a machine, after a D2R patch, after a D2RLoader update that changes its
//   compiler, or when the snapshot is damaged), D2RCore's InitializeDataTables
//   runs the game's table load twice:
//
//     pass 1  unmounts mods/<mod>/<mod>.mpq, loads every table from the base
//             game, reloads sounds.txt from the base game for the snapshot
//             (D2RCore 0x267C60), then mounts the mod again.
//     pass 2  frees the tables except sounds.txt and loads everything again from
//             the mod. sounds.txt is never reloaded.
//
//   The session then runs with the base game's sounds.txt while every other table
//   comes from the mod. Every row a mod appended to sounds.txt is missing, so
//   monsounds.txt Taunt, skills.txt sound columns and the rest link to sound rows
//   that do not exist. The first boss taunt that uses one crashes the client in
//   the sound group picker (0x1400DA360, read at 0x1400DA4A0).
//
// The fix: a table may only be linked against a sounds.txt that came from the
// same files. Every sounds.txt load is watched and tagged with whether the mod
// archive was mounted. When the game's table load starts with the mod mounted
// while a bank still holds a sounds.txt loaded without it, that bank's sounds.txt
// is reloaded first, with the same two calls the game makes when it is asked to
// reload sounds itself (0x3016FE..0x30170F). Sessions that never build a snapshot
// are never touched.
//
// Built against D2R 3.3 (module 140000000.D2RLoader.exe, D2RLoader 1.3.1) and
// PluginSDK 0.3.0. Every address below is pinned by a byte check before
// anything is hooked.

// The game's InitializeDataTables(reloadSounds). D2RCore's InitializeDataTables
// export calls it once per pass through its import slot. Banks 2 and 3 are
// loaded; sounds.txt is reloaded first only when reloadSounds is nonzero.

constexpr std::uint8_t ExpectedInitDataTablesPrologue[] {
	0x48, 0x8B, 0xC4,                          // mov rax, rsp
	0x88, 0x48, 0x08,                          // mov [rax+8], cl
	0x55,                                      // push rbp
	0x48, 0x8D, 0x68, 0xA1,                    // lea rbp, [rax-5Fh]
	0x48, 0x81, 0xEC, 0xA0, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,  // sub rsp, 0A0h
};

// Loads sounds.txt into one bank: rebuilds the bank's sound name list (the
// linker every other table resolves sound names against) and its sound rows.
// Callers: sound init 0x1400D9960, the reload block below, and D2RCore 0x267C60.

constexpr std::uint8_t ExpectedLoadSoundsPrologue[] {
	0x48, 0x89, 0x5C, 0x24, 0x10,  // mov [rsp+10h], rbx
	0x48, 0x89, 0x74, 0x24, 0x18,  // mov [rsp+18h], rsi
	0x55,                          // push rbp
	0x57,                          // push rdi
	0x41, 0x56,                    // push r14
};

constexpr std::uint8_t ExpectedSoundReloadBlock[] {
	0x74, 0x10,                    // je past the reload (reloadSounds is 0)
	0x48, 0x8B, 0xCF,              // mov rcx, rdi      bank tables
	0xE8, 0xC8, 0xFE, 0x0A, 0x00,  // call 0x3B15D0     load sounds.txt
	0x48, 0x8B, 0xCF,              // mov rcx, rdi
	0xE8, 0x30, 0xF7, 0x0A, 0x00,  // call 0x3B0E40     finish the load
};

constexpr std::uint8_t ExpectedBankTableRead[] {
	0x48, 0x8D, 0x0D, 0xB9, 0x9A, 0x79, 0x02,  // lea rcx, [rva 2A9A580h]
	0x48, 0x03, 0xC0,                          // add rax, rax
	0x48, 0x8B, 0x04, 0xC1,                    // mov rax, [rcx+rax*8]
};

// Sounds row count of a bank, read only for the log and the console command.
// DATATBLS_GetSoundsTxtRecordCount 0x3B20C0: mov cl, 3 / call 0x300A90 /
// mov rbx, [rax+4E8h].

constexpr std::uint8_t ExpectedSoundsCountRead[] {
	0xB1, 0x03,                                // mov cl, 3
	0xE8, 0xC3, 0xE9, 0xF4, 0xFF,              // call 0x300A90
	0x48, 0x8B, 0x98, 0xE8, 0x04, 0x00, 0x00,  // mov rbx, [rax+4E8h]
};

struct Witness {
	std::uint64_t       rva;
	const std::uint8_t* bytes;
	std::uint32_t       size;
	const char*         what;
};

// Soft Hit Removal
//
// Kills made by holy aura damage, Iron Maiden and reflected damage count as
// real kills again: kill credit, the kill counter and "on kill" procs.
//
// Port of the ESR D2R 2.4 memory patches 385075, 388E2C, 36C647 and 3502EE to
// D2R 3.3 on D2RLoader 1.3.0. Every byte below was read out of the live
// D2RLoader.exe image (base 0x140000000) and disassembled before being relied
// on. Built against PluginSDK v4.
//
// ===========================================================================
// What a soft hit is
// ===========================================================================
// A damage packet carries a 16-bit result-flags word at +4. Flag 0x20 makes
// the hit silent for the event system: the kill and killed events, and the
// damage events, only fire when it is clear (byte-verified on 2.4, same logic
// in D2MOO's SUNITDMG_ExecuteEvents, 3.3 0x44CE80). The sources below force
// that flag on top of the skills.txt ResultFlags column.
//
// ===========================================================================
// Sites
// ===========================================================================
// 1. Holy Fire / Holy Shock, skills.txt srvdofunc 66, sub_1405630E0.
//    Table 0x238EA00, read by the skill do-handler at 0x43AEF9
//    (mov r10,[rcx+rax*8+238EA00h], index bound 190), slot 66 at 0x238EC10.
//      563575  0F B7 87 86 01 00 00  movzx eax, word [rdi+186h]   ; ResultFlags
//      56357C  66 83 C8 20           or    ax, 20h               ; removed
//      563580  66 09 85 94 00 00 00  or    [rbp+94h], ax         ; packet +4
//      563587  8B 87 88 01 00 00     mov   eax, [rdi+188h]       ; HitFlags
//
// 2. Holy Freeze, skills.txt srvdofunc 81, sub_140563720, slot 81 at 0x238EC88.
//    Same block, the or at 563C0B. Identity cross-checked through its target
//    callback 0x561480: the monstats ColdEffect gate and the 20% state 107 roll.
//
// 3. Iron Maiden, event function 4, sub_14055B7E0.
//    Event function table 0x238E5C0 (index 0 null, 5 = Life Tap 0x55BCD0, and
//    7, 9, 14, 15, 16, 19, 20, 21 all match Ruff's corpus), slot 4 at 0x238E5E0.
//    The 3.3 compiler builds the flags in cx, so the 2.4 bytes 66 83 C8 20 do
//    not exist here:
//      55BB87  0F B7 8E 86 01 00 00  movzx ecx, word [rsi+186h]  ; ResultFlags
//      55BB8E  66 0F C5 C1 02        pextrw eax, xmm1, 2         ; 0, zeroed packet
//      55BB93  66 83 C9 20           or    cx, 20h               ; removed
//      55BB97  66 0B C1              or    ax, cx
//      55BB9A  66 89 44 24 54        mov   [rsp+54h], ax         ; packet +4
//
// 4. Attacker Takes Damage, event function 6, sub_140582B20, slot at 0x238E5F0.
//    2.4 wrote mov eax,4021h -> mov eax,1. 3.3 loads the constant into ecx:
//      582B71  8B 85 18 01 00 00     mov   eax, [rbp+118h]       ; stat << 16 | layer
//      582B83  E8 ...                call  2F5C60                ; the item stat value
//      582BC6  B9 21 40 00 00        mov   ecx, 4021h            ; -> mov ecx, 1
//      582BED  66 0B C1              or    ax, cx
//      582BFE  66 89 44 24 34        mov   [rsp+34h], ax         ; packet +4
//      582C08  C7 45 74 8D 00 00 00  mov   [rbp+74h], 8Dh        ; hit class
//
// 5. Thorns percent reflect, sub_1404395E0 (D2MOO SKILLS_ApplyThornsDamage).
//    Reached only through two wrappers: 0x436FC0 passes stat 131 (thorns_percent)
//    and 0x436FE0 passes stat 208. Not part of the 2.4 set, off by default.
//      43975A  B8 21 40 00 00        mov   eax, 4021h            ; -> mov eax, 1
//      439766  66 89 44 24 34        mov   [rsp+34h], ax         ; packet +4
//    43975A is where three branches land. The replacement has the same length,
//    so every entry stays on an instruction boundary.
//
// In every window the flags produced by the changed instruction are dead: the
// next flag-writing instruction overwrites them before anything reads them.
// Stock and patched windows were decoded with capstone and resynchronise on
// the same instruction boundaries right after the change.
//
// Flag 0x4000 (sites 4 and 5): D2MOO names it SOFTHIT. It steers the struck
// unit's hit-reaction handling and gates no event. The 2.4 patch wrote 0x0001,
// so this port writes the same.
//
// Sites 1, 2, 3 and 5 are same-length byte swaps in the game image. Each one is
// checked against a witness window first, read back after writing, and put
// back on unload.
//
// Site 4 lives in D2RCore.dll since D2RLoader 1.3 (checked on 1.3.1). D2RCore
// registers its own handler for event function 6, and that handler never calls
// the game's sub_140582B20, so a change at 582BC6 would never run. D2RCore's
// handler (entry 6 of the table RegisterWideSkillEffect hands its registrar)
// reads the stat and calls D2RCore's reflect helper, which writes the packet's
// result flags itself:
//     helper+67h  66 C7 45 B4 21 40   mov word [rbp-4Ch], 4021h
// Event functions 10 and 11 (element 2 and 1) and one more D2RCore reflect path
// use the same helper, so the store becomes a jump to a small stub that writes
// 0001h only when the helper was called by the event 6 handler, and 4021h in
// every other case, exactly as before. The helper's return address sits at
// [rbp+168h] (six pushes, sub rsp,1B8h, lea rbp,[rsp+80h]). Everything is
// found through the RegisterWideSkillEffect export and checked byte for byte:
// the export stub, the event 6 handler up to its helper call, and the helper
// through the store. The six bytes are put back on unload.

// push rax / mov rax, <event 6 return address> / cmp [rbp+168h], rax / pop rax
// jne stock / mov word [rbp-4Ch], 1 / jmp resume / stock: mov word [rbp-4Ch], 4021h
// resume: jmp qword ptr [rip+0] -> helper+6Dh. No flags are live at the store:
// the next instruction, cmp r14d,3, sets them.

struct Site {
    const char*         key;
    const char*         name;
    std::uint64_t       windowRva;
    const std::uint8_t* window;
    std::uint32_t       windowSize;
    std::uint32_t       patchOffset;
    const std::uint8_t* replacement;
    std::uint32_t       patchSize;
    std::uint64_t       slotRva;       // 0 when the handler is not reached through a table
    std::uint64_t       handlerRva;
    std::uint64_t       identityRva;   // 0 when there is no extra witness
    const std::uint8_t* identity;
    std::uint32_t       identitySize;
    bool                enabledByDefault;
};

// Specializations
//
// A specialization system in one plugin:
//   - a "Specialization" entry on the character sheet: a + while the character
//     has points to spend, a - once it has learned something and has no point
//     left. Both open the specialization window.
//   - the specialization window: a plugin panel whose layout and art belong to
//     the mod.
//   - learning a perk: the host writes the perk's stat on the character (its
//     own value, saved with the character) and takes one point.
//   - Magic and Physical resistance rows on the character sheet.
// The perks do nothing by themselves. The mod gives their stats a meaning
// (softcode, other plugins). README.md is the setup guide for modders.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Rules
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Points are the character's own value of points_stat (what a pSpell, a quest
//   or another plugin adds; points granted by items are not counted). A perk is
//   learned when the character's own value of its stat is above 0. A perk can
//   be learned when
//     - the character has at least 1 point,
//     - no perk of another specialization is learned (the first perk picks the
//       specialization for good),
//     - every row above it has a learned perk of this specialization,
//     - no perk of its own row is learned.
//   With Shift a learned perk is unlearned when respec_stat is set, the
//   character's own value of it is at least unlearn_cost and no perk of a row
//   below it is learned (only the deepest learned row, so the rows stay taken
//   in order). Its stat goes back to 0, the point comes back and unlearn_cost
//   is taken from respec_stat.
//   The client only asks. The host checks the same rules against its own copy
//   of the character before it writes anything.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The character sheet (client)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   14E9F80 is the keyboard/mouse CharacterStatsPanel update. It calls
//   14E6E70 (panel, player) for the resistance rows: that loop walks magic,
//   fire, lightning, cold and poison, but has no text field for magic, and
//   physical is not in it. 14E6E70 is hooked: after the game fills its rows the
//   plugin fills MagicText and PhysicalText the way the stock rows are filled.
//     value    the stat total (2F5020)
//     floor    the sheet's own floor, read live from 14E7299 (mov eax, imm32),
//              so a patch that moves it moves these two rows with the others
//     magic    cap = min(maxmagicresist + 75, 95). The 75 (14E728E) and the 95
//              (14E7295) are read live from the stock loop. No difficulty
//              penalty: the damage code 4523E0 adds it to every resistance
//              except stats 36 and 37.
//     physical cap = the damage code's own physical cap, read live from 4524D6
//              (4523E0 uses it when the resistance has no max stat)
//     colour   gold at the cap, red below 0, otherwise the panel's default
//              colour (panel +168h), the stock rule of 14E72BC..14E72FC. The
//              text colour of a TextBoxWidget is at +0BCh; 908690 (out, index,
//              5) gives a colour by index (1 red, 4 gold).
//     text     with 846210() set, the d2r:CharacterSheetResistanceStat format,
//              otherwise the bare number, as 14E7303 does.
//   The same hook drives SpecializationButton and SpecializationTitle: 856220
//   (widget, name) finds a direct child, vtable +50h sets visible, vtable +58h
//   gives the widget's type descriptor. SpecializationButton is one button with
//   six frames (+ normal, hovered, pressed, then - normal, hovered, pressed).
//   Two buttons on one spot do not work: a hidden widget still takes the mouse,
//   so the hidden one on top got every press and the visible one never showed
//   its pressed frame. The plugin writes the button's frame numbers instead
//   (normalFrame +7E8h, pressedFrame +7ECh, hoveredFrame +7F0h, disabledFrame
//   +7F4h, the shown frame +710h; 870200 presses with them), and while hidden
//   the button is disabled (+67Ah, cleared by 86EFB0), so its spot takes no
//   press, no sound and no click.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The window (client)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   An SDK plugin panel, localId SpecializationPanel. D2RCore loads a plugin
//   panel from data/global/ui/layouts/<plugin-id>/<localId>hd.json, whose root
//   must be named <plugin-id>/<localId>. The layout is compiled into the
//   plugin (SpecializationPanelLayout.inc, generated from
//   SpecializationPanelhd.json) and registered at that path, so a mod needs no
//   layout file; a mod replaces it with its own
//     data/global/ui/layouts/celestialrayone.specializations/SpecializationPanelhd.json
//   Text in a plugin panel must be a full reference (@d2r:Key), or D2RCore
//   refuses the layout; mod strings loaded the normal way are d2r: strings.
//   Widget names, all optional (a missing widget is skipped); s = specialization,
//   r = row, p = perk, all from 1:
//     Tab<s>, TabActive<s> (viewed), TabChosen<s> (chosen), TabLocked<s>
//     Page<s>                container of specialization s, shown while viewed;
//                            images only: Glow<r>_<p> (can be learned now),
//                            Learned<r>_<p>, Locked<r>_<p> (shade on a perk not
//                            learned and not learnable), plus the icons
//                            (Icon<r>_<p>, which the plugin leaves alone)
//     Hover<r>_<p>           one hover area per perk position, shared by all
//                            pages, after the pages so nothing hidden sits on it
//     Perk<r>_<p>            optional, at window level: shown only where a click
//                            does something; hovering it counts as the hover area
//     Tip<r>_<p>             the tooltip under that position: TipBack1..TipBack14
//                            (background for that many text lines), TipTitle,
//                            TipText, TipHint1..TipHint13 (the gray unlearn line,
//                            the box of the text line it starts on). The plugin
//                            breaks the text into lines for a TipText 672 units
//                            wide at $XMediumFontSize (TipLineWidth, GlyphWidth)
//                            and shows the background of exactly that height.
//     PointsAvailable        text box: @d2r:SpecializationsPointsAvailable, %d the
//                            unspent points
//   Click messages, all "PanelManager:OpenPanel:<text>", consumed by the plugin:
//     SpecializationsOpen               open / close the window
//     SpecializationsClose              close it
//     SpecializationsTab<s>             view specialization s
//     SpecializationsPerk.<r>.<p>       learn (or, with Shift, unlearn) that perk
//                                       of the specialization on screen (optional:
//                                       a click on a perk's hover area does the same)
//   Every page's widgets sit at the same place. Hidden pages still take mouse
//   input for their children, so the hover areas exist once, at window level.
//   A left click (right click with swapped buttons) released over the same
//   hover area it was pressed on learns that perk, or unlearns it with Shift.
//   Space closes the window like the game's own panels (not while the chat
//   line has the keyboard). With no points and no learned perk the window does
//   not open: a hidden button still takes clicks on its spot.
//   The UI keeps the hovered widget at +178h of the object 846020 returns (the
//   widget tooltip drawer 875980 tests it). The window is found by name, as an
//   ancestor of the hovered widget (parent at +30h) or among the children of
//   the UI root (children at +58h, count at +60h, name at +8h, 85622F).
//   While the window is open a UI task refreshes it on every root widget update.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The host
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   A click sends (player unit id, specialization, row, perk) over the plugin's
//   network channel. The host runs the request on its game thread: the player
//   is found through the server's client registry (485290 / 485320 / 484CDB,
//   48FE80), the rules are checked on the server unit, then 2F7D10 writes the
//   perk's own value and the points. Rows with Saved = 1 reach the client by
//   themselves.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Native pieces, D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image, all checked byte for byte at
// load. Nothing is hooked when a check fails.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   14E6E70  sheet resistance rows (panel, player), hooked
//   14E728C  lea ecx, [rdi+disp8] / cmp ecx, imm8 / jl / mov ecx, imm32
//   14E7299  mov eax, imm32 (the sheet floor) and the clamp after it
//   4524D5   mov eax, imm32 (the physical cap in the damage code)
//   856220   child widget by name     86DD50  text box set text
//   846020   UI state object          875980  widget tooltip drawer (+178h, +190h)
//   8562A0   widget rectangle (parent at +30h)
//   908690   colour by index          846210  resistance format switch
//   2F5020   stat total (unit, stat, layer), loader thunk into D2RCore
//   2F48C0   own value (unit, stat, layer), loader thunk into D2RCore
//   2F7D10   own value write (unit, stat, value, layer), loader thunk
//   34B9D0   unit type    34A330  unit id    8B2D0 / 9A480  local player
//   48FE80   server unit lookup (game, type, id)
//   485290 / 485320 / 484CDB  the server's client registry (2AAECA0, ready
//            flag 2AAF4A0)

// 14E6E70 prologue, the bytes the hook displaces: push rbp / push rbx /
// push rdi / push r12 / push r13 / push r14 / push r15 / lea rbp, [rsp-170h] /
// sub rsp, 270h. Nothing relative; the next instruction is the rip-relative
// cookie load.

// 875980: push rdi / sub rsp, 40h / mov rdi, rcx / call 846020 /
// cmp [rax+178h], rdi / je / call 846020 / cmp [rax+190h], rdi / jne.
// 87598E (875980 + 0Eh): cmp [rax+178h], rdi / jz / call / cmp [rax+190h], rdi /
// jnz. Only these two offsets are used, so the witness starts after the
// prologue: the respec plugin hooks 875980 itself, which rewrites the prologue.
// 870200, the button press: add rcx, 680h (its image) / mov edx, [rbx+7ECh]
// (pressedFrame) / call 859700 (image frame = edx, stored at image+90h) /
// mov byte [rbx+679h], 1 (pressed).

// 86EFB0, the button enable: mov eax, [rcx+7F4h] (disabledFrame) / mov byte
// [rcx+67Ah], 0 (disabled) / cmp [rcx+710h], eax (the shown frame).
// 1A0C00 plays a sound by its sounds.txt name: rcx name, rdx/r8d/r9d 0
// (1414E9F1C, the character sheet's stat point, plays cursor_stat_point_one_hd
// this way). It looks the name up (1D9760) and plays that row (1A0780); a name
// sounds.txt does not have plays nothing. Row 0 is ESOUND_NONE: silence.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
    bool                callable;
};

// Spelldesc Decimals
//
// Lets an item's spelldesc string show its number with decimals: the string
// uses %.1f, %.2f or %.3f and calc1 gives the number in tenths, hundredths or
// thousandths.
//
// Everything below was read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.0 process image and is
// checked byte for byte at load. Nothing is installed when a check fails.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where the number is printed
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The spelldesc line builder 2C1B80 (items record in rdi) evaluates calc1
//   ([rdi+0A4h]) with the item formula evaluator 3B4880, which returns an int.
//   Modes 4 and 2 (mode 2 after its stat1 life or mana scaling at 2C1D5F) both
//   continue at 2C1C93 and print the number through one call:
//     2C1C93  0F B7 8F B6 00 00 00   movzx ecx, word [rdi+0B6h]  ; spelldescstr
//     2C1C9A  E8 91 9A B6 03         call  GetNamespacedStringById
//                                    (loader thunk 3E2B730 in 1.3.1, 3E2B65C
//                                    in 1.3.0; the only byte change)
//     2C1C9F  48 8B D0               mov   rdx, rax              ; the string
//     2C1CA2  48 8D 8C 24 80 00 00 00  lea rcx, [rsp+80h]        ; 512-byte buffer
//     2C1CAA  44 8B C3               mov   r8d, ebx              ; the number
//     2C1CAD  E8 DE 7F E4 FF         call  109C90                ; format
//   109C90 is int f(char* out, const char* format, ...): it spills the
//   variadic registers and calls the game's formatter 122A670 with a 512-byte
//   limit. Mode 3 prints its number with a hard-coded "%d" at 2C1D14 instead,
//   so it keeps whole numbers.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The change
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The call at 2C1CAD goes to HookedFormat instead, which receives the same
//   three registers. When the string's first conversion is %.1f, %.2f or
//   %.3f, it writes the number as fixed point (value / 10^N with N decimals,
//   in exact integer maths), swaps that conversion for %s and hands the new
//   string and the digits to 109C90. Any other string goes to 109C90 exactly
//   as the game passed it.

// skill_srcdam_calc_plugin.cpp
//
// Drives skills.txt "SrcDam" from a skills.txt formula column (calc1 .. calc10)
// instead of the static byte in the row.
//
// Port of the 2.4 "skills.txt srcdam -> prgcalc3" three-entry byte patch set to
// D2R 3.3.93847. The 2.4 version needed a hook plus a 140-byte code cave because
// it had to splice into the middle of the skill-start function; 3.3 exposes a
// clean server entry point and a proper formula-evaluator wrapper, so the whole
// thing is one inline hook on a function entry with no cave.
//
// Verified against the D2RLoader image (base 0x140000000):
//   0x0043ACB0  D2GAME_SKILLS_ServerDoHandler(game, unit, skillId, skillLevel,
//                                             consumeResources, itemCast, itemEffect)
//               Resolves the skills.txt row at entry, then dispatches srvdofunc.
//   0x00097790  DATATBLS_GetSkillsTxtRecordForContext(dataContext, skillId)
//               Record stride 0x2EC (748 bytes).
//   0x003B5160  SKILLS_EvaluateSkillFormula(dataContext, unit, calcHandle,
//                                           skillId, skillLevel)
//               Bounds-checks the handle against the expression pool itself and
//               returns 0 for a blank or out-of-range cell.
//   0x003BFFC1  cmovne ecx, eax inside the missile damage init (0x003BF9B0).
//               Vanilla discards the skills.txt srcdam whenever the missile's own
//               missiles.txt SrcDamage cell is blank (0xFF).
//
// Record offsets, taken from the skills.txt column table in the loader at
// 0x00302380 and cross-checked against the code that reads them:
//   SrcDam  byte  at 589   (read at 0x003BFEF8)
//   calc1 .. calc10 dwords at 400, 404, 408, 412, 416, 420, 424, 428, 432, 436
//
// The SrcDam byte lives in the shared compiled skills table, so a written value
// stays there until something writes it again. The row's static SrcDam is
// captured the first time the plugin touches it, put back whenever the formula
// evaluates to 0 or less, and put back on unload.

using EvaluateSkillFormulaFn = std::int32_t(__fastcall*)(
	std::uint8_t  dataContext,
	void*         unit,
	std::uint32_t calcHandle,
	std::int32_t  skillId,
	std::int32_t  skillLevel) noexcept;

// Staffmods: the class skills an item rolls because of its item type
// (itemtypes.txt StaffMods), shown as "+N to <Skill> (<Class> Only)".
//
// See celestialrayone.staffmods.toml for the behaviour write-up.
//
// THE ROUTINE. 0x58AF50 (game, item, itemLevel, firstClassSkill,
// classSkillCount, dropBonus) rolls the staffmods of every non-Classic item.
// Its only caller, 0x58A050, takes the class from the item type's StaffMods,
// the class skill count from 0x33CB30 and the first class skill from 0x33DDE0,
// and sends Classic-format items (item format 0) to the separate classic
// routine 0x58B7C0 instead. Staffmods are rolled after the affixes are applied
// (the rare roll 0x58A120 calls 0x58A050 at 0x58A536, after its prefixes and
// suffixes are assigned and their properties written).
//
// THE CHANGE. The routine is replaced by the same steps, rolled in the same
// order on the item seed, plus a level cap:
//   cap = the item's Required Level before staffmods, at least 1, from the
//         game's own Required Level function 0x376DE0 (a loader thunk into
//         D2RLoader's ReadWideItemLevelRequirement, which handles a null unit
//         on its own path) called with no unit, so no class adjustment.
//   A staffmod whose tier is above the cap (tier reqlevel > cap) drops to the
//   highest tier at or below the cap that has skills for the class. A skill
//   whose skills.txt reqlevel is above the cap is never given (a failed try).
// When the cap allows every tier, every roll and result is the vanilla one.
//
// VANILLA STEPS, KEPT:
//   count: dropBonus + rand(100): >90 three, >70 two, >30 (or any dropBonus)
//     one, otherwise none
//   base tier from the item level: <=11 1, <=18 2, <=24 3, <=36 4, above 36
//     5 (item format 100+) or 4
//   per staffmod: rand(100): >80 +1, >30 +0, >10 -1, otherwise -2; at least
//     1; low quality (quality 1) at most 4
//   Reign of the Warlock games (data context 3): tier t is the class skills
//     whose reqlevel is exactly 1/6/12/18/24/30; an empty tier gives that
//     staffmod nothing. Other games: firstClassSkill + 5*(t-1) + rand(5).
//   6 tries per staffmod; a try fails on a skills.txt itypea1 the item is not
//     or on a skill the item already rolled. (Vanilla accepted a try past the
//     end of skills.txt; it is a failed try here, since its reqlevel cannot
//     be checked. Unreachable with real data.)
//   value: 1 on low quality items of format 100+, otherwise
//     dropBonus/2 + rand(100): >=90 3, >=60 2, otherwise 1. Written as
//     item_singleskill (107), layer = skill id, into the item's stat list with
//     flag 40h, allocated and posted when missing.
//
// THE SKILL ID ARGUMENT. The stat write 0x2F7C00 is a loader thunk into
// D2RCore's SetWideListStat, which reads its fifth argument (the layer, here
// the skill id) as a full 32-bit value and keeps all 32 bits in the stat's key.
// The game's own code only writes the low 16 bits of that argument, so
// D2RLoader patches the original routine (0x58B683 -> 0x143E2D7A0) to zero the
// upper 16 bits before the call. This plugin replaces that routine, so it must
// pass the skill id as a whole 32-bit value itself: with only 16 bits written,
// the upper half is left over from the stack, the item keeps a skill id past
// the end of skills.txt (the extended save format writes all 32 bits of a stat
// parameter, so the bad id reaches the client and the save file), and the
// staffmod shows as an empty tooltip line.
//
// Built against D2R 3.3 in the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image (base
// 0x140000000), PluginSDK 0.3.0. The hook site and every helper entry are
// checked byte for byte before anything is installed.

struct Witness {
	std::uint64_t       rva;
	const std::uint8_t* bytes;
	std::uint32_t       size;
	const char*         name;
};

// Stance Dance
//
// "Stance Dance" in the Controls menu, in its own "Ascendancies" section, with
// no default key. While the ascendancy gives the stance stat (itemstatcost row
// 681 by default), each press moves the character's stat 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1. The
// rules are in stance_rules.h.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The stat
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The ascendancy item gives the stat 1. The plugin owns the character's own
//   value of the stat (its base value, apart from items and states) and keeps
//   it at 0, 1 or 2, so the total the game reads is 1, 2 or 3. It writes the
//   host's copy of the character; for rows with Saved = 1 the game sends the
//   change on to that player's client.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// From the key to the host
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The Controls action queues a UI task, which reads the local player's unit
//   id and sends it to the host over the plugin's network channel. Single
//   player and a TCP/IP host connect to their own host inside the process; a
//   TCP/IP client connects over the network. A press made before the channel
//   is up waits (up to 5 seconds) and is sent when it connects. Holding the
//   key is one press: a press counts only after the key was released.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The host
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   A game-thread task runs once per game update. It re-arms itself through a
//   UI task, so it never queues itself on the queue that is running it. Each
//   run:
//     1. lists every player: the server's client registry, each client's
//        player resolved through the game's own server unit lookup
//     2. applies the key presses that arrived, each to the player whose unit
//        id it carries, once D2RLoader confirms the sender is a player of the
//        game
//     3. keeps every player's part legal: removed without the ascendancy,
//        otherwise within 0 .. 3 - ascendancy
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Native pieces, D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image, all checked byte for byte at
// load. Nothing is patched or hooked.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   2F5020  stat read (unit, stat, layer), loader thunk into D2RCore
//   2F48C0  own value read (unit, stat, layer), loader thunk into D2RCore
//   2F7D10  own value write (unit, stat, value, layer), loader thunk into
//           D2RCore
//   34B9D0  unit type ([unit+0])      34A330  unit id ([unit+8])
//   8B2D0   local player index        9A480   local player from that index
//   48FE80  server unit lookup (game, type, id): the game's hash buckets for
//           that type, chained through unit+158h
//   The server's client registry: 256 buckets at 2AAECA0, ready flag 2AAF4A0
//     485290  registry reset: zeroes the 256 buckets, then sets the flag
//     485320  client lookup by id: the id at +0, the next client at +630h
//     484CDB  in the client's player lookup: unit type +26Ch, unit id
//             +270h, game +2B0h, then 48FE80
//   Clients are added (484150) and removed (485AB0) only together with their
//   game, on the game thread, so the registry is read only from game-thread
//   tasks.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

//
// Stance Dance rules, kept apart from the game code so they can be tested on
// their own.
//
//   total       the stance stat as the game reads it, everything added up
//   base        the character's own value of that stat: the plugin's part
//   ascendancy  total - base: what the ascendancy item (and anything else
//               that is not the character's own value) gives
//
// The ascendancy gives 1 and the plugin's part is 0, 1 or 2, so the total is
// 1, 2 or 3. A key press moves it 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Finisher state protection  -  D2RLoader plugin (D2R 3.3)
//
// Port of the two ESR 2.4 memory patches:
//     "Protect states 208/223/234/250 from finisher teardown - Hook"  35623C
//     "Protect states 208/223/234/250 from finisher teardown - Cave"  19E7D0
//
// What it does
//     Every server tick the game walks a unit's progressive/aura states and,
//     for each one, calls a small helper that subtracts the skill's decquant
//     from the remaining charge count. When the count reaches zero (or the
//     skill row pointer is null) the helper takes the EXPIRE path and the
//     state is torn off the unit. For a handful of ESR states that must
//     survive the finisher discharge, this plugin intercepts the expire path,
//     compares the state id against a configured list and, on a match, does
//     exactly what the "a charge still remains" path does: it sets the
//     survive flag in the caller's frame and returns without running either
//     expire call. Any other state id replays the two stolen instructions and
//     falls straight back into the stock expire, byte for byte.
//
//     The survive flag is the part that keeps the CLIENT overlay alive. The
//     caller reads that byte back after the loop; when it is clear it runs the
//     state-bitmap merge and the refresh that tells the client the state is
//     gone. Setting it keeps the server state AND stops the client being told
//     to drop the overlay. Leaving it out was the bug that took the 2.4
//     version three iterations to find.
//
// 2.4 -> 3.3 map (every line re-derived from the 3.3 image, nothing carried
// over on trust)
//
//     role                     2.4 (D2R.exe)       3.3 (D2RLoader image)
//     -----------------------  ------------------  ---------------------
//     charge/expire helper     sub_1403560C0       sub_14056BFE0  (0x1B3)
//     its caller, the state    sub_140355B00       sub_14056C3E0  (0x5F4)
//       walk + srvdofunc
//       dispatch
//     arg-pack register        rbx (3560C9)        rdi (56BFE6)
//     count > 0 gate           356107 jle 35623C   56C041 jle 56C15C
//     skill row != 0 gate      356115 jz  35623C   56C04E jz  56C15C
//     survive flag write       35611B/35611F       56C054/56C05D
//     expire block entry       35623C              56C15C   <- hook site
//     resume after stolen      356243              56C163
//     clean exit               356264              56C188
//     srvdofunc table          1414BF500           14238EA00
//
//     The arg pack grew from 7 slots to 11 and the order changed, so the two
//     offsets the cave uses moved:
//
//     pack slot                2.4        3.3
//     -----------------------  ---------  ---------
//     &pUnit                   +0x18      +0x00
//     &pGame                   -          +0x08
//     &skillId                 +0x00      +0x10
//     &progressiveCount        +0x08      +0x18
//     &rollFlagByte            -          +0x20
//     &pSkillsTxtRecord        +0x10      +0x28
//     &pStatList               +0x20      +0x30
//     &surviveFlagByte         +0x28      +0x38     <- written on a match
//     &stateId (int16)         +0x30      +0x40     <- read and compared
//     &auraStatCalc inputs     -          +0x48, +0x50
//
//     Proof for the two that matter, read straight out of the image:
//       56C08D  mov rax,[rdi+40h] / 56C09A movsx edx,word [rax] / call
//               STATES_ToggleState(unit, stateId, 1)      -> +0x40 is the id
//       56C054  mov rax,[rdi+38h] / 56C05D mov byte [rax],1 -> +0x38 is the flag
//     And the caller builds all eleven slots unconditionally, one lea of its
//     own stack locals after another, at 56C554..56C5BB - so every slot is a
//     valid pointer into one ~0x120 byte frame on every call.
//
// How it is installed
//     The 2.4 version parked its cave in the body of an unused client skill
//     function. That is what later armed a crash when a skills.txt row started
//     using that column number. Here the same ~120 bytes live in a page the
//     plugin allocates within jump range of the image, so no game function is
//     stomped and no txt column can ever dispatch into it. The only byte the
//     plugin writes inside D2R's own code is the 7-byte jmp at the expire
//     block entry, and it is written only after the loader has verified the
//     original bytes.
//
//     The frame-locality guard from the 2.4 cave is kept as-is: before
//     dereferencing a pack slot the relay checks the pointer lands within
//     +/-4KB of the pack itself. The pack slots are the caller's own stack
//     locals, so a real slot always passes and a corrupted one is never
//     touched. 2.4 measured this: no guard left 392 faults in the stress
//     emulation, a null+canonical guard left 140, this guard left 0.
//
// Console command "finisherstates" reports the install state and how many
// expires were seen and protected.
//
// Runtime settings live in
//     d2rloader/config/celestialrayone.finisher-state-protection.toml
// The file is written with the defaults below on first load.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The clean exit: restores rsi, then add rsp,40h / pop rdi / ret. No
// dereference on the way out, which is why the protected path lands here and
// not on the epilogue at 56C18D (that one is only reachable before rsi is
// spilled).

// 56C163 mov rdx,[rdx] / mov rcx,[rcx] / call STATLIST expire
// 56C16E mov rax,[rdi+8] ... movzx ecx,byte[rcx+106h] / call STATLIST free
// 56C188 mov rsi,[rsp+50h]

// 56C03B mov rax,[rdi+18h] / cmp [rax],esi / jle 56C15C
// 56C047 mov rax,[rdi+28h] / cmp [rax],rsi / jz  56C15C
// 56C054 mov rax,[rdi+38h] / nop / mov [rsp+20h],esi / mov byte[rax],1
// Proves both gates land on the hook site and that +0x38 is the survive flag.
// D2RLoader 1.3.1 widened the store at 56C059 from 16 bits (66 89 74 24 20,
// mov [rsp+20h],si) to 32 bits (90 89 74 24 20). The relay never replays it.

// D2RLoader 1.3.1 accepts a jmp-rel32 patch only when its target is inside
// D2R.exe, and the relay page is not. The hook at 56C15C therefore jumps to a
// 5-byte "jmp relay" trampoline in the int3 run after sub_14056BFE0's ret at
// 56C192 (56C193..56C19F, the next function starts at 56C1A0), and the
// trampoline jumps on to the relay. A jmp changes no register, flag or stack
// slot, so the relay sees exactly what the hook site saw.

// 56C9B0 test eax,eax / jnz / cmp [rsp+40h],al / jnz / mov rcx,[rbp+38h] /
// call the state-bitmap merge + client refresh. This is the consumer of the
// survive flag: the merge runs only while the flag is still clear.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t size;
    const char* what;
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relay page layout
//
//   +0x0000  0xCC filler. Code starts past any conceivable prolog length so
//            the host function's unwind data describes the relay correctly.
//   +0x0100  relay code (93 + 7 * stateCount bytes, 205 max)
//   +0x01D0  resume slot   -> ExpireResumeRva
//   +0x01D8  clean exit    -> CleanExitRva
//   +0x1000  protected counter (second page, read/write, never executable)
//   +0x1008  passed-through counter
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relay code
//
// Entered from 56C15C with rdi = the arg pack. rax, rcx and rdx are all dead
// there: the stock code at 56C15C..56C166 overwrites rcx and rdx before using
// them, and rax is not read again until 56C16E. Flags are dead too, the
// conditional jump that reached this block has already consumed them.
//
//     lock inc [passed-through counter]      only on the stock path
//     mov   rax,[rdi+40h]                    &stateId
//     mov   rdx,rax / sub rdx,rdi
//     add   rdx,1000h / cmp rdx,2000h / ja   frame-locality guard
//     movzx edx,word [rax]                   the state id
//     cmp   dx,<id> / je protected           once per configured id
//   stock:
//     mov   rdx,[rdi+30h]                    replay the two stolen
//     mov   rcx,[rdi]                        instructions
//     jmp   [resume slot]                    -> 56C163, stock expire
//   protected:
//     lock inc [protected counter]
//     mov   rax,[rdi+38h]                    &surviveFlag
//     <the same frame-locality guard>
//     mov   byte [rax],1                     what 56C05D does when a charge
//     jmp   [exit slot]                      remains  -> 56C188, clean exit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The relay is entered mid-function with the host's frame already established,
// so a stack walk out of it must use the host's unwind data. Code starts
// 0x100 into the page, past any prolog length, so the whole host frame unwinds.

// ===========================================================================
// Summon Enhancements (celestialrayone.summon-enhancements)
// ===========================================================================
//
// Formerly Bind Demon Persistence and Auras. Server side.
//   - Every bound demon is saved and restored, for every class.
//   - The summons of the skills in summon_skills are saved and come back
//     when the character enters the world.
//   - Bound demons with Aura Enchanted roll their aura from a list.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. How the game saves a bound demon (D2R 3.3.93847, D2RLoader image)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Player save 52F090 writes the demon block last, through 533AE0, and only
//   in a RotW game (Game+106h == 3). It runs twice per save: once into a 32 KB
//   measuring buffer, once into the real buffer.
//   533AE0 (game, player, cursor**, end) writes one block:
//     word 1, dword 666Ch with byte 2 = "has demon", then 5Ch header bytes and
//     the stat blob. The demon comes from the serializer 533CE0, which refuses
//     unless: the player is class 7, 4FF1A0 (game, owner, 21, 1) returns the
//     FIRST pet of pettype 21, that pet has state 207 (3351B0), the monster
//     has a monpet.txt row (D2RCore GetMonPetRecordForUnit), and the block
//     fits ("[PLAYERSAVEWrite] Save too large, Demon will be deleted.").
//     So one demon at most, and only whichever is first in the pet list.
//   4FF1A0 (game, owner, petType, includeFlagged): player data 34B240 +98h is
//     the pet container, its first qword the bucket array, 20h bytes per pet
//     type: +0 first node, +8 count, +0Ch max, +10h pool. Node: +4 unit guid,
//     +18h next. The unit comes from 48FE80 (game, 1, guid).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. How the game restores it
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Load 52E910 calls the block reader 531510 (game, player, cursor**, end,
//   version) and then never looks at the cursor again, so bytes after the
//   block are ignored. 531510 checks word 1 and dword 666Ch (byte 2 <= 1),
//   copies the header and blob and calls the restore 531790, which recreates
//   the monster and binds it again (51E0F0 -> 51EF90). The bind adds the pet
//   through 501550, which first sets the pettype cap with 501830 (game, owner,
//   type, petmax, enforce) and trims pets over the cap. petmax is worked out
//   while the save is still loading, before items and +skills count.
//   A block is 62h bytes plus the blob, whose size is the header's first word.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Every bound demon (save_every_demon)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Save: 533AE0 is called once per bound demon. During each call 4FF1A0
//   returns that demon instead of the first pet, so every native rule above
//   still decides whether the demon is written. A demon the game refuses is
//   rolled back and named in the log with the rule it failed. With no demon
//   written the native call runs once as before (an empty block).
//   Load: after the native block, every following block that holds a demon is
//   read by the same native reader. While a save loads, the Bind Demon cap
//   (pettype 21) is raised to fit the demons already restored plus the one
//   being added, so a cap worked out before items count cannot remove them.
//   Without the plugin the game reads only the first block and ignores the
//   rest, so the character still loads with its first demon.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Bound demons of any class (demon_all_classes)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   533CE0 compares the class with 7 at 533D38 (cmp eax,7 / jne), and the
//   compiler reuses that 7 to build the 4FF1A0 arguments right after it
//   (lea r9d,[rax-6] / mov rdx,rdi / lea r8d,[rax+0Eh] = 1, player, 21).
//   Removing only the jne would hand other classes a wrong pettype, so the
//   20 bytes 533D38..533D4B become mov r9d,1 / mov rdx,rdi / mov r8d,21 /
//   nop5: the Warlock's own arguments for every class. 533D4C (mov rcx,r13)
//   and the call follow unchanged. No branch of 533CE0..5341BE lands inside
//   the range.
//   Restore: 531790 only rebuilds a demon when 33DD40 (player, Bind Demon, 1)
//   finds the skill; the id is the mov edx at 531808 (382 in the stock image,
//   692 after warlock-hardcoded-ids-fix.json), read live. 52E910 reads the
//   items before the demon block, but item stats only count later, so a
//   character that has Bind Demon only from items (a set bonus oskill) does
//   not have it yet. Its demon blocks are kept by the plugin instead of being
//   handed to 531510, and given to the same native reader when the character
//   enters the world: 485350 (client, player, game, room, x, y) places the
//   player in a room and ends with 53BC60 (game, player), which warps every
//   pet to the player through 500A40. After the native call the plugin reads
//   the kept blocks (the demon is created without a room, as at load) and
//   calls 53BC60 again to place it. A character that still has no Bind Demon
//   keeps the blocks for its next world entry in the same game.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Summons (save_summons, summon_skills)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Save: after the demon blocks, one block: "SUMN", format 1, a reserved
//   byte, a u16 count, then per summon the skills.txt id and the monstats row
//   (u16 each). A pet is matched to a listed skill by pettype (skill record
//   +112h); when several listed skills share the pettype, by summon monster
//   (+110h). pettype 21 (binddemon) is left to the demon blocks.
//   Load: the block is read after the demon blocks and kept until the
//   character enters the world (485350, after the native call), so skill
//   levels and petmax count items. Each summon is made again the way its
//   srvdofunc (skill record +4Eh, read by the do handler at 43AED3 to index
//   238EA00) makes it, at the player's feet: SummonPet 4350E0 with flag 1
//   and the player's path position (341A20 x, 341A30 y of unit+38h), the
//   arguments block laid out as srvdofunc 31 builds it at 55E6F5.
//     16  558000 Valkyrie: class and mode from 433800, SummonPet, 55FB50
//         with 0, the +194h calc as the pet level for 55EF80, 48B890,
//         48B720 at frame +20, state 93, 34EF50, 58D950.
//     31  55E440 skeletons: 55EDB0, 433800, SummonPet, 55FB50 with 0, 55A5D0
//         with element 0 (ESR's montype.txt element column is empty), 55EF80,
//         55EC60, 58D950.
//     56  55C080 golems: summon +110h, mode +113h, SummonPet, 55B040,
//         48FDE0 then 480AD0, 58D950.
//     119 575990 druid summons: 433800, SummonPet, 58D950, 55FB50 with the
//         +194h calc (at least 1), 55EF80.
//   Left out, because they belong to casting: the target corpse of 31 and of
//   56's corpse variant, the target check 432B70 of 119, and 34E790, which
//   sets the "srvdofunc ran" flag 40h of unit+124h. petmax is the +114h calc
//   at the current level; a summon is skipped when it works out below 1, and
//   the pet list trims to petmax as usual. The skill and its level come from
//   33DD40 and 33D1E0 (the restore's own calls at 531814 and 531A1B).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. Where a bound demon's aura is rolled (aura list feature)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Monster mod 30 (Aura Enchanted) runs D2RCore!ApplyAuraEnchanted(unit, mod,
//   flag). The game's monster mod table slot 0x2395FE0 + 30*8 points at it
//   through a loader thunk, and D2RCore!ApplyBindDemonBoundStats also calls it
//   directly. A demon reaches it at three moments:
//     a. New bind: the bind conversion 51EF90 adds the mods from Bind Demon's
//        skills.txt calc2-calc5 through ApplyUMod 4995E0, called at 51F333.
//     b. End of every bind, new or restored: 51EF90 calls
//        D2RCore!ApplyBindDemonBoundStats at 51F3AE. With
//        aura_enchanted_selection "3.2" it rolls Aura Enchanted again for a
//        demon that has mod 30. With "3.1" it does not.
//     c. Restore: 531790 re-applies the saved mods through 49B3D0, called at
//        5319FF, before it runs the bind conversion. 49B3D0 is only called
//        when the saved rank flags are nonzero (5319E9), which is why a plain
//        monster comes back without its mods.
//   D2RCore's roll keys on the monster's name seed (38E370): slot =
//   (seed * 6AC690C5h + 29Ah) mod slots, level = monster level / divisor,
//   clamped 1..99, then set-skill 433910 (unit, skill, level, 1) and
//   select-skill 438A70 (unit, 0, skill, -1), the active right-hand aura.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 7. What the aura list feature does
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   - A detour on D2RCore!ApplyAuraEnchanted. While the plugin has marked a
//     unit as the demon being bound or restored (thread local), a roll for
//     that unit picks from the TOML list instead. Every other unit goes to
//     the original routine. The detour copies the 19 byte prologue (8 pushes,
//     sub rsp,148h, no relative operands) into a trampoline.
//   - Three call relays mark the demon around the calls in 6a, 6b and 6c. The
//     relay for 6b also rolls once after the call when D2RCore did not (the
//     "3.1" setting), so every bound demon with mod 30 gets a list aura.
//   - One call relay on 51F2C6, where 51EF90 hands normal monsters and minions
//     a new random name seed through D2RCore!PrepareBindDemonRules. For a
//     restore (51EF90's newBind is 0) of a demon with mod 30 the relay passes
//     the demon's current seed, which 49B3D0 just restored from the save, so
//     a restored minion keeps its aura. The relay stub reads newBind from
//     51EF90's frame, [rsp+330h] at the call site, the byte 51F2CB tests.
//   - The pick: every entry whose min_level <= monster level, in list order;
//     index = (seed * 6AC690C5h + 29Ah) mod eligible count, D2RCore's hash.
//     The default list reproduces D2RCore's "3.2" pet column exactly.
//   - A demon without a name seed gets one first. 51EF90 re-seeds only
//     monsters that are not superunique, champion or unique (51F295: test
//     byte [MonsterData+1Ah],0Eh), so an elite keeps the seed it spawned with,
//     and the ones the game never names from a seed (prime evils) have 0.
//     Seed 0 would give every such demon the same entry (666 mod the eligible
//     count). Such a demon takes one step of its own generator, exactly the
//     step 51EF90 takes for a normal monster (UNITS_GetSeed 34A1E0, called at
//     51F29E: low * 6AC690C5h + high, both halves stored back, the low word is
//     the seed), and the seed is stored with the game's SetNameSeed 38E7B0
//     (MonsterData+18h). That function is taken from the import slot
//     D2RCore!PrepareBindDemonRules calls it through (+1Bh), whose live
//     pointer must be the game's 38E7B0. The seed is saved with the demon, so
//     the restore keeps it and the aura.
//   - The list is published as the "aura-enchanted" service, version 1, the
//     same name and table shape Monster Affixes publishes (candidates(kind,
//     skills, capacity): kind 0 = every skill of the auras key, kind 1 =
//     none), so Monster Display looks for a bound demon's aura in its skill
//     list and names it.
//   - A skill id outside skills.txt is never set: it is checked against the
//     row count at DataTables+11B8h, the bound the skills-record lookup 97790
//     checks (its call to DataTables 300A90 is part of the witness).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 8. How the features meet
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The persistence hooks own five function entries (4FF1A0, 501830, 485350,
//   533AE0, 531510) and the 20 bytes at 533D38. The aura feature patches four
//   call sites inside 51EF90 and 531790 and the entry of
//   D2RCore!ApplyAuraEnchanted. No byte is shared. Every demon the plugin
//   restores, at load or on entering the world, goes through the native
//   restore 531790, so each one passes the aura relays like the first one.
//   Each feature installs on its own: one refusing never takes another down.
//   Unit facts the aura code relies on, read the same way by D2RCore: unit+0
//   type (1 = monster), +4 class id, +8 unit id, +10h MonsterData, +1BDh data
//   context; MonsterData+20h is the 9 byte monster mod list. Monster level is
//   stat 12 through 2F5020 (unit, stat, layer).
//
// ===========================================================================

// The summon block the plugin writes after the bound demon blocks:
// "SUMN", format, a reserved byte, a u16 count, then per summon the
// skills.txt id and the monstats row (u16 each).

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

// The demon 4FF1A0 must return while one block is written, and how deep the
// current thread is inside a demon block read. Saves and loads run on the
// server thread, so each thread keeps its own.

// 55E6F5..55E73D: srvdofunc 31 fills the SummonPet arguments (flags 1 at
// +0, owner +8, monster +10h, AI 0 at +14h, mode +18h, x +1Ch, y +20h,
