// 531510: the native block, every following block that holds a demon, then
// the summon block. The demons of a character without Bind Demon and the
// summons wait for the character to enter the world.

// 51EF90 at 51F29B: non-elite test, new name seed from the unit RNG, call
// D2RCore!PrepareBindDemonRules (thunk 3E2B47E) at 51F2C6, then
// cmp byte ptr [rsp+330h],0 (newBind) at 51F2CB.

// One step of the demon's own generator, the step 51EF90 takes for a normal
// monster (51F2A3): low * 6AC690C5h + high, both halves stored back, the low
// word is the seed. A 0 would leave the demon without a seed, so it steps on.

// Summon Resist Cap
//
// Allied monsters resolve their resistances through the same capped branch the
// game already uses for players: base 75, raised by the per-element max-resist
// stat, hard-ceilinged at 95, floored at -100. Physical (damageresist) keeps
// the engine's own 50 cap. The nightmare/hell difficulty penalty is not applied
// to them.
//
// Which monsters get capped: every monster whose monstats.txt Align byte
// (record + 0x87) is 1, plus the rows listed in [allies] extra_monster_ids.
// Every other monster keeps the engine's uncapped path.
//
// Uncap pairs (3.3.0): [uncap] pairs lists [monster id, stat id] pairs. A
// capped monster of that monstats row is NOT capped while the player who owns
// it has a non-zero total of that stat. It then takes the engine's uncapped
// path exactly like an ordinary monster, so it can pass 100 and become immune.
// Owner and stat are read at every resolution, so the exception follows the
// stat as it comes and goes.
//
// 3.3.1:
//   - [uncap] pairs also takes a single pair written flat, pairs = [423, 511].
//     3.3.0 only read the nested form [[423, 511]] and dropped the flat one
//     with nothing but a log warning, so the pair never existed and the
//     summon stayed capped.
//   - A UTF-8 byte order mark at the start of the TOML is skipped, and a key
//     the plugin does not know, or one written under the wrong [section], is
//     named in the log instead of being ignored silently.
//   - The log lists the pairs read at load, and for every paired summon it
//     writes one line whenever its result changes: the owner found through
//     the owner link, the value of each paired stat on that owner and the
//     decision. summon-resist-cap shows the latest of these lines.
//
// 3.4.0, bound demons:
//   - With Bound Demon Scaling (celestialrayone.bound-demon-scaling) loaded, a
//     bound demon is decided as its pseudo row: that row's Align byte, the
//     extra_monster_ids and the [uncap] pairs all use the pseudo row's id,
//     through the same decision every other monster takes. The row comes from
//     that plugin's "stats-row" service, version 1, asked on every decision.
//   - Every other unit, and every bound demon while that plugin is missing, off
//     or has no pseudo row, is decided by its own monstats row exactly as
//     before. The service is looked up on first use and again every 5 seconds
//     while it is missing; the log says once which of the two it is.
//
// Cap decision service (3.3.0): the gate's decision is also published through
// D2RLoader's plugin communication service as "cap-decision", version 1 (see
// CapDecisionService below). Monster Display asks it for every monster it
// shows, so its numbers follow the Align column, extra_monster_ids and the
// uncap pairs exactly, with no second copy of these settings to keep in sync.
//
// Verified sites, D2RLoader image (3.2.92777 / 3.3.93847 common space):
//   0x4523E0  SUNITDMG_ApplyResistancesAndAbsorb(ctx, resistanceRecord, dontAbsorb)
//   0x452468  difficulty-penalty gate, reads ctx+0x24
//   0x45249C  cap gate: 39 6E 24 (cmp [rsi+24h], ebp) + 75 57 (jnz 0x4524F8)
//   0x4524A1  first instruction of the capping block
//   0x4524F8  first instruction after the capping block
//   0x34B9D0  UNITS_GetUnitType(unit)
//   0x349860  UNITS_GetUnitClassId(unit) -> [unit+4], the monstats row
//   0x34A0E0  UNITS_GetUnitDataContext(unit)
//   0x0976E0  DATATBLS_GetMonStatsTxtRecordForContext(context, classId),
//             returns records + 508 * classId
//   0x4A53C0  D2GAME_GetMinionOwner(unit) -> owner unit, or null
//   0x2F5020  STATLIST_GetUnitStat(unit, statId, layer), the loader's thunk to
//             D2RCore!ReadWideUnitStat
//
// The resolver reads ctx+0x24 exactly twice and nowhere else. A non-zero value
// skips the difficulty penalty at 0x452468 and then skips the entire capping
// block at 0x45249C. That single flag is why an ally can pass 100 and become
// immune. ctx+0x18 is the defender: the resolver reads the resistance itself
// from it (ReadWideUnitStat(ctx[3], resist stat) at 0x452436).
//
// This plugin replaces the five bytes of the cap gate ONLY, with a jmp rel32
// into a relay page, and leaves the function entry pristine so plugins that
// hook the entry (monsterdisplay, for one) keep working. Patching the gate
// rather than the entry also means the penalty gate has already run and already
// skipped the penalty by the time the relay is reached, so nothing has to be
// done about the difficulty penalty at all.
//
// D2RLoader 1.3.1: the loader now accepts a rel32 jump only when its target is
// inside D2R.exe, and the relay page is not. The gate therefore jumps to a
// 5-byte "jmp relay" trampoline in the int3 padding after the resolver
// (0x4526B6; the resolver's ret is at 0x4526B5, the next function starts at
// 0x4526C0), and the trampoline jumps on to the relay. A jmp changes no
// register, flag or stack slot, so the relay sees exactly what the gate saw.
// Re-verified against the 1.3.1 image: rsi = ctx (mov rsi,rcx at 0x4523FD)
// and ebp = 0 (xor ebp,ebp at 0x4523F5) with no later write to either before
// the gate; the gate, both continuations (0x4524A1 mov edx,[r14+0Ch],
// 0x4524F8 cmp dword [r14+8],24h, neither reads the flags) and the four game
// functions the callback calls are unchanged.
//
// Owner lookup, 0x4A53C0, read from the 1.3.1 image for 3.3.0:
//   Returns null unless UNITS_GetUnitType says monster (1). Otherwise it
//   follows MonsterData [unit+10h] to the AI control block [+30h] and returns
//   SUNIT_GetServerUnit (0x48FE80) for the game at [ai+60h], the owner type at
//   [ai+6Ch] and the owner id at [ai+68h]; null when a link is missing or the
//   owner has left. AIGENERAL_SetOwnerData (0x4A5800) writes those fields. The
//   summon spawn helper 0x4350E0 calls it at 0x435373 with the caster's own
//   type and id, right after adding the summon to the caster's pet list
//   (0x4FEB00), and Revive does the same at 0x55EAF1, so a summon's owner is
//   the player who cast it. The game uses this same lookup to credit an
//   owner's magic find for a minion's kill (0x44237B). A minion owned by
//   another minion is followed up the chain, at most MaxOwnerDepth links, until
//   a player is reached; a loop or any other unit type ends with no owner.
//
// Stat read, 0x2F5020: D2RLoader 1.3.x replaces its first 10 bytes with an
//   import thunk into D2RCore's wide stat store:
//     2F5020  FF 25 xx xx xx xx   jmp [rip+disp32]   ; D2RCore!ReadWideUnitStat
//     2F5026  90 90 90 90         nop x4
//     2F502A  48 89 74 24 20 ...  untouched remainder of the old body
//   ReadWideUnitStat (D2RCore 1.3.1 export +0x831DE0, body +0x3D8BD0) returns
//   the unit's total for (statId, layer), and 0 for a null unit, a unit with
//   no stat list, or a stat id of 0x8000 or more. It builds its lookup key from
//   the full 32-bit r8d, so the layer is passed as a 32-bit 0. disp32 locates
//   the loader's import slot, which moves between loader builds, so the check
//   pins only the thunk shape and the old body. The owner lookup and the stat
//   read are checked, and used, only when pairs are configured.

// D2GAME_GetMinionOwner entry, 36 bytes from the 1.3.1 image:
//   4A53C0  40 53 / 48 83 EC 20      push rbx / sub rsp,20h
//   4A53C6  48 8B D9                 mov rbx,rcx
//   4A53C9  E8 02 66 EA FF           call 0x34B9D0       ; UNITS_GetUnitType
//   4A53CE  83 F8 01                 cmp eax,1           ; a monster?
//   4A53D1  0F 85 9E 00 00 00        jne 0x4A5475        ; no: return null
//   4A53D7  48 85 DB / 74 0D         test rbx,rbx / je 0x4A53E9
//   4A53DC  48 8B CB                 mov rcx,rbx
//   4A53DF  E8 EC 65 EA FF           call 0x34B9D0

// STATLIST_GetUnitStat as D2RLoader 1.3.x leaves it: the thunk's
// jmp [rip+disp32] opcode, its four NOPs, and the untouched start of the old
// body (mov [rsp+20h],rsi / push rdi / sub rsp,20h / movzx ebp,r8w /
// mov edi,edx / mov rbx,rcx / test rcx,rcx).

// Owner link, read only for the log. D2GAME_GetMinionOwner (0x4A53C0) reads
// MonsterData [unit+10h] -> AI control [+30h] -> game [+60h], owner id
// [+68h], owner type [+6Ch]; SUNIT_GetServerUnit (0x48FE80) matches the unit
// id at [unit+8].

// monstats.txt Align. Located in the column parser at sub_14039DB30, where the
// "Align" descriptor sits at rbp+0x340 and its record-offset store is
// mov qword [rbp+0x350], 0x87, and CONFIRMED in game: an allied row reads 1
// here while ordinary monsters read 0. 0 = enemy, 1 = ally, 2 = neutral.

// The stack pointer is held in RBP across the callback. RBP is callee-saved
// in the x64 calling convention, so EvaluateCapGate and every game function
// it calls must hand it back unchanged. Up to 3.1.0 the relay held it in
// R11, which is volatile: any callee may overwrite R11, and whenever one did,
// the relay loaded that garbage into RSP and the next pop faulted with an
// unusable stack, which Windows answers by ending the process with no crash
// report. RBP itself is pushed first and popped before the branch, so the
// host function gets its own RBP back on both exits.

// The relay stays mapped and the gate keeps calling it, so the callback
// must remain valid. Disarmed, it reproduces vanilla behaviour exactly,
// and the cap-decision service answers -1.

// Terror Zone Density
//
// Terrorized levels spawn more monsters and more champion and unique packs.
// Settings: celestialrayone.terror-zone-density.toml. Console command: tzdensity.
//
// Everything below was read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process dump (md5
// 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d) with IDA, and out of D2RCore.dll 1.3.1
// (SHA-256 2a868d013d2e0830bd2d9e04b918b19e46a73cf726c833e70d089b948fdeb5a2).
// Every game address the plugin uses is guarded by a byte witness at load.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// When monsters are created
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Not when the game starts. A room gets its monsters the first time it
//   activates, that is when a player comes near it:
//     0x4042A0  room activation (game, room): D2RCore PopulateRoomSpecialMonsters,
//               then, for a room not filled yet: preset monsters 0x48F190,
//               objects 0x503790, 0x510F00, random monsters 0x50ACF0, and
//               0x2F04E0 (room, 1) marks the room filled.
//   Single player and multiplayer work the same way: this runs where the game
//   is hosted, and a room activates when any player comes near it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where the numbers come from
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Every level has a monster region record: game+1D0h -> table[level id].
//   D2RCore's InitializeGameMonsterRegions allocates the table when the game
//   is created and the game fills each record from levels.txt for the game's
//   difficulty. The fields used here:
//     +2B8h int32  density (MonDen of the game's difficulty)
//     +2BCh uint8  minimum packs (MonUMin)
//     +2BDh uint8  maximum packs (MonUMax)
//   The random-monster pass 0x50ACF0 reads them every time a room fills:
//     0x50AD92  cmp [region+2B8h],0 / je end            density 0: no monsters
//     0x50AE10  density capped at 10000 and written back
//     0x50AF03  cmp roll % 100000, [region+2B8h]        one roll per 3x3 subtiles
//     0x50AF40  call 0x419D20 (region, room)             pack or normal group
//   0x419D20 compares +2BCh and +2BDh with the packs the level already spawned
//   (+2C8h). Terror zones do not touch these fields: 0x508F60 only sets the
//   region's terror flag (+2E8h), terror level (+2ECh) and terror config (+2F0h).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What the plugin does
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   An inline hook on 0x50ACF0 runs before the random-monster pass of every
//   room. It reads the room's level (0x2EFC10, the call 0x50ACF0 makes first),
//   asks the game whether that level is terrorized right now (0x489B70, the
//   test the game uses for terror monster levels at 0x508FAA), and reads the
//   level's own values from levels.txt for the game's difficulty. Then:
//     terrorized      the record gets the boosted values
//     not terrorized  the record gets its levels.txt values back, but only if
//                     it still holds this plugin's boosted values
//   A record holding anything else (changed by something else) is left alone
//   and reported once in the log. Nothing is stored between rooms, so a new
//   game, another difficulty or a terror zone rotation needs no bookkeeping.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// levels.txt row (compiled), from the column table built at 0x32EAC0
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   rows at data tables+1460h, row count at +1468h, 18Ch bytes per row,
//   row index = level id (0x32C7ED reads it that way)
//     +22h int16    MonLvlEx(H)
//     +24h int32[3] MonDen, MonDen(N), MonDen(H)
//     +30h uint8[3] MonUMin, MonUMin(N), MonUMin(H)
//     +33h uint8[3] MonUMax, MonUMax(N), MonUMax(H)
//   data tables of a game: 0x300A90 (game+106h)
//   game difficulty: game+104h, uint8 (0x48FF59)
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ABIs (x64)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   random-monster pass  0x50ACF0 (game, room) -> void
//   room level id        0x2EFC10 (room) -> int32, 0 for no room
//   level terrorized     0x489B70 (game, level id) -> bool
//   data tables          0x300A90 (data context byte, 0 to 3) -> tables

// 0x50ACF0, hook prologue: mov r11,rsp / push rbx / push rsi / sub rsp,0B8h.
// Twelve bytes, whole instructions, nothing relative; the next instruction
// is the security cookie load.

// 0x50AD0E: cmp [monsters on],0 / je end / room level 0x2EFC10 (room) /
// mov rcx,[game+1D0h] / call 0x508740 (table, level) / test / je end / argument
// setup of the D2RCore call at 0x50AD59 (not included).

// 0x50AD5E: after the D2RCore call. Region +0Ch room count, cmp [region+2B8h],0 /
// je end, the Chaos Sanctum check, the room box list, then the populated
// level 0x2EFD00, the second region lookup and the density read capped at
// 10000 and written back to [region+2B8h].

// 0x419D20, pack choice (region, room): movzx edi,[region+2C8h] packs spawned /
// movzx r14d,[region+2BDh] maximum / cmp dil,[region+2BCh] minimum / room
// counts +0Ch and +04h.

// 0x489B70, level terrorized (game, level id): region lookup, the game's list
// of terrorized levels (game+5820h, 90h per entry), cross-checked against
// the region's own flag (0x509580).

// 0x32ED42, levels.txt column table, names: MonLvlEx(H) store, then lea of
// "MonDen" "MonDen(N)" "MonDen(H)" "MonUMin" "MonUMax" "MonUMin(N)" "MonUMax(N)"
// "MonUMin(H)" "MonUMax(H)" into their column entries.

// 0x32EE29, the same column entries, type and offset: MonLvlEx(H) word +22h,
// MonDen dword +24h +28h +2Ch, MonUMin byte +30h +31h +32h, MonUMax byte +33h
// +34h +35h.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

// Transfer to Stash
//
// A "Transfer to Stash" button on the inventory panel, shown only while the
// stash is open, that sends every Advanced Stash item in the inventory and in
// the Horadric Cube to the stash. Shift + left click (or holding A on a
// controller) also sends everything else to the shared stash afterwards.
// Where the gold display sits is up to the mod's own inventory layout; the
// plugin does not move it.
//
// Everything below was read out of the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image, and
// every entry used here is checked byte for byte at load. Nothing is installed
// when a check fails.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. The Ctrl+click this copies, 2AAAA0 (the whole function)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Both inventory click handlers (228AB0 legacy, 2C7540 modern) run it on a
//   Ctrl+click while the stash (UI state 18h) is open, once 15A0B0 (item) says
//   the item may go to the Advanced Stash. 15A0B0 checks UI state 18h, then
//   the item class in the Advanced Stash registry (15F320), which only holds
//   classes that have a slot in the stash layout. 2AAAA0 then does:
//     item = widget->vtable[C8h](widget)
//     if (1C7360(item) == 0)                         the item is not busy
//         dest = 46DA50(9A480(8B2D0()))              the Advanced Stash owner
//         if (dest != 0)
//             placement = { 0, 0, engaged 0 }
//             15F8B0(item, dest, 4, 36CFE0(item), 1, &placement)
//     1A0780(3, 0, 0, 0, 0)                          sound 3
//   The plugin runs the same sequence for every item, with the widget lookup
//   replaced by a walk over the player's items (34A360 -> 388C10 / 38ABA0),
//   keeping those on page 0 (inventory) and page 3 (cube). Sound 3 plays once
//   per press instead of once per item.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Why cube items go straight to the stash
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   15F8B0 with mode 1 and an owner carrying state 209 (the Advanced Stash)
//   never looks at the source page: it checks the class registration again
//   (15F320) and calls 159A30, which sends packet 63h, operation 0, item GUID,
//   owner GUID. The server handler 4C4AF0 -> 4F92F0 then needs:
//     - 46DC00: the item's items.txt record +1BDh, which is misc.txt
//       AdvancedStashStackable, not 0
//     - the owner's slot for the class, when it has one, below its cap
//     - 472590 lifting the item from where it sits. 474220 has its own page 3
//       (cube) branch, and 474700 accepts pages 0 and 3 anywhere (page 4 only
//       near the bank).
//   So a cube item needs no stop in the inventory and no free inventory space.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Busy items
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   When 15F8B0 sends a transfer it calls 1C6AC0(item), which gives the item a
//   mode 2 stat list (state 54). 1C7360 reports such an item as busy until the
//   server answers, so pressing the button again before that skips it.
//   1C7360 is called but not checked: the whirlwind plugin hooks it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. The inventory panel, 22BA70 (hooked)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   PlayerInventoryPanel's update, slot 2 of the table at 1CE36D0 (its only
//   reference). It refreshes gold_amount and gold_button, switches the gold
//   button to deposit while UI state 18h is open, and ends in the base widget
//   update. The hook finds the button by name and shows it only while the
//   stash is open, then runs the original.
//     856220 (widget, name)       direct child by name: children at +58h,
//                                 count at +60h, name at child +8h
//     8562A0 (widget, rectOut)    rect at +70h {x, y, width, height} in parent
//                                 space (read for the console)
//     widget vtable +48h / +50h   set enabled / visible: for ButtonWidget
//                                 837D0 writes +50h, F02A0 writes +51h
//     widget vtable +58h          the widget's type; a type's parent at +10h
//     86F0B0                      ButtonWidget type
//   The widget parent is at +30h.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. The buttons
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The buttons are part of the mod's own inventory layouts, so their place,
//   size, font and d-pad links are set there:
//     transfer_to_stash_button             keyboard/mouse inventory
//     transfer_to_stash_controller_button  controller inventory
//   The plugin hands D2RCore their art as plugin resources (frame 0 normal,
//   1 pressed, 2 disabled, 3 hovered) and the keyboard/mouse tooltip string,
//   and its UI message listener consumes their click messages and queues the
//   transfer on the UI thread.
//     keyboard/mouse  368x58 per frame (184x29 px at 1080p)
//     controller      404x82 per frame (202x41 px at 1080p)
//   Up to 1.5.2 the plugin added both buttons itself as SDK child layouts;
//   D2RCore never built the controller one (1.5.2's console listed the
//   controller inventory's children: no Transfer to Stash button among them).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. The Controls key
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   "Transfer to Stash" in Options > Controls, in its own "Stash" section, with
//   no default key, through the SDK's input service. A press while the stash
//   is open queues the same transfer as the button; a key held down counts as
//   one press. The input service runs the handler during input processing, so
//   the handler only reads the stash UI state (0CE500, a byte read) and queues
//   the work on the UI thread. The key always sends materials only.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 7. Shift + left click: everything else goes to the shared stash
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The press first does section 1 (materials to their Advanced Stash tabs),
//   then moves every other inventory and cube item into the shared stash, the
//   way Ctrl+click moves an item into a shared page, one item at a time.
//   Shift is read with GetAsyncKeyState when the click message arrives, the
//   same test the game's own item widgets make (120A100 with VK_SHIFT).
//
//   Shared stash pages. Each page is a hidden player unit (a proxy, state 186)
//   listed in the local player's inventory at +68h, the list the Advanced Stash
//   owner is in too. Per entry: 2EF880 the proxy id (+0), 38CD60 remove-only
//   (+8), 38CD50 active (+9), 38CD80 type (+0Ah, 1 = the Advanced Stash),
//   38CD70 next (+10h). A page can take items when it is not the Advanced
//   Stash, not remove-only and active; the server refuses the other two kinds
//   (4C5570 / 4C7690 log "remove-only" and "inactive"). Pages are numbered
//   in that list order, skipping the Advanced Stash and remove-only entries,
//   and only the first 388C70() of them are shared tabs (388C70 is the shared
//   tab count the bank and the gold limit use). That is exactly how D2RCore
//   numbers the normal shared tabs (D2RCore 41C250), so page 1 here is tab 1
//   in game. Pages are filled in that order. 09A5D0(id, 0) gives the proxy
//   unit on this client.
//
//   The request. 15F8B0 sends opcode 55h (CCMD_GRIDSHAREDSTASHTRANSACTION)
//   through 0EC8F0(55h, item id, proxy id, source page, source position,
//   destination page 4, destination position, 0), but always with the proxy
//   of the page on screen (UI model key 8D4D18A675312BD4), so the plugin calls
//   0EC8F0 itself with each page's own id. Source position is 1601E0(item),
//   the destination cell is INVENTORY_FindFreePosition 3865B0 on the proxy's
//   page 4 grid (34A410(proxy, 4, !08D3B0())). Then 1C6AC0 marks the item busy,
//   as 15F8B0 does. The server handler 4C7690 runs the same 3865B0 search on its
//   own copy of the page and refuses the request unless the cell matches, so
//   only one shared request is in flight at a time: the next item is placed
//   once the server has answered (the item left the inventory or cube, or its
//   busy mark was cleared). That keeps every request identical to Ctrl+click.
//
//   Items that stay where they are: Tome of Town Portal (tbk), Tome of
//   Identify (ibk), the Horadric Cube itself (box), items 424550(player, item)
//   refuses for the shared stash (the game's own rule, items.txt record +13Ah)
//   and anything still on its way somewhere (busy). The cursor must be empty;
//   the server refuses 55h while the player holds an item (4C7690, 388A70).
//
//   Multiplayer. Both requests are the client's own Ctrl+click requests (63h
//   and 55h), so the host checks them as usual: the plugin is needed only by
//   the player who presses the button, on the host or on a TCP/IP client.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 8. Controller: tap and hold
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   ButtonWidget input 8F0F00 (hooked) activates a focused button on the
//   InputMessage command 1E439768F7DD4594 with payload action +10h = 0. The
//   payload byte +19h is the hold flag: the item widgets (2A98B0) use it for
//   their "hold" variants of the same action. The hook only notes, for the
//   plugin's controller button, whether the activation was a tap or a hold,
//   then runs the original. The controller button sends its own click text,
//   so the UI message listener knows the note belongs to it: tap sends the
//   materials, hold sends everything. This hook is optional: when its byte
//   check or install fails, the rest of the plugin still works and a hold
//   counts as a tap.

// The keyboard/mouse tooltip, as a string of the plugin's own namespace. A
// layout field shows its text as written, so a color code there is drawn as
// plain characters; strings loaded from a string table have their color codes
// turned into the renderer's marker (U+E07E) by D2RCore's string loader, like
// the game's own strings. Two lines, each starting with the tooltip gray (color
// code 5). Every game language gets the same English text, since a missing
// translation shows nothing. A mod can replace it from its own string files
// with the full key celestialrayone.transfer-to-stash:TransferButtonTooltip.

struct Witness {
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
    const char*         name;
};

using TransferFn           = std::uint8_t(__fastcall*)(void* item, void* owner, std::uint8_t destinationPage,
                                                       std::uint8_t sourcePage, std::uint8_t mode,
                                                       TransferPlacement* placement);

using SharedRequestFn      = void(__fastcall*)(std::uint8_t command, std::uint32_t item, std::uint32_t proxy,
                                               std::uint32_t sourcePage, std::uint32_t sourcePosition,
                                               std::uint32_t destinationPage, std::uint32_t destinationPosition,
                                               std::uint32_t fromShared);

// ===========================================================================
// Transmute Hotkey (celestialrayone.transmute-hotkey)
// ===========================================================================
//
// D2RLoader's own "Transmute Horadric Cube" action (Controls menu) works with
// the cube closed and repeats while the key is held. The TOML documents the
// behaviour.
//
// Built against D2R 3.3 in the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image (base
// 0x140000000), D2RCore.dll 1.3.1-beta and PluginSDK 0.3.0. Client side.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. The native action
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   D2RCore registers the action "cube_transmute" ("Transmute Horadric Cube")
//   at D2RCore+0x1CD9CA with the callback D2RCore+0x328950. The callback:
//     - returns Ignored unless the event kind ([event+10h]) is 1 (pressed), so
//       holding or releasing the key does nothing;
//     - returns Ignored unless panel 0x19 (Horadric Cube) or 0x18 (stash) is
//       open, through game 0xCE500;
//     - otherwise posts the UI message "Convert" to HoradricCubePanelMessage or
//       BankPanelMessage, exactly like clicking Convert.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. What Convert does
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Both panels handle Convert with two calls that take no arguments: the
//   cube panel's message handler 0x2CCFA0 (at 0x2CD03A) and the stash panel's
//   0x23BCF0 (at 0x23C23C) both run 0x2CC060 then 0x2CD720. 0x2CD720 walks
//   the local player's inventory, collects the items on page 3 (the cube),
//   finds the cube itself (item code 'box '), sorts the list and sends it
//   through the CCMD_TRANSMUTE builder 0xED120. Nothing in it needs a panel.
//   The server side (packet handler 0x4C1590, check 0x4F2490) only refuses a
//   dead player, state 54, an item on the cursor, a list that does not match
//   the cube's contents, or a cube that is not a 'box ' item, and applies the
//   transmute in a later update (0x4ADAD0). It does not require the cube to
//   be open.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. What the plugin does
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The callback's 17 byte prologue (push rbp / push rsi / sub rsp,268h /
//   lea rbp,[rsp+80h]) is replaced with a jump to TransmuteAction, which:
//     - on press, transmutes at once (0x2CC060 then 0x2CD720, the Convert
//       sequence) and starts the repeat;
//     - while the key is held, transmutes again after repeat_delay_ms, then
//       every repeat_interval_ms, polled on the UI thread through D2RLoader's
//       ThreadService;
//     - stops on release, when the bound key (and its modifier) is no longer
//       physically down, or outside a game (D2RCore!IsInGame).
//   The whole native callback body and its registration are byte-checked
//   before anything is written, and the prologue is put back on unload.
// ===========================================================================

// Trap Owner Credit
//
// Credits the kills and the weapon-damage hits of listed monsters (assassin
// traps by default) to the player who owns them. The behaviour write-up is the
// embedded TOML below (celestialrayone.trap-owner-credit.toml); this header is
// the native map.
//
// Built for D2R 3.3 in the D2RLoader 1.3.1 process image (base 0x140000000),
// PluginSDK 0.3.0. Every byte below was read out of that image and
// disassembled before being relied on. Every window is checked at load: a part
// whose bytes do not match is left out and named in the log, the rest runs.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where the game fires the events: SUNITDMG_ExecuteEvents 0x44CE80
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   (game rcx -> r15, attacker rdx -> r14, defender r8 -> rsi,
//    bMissile r9d -> ebx, D2Damage* argument 5 -> rdi)
//   44CF98  movzx eax, word [rdi+4]        result flags
//   44CF9C  test al,20h / jne 44D001       soft hit: no hit events
//   44CFA0  test al,1   / je  44D001       no successful hit: no hit events
//   44CFA4  test ebx,ebx / je 44CFD1       melee branch
//   44CFA8  mov eax,[rdi] / test al,20h / je 44D001
//           missile: no hit events at all unless hit flag 20h is set.
//           SUNITDMG_FillDamageValues 0x44C030 sets it at 44C0F1 when it adds
//           the attacker's weapon damage (SrcDam).
//   44CFAE  test al,al / js 44CFCA         hit flag 80h: no domissiledamage
//   44CFC5  E8 A6 05 00 00  call 44D570    domissiledamage (6), attacker  RELAYED
//   44CFE4  E8 87 05 00 00  call 44D570    domeleedamage (5), attacker    RELAYED
//   44CFFC  E8 6F 05 00 00  call 44D570    damagedinmelee / damagedbymissile
//                                          (1 / 2) on the defender, untouched
//   44D4FE  movzx eax, word [rdi+4] / test al,20h / jne / test al,2 / je
//   44D51D  E8 4E 00 00 00  call 44D570    killed (10) on the defender, untouched
//   44D535  E8 36 00 00 00  call 44D570    kill (9) on the attacker       RELAYED
//   The three relayed calls pass (game, event, attacker, defender, D2Damage*)
//   and their return value is never read.
//
// D2GAME_ApplyUnitStatEvent 0x44D570 (game, eventId, unit, other, damage)
//   builds its callback object at rsp+30h and calls the dispatcher at 44D5F2
//   with (game, eventId, unit, other, damage, &object), returning its result.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Damage over time deaths: D2GAME_MONSTER_ApplyStatRegen 0x448C00
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The negative life tick (poison, bleed, burning, Open Wounds).
//   448DC7..448E2E  killer = SUNIT_GetServerUnit(game, owner type, owner id)
//                   of the state 2 stat list, else of the state 62 list
//   448EA4  E8 37 F3 13 00  call 5881E0    killed (10) on the victim, untouched
//   448EF5  test rbx,rbx / je              no killer: no kill event at all
//   448F17  [rsp+20h] = r15 = 0 (no damage packet), [rsp+28h] = &object
//   448F36  E8 A5 F2 13 00  call 5881E0    kill (9) on the killer         RELAYED
//
// 0x5881E0 is D2RLoader's jmp into D2RCore's event dispatcher, called with
// (game, eventId, unit, other, damage, object) -> int32.
//
// Every other code caller of 0x44D570 and 0x5881E0 was read as well. They fire
// events 0, 3, 7, 11, 12, 13, 15 and 16 only. So the four relayed calls are
// every place the game fires kill, domeleedamage and domissiledamage.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Owner, type, class
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   D2GAME_GetMinionOwner 0x4A53C0: MonsterData [unit+10h] -> AI control
//     [+30h] -> SUNIT_GetServerUnit(game [+60h], type [+6Ch], id [+68h]).
//     It asserts when MonsterData or the AI control block is missing
//     (4A5416, 4A543A), so the plugin checks both pointers first.
//   SUNIT_IsDead 0x34C2C0: dead flag [unit+124h] & 10000h, players in mode 0
//     or 17, monsters in mode 0 or 12.
//   UNITS_GetUnitType 0x34B9D0 returns [unit+0]; UNITS_GetClassId 0x349860
//     returns [unit+4], the monstats.txt row. Both assert on a null unit, so
//     the plugin reads the two fields itself after its own null check.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// How the calls are relayed
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Only the rel32 of each call changes. It lands in a page allocated next to
//   the image that holds two FF 25 absolute jumps (target qword 8-byte
//   aligned at +8 of each 16-byte slot), one into HookedUnitEvent
//   (the three 0x44D570 calls) and one into HookedDotKill (the 0x5881E0 call).
//   Both hooks take the callee's exact arguments and make the original call,
//   so nothing else in either function sees a difference. The function
//   entries 0x44D570 and 0x5881E0 stay untouched. D2RLoader restores the four
//   calls when the plugin unloads; on unload the plugin also points both relay
//   jumps at the native callees, and the page is never freed.

struct CallSite {
    const char*         name;
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    std::uint64_t       windowRva;
    const std::uint8_t* window;
    std::uint32_t       windowSize;
    std::uint64_t       nativeTarget;
    std::size_t         relaySlot;
};

// On unload both relay jumps are pointed straight at the native callees, so
// the four calls behave exactly like stock whether D2RLoader has restored them
// yet or not, and nothing can jump into the unloaded DLL.

// D2RLoader restores the four calls. Until then the relay jumps straight to
// the native callees, and a thread already inside a hook passes its event
// through unchanged. The relay page stays mapped: a thread may be in it.

// Treasure Goblin
//
// A new monster AI for treasure goblins, selected through its own monai.txt
// row, plus a timed escape: after a set time the goblin stops, a portal opens
// next to it, and three seconds later both are gone. The goblin never
// dies on the way out, so it drops nothing and gives no experience.
//
// Written for D2R 3.3 on D2RLoader 1.3.1. Every address and byte below was read
// out of the live D2RLoader.exe process image (md5 8af7a47ce9077de0378c69af2930854d)
// and disassembled before being relied on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. How a monster gets its AI
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   monstats.txt's AI column is stored as a monai.txt row number, a signed
//   word at monstats record +0x52. 0x4A36C0 turns it into a 0x20-byte record
//   of the built-in AI table at 0x2396E90 (155 rows):
//     +0x00 target mode. 2 = the AI also runs when no target is in reach.
//     +0x08 setup routine, optional
//     +0x10 think routine, called as think(game, monster, tick)
//     +0x18 special-state routine, optional
//   A row number above 154 falls back to row 0, the do-nothing AI.
//   0x4A36C0 is asked when the AI is set up (0x4A2690 stores the record's
//   think routine in the monster's AI control at +0x08) and on every AI tick
//   (0x4A2A00 reads the target mode, picks the target, then calls the stored
//   think routine). The hook on 0x4A36C0 hands monsters whose row is the
//   TreasureGoblin row this plugin's record instead of row 0. The game does
//   not keep monai.txt names after loading, so the row is found by reading the
//   same monai.txt the game loaded and checking its row count against the
//   loaded table.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. The tick the think routine receives
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   +0x10 target picked for target mode 2 (0x595750), may be null
//   +0x30 monstats2 record. Mode flags at record +0xF0, bit 15 = mRN.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Running
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   AITACTICS_MoveToTarget 0x4A8A10 (game, unit, target, mode, x, y, step,
//   flags). Mode 15 runs, mode 2 walks; the game itself turns a run into a
//   walk under Decrepify. With flags 0 a refused move returns 0 and changes
//   nothing, so the next direction can be tried at once. When a walk or run
//   ends the game calls the AI tick straight away, so runs chain without a
//   pause. AITACTICS_IdleInNeutralMode 0x4A6D10 (game, unit, frames) stops in
//   neutral and books the next tick (event 2) frames later.
//   The ground check is the game's own line test COLLISION_RayTrace 0x363F20
//   (room, start, end, mask): 0 when the whole line is free, else 1 with the
//   blocked point written to end. It crosses room borders by itself. The mask
//   is the monster pathing mask 0x3C01 (wall, object, door, no-path, pet).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. The portal
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The portal is objects.txt row 189, the portal Cain and the freed Act 5
//   prisoners leave through (the A5Q2 and A1Q4 quest code spawns it the same
//   way). Row 189 is Selectable 0 in every mode, OperateFn 0 and SubClass 0.
//   Clickability is not something the server can hand a client: every client
//   recomputes it in its own 0x34E5F0 (unit flags +0x124, bit 0x2, from
//   objects.txt Selectable of the new mode, record +0x85) whenever the object
//   is created or changes mode. Row 59, the town portal, is Selectable 1 once
//   open, so it can never stay unclickable; row 189 is never clickable, on any
//   client, and has no portal label or use.
//   Its InitFn 61 (0x5DBF20) sets the opening mode and books a quest update
//   (event 7) 25 frames later. The quest code behind that event belongs to Cain
//   and the Act 5 prisoners, so the plugin deletes the event and drives the
//   portal itself. Spawning follows the game's own portal spawn,
//   D2GAME_CreatePortalObject 0x432CE0 (block at 0x432E05, verified byte for
//   byte):
//     free spot  0x3632F0 (room, &spot, &spot, 3, 0x3E01, 0xC01, 0)
//     room       0x38DFB0 (room, x, y)
//     spawn      0x490410 (2, 189, x, y, game, room, 1, 1, 0, 0)
//     quest      0x48B890 (game, portal, 7, 0) deletes the booked event
//     opening    0x34E5F0 (portal, 1)
//     open       after (objects.txt record +0x9C >> 8) + 1 frames, mode 2
//   Removal is the game's own portal removal: 0x2EF4D0 (room, 2, guid) tells
//   the clients, then 0x48FAA0 (game, unit).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Timers and leaving
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   The game's timer setup 0x48BE80 (game, unit, event, frame, callback, id,
//   param, extra) stores an optional callback at timer +0x48; the timer run at
//   0x48C545 calls callback(game, unit, event, id, param) instead of the unit's
//   default handler. The timer code is built for a unit removing itself during
//   its own timer. Event 11 is used: monsters never use it and nothing deletes
//   it, and the callback replaces the default handler anyway.
//     goblin  one timer at escape + 75 frames: leaves (skipped when dead)
//     portal  one timer at the end of its opening, one at escape + 75 frames
//   Leaving is the sequence the 3.3 Act 5 prisoners use to walk out through
//   their portal (0x5D0A58, verified byte for byte): stop path 0x342B40, delete
//   the AI tick 0x48B890, tell the clients 0x2EF4D0, free collision 0x348B00,
//   leave the room 0x38EFE0, remove 0x48FAA0. No death: no loot, no experience.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. Fields read
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   unit  +0x00 type, +0x04 class, +0x08 guid, +0x0C mode, +0x10 monster or
//         object data, +0x38 path, +0x124 flags (0x2 clickable, 0x10000 dead)
//   path  +0x02 x, +0x06 y (words)
//   game  +0x106 data table context, +0x170 frame
//
//   The callbacks and the cached think routine point into this DLL: do not
//   unload the plugin while a game is running.

// 0x4A36C0, hooked: mov [rsp+20h],rsi / push rdi / sub rsp,30h /
// mov [rsp+40h],rbx / xor edi,edi / mov [rsp+50h],rbp / mov ebp,edx /
// mov rbx,rcx. Whole instructions, no branch, no RIP-relative operand.

// 0x48C545 timer run: mov r10,[rbx+48h] / mov rcx,rdi / mov rdx,[rbx+8] /
// test r10,r10 / jz default / mov eax,[rbx+1Ch] / mov r9d,[rbx+18h] /
// movzx r8d,byte [rbx] / mov [rsp+20h],eax / call r10

using MoveToTargetFn      = std::int32_t (*)(void* game, void* unit, void* target, std::int32_t mode,
                                             std::int32_t x, std::int32_t y, std::uint8_t step,
                                             std::int16_t flags) noexcept;

using InitTimerFn         = void* (*)(void* game, void* unit, std::int32_t event, std::uint32_t frame,
                                      void* callback, std::int32_t id, std::int32_t param,
                                      std::int32_t extra) noexcept;

using GetFreeCoordsFn     = void* (*)(void* room, Coord* spot, Coord* field, std::int32_t unitSize,
                                      std::uint32_t mask, std::uint32_t fieldMask,
                                      std::int32_t neighbourRooms) noexcept;

using AllocUnitFn         = void* (*)(std::int32_t type, std::int32_t classId, std::int32_t x, std::int32_t y,
                                      void* game, void* room, std::int16_t init, std::int32_t mode,
                                      std::int32_t unused, void* unusedPointer) noexcept;

struct Witness {
    const char*         name;
    std::uint64_t       rva;
    const std::uint8_t* bytes;
    std::uint32_t       size;
};

// Uber Affixes
//
// Sets the affixes (monumod.txt unique monster mods) the six uber bosses get
// when they spawn, replacing the fixed list the game hardcodes for each one.
// See celestialrayone.uber-affixes.toml for the behaviour write-up.
//
// Read out of the live D2RLoader 1.3.1 image (D2R 3.3.93847, base
// 0x140000000) in IDA before being relied on:
//
//   0x494A60 (spawn, monster), the special-monster setup. Its only code
//       reference is the call at 0x4943F2 in the monster spawn 0x493CC0,
//       which ignores the return value, so it runs once for every monster
//       that spawns. spawn+0x00 is the game (mov rdi,[rcx] at 0x494A78) and
//       spawn+0x18 the monstats.txt row (mov ebp,[rcx+18h] at 0x494A72). It
//       switches on the row's base monster, and for the six uber rows it does
